\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 More than 10,000 US hotel workers strike on Labor Day weekend\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Tait \par \uc2 422 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2024\par 07:40 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Staff demand wage increases and reversal of pandemic-era cutbacks that impose \u8216\'20\'18painful\u8217\'20\'19 working conditions\par \par \uc2 Thousands of US hotel workers went on strike on Sunday for improved pay and conditions in a dispute likely to disrupt many Labor Day weekend holiday travelers, amid union warnings that industrial action could escalate.\par \par \uc2 More than 10,000 workers walked off the job at hotels in Boston, Seattle, Honolulu, Kauai and Greenwich, Connecticut, as well as the Californian cities of San Francisco, San Diego and San Jose after contract talks with the establishments\u8217\'20\'19 owners collapsed.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  union, which represents workers in hotels, casinos and airports across the US and Canada, warned that staff in other city were ready to join the strike.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CStrikes have also been authorised and could begin at any time,\u8221\'20\'1D a union statement said, adding that hotels in Baltimore, Providence, Oakland, New Haven could be affected.\par \par \uc2 Staff are demanding wage increases and the reversal of pandemic era job cuts that union organisers say has increased the workload of remaining workers and imposed \u8220\'20\'1Cpainful\u8221\'20\'1D working conditions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe hotel industry has rebounded from the pandemic, and room rates are at record highs,\u8221\'20\'1D Gwen Mills, \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s international president, said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CBut hotel workers can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to live in the cities that they welcome guests to. Too many hotel workers have to work two or sometimes three jobs in order to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe won\u8217\'20\'19t accept a \u8216\'20\'18new normal\u8217\'20\'19 where hotel companies profit by cutting their offerings to guests and abandoning their commitments to workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The union said that, as of Sunday morning, the strike had impacted 24 hotels in eight cities.\par \par \uc2 It was taking place on a weekend which was expected to be the busiest on Labour Day records, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/2024/09/01/hotel-strike-union/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to Transport Security Administration}}}\uc2  forecasts.\par \par \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 , which has more than 275,000 members, has accused the hotel industry of using cutbacks triggered by Covid-related lockdowns to permanently cut staff and reduce guest services.\par \par \uc2 It has asked traveling guests at affected hotels to cancel their visit and demand refunds.\par \par \uc2 The strike, which is scheduled to last three days, follows months of talks between workers and the Hilton, \uc2 Hyatt\uc2 , \uc2 Marriott\uc2  and \uc2 Omni\uc2  hotel chains.\par \par \uc2 Hyatt\uc2  said it was disappointed by the decision to strike. \u8220\'20\'1CWe look forward to continuing to negotiate fair contracts and recognise the contributions of \uc2 Hyatt\uc2  employees,\u8221\'20\'1D said Michael D\u8217\'20\'19Angelo, the firm\u8217\'20\'19s head of labour relations.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240901ek91001s2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
