\par\par \uc2 Environment\par \b \uc2 US delivery workers swelter in record heat \u8211\'20\'13 many without AC in their vans\b0\par\par\uc2 Dharna Noor \par \uc2 1,588 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2024\par 14:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Amid rising temperatures last year, unionized \uc2 UPS\uc2  workers made heat an issue \u8211\'20\'13 but despite a key contract win, workers say little progress has been made\par \par \uc2 Seth Pacic works as a \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  delivery driver in Dallas, Texas. In the summer, he has a second \u8220\'20\'1Cfull-time job\u8221\'20\'1D: staying cool.\par \par \uc2 Each morning, Pacic packs his cooler full of ice, water jugs and hydrating foods such as pickles and grapes. He straps frozen cooling packs to his wrists, hangs another around his neck and ensures he has powdered sports drinks and a battery-operated fan on hand.\par \par \uc2 Off the clock, Pacic organizes his \u8220\'20\'1Cwhole diet\u8221\'20\'1D around hydration, eating many electrolyte-rich yogurts, fruits and dark green vegetables while consuming two gallons of water a day.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m seriously willing to try almost anything to mitigate heat issues,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Pacic, who is also a union steward, says these lengths help him to avert heat exhaustion, but the sweltering summer temperatures still take a toll. Dizziness and lightheadedness are \u8220\'20\'1Cpar for the course\u8221\'20\'1D; nausea and brain fog are common.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ll be in the back of the van looking for a package, and I\u8217\'20\'19ll forget where I am and have to step out to see which stop I\u8217\'20\'19m at,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat happens every summer to me and basically every driver.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The company\u8217\'20\'19s dark aluminum package vans can amplify outdoor heat, with workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vice.com/en/article/m7ggnv/ups-drivers-are-sharing-heat-readings-of-120f-inside-their-trucks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 having recorded temperatures}}}\uc2  over 120F (49C) in the vehicles.\par \par \uc2 The heat is not merely uncomfortable but also dangerous, with drivers suffering {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wfaa.com/article/news/local/heat-blamed-north-texas-ups-driver-illness-work-deliveries/287-c92cb62d-7e54-46ad-9d06-1cc76d811ba1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 heatstroke}}}\uc2  on the job each summer and incidents turning {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kwtx.com/2021/10/28/family-waco-ups-worker-who-died-job-file-wrongful-death-lawsuit/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fatal}}}\uc2  in some cases. Pacic has seen these dangers in his own region: this month, a \uc2 UPS\uc2  driver was hospitalized after getting into an accident while experiencing heat exhaustion, union officials {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcdfw.com/news/local/ups-driver-crashes-in-north-texas-due-to-alleged-heat-exhaustion/3623936/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 say}}}\uc2 . And last August, Chris Begley, a 28-year \uc2 UPS\uc2  veteran, suffered a medical emergency while driving in 103F heat. He died four days later.\par \par \uc2 Amid spiking temperatures last summer, 340,000 unionized \uc2 UPS\uc2  workers made heat a key issue during labor negotiations with their employer. They secured a major win when, as part of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://about.ups.com/us/en/newsroom/press-releases/people-led/ups-statement-agreement-with-teamsters-on-heat-safety.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new union contract}}}\uc2 , the company {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://about.ups.com/us/en/newsroom/press-releases/people-led/ups-statement-agreement-with-teamsters-on-heat-safety.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreed}}}\uc2  that each of the iconic chocolate-brown package vans it purchased after 1 January would include air conditioning \u8211\'20\'13 part of a commitment to equip 28,000 package cars with the cooling devices by the contract\u8217\'20\'19s end on 31 July 2028.\par \par \uc2 Today, however, the union says \uc2 UPS\uc2  has made little progress toward that goal. CNN reported last month that it has not purchased any new vans since 1 January, and as such, only a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2024/06/22/business/ups-delivery-vans-air-conditioning/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 small fraction}}}\uc2  of its delivery drivers have access to cooling technology.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C[T]he company is behind on air conditioning, and that is unacceptable,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kara Deniz, a spokesperson for the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2 , which represents unionized \uc2 UPS\uc2  workers.\par \par \uc2 Jim Mayer, a spokesperson for \uc2 UPS\uc2 , would not confirm whether or not the company has purchased any vans since 1 January. He said that the company has \u8220\'20\'1Chundreds of vehicles with AC operating on the roads today\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe will continue to purchase and deploy new vehicles with AC as quickly as possible,\u8221\'20\'1D he said in an email.\par \par \uc2 But workers interviewed by the \uc2 Guardian\uc2  say they have never seen air conditioning in one of \uc2 UPS\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s delivery vans \u8211\'20\'13 only in other company vehicles, such as trailers and small \u8220\'20\'1Csprinter\u8221\'20\'1D cargo vans which are too small to use for full-day delivery routes. Mayer declined to clarify.\par \par \uc2 John Cashion, a \uc2 UPS\uc2  mechanic in Texas\u8217\'20\'19s Fort Worth area, said he thinks \uc2 UPS\uc2  prepared for its agreement to purchase air conditioned vans ahead of time \u8211\'20\'13 by buying more vehicles ahead of negotiations. In late 2022, he saw the company purchase dozens of new company vans, he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think \u8230\'20\'26 \uc2 UPS\uc2  knew what they were going to do and that\u8217\'20\'19s why the contract language reads how it reads,\u8221\'20\'1D Cashion added.\par \par \uc2 Mayer did not respond to the comments, but said \uc2 UPS\uc2  is \u8220\'20\'1Con plan or ahead of schedule on all our contractual commitments\u8221\'20\'1D related to heat.\par \par \uc2 In last year\u8217\'20\'19s union contract, \uc2 UPS\uc2  also agreed to provide drivers with one gallon water bottles and ice. Mayer said it has supplied over 96,000 water jugs to drivers and ensured all employees have access to cool water and ice.\par \par \uc2 The company also committed to retrofitting existing vans with fans, airflow-increasing vents, and exhaust heat shields. So far, the company has installed over 200,000 fans in package vans, and has equipped more than 74,000 delivery vehicles with air vents and over 76,000 delivery vehicles with exhaust heat shields. The latter can lower vehicle floor temperatures by 17F, the company says.\par \par \uc2 Workers the Guardian spoke to say the technologies are, to varying degrees, ineffective \u8211\'20\'13 the fans too weak, the heat shields too small. None of these other provisions can be a replacement for AC, they add.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think a lot of people read about the contract last summer and now they think \uc2 UPS\uc2  drivers have AC,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ben Reynolds, a delivery driver and union steward in Kansas City, Missouri. \u8220\'20\'1CBut that\u8217\'20\'19s just not the case.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Asked about the complaints, Mayer said: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s clear you\u8217\'20\'19ve been talking to a small group of unhappy employees who aren\u8217\'20\'19t representative of the hundreds of thousands of \uc2 UPS\uc2  employees across the country.\u8221\'20\'1D In stories published on its website, he said, the company has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://about.ups.com/us/en/our-stories/people-led/how-one-ups-driver-mastered-arizona-s-desert-summers.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 highlighted}}}\uc2  other {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://about.ups.com/us/en/our-stories/people-led/ups-driver-joel-reyez-shares-his-tips-to-working-in-the-heat.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 workers}}}\uc2  who say their personal habits and company support have helped them beat the heat.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe company is doing their part; as drivers, we have to do ours,\u8221\'20\'1D one employee with 20-year\u8217\'20\'19s driving experience said in one post on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://about.ups.com/us/en/our-stories/people-led/ups-driver-joel-reyez-shares-his-tips-to-working-in-the-heat.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 company\u8217\'20\'19s}}}\uc2  website.\par \par \uc2 Mayer also noted that \uc2 UPS\uc2  has partnered with various firms to advance heat safety. It has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://about.ups.com/us/en/our-company/great-employer/5-ways-ups-is-improving-heat-safety-for-employees.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 worked with the brand Mission}}}\uc2 , which specializes in cooling fabrics, to distribute hats and other specialized garments. And it has partnered with experts from the Gatorade Sports Science Institute and the University of Connecticut\u8217\'20\'19s Korey Stringer Institute \u8220\'20\'1Cto study various working conditions and further improve our training to help our employees work safely\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Korey Stringer Institute\u8217\'20\'19s chief executive officer, Doug Casa, said when the partnership officially began 18 months ago, \uc2 UPS\uc2  was already \u8220\'20\'1C already very proactive with team safety strategies\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said he supports \uc2 UPS\uc2  bringing on air conditioned vehicles, but said that is \u8220\'20\'1Cjust one small piece of the puzzle\u8221\'20\'1D of heat safety since many drivers spend so much time out of their vehicles on their shift. \u8220\'20\'1CI need those people to also be doing other things to keep themselves safe,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, though he added that \uc2 UPS\uc2  should always be providing them with the materials to do so.\par \par \uc2 But in the absence of the new air conditioned brown vans, employees are still suffering.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSometimes it feels like they don\u8217\'20\'19t care about us at all,\u8221\'20\'1D said Pacic.\par \par \uc2 The prioritization of efficiency makes matters worse, workers interviewed by the Guardian say. They say managers encourage employees to move quickly and limit their breaks, despite research that shows additional rest promotes safety amid extreme temperatures.\par \par \uc2 The company also track company vehicles, pushing workers to slash seconds from minor aspects of their routes and even reprimanding them for taking the time to fill their water jugs once they are on the clock, said Reynolds. And in his building, they have been giving workers longer hours, making it more difficult to recuperate after long shifts in the heat, he added.\par \par \uc2 Reynolds says two drivers in his shop have had to be taken to hospital due to heat illness this summer. In one case, the \u8220\'20\'1Cpassed out\u8221\'20\'1D employee was found by a passerby who called an ambulance, he said. On both occasions, he said management appeared more concerned about recording lost time injuries \u8211\'20\'13 an injury sustained on the job resulting in lost work hours \u8211\'20\'13 than about their wellbeing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was like, \u8216\'20\'18are you out of your mind,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut that\u8217\'20\'19s the company culture.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Asked about the allegations, \uc2 UPS\uc2  said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe safety of our employees is our top priority.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile we ask our drivers to work efficiently to serve customers, we also are monitoring to make sure they take their breaks, particularly in hot weather. And if an employee shows signs of heat related illness, our policy is to immediately seek medical attention,\u8221\'20\'1D Mayer said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re confident that our policies are followed by an overwhelming majority of our drivers and management. And we take corrective action when we become aware that a policy is not being followed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mayer also disputed the characterization of both heat illness incidents. \u8220\'20\'1CIn both cases, we learned our management team responded quickly and neither driver was found passed out by a passerby.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As temperatures get hotter due to global warming, access to AC will be all the more important for delivery drivers. Summer temperatures {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2023/09/07/texas-hottest-summer-2023/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 across}}}\uc2  the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kcur.org/news/2023-12-19/2023-has-been-one-of-missouris-hottest-years-on-record-and-one-of-its-driest-too"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 country}}}\uc2  are becoming more severe; last month, the globe saw its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/article/2024/jul/23/world-temperature-records-shattered-hottest-day-climate-crisis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hottest day in history}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the absence of more safety measures, Reynolds said he and other stewards are encouraging workers to move slowly and safely.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s stuff we deliver to hospitals, but more often we\u8217\'20\'19re talking about bullshit I\u8217\'20\'19m delivering that customers ordered from \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , and people are dying over it,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe should be well past the point of that being acceptable, as a society. The only question from \uc2 UPS\uc2  should be: \u8216\'20\'18hat are we doing to stop this from ever happening again?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240823ek8n0018j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
