\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 New US job market numbers weaker than expected as Fed eyes interest rate cut\b0\par\par\uc2 Dominic Rushe in New York \par \uc2 479 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 August 2024\par 18:40 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The US created 818,000 fewer jobs than first calculated as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  hints at a rate cut\par \par \uc2 The US job market appears weaker than first thought, according to official figures released on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The US created 818,000 fewer jobs than first calculated in the 12 months to the end of March, a 0.5% decrease, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesprelbmk.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quarterly census}}}\uc2  of employment and wages.\par \par \uc2 The revision was in line with economists\u8217\'20\'19 predictions but it still suggests that hiring has not been as robust as the official figures first showed. The figures indicate that employers have been adding about 174,000 jobs per month, a solid pace but 28% lower than the previously reported rate of about 242,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 The news comes as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  weighs a cut in its benchmark interest rate, the first since March 2020. The chair of the Fed, \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , has signaled that the central bank is leaning towards cutting rates after raising them to tamp down inflation.\par \par \uc2 Minutes from the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s meeting in July were released on Wednesday. \u8220\'20\'1CThe vast majority\u8221\'20\'1D of participants \u8220\'20\'1Cobserved that, if the data continued to come in about as expected, it would likely be appropriate to ease policy at the next meeting\u8221\'20\'1D, the summary stated.\par \par \uc2 Powell will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/19/federal-reserve-poised-begin-cutting-interest-rate-early-september"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 give an update}}}\uc2  on his views this Friday at the central bank\u8217\'20\'19s annual meeting in Jackson Hole, Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 The news also comes in an election season when the economy is the top priority for voters. The outgoing president, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/22/poll-economy-recession-biden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 received low marks}}}\uc2  from voters for his handling of the economy despite a remarkable recovery from the coronavirus pandemic. While inflation is fading, voters remain unhappy about prices.\par \par \uc2 About 16m jobs have been created since Biden took office and average unemployment has remained lower than during any administration in 50 years. But Republicans were quick to seize on the announcement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is the largest downward revision to employment in 15 years,\u8221\'20\'1D @RNCResearch said on X (formerly \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ). The 0.5% revision is the largest since 2009.\par \par \uc2 The largest downward revision was in professional and business services, which added 358,000 fewer jobs than first thought. In other areas, leisure and hospitality was revised down by 150,000 jobs, manufacturing had 115,000 fewer jobs, and the trade, transportation and utilities category was revised downward by 104,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNon-farm payroll growth from April 2023 to March 2024 looks to be softer than first thought, but not worryingly so. That supports our view that when the Fed cuts interest rates it will do so in 25bp [basis points] steps, rather than a larger 50bp cut,\u8221\'20\'1D Olivia Cross, North America economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 , wrote in a note to investors.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240821ek8l002ei\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
