\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Washington \uc2 DC council\uc2  member arrested on bribery charges\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Tait in Washington \par \uc2 638 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 August 2024\par 19:42 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Trayon White could face up to 15 years in prison for allegedly accepting $156,000 to aid private companies\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/washington-dc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington DC}}}\uc2  council member has been arrested on charges of accepting $156,000 in bribes in exchange for using his political position to help private companies secure public contracts for violence prevention.\par \par \uc2 Trayon White, a Democrat and former unsuccessful mayoral candidate who has also previously been accused of spreading antisemitic conspiracy theories, was arrested after \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents raided a luxury high-rise apartment where he had been staying in the capital\u8217\'20\'19s Navy Yard neighbourhood, on Sunday, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reported.\par \par \uc2 His arrest followed a \uc2 US Department of Justice\uc2  investigation. White made a brief appearance in court on Monday and a judge agreed to release him while he awaits trial, according to the Post. The judge told him that if convicted, he faces a maximum 15 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 White, 40, represents Ward 8, a predominantly Black area in south-east Washington marked by urban deprivation and gun violence. He chairs the council\u8217\'20\'19s committee on recreation, libraries and youth affairs \u8211\'20\'13 which oversees several public agencies.\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/usao-dc/pr/dc-city-councilmember-arrested-bribery-charge"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement}}}\uc2 , the justice department alleged that White had accepted bribes in exchange for using his position to pressure officials with two agencies, the department for youth rehabilitation services and the office of neighborhood safety and engagement, to extend contracts worth $5.2m for two companies that provided violence intervention services.\par \par \uc2 The statement said White had been captured on video agreeing to accept bribes that were calculated at 3% of the total value of the contracts. He had accepted separate payments of $35,000 in cash on four occasions stretching back to June, with the most recent exchange taking place on 9 August.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBecause the investigation into the alleged bribery scheme involved contracts that could soon be awarded and other potential official acts that could be taken, our office took swift steps to address the alleged crimes we were investigating,\u8221\'20\'1D said Matthew Graves, the US attorney for the District of Columbia.\par \par \uc2 White is also accused of accepting a $20,000 bribe to resolve a contract dispute for one of the companies by pressing high-level officials, according to an affidavit from an \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigator.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2 FBI\uc2  informant who agreed to submit a guilty plea to fraud and bribery charges, reported giving White travel gifts to the Dominican Republic and Las Vegas as well as paying him bribes.\par \par \uc2 A one-time protege of the former Washington DC mayor Marion Barry, White was first elected to the council in 2016, and is expected to be elected for a third term in November, when he is up against a Republican challenger.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, he was accused of antisemitism after posting a video on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  that promoted a conspiracy theory that an unexpectedly heavy snowfall had been due to \u8220\'20\'1Cthe Rothschilds [a historically prominent Jewish banking family] controlling the climate to create natural disasters\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He later apologised and claimed he was unaware that the theory was antisemitic. Video footage subsequently emerged of him asking a question at a council meeting based on the premise that the Rothschilds controlled the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  and \uc2 US federal government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 White is known for on-the-ground activism and for often turning up at crime scenes when there is community anger.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, he launched a campaign to become Washington\u8217\'20\'19s mayor that failed to get past the primary stage but prompted an investigation by the Office of Campaign Finance. The office imposed a $40,000 fine this year, stating the committees for his mayoral campaign and his successful 2020 re-election bid had failed to provide documentation it requested to explain discrepancies in reported expenditures and contributions. It later said White had appealed against the 2020 sanction.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240819ek8j002s3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
