\par\par \uc2 Books\par \b \uc2 Apprentice in Wonderland by Ramin Setoodeh review \u8211\'20\'13 how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s big break changed America\b0\par\par\uc2 Peter Conrad \par \uc2 966 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 August 2024\par 13:41 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The inside story of the creation of the reality show featuring the former US president shows how politics was transformed into a branch of the entertainment industry\par \par \uc2 Every time someone complains that our new prime minister is boring, I rue the day that politics became a performing art. The degradation began, as the entertainment journalist Ramin Setoodeh relates, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was catapulted into power by The Apprentice, a reality/talent/gameshow over which he presided on NBC from 2004 until 2017. Before this, Trump was best known as a loud-mouthed, laughable vulgarian, a fixture in tabloid gossip columns whose business career mostly consisted of bankruptcies. The British producer Mark Burnett endowed him with a new persona as a charismatic leader, a \u8220\'20\'1Cgodlike character\u8221\'20\'1D worshipped by teams of ruthless young entrepreneurs who fought for the chance to serve as apprentices in his property company. It was in this phoney guise that Trump won the election in 2016; installed in Washington, he nationalised the show\u8217\'20\'19s cut-throat scenario by stoking social and ideological feuds, then sat back to enjoy the mayhem that ensued.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s rabid animosity energised The Apprentice. The show\u8217\'20\'19s ethos was supposedly aspirational, but success proved less telegenic than the gloating spectacle of failure: at the climax of every episode Trump eliminated losers by abusively booming: \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re fired!\u8221\'20\'1D This catchphrase became a clarion call. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen I said it,\u8221\'20\'1D he boasts to Setoodeh, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe whole building shook. The place just reverberated. People were screaming, they went crazy.\u8221\'20\'1D Was their reaction ecstasy or hysterical alarm? Either way, they heard a megaphonic deity trumpeting doom.\par \par \uc2 Is Trump himself authentically malign, or just pretending to be? He probably doesn\u8217\'20\'19t know\par \par \uc2 As president, Trump shrank from recreating that eschatological reign of terror. Afraid of real-life confrontations, he sacked chiefs of staff and cabinet members remotely, in small-voiced tweets, not thundering public denunciations. But in interviews with Setoodeh after he was voted out of office in 2020, this hunched, dejected \u8220\'20\'1Cshadow of a famous man\u8221\'20\'1D physically bulks up again as he recalls a time when he was judge, jury and executioner. Play-acting authority on television was his forte; by contrast, running the country turned out to be both a chore and a bore.\par \par \uc2 On The Apprentice, as in the White House, Trump disdained preparation and refused to read briefs, \u8220\'20\'1Cpurely focused on maximising his screen time\u8221\'20\'1D. The only protege to whom he paid attention was the back-stabbing diva \uc2 Omarosa Manigault Newman\uc2 , who saw herself as a female Trump. He subsequently eased her into a job at the White House, where what Setoodeh calls her \u8220\'20\'1Cweaponised incompetence\u8221\'20\'1D soon caused her to be marched off the premises in disgrace. Although she then underwent a \u8220\'20\'1Ctotal Trump detox\u8221\'20\'1D, he still speaks of \uc2 Manigault Newman\uc2  admiringly. He tells Setoodeh that in her first season on the show \u8220\'20\'1Cshe was evil\u8221\'20\'1D, which from him is high praise, then adds that the next year \u8220\'20\'1Cshe tried to be evil \u8211\'20\'13 and when you try it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t work\u8221\'20\'1D. It\u8217\'20\'19s a revealingly self-reflexive remark: is Trump himself authentically malign, or just pretending to be? He probably doesn\u8217\'20\'19t know. As one would-be apprentice puts it, \u8220\'20\'1CTrump conducts himself like an actor playing Trump\u8221\'20\'1D; to further complicate matters, he plays the part badly.\par \par \uc2 Listening as he rants and rambles, Setoodeh likens him to \u8220\'20\'1Ca novelty talking Trump doll, its battery on the fritz\u8221\'20\'1D. But that battery has recently been recharged: he now resembles a dummy perched on a ventriloquist\u8217\'20\'19s knee, compliantly voicing the diatribes of the homegrown fascists who are his handlers. He has also revived the demeaningly competitive format of the show that launched him, and at a rally this July he claimed to be remaking The Apprentice by goading JD Vance, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  and Tim Scott to outdo one another in sycophancy as they vied to become his vice-presidential running mate.\par \par \uc2 The framing conceit of Setoodeh\u8217\'20\'19s book comes from Alice in Wonderland. Trump, he argues, \u8220\'20\'1Ctook America through the looking glass\u8221\'20\'1D and warped government into nonsensical farce. Other literary antecedents cast darker shadows. Burnett \u8220\'20\'1Cenvisioned a Lord of the Flies society\u8221\'20\'1D and devised initiatic trials as exercises in psychological torture. Auditions even included invasive STD tests. One male competitor shudders as he describes \u8220\'20\'1Ca funnel that they stuck in there\u8221\'20\'1D; scraping his urethra, it extracted a sample that somehow testified to his aptitude for a business career. The ritualised firings were brutal, \u8220\'20\'1Ccarried out like public floggings\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The show\u8217\'20\'19s title had an equally sinister provenance. Burnett chose it as a homage to The Sorcerer\u8217\'20\'19s Apprentice, Goethe\u8217\'20\'19s ballad about a trainee magus who runs amok with his master\u8217\'20\'19s spells. In the poem, acted out by Mickey Mouse in Walt Disney\u8217\'20\'19s Fantasia, the absent sorcerer returns to chasten the apprentice and immobilise all those strutting broomsticks and sloshing pails of water. Trump\u8217\'20\'19s mischief-making, however, has continued unchecked, and his current wheeze is to pretend that the burgeoning crowds at \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s rallies are conjured up by AI, like a digital version of Mickey Mouse\u8217\'20\'19s phantasmagoric broomsticks.\par \par \uc2 Setoodeh \u8211\'20\'13 whose parents emigrated from Iran in the 1970s to live the American dream \u8211\'20\'13 seems so resigned to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s victory in November that he raises a white flag in the book\u8217\'20\'19s dedication. Choking on the fatal name in disgust or dismay, he offers it \u8220\'20\'1CTo my dad, who is voting for him\u8221\'20\'1D. Sticking to pronouns, let\u8217\'20\'19s hope that more Americans vote for her.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Apprentice in Wonderland by Ramin Setooheh is published by \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2  (\u163\'00\'A322). To support the Guardian and Observer order your copy at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://guardianbookshop.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 guardianbookshop.com}}}\uc2 . Delivery charges may apply\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240821ek8i002yg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
