\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Journalist Jeff German was stabbed to death. Las Vegas watches as the accused politician goes on trial\b0\par\par\uc2 Andrew Gumbel \par \uc2 1,297 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 August 2024\par 11:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 German had written about workplace harassment by Robert Telles, a county lawyer, before he was killed\par \par \uc2 Jeff German was a doggedly old-school investigative reporter in Las Vegas who didn\u8217\'20\'19t care what mobsters or tainted politicians he offended. After more than 40 years on the job, he had received so many threats and angry phone messages he stopped paying attention to them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI get that stuff all the time. It\u8217\'20\'19s not a big deal,\u8221\'20\'1D he told his editor at the Las Vegas Review Journal.\par \par \uc2 It was the summer of 2022, and the editor, Rhonda Prast, had seen threatening texts and social media messages from a local politician who German had been writing about and considered reporting them to upper management.\par \par \uc2 German saw no particular reason to fear the politician, a short, bullet-headed lawyer named Robert Telles who headed an office that settles the estates of county residents who die without a will. At the time, he had two much more chilling messages on his voicemail from hard, angry men calling him every name in the book and threatening to come after him.\par \par \uc2 Even those didn\u8217\'20\'19t bother him. Earlier in his career, German had stared down Tony Spilotro, a notorious, cold-eyed mafioso who was memorably portrayed by Joe Pesci in the Martin Scorsese movie Casino, and came away unscathed. Prast, too, did not think Telles meant her reporter physical harm. \u8220\'20\'1CI thought he was going to sue us,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Yet, as a murder trial now underway in Las Vegas has revealed, there may have been more to Telles than met the eye. According to the prosecution, Telles became so exasperated with the stories German was writing about him \u8211\'20\'13 stories depicting him as a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reviewjournal.com/investigations/county-office-in-turmoil-with-secret-video-and-claims-of-bullying-hostility-2577147/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nightmare boss}}}\uc2  who harassed and bullied his staff and conducted an inappropriate relationship with a subordinate \u8211\'20\'13 that he drove to German\u8217\'20\'19s house on 2 September 2022, and stabbed him to death in broad daylight after learning that his electronic communications with the subordinate were about to be made public.\par \par \uc2 German, who was 69, was left to die in a row of bushes along the side of his house. It was 24 hours before neighbours, alarmed by the fact that he\u8217\'20\'19d left his garage door open and was not responding to messages, walked around his house and discovered the body.\par \par \uc2 Once police arrived, however, it took just four days to gather enough evidence to arrest Telles. Security video footage gathered between Telles\u8217\'20\'19s house and German\u8217\'20\'19s showed a maroon GMC Yukon Denali travelling between the two and a man dressed improbably in a wide-brimmed straw hat and a reflective orange jacket walking to and from German\u8217\'20\'19s house with a grey bag slung over his shoulder.\par \par \uc2 In their opening statement, prosecutors invited jurors to linger, twice, over footage showing German walking over to the side of the house where his assailant lay in wait, tussling with someone in the greenery and the orange-jacketed assailant re-emerging and walking away with an eerie calm.\par \par \uc2 The day after the murder, Telles was found washing an identical Yukon Denali in his driveway. When police searched his house, they found a wide-brimmed straw hat matching the one in video footage that had been cut into pieces and stuffed into a shopping bag. They also found a pair of shoes matching the killer\u8217\'20\'19s shoved under a sofa. One of the shoes had been cut up, much like the hat, and the pieces stuffed into a baggie.\par \par \uc2 Telles had a nasty cut on one of his fingers, and when police took a sample of his DNA they found it was a match for tissue found beneath one of German\u8217\'20\'19s fingernails, prosecutors said. When investigators examined Telles\u8217\'20\'19s phone, they found he had searched for German\u8217\'20\'19s house on \uc2 Google\uc2  Maps shortly before the murder.\par \par \uc2 There were other puzzling details suggesting this was hardly a professional hit job. The man in the hat and orange jacket walked aimlessly up and down the streets around German\u8217\'20\'19s house before the crime, perhaps in the mistaken belief that this might confuse eyewitnesses. After the murder, he returned to his car on a neighbouring street, only to return to the scene of the crime shortly afterwards \u8211\'20\'13 to check that German was dead, perhaps, or to retrieve something he might have dropped. Committing the crime during daylight hours made it almost ridiculously easy to track his movements via home video security systems.\par \par \uc2 The district attorney\u8217\'20\'19s office has described the evidence against Telles as \u8220\'20\'1Coverwhelming\u8221\'20\'1D, a characterisation Telles and his legal team hotly dispute. Indeed, Telles\u8217\'20\'19s lawyer, Robert Draskovich, waved away much of the evidence against his client in his own opening statement, saying Telles was the victim of a political witch- hunt and that the only rational explanation for cutting up the hat and shoes was that this made it easier for the police to \u8220\'20\'1Cconceal and plant\u8221\'20\'1D them in Telles\u8217\'20\'19s house.\par \par \uc2 Draskovich portrayed his client as an anti-corruption crusader determined to push back against the \u8220\'20\'1Cold guard\u8221\'20\'1D at the public administrator\u8217\'20\'19s office, which he said cut sweetheart deals with a local real estate agency to make money off the houses it handled. He described Telles as \u8220\'20\'1Cblowing the whistle\u8221\'20\'1D, even though Telles was the elected leader of the office whereas whistleblowers are typically subordinates who witness wrongdoing by their superiors.\par \par \uc2 But it was precisely such whistleblowers who became German\u8217\'20\'19s sources inside the office. Four of the women he interviewed later went public and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ktnv.com/13-investigates/four-employees-file-federal-lawsuit-against-clark-county-and-robert-telles-over-hostile-work-environment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed a lawsuit}}}\uc2  against Telles and the county in which they described a workplace where employees were forbidden to talk to each other, the boss made sexual advances to several women and imposed punitive restrictions on those who resisted.\par \par \uc2 One plaintiff, Jessica Coleman, alleged that Telles told her \u8220\'20\'1Cshe would die alone, and no one would find her for a long time\u8221\'20\'1D. She became so frightened, she said, that she contemplated hanging herself in the office to force the county to mount an investigation. Telles has moved to have the suit dismissed, saying it is full of lies.\par \par \uc2 Two months after German\u8217\'20\'19s death, his colleagues at the Review Journal retrieved his notes and contacted his sources and fleshed out a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reviewjournal.com/investigations/a-reporters-killing-revealed-a-decade-of-robert-telles-toxic-behavior-2679171/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 long story}}}\uc2  about Telles, tracing allegations of anger and sexual misconduct back to his days as a law student in Las Vegas. One notable episode that came to light was an arrest for domestic violence in 2020, after his wife made an emergency call complaining that he had drunk too much and was \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reviewjournal.com/investigations/911-call-to-police-from-robert-telles-wife-my-husband-is-going-crazy-2639098/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 going crazy}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Part of the investigative team\u8217\'20\'19s goal was to pay homage to their fallen colleague in the language he understood best: old-fashioned, muck-raking journalism. \u8220\'20\'1CAll we did after Jeff was killed was work on the investigation and deal with our trauma and grief,\u8221\'20\'1D Prast said.\par \par \uc2 In a precarious news business, however, in which investigations are expensive and newsroom budgets strapped, the Review-Journal has gone through two years of upheaval, leaving only one of German\u8217\'20\'19s colleagues still at the paper.\par \par \uc2 Prast has moved the furthest afield, to a newspaper in Bellingham, Washington. She said she was following the trial carefully and planned to fly back to Las Vegas to attend in person. \u8220\'20\'1CWe still feel his absence two years later,\u8221\'20\'1D she said of German. \u8220\'20\'1CI still dream about him at night. I have his picture on his desk. I hope that what people remember is not Robert Telles and what a terrible person he is, but Jeff the first-class human being and journalist, working to change people\u8217\'20\'19s lives for the better.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240817ek8h0015x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
