\par\par \uc2 Environment\par \b \uc2 Danish wind power giant \u216\'00\'D8rsted delays major US offshore project\b0\par\par\uc2 Jillian Ambrose Energy correspondent \par \uc2 510 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 August 2024\par 11:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 News follows scrapping of two other Atlantic windfarms and axing of hundreds of jobs as costs surgeBusiness live \u8211\'20\'13 latest updates\par \par \uc2 The Danish company developing the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest offshore windfarm in the North Sea has been forced to delay a major project off the north-east coast of the US, months after cancelling two nearby developments and cutting hundreds of jobs.\par \par \uc2 \u216\'00\'D8rsted\uc2  has pushed back the start of commercial operations at its 704 megawatt Revolution Wind project off the coast of Rhode Island and Connecticut by a year, to 2026.\par \par \uc2 The company behind the giant Hornsea windfarms off the Yorkshire coast said the construction delay would contribute to an impairment cost of 3.2bn Danish kroner ($472m) in its second-quarter financial results.\par \par \uc2 The latest financial hit to \u216\'00\'D8rsted includes costs relating to its decision last year {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/nov/01/rsted-cancels-two-us-offshore-windfarm-projects-at-33bn-cost#:~:text=Denmark's%20%C3%98rsted%20has%20cancelled%20two,facing%20the%20global%20wind%20industry."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to scrap two major windfarms}}}\uc2  off the New Jersey coast, Ocean Wind I and II, amid surging costs facing the global wind power industry.\par \par \uc2 Within weeks of laying bare the company\u8217\'20\'19s financial turmoil, its chief financial officer, Daniel Lerup, and chief operating officer, Richard Hunter, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/14/wind-developer-orsted-bosses-exit-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreed to step down}}}\uc2  with immediate effect because the company needed \u8220\'20\'1Cnew and different capabilities\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In February, \u216\'00\'D8rsted set out moves to cut up to 800 jobs and pull back from Spain, Portugal and Norway while reining in plans to expand its offshore wind business. It said it would suspend dividend payments to shareholders covering the 2023-25 financial years in an attempt to recover from a chaotic 12 months.\par \par \uc2 The company, which is majority owned by the Danish government, said the \u8220\'20\'1Creset plan\u8221\'20\'1D was designed to make it leaner and more efficient after soaring cost inflation and higher interest rates collided with supply chain disruptions to upend construction plans across the industry.\par \par \uc2 \u216\'00\'D8rsted\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief executive, \uc2 Mads Nipper\uc2 , pointed to the company\u8217\'20\'19s better than expected earnings from its portfolio of operating windfarms as evidence its existing business was performing well.\par \par \uc2 Earnings for the second quarter rose to 5.27bn kroner, up almost 60% from the same months last year and well above the average forecast of 4.41bn kroner predicted by industry analysts.\par \par \uc2 The company remains on track to deliver potentially the world\u8217\'20\'19s biggest offshore windfarm, the 2.9GW Hornsea 3, also off the Yorkshire coast, by 2027, in a major boost to the new Labour government\u8217\'20\'19s goal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/article/2024/jul/25/offshore-wind-to-power-20m-homes-within-five-years-starmer-to-pledge"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to quadruple the UK\u8217\'20\'19s offshore wind capacity}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \u163\'00\'A38.3bn project is expected to generate enough green electricity to power the equivalent of 3m UK homes. The project will join the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2016/feb/03/worlds-largest-offshore-windfarm-to-be-built-off-yorkshire-coast"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1.3GW Hornsea 1 offshore windfarm}}}\uc2  and the 1.3GW Hornsea 2, which together are capable of generating enough electricity to provide 2.5m UK homes with green power. The final development, Hornsea 4, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/jul/12/hornsey-four-offshore-windfarm-given-green-light-after-five-month-delay"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 received approval last year}}}\uc2  and could have a capacity of up to 2.6GW.\par \par \uc2 In total, the Hornsea offshore wind zone will generate enough clean electricity to power the equivalent of 8m UK homes.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240815ek8f001pe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
