\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US inflation dipped in July as Federal Reserve expected to cut interest rates\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani in New York \par \uc2 741 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 August 2024\par 13:43 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Prices rose at an annual rate of 2.9% in July, as inflation dipped below 3% for the first time since 2021\par \par \uc2 US annual {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/inflation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 inflation}}}\uc2  rate dipped below 3% in July for the first time since 2021, a relief to investors who are expecting the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to cut interest rates next month as a sense of unease has settled over Wall Street after signs of a cooling labor market.\par \par \uc2 Prices rose at an annual rate of 2.9% in July while core inflation, which does not account for the volatile food and energy industries, climbed 3.2% over the previous 12 months and 0.2% since June.\par \par \uc2 The latest reading of the the Consumer Price Index (CPI), which tracks prices of consumer goods and services, comes as the political battle over the US economy is heating up. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/cf9a7c4d-3b82-4867-892c-f4f95daebbc7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recent poll}}}\uc2  showed the Democratic presidential candidate, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/kamala-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kamala Harris}}}\uc2 , had pulled ahead of her Republican rival, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2 , on who voters trust with the economy. The poll was a marked shift from the many polls that have shown the former president ahead of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/joebiden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Joe Biden}}}\uc2  on economic issues.\par \par \uc2 Paul Ashworth\uc2 , chief North America economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 , said the details of the report were \u8220\'20\'1Ca little disappointing\u8221\'20\'1D noting that rent prices, which have been a big driver of inflation, were higher in July than June.\par \par \uc2 But overall he said the report was \u8220\'20\'1Cbest described as mildly encouraging\u8221\'20\'1D and should support a quarter-point cut in interest rates when the Fed meet in September but, \u8220\'20\'1Cat the same time, doesn\u8217\'20\'19t suggest price pressures are collapsing in a way that could warrant a bigger 50bp [basis point] reduction\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Though the recent report is unlikely to shake markets, uncertainty remains in Wall Street after last week\u8217\'20\'19s sell-off {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/05/us-stock-market-recession-dow-close"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sowed panic}}}\uc2  among investors.\par \par \uc2 Investors considered this report one of the main indicators for whether the Fed will start cutting interest rates next month. Interest rates have been at 5.25% to 5.5%, a two-decade high, for more than a year, and it is unclear whether the Fed can achieve a so-called \u8220\'20\'1Csoft landing\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 slowing price increases without inciting a recession.\par \par \uc2 For much of the last year, it seemed like the Fed had achieved a soft landing. The rate of inflation was slowly declining \u8211\'20\'13 it peaked at 9.1% in June 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 while the labor market held steady. When the Fed announced it would not change interest rates at the end of July, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jul/11/us-inflation-fall-june"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 inflation}}}\uc2  in June was 3%, a 0.3% decrease from the month before, while unemployment was at 4.1%.\par \par \uc2 But any rosy outlook for a soft landing was diminished just over a week later, when July\u8217\'20\'19s job figures were released, showing that hiring {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/02/us-economy-jobs-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 slowed}}}\uc2  to a level much lower than expected, and the unemployment rate rose to 4.3%, the highest since October 2021.\par \par \uc2 Markets quickly panicked, leading to a massive sell-off on 5 August that saw the S&P, Dow Jones and Nasdaq all down by 2.6% by the end of the day and increasing fears that the US economy was entering a recession.\par \par \uc2 Those fears turned out to be premature, at least for now. By Thursday, markets {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/08/wall-street-stock-market-crash"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rallied}}}\uc2  after a weekly report showed a drop in jobless claims \u8211\'20\'13 a sign that there is still some strength in the labor market, despite Wall Street fears. The S&P 500 ended up rising 2.3% in one day, its biggest jump since November 2022.\par \par \uc2 Investors and economists are expecting the Fed will cut rates at its next meeting 18 September. Still, some Fed officials have indicated weariness that rates should be cut.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CInflation is still uncomfortably above the committee\u8217\'20\'19s 2% goal,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Michelle Bowman\uc2 , a Fed governor, said in public remarks last week. \u8220\'20\'1CI will remain cautious in my approach to considering adjustments to the current stance of policy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For the Fed, it is essentially a balance between price increases and the job market. In a statement following its last minute, the Fed said it \u8220\'20\'1Cis attentive to risks to both sides of its dual mandate\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 At the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s last meeting, the chair, \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/jul/31/federal-reserve-interest-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  officials are no longer laser-focused on inflation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe think we don\u8217\'20\'19t need to be 100% focused on inflation because of the progress that we\u8217\'20\'19ve made,\u8221\'20\'1D Powell said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240814ek8e001ur\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
