\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Amazon\uc2  says US labor watchdog that filed labor charges violates constitution\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,046 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 August 2024\par 20:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Our voices are only going to get louder\u8217\'20\'19, workers warn tech giant after Atlanta warehouse writes up employees\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/amazon"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amazon}}}\uc2  has argued the country\u8217\'20\'19s top labor watchdog is violating the constitution as the company fights to dismiss unfair labor practice charges, leaning on a recent conservative {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US supreme court}}}\uc2  ruling.\par \par \uc2 In a filing last month, attorneys representing the technology giant pushed back against a complaint issued by the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ) after two {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/state-of-georgia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Georgia}}}\uc2  workers alleged that they faced retaliation, surveillance and interrogation after exercising their right to organize.\par \par \uc2 The workers, based at the ATL6 \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse on the outskirts of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/atlanta"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Atlanta}}}\uc2 , filed charges in 2023. The \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s regional office issued a complaint against \uc2 Amazon\uc2  after finding merit in the charges. A court hearing is scheduled for October.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , which denies the allegations, is seeking to dismiss the complaint on constitutional grounds.\par \par \uc2 As a string of corporations facing charges of labor law violations attack the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s July filing came just one month after the supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jun/28/us-supreme-court-chevron-doctrine-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned}}}\uc2  the \uc2 Chevron\uc2  doctrine, undermining the authority of federal agencies to interpret the laws they administer, deferring to the courts whether a federal agency\u8217\'20\'19s interpretation of a law should stand.\par \par \uc2 The NRLB\u8217\'20\'19s complaint against \uc2 Amazon\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cshould be dismissed because the General Counsel\u8217\'20\'19s interpretation of the Act and requests to the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  in this case implicates the Major Questions Doctrine and associated principles of non-delegation and therefore violate Article I of the United States Constitution,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Amazon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s attorneys wrote in their response, a copy of which was obtained by the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  complaint \u8220\'20\'1Cshould be dismissed\u8221\'20\'1D, they added, because the agency\u8217\'20\'19s procedures \u8220\'20\'1Cviolate Article II of the United States Constitution\u8221\'20\'1D by involving \u8220\'20\'1Cthe exercise of significant authority by an Officer of the United States who is improperly insulated from the President\u8217\'20\'19s removal power\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cconcurrent exercise of legislative, executive and judicial power in this proceeding violates the separation of powers established by the United States Constitution,\u8221\'20\'1D the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  attorneys claimed, \u8220\'20\'1Cand the guarantee of due process found in the Fifth Amendment to the United States Constitution.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Such arguments were once considered conservative fringe arguments, according to an attorney advising the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers and United for Respect, a non-profit labor advocacy group. \u8220\'20\'1CI think this is part of a broader effort on the part of conservative voices in the country \u8211\'20\'13 retail employers specifically \u8211\'20\'13 attempting to dismantle the administrative state and attacking the agencies tasked with protecting workers rights,\u8221\'20\'1D said Bianca Agustin, co-director of United for Respect. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a concerted effort on the industry\u8217\'20\'19s part because of the rise in activism at the worker level.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  declined to comment on the arguments by \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , but its general counsel, \uc2 Jennifer Abruzzo\uc2 , recently condemned arguments made by employers challenging the agency\u8217\'20\'19s constitutionality.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is no way we are going to succumb to the pressures imposed in addressing challenges and defending the constitutionality of our agency\u8217\'20\'19s structure,\u8221\'20\'1D Abruzzo said in response to similar arguments against the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  made by \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s, \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and Energy Transfer during a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rooseveltinstitute.org/event/preserving-the-administrative-state-threats-to-administrative-law-enforcement-in-the-courts/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 panel}}}\uc2  with the Roosevelt Institute in April. \u8220\'20\'1CThese esoteric legal arguments came about, why?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBecause we dared to issue a complaint against \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  after it unlawfully fired eight workers for speaking up about their workplace concerns, and then \uc2 Amazon\uc2  jumps on the bandwagon, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  jumps on the bandwagon, Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s, others get into the action \u8211\'20\'13 just because we are trying to hold them accountable for repeatedly violating workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights to organize and collectively bargain.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The two \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers \u8211\'20\'13 Ron Sewell, who is a learning ambassador, and Nancy Regimbal, who works in the transportation department \u8211\'20\'13 have both worked at the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse for seven years and, due to their seniority, had taken a leading role in conveying employee concerns with policy changes.\par \par \uc2 Back in May 2023, Sewell met with Regimbal in a parking lot down the street from the warehouse. Sewell wanted to help out another worker who was disputing transfers of temporary staff to another warehouse, and a policy change where part-time \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers would no longer be compensated for a full four-hour shift if sent home early.\par \par \uc2 According to Sewell and Regimbal, an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  shuttle van with management appeared where they were. They were asked what they were doing, and later were contacted by an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  investigator and received disciplinary write-ups alleging they were not supposed to be in the parking lot. Both workers disputed this and alleged that Sewell was followed to the parking lot by \uc2 Amazon\uc2  managers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI never received a write-up in my seven years at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  because I know all the rules in and out, and I follow them,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sewell. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was because I was organizing, I was talking to associates, I was getting other people to follow me, and they wanted to get rid of me and they saw this as an easy way to get rid of me, to make up something and say they\u8217\'20\'19re going to go with this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Regimbal also argued she had never before received a write-up in her seven years of employment at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and alleged her write-up was retaliation, as she was posting concerns and complaints from co-workers who wanted to do so anonymously in the days before the write-up.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe investigators, managers, they jumped over the line in many areas,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey didn\u8217\'20\'19t follow the correct steps in regards to addressing any of these issues they are so-called writing us up on.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She appealed her write-up but said she never received a report or concrete answers on why she was written up.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI literally have a stack of documents, pictures and chats, to show my side of the story,\u8221\'20\'1D added Regimbal. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not going to let them tear us down. We have been speaking out about heat issues, safety issues, water issues, pay issues, you name it, and our voices are only going to get louder.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  did not respond to multiple requests for comment on their attorney\u8217\'20\'19s arguments or the allegations included in the unfair labor practice charges.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240813ek8d001jm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
