\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US workers launch Heat Week to fight for \u8216\'20\'18the right to water, shade and rest\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Dharna Noor \par \uc2 818 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 August 2024\par 17:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In probably the hottest summer ever, workers are organizing in 13 cities to raise alarm about workplace heat exposure\par \par \uc2 As temperatures in Baltimore neared 100F earlier this month, 36-year-old sanitation worker Ronald Silver II {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/baltimore/news/baltimore-dpw-worker-who-died-from-overheating-was-trying-to-get-some-water-resident-said/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 died}}}\uc2  after he was found lying on the hood of a car and asking for water.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s the kind of tragic workplace heat-related death that advocates say could have been avoided with the right labor protections. So this week, during what will probably be the US\u8217\'20\'19s hottest summer on record , frontline workers are organizing actions in 13 cities across the country, raising the alarm about workplace heat exposure.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to keep struggling until the right to water, shade and rest is given to all workers in this country,\u8221\'20\'1D Lourdes Cardenas, an agricultural worker with \uc2 United Farm Workers\uc2  in California, told reporters on Monday speaking via translator.\par \par \uc2 As part of Heat Week, Cardenas and other farm workers are joining fast-food workers represented by the Union of Southern Service Workers and Starbucks Workers United, as well as laborers from other sectors, to lead marches and town halls.\par \par \uc2 Airport service workers will deliver letters to the country\u8217\'20\'19s four largest airlines calling for heat protections, and on Thursday, workers and allies from across sectors will take a coordinated drink of water in a display of solidarity aimed at showing the importance of workplace heat safety.\par \par \uc2 The campaign comes one month after the White House {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/article/2024/jul/02/biden-extreme-heat-proposaal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 revealed a long-anticipated proposal}}}\uc2  to establish the country\u8217\'20\'19s first-ever federal workplace heat standard, which, if finalized, would require access to water, shade, breaks and training for about 36 million workers. But the rule could take many months for the \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration (Osha)\uc2  to finalize, and could be torpedoed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if he wins November\u8217\'20\'19s presidential election.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen and if it\u8217\'20\'19s implemented, it will save lives, but only if we hold [the Biden administration] accountable and keep the standard strong,\u8221\'20\'1D the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2  president, April Verrett, said about the proposal on the Monday press call.\par \par \uc2 Verrett and other Heat Week organizers say the labor movement cannot afford to wait for Osha to finalize those rules. So they are demanding all US employers meet the standards laid out in the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s heat proposal immediately.\par \par \uc2 They are also \u8220\'20\'1Ccalling on elected officials at every level to ensure that every worker in their jurisdiction, including public and private sector workers, are shielded from dangerous heat\u8221\'20\'1D, Verrett said. And they are bringing extreme-heat protections, such as mandatory shade and water breaks, to bargaining tables across the country in an effort to enshrine them in union contracts.\par \par \uc2 Workers on the press call laid out the risks posed by sweltering workplace temperatures. One of them was Cecilia Ortiz, an airport worker in Phoenix who assists customers with special needs.\par \par \uc2 Ortiz said she \u8220\'20\'1Cloves\u8221\'20\'1D her job, but that it was \u8220\'20\'1Cchallenging\u8221\'20\'1D, especially in the heat when she is forced to don a heavy polyester uniform and walk 10 to 15 miles a day while pushing wheelchairs and luggage. Often, she must also stand on jet bridges that connect airport terminal gates to airplanes, where temperatures often reach 100F. Despite these conditions, she said, she is only paid $16 an hour \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cpoverty wages\u8221\'20\'1D, she said.\par \par \uc2 A fast-food worker in Columbia, South Carolina, Shae Parker, said she {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/16/waffle-house-workers-strike-minimum-wage-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 went on strike}}}\uc2  last year. \u8220\'20\'1CWe definitely experienced the heat crisis,\u8221\'20\'1D said Parker, who said workers were required to wear heavy shirts with aprons, and added that the restaurant\u8217\'20\'19s air conditioners were frequently broken and that employees were forced to pay for water.\par \par \uc2 This year, Parker said she had to leave her shift early and \u8220\'20\'1Con a stretcher\u8221\'20\'1D to go to the emergency room due to heat exhaustion. She was not paid for the remainder of her shift, she said.\par \par \uc2 Texas representative Greg Casar, a member of the progressive congressional \u8220\'20\'1CSquad\u8221\'20\'1D and Green New Deal advocate, also spoke on Monday\u8217\'20\'19s call.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBefore I was an elected official, I was a labor organizer, and we organized at workplaces, but unfortunately, I also had to organize vigils to remember fallen workers,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 From 1992 to 2022, 986 workers across the United States {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epa.gov/climate-indicators/closer-look-heat-related-workplace-deaths#:~:text=From%201992%20to%202022%2C%20a,reported%20data%20(Figure%201)."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 died from exposure to heat}}}\uc2 , federal data shows. As the climate crisis pushes up summer temperatures, those numbers could {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/nation/2023-set-a-record-for-u-s-heat-deaths-why-2024-could-be-even-deadlier"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rise dramatically}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Amid rising summer temperatures, workers have increasingly been organizing against excessive heat exposure. But in the absence of better protections, workers such as Baltimore\u8217\'20\'19s Ronald Silver II could continue to die , advocates on the call said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe was a human being and he deserved better,\u8221\'20\'1D Verrett said about Silver\u8217\'20\'19s death. \u8220\'20\'1CThis kind of tragedy is unacceptable. It should not take workers dying on the job for employers to act.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240813ek8d002bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
