\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 13,500 US hotel workers hold strike votes over pay and conditions\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 837 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 August 2024\par 11:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re asking and fighting for what we deserve,\u8217\'20\'19 a worker says before votes this week from Boston to Honolulu\par \par \uc2 About 13,500 hotel workers across Boston, Honolulu, Providence and San Francisco will vote on whether to strike this week as they push for significant wage increases and protections against job cuts.\par \par \uc2 Employees at leading chains including \uc2 Hilton\uc2 , \uc2 Hyatt\uc2 , \uc2 Marriott\uc2  and \uc2 Omni\uc2  will decide in the coming days whether to approve the walkouts. The hotel industry stands accused of having used the Covid-19 crisis to reduce staffing and increase workloads.\par \par \uc2 Workers were hit on multiple fronts in recent years, as thousands faced layoffs and furloughs during the pandemic, only to return to work with stagnant wages as inflation soared, and higher workloads due to policies such as the elimination of daily room cleanings, causing rooms to be messier and coinciding with staff reductions.\par \par \uc2 Now \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  is launching the first wave of strike votes among more than 40,000 hotel workers it represents across 20 cities in the US and Canada, who face union contract expirations this year.\par \par \uc2 If workers vote to authorize a strike in the first four cities, one can be called at any time once their contract expires, with some already having expired, and others set to expire by the end of August.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe hotels are not thinking about their workers, they\u8217\'20\'19re only thinking about the money that they make,\u8221\'20\'1D said Anabel Castro, a housekeeper at Marriott\u8217\'20\'19s Westin Moana Surfrider in Honolulu for over 20 years. \u8220\'20\'1CThey should give us what we\u8217\'20\'19re asking for. We\u8217\'20\'19re only asking and fighting for what we deserve.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Castro was out of work for about two years following the onset of Covid-19, which prompted shutdowns across the hotel industry in March 2020. She made desserts and sold them to try to make up the income she lost from being furloughed.\par \par \uc2 But now she is still struggling, working three jobs to support herself and her family, while having to take on extra workloads since she returned to work at the hotel, where she cleans 14 rooms a day. \u8220\'20\'1CIt doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter how many checkouts we have, and when we have checkouts, it\u8217\'20\'19s really messy,\u8221\'20\'1D explained Castro. \u8220\'20\'1CSometimes you have to return to the rooms because of the condition the rooms are left in.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Theresa Fiorino, a room service cashier at the Hilton Boston Logan Airport for 29 years, said workers at her hotel were asking for a $10 wage increase over four years of the contract but claimed the hotel was not even meeting the workers halfway on their proposal.\par \par \uc2 At the start of the pandemic, Fiorino was furloughed. \u8220\'20\'1CThat day, I was pretty much in tears because I didn\u8217\'20\'19t think I was ever going to walk back in that building again,\u8221\'20\'1D she recalled.\par \par \uc2 But when she was eventually brought back to work in the room service department, she said staffing was reduced, forcing her and other workers to take on extra workloads. She works another part-time job as well, to try to cover basic living expenses.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re running everything bare minimum,\u8221\'20\'1D claimed Fiorino. \u8220\'20\'1CThe cost of everything is insane. Nobody can survive. One job should be enough, but that\u8217\'20\'19s just not the case.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hilton\uc2  and \uc2 Marriott\uc2  did not respond to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Other hotel workers have also criticized staffing reductions, workload increases and policies that came out of the pandemic such as the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jul/05/overworked-underpaid-hotel-workers-employees"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 elimination}}}\uc2  of daily room cleanings in hotels.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI sleep with pain, I wake up with pain, I go to work with pain,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jianci Liang, a housekeeper at the Hilton Boston Park Plaza for seven years. \u8220\'20\'1CSince we returned to work after Covid, there are about 20 fewer room attendants on the regular schedule. Without the proper staffing, my job is getting harder and harder.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen the rooms are sold out, we have to cover the work of the missing people. I have no choice but to work, because it\u8217\'20\'19s hard to save money or set aside funds for an emergency.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hotel staffing per room occupancy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://unitehere.app.box.com/s/r2pamwcphbonlux5a9lckheq7298na50"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decreased }}}\uc2  by 13% from 2019 to 2022 in the US, \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  has noted, while US hotel industry profits surpassed 2019 levels to reach {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://unitehere.app.box.com/s/r2pamwcphbonlux5a9lckheq7298na50"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $101.3bn}}}\uc2  in 2022.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMomentum is building for strikes this year because workers are at a breaking point while the hotel industry is making record profits,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s international president, Gwen Mills. \u8220\'20\'1CHotel companies took advantage of Covid to make severe cuts to staffing and guest services, and now workers say that their jobs are more painful than ever.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMeanwhile, wages aren\u8217\'20\'19t enough to cover the cost of living, and many workers have two or even three jobs. This is a fight about the future of the industry for guests and workers alike, and our members are putting the hotels on notice that they\u8217\'20\'19re ready to strike for what their families need.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240806ek86001gw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
