\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Sam Altman\uc2  is becoming one of the most powerful people on Earth. We should be very afraid\b0\par\par\uc2 Gary Marcus \par \uc2 2,753 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2024\par 15:05 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Sam Altman\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ChatGPT promises to transform the global economy. But it also poses an enormous threat. Here, a scientist who appeared with Altman before the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  on AI safety flags up the danger in AI \u8211\'20\'13 and in Altman himself\par \par \uc2 On 16 May 2023, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/sam-altman"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sam Altman}}}\uc2 , \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s charming, softly spoken, eternally optimistic billionaire CEO, and I stood in front of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.techpolicy.press/transcript-senate-judiciary-subcommittee-hearing-on-oversight-of-ai/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US Senate judiciary subcommittee meeting on AI oversight}}}\uc2 . We were in Washington DC, and it was at the height of AI mania. Altman, then 38, was the poster boy for it all.\par \par \uc2 Raised in St Louis, Missouri, Altman was the Stanford dropout who had become the president of the massively successful \uc2 Y Combinator\uc2  startup incubator before he was 30. A few months before the hearing, his company\u8217\'20\'19s product ChatGPT had taken the world by storm. All through the summer of 2023, Altman was treated like a Beatle, stopping by DC as part of a world tour, meeting prime ministers and presidents around the globe. US Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  gushed: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve never met anyone as smart as Sam\u8230\'20\'26 He\u8217\'20\'19s an introvert and shy and humble\u8230\'20\'26 But\u8230\'20\'26 very good at forming relationships with people on the Hill and\u8230\'20\'26 can help folks in government understand AI.\u8221\'20\'1D Glowing portraits at the time painted the youthful Altman as sincere, talented, rich and interested in nothing more than fostering humanity. His frequent suggestions that AI could transform the global economy had world leaders salivating.\par \par \uc2 Little by little, I realised that I, the Senate, and ultimately the American people, had probably been played\par \par \uc2 Senator Richard Blumenthal had called the two of us (and \uc2 IBM\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Christina Montgomery\uc2 ) to Washington to discuss what should be done about AI, a \u8220\'20\'1Cdual-use\u8221\'20\'1D technology that held tremendous promise, but also had the potential to cause tremendous harm \u8211\'20\'13 from tsunamis of misinformation to enabling the proliferation of new bioweapons. The agenda was AI policy and regulation. We swore to tell the whole truth, and nothing but the truth.\par \par \uc2 Altman was representing one of the leading AI companies; I was there as a scientist and author, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/01/06/opinion/ezra-klein-podcast-gary-marcus.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 well known for my scepticism}}}\uc2  about many things AI-related. I found Altman surprisingly engaging. There were moments when he ducked questions (most notably Blumenthal\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1CWhat are you most worried about?\u8221\'20\'1D, which I pushed Altman to answer with more candour), but on the whole he seemed genuine, and I recall saying as much to the senators at the time. We both came out strongly for AI regulation. Little by little, though, I realised that I, the Senate, and ultimately the American people, had probably been played.\par \par \uc2 * * *\par \par \uc2 In truth, I had always had some misgivings about \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 . The company\u8217\'20\'19s press campaigns, for example, were often over the top and even misleading, such as their fancy demo of a robot \u8220\'20\'1Csolving\u8221\'20\'1D a Rubik\u8217\'20\'19s Cube that turned out to have special sensors inside. It received tons of press, but it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://garymarcus.substack.com/p/about-that-openai-breakthrough"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ultimately went nowhere}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For years, the name \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 which implied a kind of openness about the science behind what the company was doing \u8211\'20\'13 had felt like a lie, since in reality it has become less and less transparent over time. The company\u8217\'20\'19s frequent hints that AGI (artificial general intelligence, AI that can at least match the cognitive abilities of any human) was just around the corner always felt to me like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://garymarcus.substack.com/p/dear-elon-musk-here-are-five-things"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unwarranted hype}}}\uc2 . But in person, Altman dazzled; I wondered whether I had been too hard on him previously. In hindsight, I had been too soft.\par \par \uc2 I started to reconsider after someone sent me a tip, about something small but telling. At the Senate, Altman painted himself as far more altruistic than he really was. Senator John Kennedy had asked: \u8220\'20\'1COK. You make a lot of money. Do you?\u8221\'20\'1D Altman {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.techpolicy.press/transcript-senate-judiciary-subcommittee-hearing-on-oversight-of-ai/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 responded}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CI make no\u8230\'20\'26 I get paid enough for health insurance. I have no equity in \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D elaborating that: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m doing this \u8217\'20\'19cause I love it.\u8221\'20\'1D The senators ate it up.\par \par \uc2 Altman wasn\u8217\'20\'19t telling the full truth. He didn\u8217\'20\'19t own any stock in \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , but he did own stock in \uc2 Y Combinator\uc2 , and \uc2 Y Combinator\uc2  owned stock in \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 . Which meant that Sam had an indirect stake in \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://openai.com/our-structure/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fact acknowledged on OpenAI\u8217\'20\'19s website}}}\uc2 . If that indirect stake were worth just 0.1% of the company\u8217\'20\'19s value, which seems plausible, it would be worth nearly $100m.\par \par \uc2 That omission was a warning sign. And when the topic returned, he could have corrected it. But he didn\u8217\'20\'19t. People loved his selfless myth. (He doubled down, in a piece with Fortune, claiming that he didn\u8217\'20\'19t need equity with \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  because he had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fortune.com/2023/06/22/sam-altman-openai-enough-money-bloomberg-technology-summit/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cenough money\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 .) Not long after that, I discovered \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  had made a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ccn.com/analysis/openai-altman-rain-chip/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deal with a chip company that Altman owned a piece of}}}\uc2 . The selfless bit started to ring hollow.\par \par \uc2 The discussion about money wasn\u8217\'20\'19t, in hindsight, the only thing from our time in the Senate that didn\u8217\'20\'19t feel entirely candid. Far more important was \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s stance on regulation around AI. Publicly, Altman told the Senate he supported it. The reality is far more complicated.\par \par \uc2 On the one hand, maybe a tiny part of Altman genuinely does want AI regulation. He is fond of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/03/31/technology/sam-altman-open-ai-chatgpt.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 paraphrasing Oppenheimer}}}\uc2  (and is well aware that he shares a birthday with the leader of the Manhattan Project), and recognises that, like nuclear weaponry, AI poses serious risks to humanity. In his own words, spoken at the Senate (albeit after a bit of prompting from me): \u8220\'20\'1CLook, we have tried to be very clear about the magnitude of the risks here\u8230\'20\'26 My worst fears are that we cause significant \u8211\'20\'13 we, the field, the technology, the industry \u8211\'20\'13 cause significant harm to the world.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Behind closed doors, Altman\u8217\'20\'19s lobbyists keep pushing for weaker regulation of AI, or none at all\par \par \uc2 Presumably Altman doesn\u8217\'20\'19t want to live in regret and infamy. But behind closed doors, his lobbyists keep pushing for weaker regulation, or none at all. A month after the Senate hearing, it came out that \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  was working {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://time.com/6288245/openai-eu-lobbying-ai-act/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to water down the EU\u8217\'20\'19s AI act}}}\uc2 . By the time he was fired by \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  in November 2023 for being \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://openai.com/index/openai-announces-leadership-transition/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 not consistently candid}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D with its board, I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t all that surprised.\par \par \uc2 At the time, few people supported the board\u8217\'20\'19s decision to fire Altman. A huge number of supporters came to his aid; many treated him like a saint. The well-known journalist Kara Swisher (known to be quite friendly with Altman) blocked me on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  for merely suggesting that the board might have a point. Altman played the media well. Five days later he was reinstated, with the help of \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s major investor, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , and a petition supporting Altman from employees.\par \par \uc2 But a lot has changed since. In recent months, concerns about Altman\u8217\'20\'19s candour have gone from heretical to fashionable. Journalist Edward Zitron {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wheresyoured.at/false-prophet/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  that Altman was \u8220\'20\'1Ca false prophet \u8211\'20\'13 a seedy grifter that uses his remarkable ability to impress and manipulate Silicon Valley\u8217\'20\'19s elite\u8221\'20\'1D. Ellen Huet of Bloomberg News, on the podcast {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/audio/2024-06-05/foundering-openai-e1-most-silicon-valley-man-alive-podcast"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Foundering}}}\uc2 , reached the conclusion that \u8220\'20\'1Cwhen [Altman] says something, you cannot be sure that he actually means it\u8221\'20\'1D. Paris Marx has warned of \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://disconnect.blog/sam-altmans-self-serving-vision-of-the-future/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sam Altman\u8217\'20\'19s self-serving vision}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. AI pioneer Geoffrey Hinton recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/jonerlichman/status/1801970281239511146?s=61"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 questioned Altman\u8217\'20\'19s motives}}}\uc2 . I myself wrote an essay called the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://garymarcus.substack.com/p/the-sam-altman-playbook"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sam Altman Playbook}}}\uc2 , dissecting how he had managed to fool so many people for so long, with a mixture of hype and apparent humility.\par \par \uc2 Many things have led to this collapse in faith. For some, the trigger moment was Altman\u8217\'20\'19s interactions {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/may/20/chatgpt-scarlett-johansson-voice"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 earlier this year}}}\uc2  with Scarlett Johansson, who explicitly asked him not to make a chatbot with her voice. Altman proceeded to use a different voice actor, but one who was obviously similar to her in voice, and tweeted \u8220\'20\'1CHer\u8221\'20\'1D (a reference to a movie in which Johansson supplied the voice for an AI). {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2024/05/21/1252623068/scarlett-johansson-demands-answers-about-the-voice-for-chatgpts-personal-assista"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Johansson was livid}}}\uc2 . And the ScarJo fiasco was emblematic of a larger issue: big companies such as \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  insist their models won\u8217\'20\'19t work unless they are trained on all the world\u8217\'20\'19s intellectual property, but the companies have given little or no compensation to many of the artists, writers and others who have created it. Actor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/04/06/technology/tech-giants-harvest-data-artificial-intelligence.html?"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Justine Bateman described}}}\uc2  it as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe largest theft in the [history of the] United States, period\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  has long paid lip service to the value of developing measures for AI safety, but several key safety-related staff recently departed, claiming that promises had not been kept. Former \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  safety researcher Jan Leike said the company {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fortune.com/2024/05/17/openai-researcher-resigns-safety/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prioritised shiny things over safety}}}\uc2 , as did another recently departed employee, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/tristanharris/status/1799221864558215467?s=61"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 William Saunders}}}\uc2 . Co-founder Ilya Sutskever departed and called his new venture Safe Superintelligence, while former \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  employee Daniel Kokotajlo, too, has warned that promises around safety {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/jam3scampbell/status/1799218184010236114?s=43"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 were being disregarded}}}\uc2 . As bad as social media has been for society, errant AI, which \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  could accidentally develop, could (as Altman himself notes) be far worse.\par \par \uc2 The disregard \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  has shown for safety is compounded by the fact that the company appears to be on a campaign to keep its employees quiet. In May, journalist Kelsey Piper uncovered documents that allowed the company to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/future-perfect/351132/openai-vested-equity-nda-sam-altman-documents-employees"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claw back vested stock from former employees}}}\uc2  who would not agree to not speak ill of the company, a practice many industry insiders found shocking. Soon after, many former \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  employees subsequently signed a letter at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://righttowarn.ai/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 righttowarn.ai}}}\uc2  demanding whistleblower protections, and as a result the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2024/05/openai-ends-harsh-non-disparagement-agreements-that-could-claw-back-millions/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 company climbed down}}}\uc2 , stating it would not enforce these contracts.\par \par \uc2 Even the company\u8217\'20\'19s board felt misled. In May, former \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  board member Helen Toner {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ted.com/talks/the_ted_ai_show_what_really_went_down_at_openai_and_the_future_of_regulation_w_helen_toner?subtitle=en"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told the }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ted.com/talks/the_ted_ai_show_what_really_went_down_at_openai_and_the_future_of_regulation_w_helen_toner?subtitle=en"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ted AI Show}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ted.com/talks/the_ted_ai_show_what_really_went_down_at_openai_and_the_future_of_regulation_w_helen_toner?subtitle=en"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  podcast}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CFor years, Sam made it really difficult for the board\u8230\'20\'26 by, you know, withholding information, misrepresenting things that were happening at the company, in some cases outright lying to the board.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 By late May, bad press for \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and its CEO had accumulated so steadily that the venture capitalist \uc2 Matt Turck\uc2  posted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/mattturck/status/1792880872661618700?s=61"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a cartoon on X}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1Cdays since last easily avoidable \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  controversy: 0.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * * *\par \par \uc2 Yet Altman is still there, and still incredibly powerful. He still runs \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , and to a large extent he is still the public face of AI. He has rebuilt the board of \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  largely to his liking. Even as recently as April 2024, homeland security secretary Alejandro Mayorkas travelled to visit Altman, to recruit him for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2024/04/26/altman-mayorkas-dhs-ai-safety-board"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 homeland security\u8217\'20\'19s AI safety and security board}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A lot is at stake. The way that AI develops now will have lasting consequences. Altman\u8217\'20\'19s choices could easily affect all of humanity \u8211\'20\'13 not just individual users \u8211\'20\'13 in lasting ways. Already, as \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  has acknowledged, its tools have been used by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/05/30/technology/openai-influence-campaigns-report.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Russia and China for creating disinformation}}}\uc2 , presumably with the intent to influence elections. More advanced forms of AI, if they are developed, could pose even more serious risks. Whatever social media has done, in terms of polarising society and subtly influencing people\u8217\'20\'19s beliefs, massive AI companies could make worse.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, generative AI, made popular by \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , is having a massive environmental impact, measured in terms of electricity usage, emissions and water usage. As \uc2 Bloomberg{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/graphics/2024-ai-data-centers-power-grids/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recently put it}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CAI is already wreaking havoc on global power systems.\u8221\'20\'1D That impact could grow, perhaps considerably, as models themselves get larger (the goal of all the bigger players). To a large extent, governments are going on Altman\u8217\'20\'19s say-so that AI will pay off in the end (it certainly has not so far), justifying the environmental costs.\par \par \uc2 I honestly don\u8217\'20\'19t think we will get to an AI that we can trust if we stay on the current path\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  has taken on a leadership position, and Altman is on the homeland security safety board. His advice should be taken with scepticism. Altman was at least briefly trying to attract investors to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/tech/ai/sam-altman-seeks-trillions-of-dollars-to-reshape-business-of-chips-and-ai-89ab3db0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $7trn investment}}}\uc2  in infrastructure around generative AI, which could turn out to be a tremendous waste of resources that could perhaps be better spent elsewhere, if (as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nautil.us/deep-learning-is-hitting-a-wall-238440/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 I}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/tsarnick/status/1800644136942367131?s=61"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 many others}}}\uc2  suspect) generative AI is not the correct path to AGI [artificial general intelligence].\par \par \uc2 Finally, overestimating current AI could lead to war. The US-China \u8220\'20\'1Cchip war\u8221\'20\'1D over export controls, for example \u8211\'20\'13 in which the US is limiting the export of critical GPU chips designed by \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , manufactured in Taiwan \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theinformation.com/articles/a-new-u-s-crackdown-is-crippling-chinas-best-hope-to-rival-nvidia?utm_source=ti_app&rc=dcf9pt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 is impacting China\u8217\'20\'19s ability to proceed in AI}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theinformation.com/articles/a-new-u-s-crackdown-is-crippling-chinas-best-hope-to-rival-nvidia?utm_source=ti_app&rc=dcf9pt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 escalating tensions between the two nations}}}\uc2 . The battle over chips is largely predicated on the notion that AI will continue to improve exponentially, even though data suggests current approaches may recently have reached a point of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://garymarcus.substack.com/p/evidence-that-llms-are-reaching-a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 diminishing returns}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Altman may well have started out with good intentions. Maybe he really did want to save the world from threats from AI, and guide AI for good. Perhaps greed took over, as it so often does.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, many other AI companies seem to be on the path of hype and corner-cutting that Altman charted. \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 formed from a set of \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  refugees who were worried that AI safety wasn\u8217\'20\'19t taken seriously enough \u8211\'20\'13 seems increasingly to be competing directly with the mothership, with all that entails. The billion-dollar startup Perplexity seems to be another object lesson in greed, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wired.com/story/perplexity-is-a-bullshit-machine/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 training on data it isn\u8217\'20\'19t supposed to be using}}}\uc2 . \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , meanwhile, went from advocating \u8220\'20\'1Cresponsible AI\u8221\'20\'1D to rushing out products with serious problems, pressuring \uc2 Google\uc2  to do the same. Money and power are corrupting AI, much as they corrupted social media.\par \par \uc2 We simply can\u8217\'20\'19t trust giant, privately held AI startups to govern themselves in ethical and transparent ways. And if we can\u8217\'20\'19t trust them to govern themselves, we certainly shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t let them govern the world.\par \par \uc2 * * *\par \par \uc2 I honestly don\u8217\'20\'19t think we will get to an AI that we can trust if we stay on the current path. Aside from the corrupting influence of power and money, there is a deep technical issue, too: large language models (the core technique of generative AI) invented by \uc2 Google\uc2  and made famous by Altman\u8217\'20\'19s company, are unlikely ever to be safe. They are recalcitrant, and opaque by nature \u8211\'20\'13 so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cblack boxes\u8221\'20\'1D that we can never fully rein in. The statistical techniques that drive them can do some amazing things, like speed up computer programming and create plausible-sounding interactive characters in the style of deceased loved ones or historical figures. But such black boxes have never been reliable, and as such they are a poor basis for AI that we could trust with our lives and our infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 That said, I don\u8217\'20\'19t think we should abandon AI. Making better AI \u8211\'20\'13 for medicine, and material science, and climate science, and so on \u8211\'20\'13 really could transform the world. Generative AI is unlikely to do the trick, but some future, yet-to-be developed form of AI might.\par \par \uc2 The irony is that the biggest threat to AI today may be the AI companies themselves; their bad behaviour and hyped promises are turning a lot of people off. Many are ready for government to take a stronger hand. According to a June poll by Artificial Intelligence Policy Institute, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theaipi.org/april-voters-prefer-ai-regulation-over-self-regulation/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 80% of American voters}}}\uc2  prefer \u8220\'20\'1Cregulation of AI that mandates safety measures and government oversight of AI labs instead of allowing AI companies to self-regulate\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 To get to an AI we can trust, I have long lobbied for a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://garymarcus.substack.com/p/a-cern-for-ai-and-the-global-governance"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cross-national effort}}}\uc2 , similar to Cern\u8217\'20\'19s high-energy physics consortium. The time for that is now. Such an effort, focused on AI safety and reliability rather than profit, and on developing a new set of AI techniques that belong to humanity \u8211\'20\'13 rather than to just a handful of greedy companies \u8211\'20\'13 could be transformative.\par \par \uc2 More than that, citizens need to speak up, and demand an AI that is good for the many and not just the few. One thing I can guarantee is that we won\u8217\'20\'19t get to AI\u8217\'20\'19s promised land if we leave everything in the hands of Silicon Valley. Tech bosses have shaded the truth for decades. Why should we expect \uc2 Sam Altman\uc2 , last seen cruising around Napa Valley in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://qz.com/sam-altman-spotted-driving-multi-million-dollar-koenigs-1851592678"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $4m Koenigsegg supercar}}}\uc2 , to be any different?\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://garymarcus.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Gary Marcus}}}\uc2  is a scientist, entrepreneur\par \par \uc2 and bestselling author. He was\par \par \uc2 founder and CEO of machine learning company Geometric Intelligence, which was acquired by \uc2 Uber\uc2 , and is the author of six books, including the forthcoming Taming Silicon Valley (MIT Press)\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240806ek85002pl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
