\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Fear of US recession rattles global markets as tech shares fall\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden and Jasper Jolly \par \uc2 909 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2024\par 12:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 42\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Europe\u8217\'20\'19s main indices all decline and Japanese equities suffer worst day since 2020 while gold hits fresh record\par \par \uc2 Stock markets in Europe, Asia and New York tumbled on Friday as fears of a US economic slump grew and technology shares were hit by underwhelming earnings.\par \par \uc2 Concerns that the US could be sliding towards a recession spurred {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/aug/02/stock-markets-nikkei-ftse-us-gdp-growth-non-farm-payrolls-nfp-bank-of-england-andrew-bailey-business-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a global sell-off}}}\uc2 , which accelerated after a poor employment report on Friday showed that the US jobs market was cooling fast, pushing up the unemployment rate.\par \par \uc2 Economists fear the US economy could be weaker than central bankers at the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  had realised, and could force the Fed into a sharp cut in borrowing costs in September \u8211\'20\'13 or even an emergency rate cut before that \u8211\'20\'13 to stimulate demand.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe sharp slowdown in payrolls in July and sharper rise in the unemployment rate makes a September interest rate cut inevitable and will increase speculation that the Fed will kick off its loosening cycle with a 50bp cut or even an intra-meeting move,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Stephen Brown\uc2 , deputy chief North America economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The weak jobs report added to anxiety after data this week showed weakness in the US manufacturing sector, and disappointing results from semiconductor maker Intel, which hammered its shares.\par \par \uc2 The tech-focused Nasdaq index lost 2.4% to finish in correction territory \u8211\'20\'13 10% off its record high \u8211\'20\'13 after the latest US non-farm payroll report showed that only 114,000 jobs were created last month, compared with the 175,000 analysts expected. In another blow, the US unemployment rate rose from 4.1% to 4.3%.\par \par \uc2 Japanese equities suffered their worst day since the Covid-19 pandemic rocked markets in 2020; the Nikkei 225 share index tumbled by 5.8% to its lowest closing level since January. The broader Japanese Topix fell 6.1%, Australia\u8217\'20\'19s ASX fell 2.5% and Hong Kong\u8217\'20\'19s Hang Seng was down 2.1%.\par \par \uc2 Europe\u8217\'20\'19s main stock indices also declined on Friday, with European technology stocks falling to their lowest level in more than six months. France\u8217\'20\'19s CAC 40 hit its lowest level since last November, down over 1%, while Germany\u8217\'20\'19s Dax lost 2%.\par \par \uc2 The Dutch chipmaking equipment manufacturer ASML\u8217\'20\'19s shares fell 9.6%, while the rival \uc2 ASM International\uc2  dropped 13.7%.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe past 24 hours have seen an increasingly precarious backdrop for risk markets, with a risk-off mood on the back of another batch of weak US data yesterday followed by mostly downbeat tech earnings overnight,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jim Reid, an analyst at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 , on Friday morning.\par \par \uc2 In London, the FTSE 100 blue-chip share index lost more than 120 points at one stage, down 1.5%.\par \par \uc2 Friday\u8217\'20\'19s sell-off followed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/01/investors-trigger-major-wall-street-sell-off-recession"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a rough day\u8217\'20\'19s trading on Wall Street}}}\uc2  on Thursday, where the Dow Jones industrial average fell 1.2%, or almost 500 points. This was triggered by data showing US manufacturing activity dropped to an eight-month low in July amid a slump in new orders, and a jump in the number of Americans filing new applications for unemployment benefits to an 11-month high.\par \par \uc2 New orders for US-manufactured goods fell by 3.3% in June, data on Friday showed, adding to anxiety that economic demand was tailing off. The Fed left US interest rates on hold on Wednesday, but hinted that a rate cut was close.\par \par \uc2 Financial markets are now pricing in a 100% chance that the Fed will cut rates in September, with a large, half-point cut seen as a 60% chance after the weak jobs report.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Fed remain wary about inflation, but this week\u8217\'20\'19s employment cost index and unit labour cost data should really have boosted their confidence that inflation is on the path to 2%. Their focus needs to be the state of the jobs market,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 James Knightley\uc2 , ING\u8217\'20\'19s chief international economist.\par \par \uc2 The US dollar weakened, lifting the pound by 0.5% to $1.28, and the euro by 1.2% to $1.092.\par \par \uc2 Intel\u8217\'20\'19s shares plunged over 28% after it announced plans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/aug/01/intel-lay-offs-shares-decline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to cut more than 15,000 jobs}}}\uc2  globally as it tries to \u8220\'20\'1Cresize and refocus\u8221\'20\'1D its business. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  shares were down 10% after it missed sales forecasts and disappointed analysts with its latest outlook.\par \par \uc2 Shares in the chipmaker \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  fell 2.7% after a report that the \uc2 US Department of Justice\uc2  had launched an investigation into complaints from competitors that it may have abused its market dominance in selling chips that power artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Russ Mould, an investment director at \uc2 AJ Bell\uc2 , said rising economic pessimism meant August had got off to a bad start for global stock markets.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAn economy going through a bad patch is one catalyst for a central bank to cut rates and hopefully stimulate activity,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis thought process is likely to be at the top of the agenda for the Fed this week after shocking US economic data that featured bigger than expected jobless claims and contraction in manufacturing. The narrative has changed from rate cuts equating to good news to rate cuts meaning measures to avoid recession.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While shares slid, gold hit a fresh record on Friday as investors flocked to safe-haven assets. Gold {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.marketwatch.com/investing/future/gc00"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 futures}}}\uc2  at one point on Friday were up $25.70 (\u163\'00\'A320.17), or 1%, to $2,506.40 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240802ek82001ut\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
