\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Trump claims he\u8217\'20\'19s pro-worker. Project 2025 will gut labor rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 2,385 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2024\par 11:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Far-right plan for a Republican presidency would undercut unions, strip child labor laws and boost corporate profits\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump }}}\uc2  proclaimed he was for \u8220\'20\'1Call the forgotten men and women\u8221\'20\'1D, in his acceptance speech at the Republican convention. His vice-presidential pick {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/jd-vance"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 JD Vance}}}\uc2  consistently portrays himself as a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jul/30/jd-vance-project-2025-book-foreword"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pro-worker populist}}}\uc2 . But an analysis of the labor chapter of Project 2025 \u8211\'20\'13 an ambitious rightwing plan to guide the next Republican presidency \u8211\'20\'13 found it has little to offer them.\par \par \uc2 Project 2025\u8217\'20\'19s labor section proposes hardly anything to improve workers\u8217\'20\'19 wages and working conditions. It is, however, chock full of recommendations that would boost corporate profits, undercut labor unions and advance the rightwing culture war.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://static.project2025.org/2025_MandateForLeadership_FULL.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Project 2025}}}\uc2  contains several recommendations that would, when taken together, cut the pay of millions of workers, especially by making overtime pay available to fewer workers, even though many Americans rely on overtime pay to make ends meet. This so-called \u8220\'20\'1CPresidential Transition Project\u8221\'20\'1D shows outright hostility toward government employee unions \u8211\'20\'13 whether police unions, firefighters\u8217\'20\'19 unions or teachers\u8217\'20\'19 unions \u8211\'20\'13 saying that Congress should consider abolishing all public sector unions. Project 2025 would further undermine unions by recommending a ban on the use of card check, one of labor\u8217\'20\'19s most effective tools to organize workers. Once a union gets a majority of employees at a workplace to sign pro-union cards, unions often point to this majority support to persuade employers to grant union recognition and bargain.\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 was undertaken by \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2  and was written by numerous Trump allies, many of whom served in his administration and many of whom are likely to serve under him again if he wins in November {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jul/08/trump-project-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 , Trump has distanced himself from the project}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s hard-right proposals, arguing, contradictorily, that he knows nothing about the project while adding that he disagrees with some of its proposals. Political analysts predict that if Trump is elected, his administration will pursue many of Project 2025\u8217\'20\'19s policies.\par \par \uc2 Worker advocates have vigorously condemned Project 2025. \uc2 Stuart Appelbaum\uc2 , president of the Retail, Wholesale and Department Store Union, said: \u8220\'20\'1CFor 900 pages, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s Project 2025 playbook dives into excruciating detail on how a Trump-Vance administration will roll back workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights, curbing the right to organize, eliminating overtime pay laws, gutting health and safety protections and protections against child labor.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The 37-page labor chapter contains recommendation after recommendation designed to make corporations and rightwing ideologues happy. With many employers complaining that today\u8217\'20\'19s low jobless rate makes it hard to find enough workers, Project 2025 recommends making it easier for 16- and 17-year-olds to work in dangerous jobs \u8211\'20\'13 jobs that federal law currently makes off-limits to workers under the age of 18.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSome young adults show an interest in inherently dangerous jobs. Current rules forbid many young people \u8230\'20\'26 from working in such jobs. This results in worker shortages in dangerous fields and often discourages otherwise interested young workers from trying the more dangerous job,\u8221\'20\'1D Project 2025 says.\par \par \uc2 The project says the Department of Labor should amend its regulations to let teenagers \u8220\'20\'1Cwork in more dangerous occupations\u8221\'20\'1D, for instance, metal-stamping plants with heavy machinery. Project 2025\u8217\'20\'19s authors seem far more concerned about assuring that more teens work in dangerous jobs than about protecting against the perils those jobs pose for young workers.\par \par \uc2 David Weil, who headed the labor department\u8217\'20\'19s wage and hour administration under {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/barack-obama"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Barack Obama}}}\uc2 , criticized this recommendation, saying the nation had adopted child labor laws partly because of the huge power imbalance between employers and young workers. \u8220\'20\'1CWe protect children from hazardous work as a statement of our values as a society and because the young people most likely to agree to hazardous work are the most vulnerable to that power imbalance,\u8221\'20\'1D Weil said.\par \par \uc2 Helping\par \par \uc2 corporations\par \par \uc2 cut\par \par \uc2 labor\par \par \uc2 costs\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 has several far-reaching proposals that would reduce corporations\u8217\'20\'19 labor costs and boost their bottom lines. The project calls for repealing the 93-year-old Davis-Bacon Act, which requires contractors on public works projects to pay the prevailing wage paid to local workers doing similar work. Davis-Bacon was enacted to prevent government contractors from winning projects by underbidding competitors through ever-lower labor costs. The nation\u8217\'20\'19s building trades unions vigorously oppose repealing Davis-Bacon because that would push down construction wages.\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 would shrink many workers\u8217\'20\'19 paychecks by calling for a law that limits when they receive {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jul/23/labor-unions-kamala-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overtime pay}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 to only when they work more than 80 hours over a two-week period, instead of the current system of working more than 40 hours in one week. That means if an employee works 55 hours one week and 25 the next, that worker won\u8217\'20\'19t qualify for overtime pay, despite working 55 hours one week.\par \par \uc2 In another proposal that would slice labor costs, Project 2025 says workers who qualify for overtime pay should be able to choose to receive compensatory time rather than time-and-a-half overtime pay. The project says this idea aims to give workers more time with their families, but many worker advocates say it is a ploy to enable employers to twist workers\u8217\'20\'19 arms so that they take comp time instead of time-and-a-half pay.\par \par \uc2 Weil, the former wage and hour director, warned that employers might abuse this proposal: \u8220\'20\'1CUnfortunately, in too many work settings, workers would be pressured into taking the accumulated time option and then have trouble using that time when they chose to do so,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 would also cut labor costs by proposing to make it easier for gig workers like \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers to be deemed independent contractors and not employees. Unlike employees, independent contractors are not covered by minimum wage and overtime laws, and employers don\u8217\'20\'19t have to pay part of their social security and Medicare taxes.\par \par \uc2 Full of anti-union provisions\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 would weaken unions or make life tougher for them in at least a dozen ways. Beyond calling for a ban on card check, it would make it easier for workers to decertify their unions. Under current law, workers can only vote to decertify, ie get rid of their union, for a short period before their union contracts expire (or when there is no contract in force). Project 2025 would allow such decertification votes any time a union contract is in force.\par \par \uc2 It would prohibit project labor agreements \u8211\'20\'13 a proposal that will infuriate building trades unions. These union-friendly agreements are used to smooth labor relations on large construction projects and often assure that projects use unionized construction contractors. Project 2025\u8217\'20\'19s authors say banning these agreements would save taxpayers\u8217\'20\'19 money by lowering labor costs.\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 calls for rescinding the Persuader Rule, which requires employers to disclose the union-busting law firms or consultants they use and how much they paid for such services. Unions often use that information to embarrass companies by showing that aggressive, anti-union companies such as \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spend millions of dollars to keep out unions and prevent bargaining that would improve pay and working conditions.\par \par \uc2 Trump already showed us his creepy agenda for workers, and frankly nothing in Project 2025 is different from his record in office\par \par \uc2 Sara Nelson\par \par \uc2 In a move that would make it harder for fast-food workers to unionize, Project 2025 calls for preventing the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  from declaring that heavily franchised companies like \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  and \uc2 Burger King\uc2  are joint employers. If deemed joint employers, \uc2 McDonalds\uc2  and \uc2 Burger King\uc2  might need to bargain with workers at unionized franchisees \u8211\'20\'13 and might be held jointly liable if a franchisee violates labor laws. The labor chapter also calls for firing \u8220\'20\'1Con day one\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Jennifer Abruzzo\uc2 , the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s vigorously pro-worker general counsel.\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 calls for maximizing the number of political employees in the Department of Labor and recommends changes to let department officials launch investigations of labor unions even when workers have not filed a complaint. Worker advocates fear that this could lead to harassment and retaliation against unions.\par \par \uc2 Among its many anti-union proposals, the labor chapter has a surprising pro-union recommendation, saying the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  should pursue more injunctions to reinstate workers who were illegally fired for supporting a union. It adds that \u8220\'20\'1Csuch firings have an immediate chilling effect on organizing\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Labor Relations\uc2  Act does not cover government employee unions, but Project 2025 calls for amending that law to require all government employee unions to do the same type of rigorous annual financial projecting and disclosure that private sector unions now do. Across the US, there are more than 200 worker centers, non-union groups that have fought vigorously on behalf of immigrant workers, and the project urges the labor department to investigate them and calls for requiring them to file detailed annual financial projects, just like labor unions.\par \par \uc2 One theme running throughout Project 2025 is a desire to make the federal administrative state less powerful and invasive. In sharp contrast, the labor chapter calls for making the labor department considerably more powerful and invasive when it comes to labor unions and worker centers \u8211\'20\'13 institutions that are widely disliked by \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s corporate backers.\par \par \uc2 In a move that union leaders vigorously oppose, the labor chapter calls for amending federal law to allow corporations to form non-union \u8220\'20\'1Cemployee involvement organizations\u8221\'20\'1D. The project says this would \u8220\'20\'1Cfacilitate voluntary cooperation on critical issues like working conditions\u8221\'20\'1D. But union leaders warn under this proposal, corporations would dominate these \u8220\'20\'1Cemployee involvement organizations\u8221\'20\'1D, with management often choosing the worker representatives and being free to ignore the committees\u8217\'20\'19 recommendations, no matter how earnestly the workers might want something. The project criticizes today\u8217\'20\'19s labor laws on unions, saying: \u8220\'20\'1CAmerica\u8217\'20\'19s one-size-fits-all approach undermines worker representation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 contains no recommendations for legislation that would amplify workers\u8217\'20\'19 voices by making it easier for workers to unionize. For instance, it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t call for letting the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  impose fines on companies that violate labor laws by, for instance, firing workers who support a union. That is one of the main provisions of the Protecting the Right to Organize Act, which is overwhelmingly favored by Democrats and opposed by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 In another proposal that labor leaders have denounced, Project 2025 would vastly revamp the federal civil service. It calls for replacing many non-partisan, professional federal employees with partisan workers far more ready to do a president\u8217\'20\'19s bidding. Congress moved against such a partisan spoils system in the 1880s and replaced it with merit-based hiring because the old system frequently led to poor administration, worse delivery of services and federal employees doing politics instead of their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Pursuing the rightwing culture war\par \par \uc2 Project 2025\u8217\'20\'19s labor chapter begins with a distinctly religious \u8220\'20\'1Cmission statement\u8221\'20\'1D, saying it aims to \u8220\'20\'1Cgive workers the support they need for rewarding, well-paying, and self-driven careers, and restore the family-supporting job as the centerpiece of the American economy. The Judeo-Christian tradition, stretching back to Genesis, has always recognized fruitful work as integral to human dignity, as service to God, neighbor, and family.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The labor chapter contains just one recommendation that would raise workers\u8217\'20\'19 pay, and it is directly related to religion. Noting that \u8220\'20\'1CGod ordained the Sabbath as a day of rest,\u8221\'20\'1D the labor chapter calls for granting workers overtime pay for whenever they work on their Sabbath.\par \par \uc2 After the mission statement, the labor section turns immediately to culture war issues. Its very first recommendation is an attack on what it calls \u8220\'20\'1Cthe DEI Revolution\u8221\'20\'1D. Project 2025 recommends reversing and eliminating any government policies that promote diversity, equity and inclusion in the workplace. Describing DEI as \u8220\'20\'1Cmanagerialist leftwing race and gender ideology\u8221\'20\'1D, the project calls for banning the \uc2 Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2  from collecting data on race and ethnicity. Such data can be used to bring lawsuits against employers for racial discrimination.\par \par \uc2 Next, the labor section attacks critical race theory, saying Congress should prohibit the federal government from using taxpayer dollars to fund any critical race theory trainings. Continuing on culture war issues, the labor chapter next suggests that federal agencies are too concerned with protecting LGBTQ+ workers and says agencies should instead focus their enforcement of sex discrimination laws on \u8220\'20\'1Cthe biological binary meaning of \u8216\'20\'18sex\u8217\'20\'19.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Project 2025\u8217\'20\'19s labor chapter stresses the importance of making jobs work for families, but it stops short of calling for policies that workers say would help them support their families and improve their work-family balance. Project 2025 says nothing about raising the $7.25-an-hour federal minimum wage, which has remained unchanged for 15 years. As president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did nothing to raise the minimum wage, and the Republican Congress blocked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s push to raise the minimum to $15 an hour.\par \par \uc2 One family-friendly measure that US workers would love to see is a federal law guaranteeing paid family and medical leave. Americans favor that idea {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.standard.com/businesses-organizations/workplace-solutions/paid-family-medical-leave/relatively-speaking/majority"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 74% to 15%}}}\uc2 . Although Project 2025 says it\u8217\'20\'19s family friendly, it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t call for such a law. According to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/ebs2.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 44% of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s lower-wage}}}\uc2  private sector workers don\u8217\'20\'19t receive paid sick leave. Moreover, the United States is the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/10/25/upshot/paid-leave-democrats.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 only rich nation}}}\uc2  without nationwide paid parental leave. Corporate America has vigorously opposed such a law, calling it an onerous employer mandate.\par \par \uc2 Trump and Project 2025\u8217\'20\'19s authors were evidently surprised by the fierce backlash against its far-right agenda. On Tuesday Paul Dans, a director of the project housed at \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 , stepped down after criticism from Trump.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTrump already showed us his creepy agenda for workers, and frankly nothing in Project 2025 is different from his record in office,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Sara Nelson\uc2 , president of the \uc2 Association of Flight Attendants-CWA\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CA sane candidate would repudiate a plan this extreme and pledge that anyone involved in creating it won\u8217\'20\'19t get within 35,000ft of an administration post. But he won\u8217\'20\'19t because Project 2025 is nothing new \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s just a plan to supercharge the worst, weirdest parts of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s own agenda.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  is co-publishing this piece with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tcf.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Century Foundation}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240804ek84000xg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
