\par\par \uc2 Books\par \b \uc2 Complicit by Reah Bravo review \u8211\'20\'13 a revisionist history of #MeToo\b0\par\par\uc2 Rebecca Nicholson \par \uc2 732 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2024\par 09:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 50\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 An attempt to understand the complexities of abuse that is more nuanced than its provocative title suggests\par \par \uc2 In 2017, Reah Bravo was one of eight women who accused her former boss, the US journalist and talkshow host \uc2 Charlie Rose\uc2 , of sexual misconduct. After initial reports in the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , a further 27 women came forward with similar allegations. Complicit is not a straight memoir about working for Rose. In fact, Bravo, now a speechwriter living in Brussels, opens with a bold and distancing statement: \u8220\'20\'1CEnough time has passed that I can say I regret calling \uc2 Charlie Rose\uc2  a sexual predator,\u8221\'20\'1D she writes. Like the title of the book, this is a provocative and arresting place to begin.\par \par \uc2 In a 2018 article for the New York Review of Books, Bravo, who was a producer on Rose\u8217\'20\'19s PBS show, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nybooks.com/online/2018/05/04/the-open-secret-of-charlie-rose/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 detailed unblocking her ex-boss\u8217\'20\'19s clogged toilet}}}\uc2 . When people responded with outrage, \u8220\'20\'1CI knew I had failed to accurately convey my message,\u8221\'20\'1D she writes here. She had not been \u8220\'20\'1Cforced\u8221\'20\'1D, as readers assumed, but saw her acquiescence to Rose\u8217\'20\'19s demeaning requests as evidence of her \u8220\'20\'1Cunflappable\u8221\'20\'1D nature in the workplace. She argues now that this was a form of complicity, and a \u8220\'20\'1Cbrutal recognition that the soft power of the patriarchy had its way with me\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 There are more examples, not just from her own experience. Bravo describes women who blame themselves, and women who blame victims for putting themselves in harm\u8217\'20\'19s way, for not running away, for not fighting back. She claims that such behaviour, conscious or otherwise, is a form of complicity, and that this plays a part in enabling predatory men.\par \par \uc2 Bravo examines some of the biggest cases of sexual misconduct, including those of \uc2 Charlie Rose\uc2 , Louis CK and \uc2 Harvey Weinstein\par \par \uc2 When broken down, this starts to seem relatively uncontroversial, despite the showy packaging \u8211\'20\'13 and Complicit starts to feel like a misleading title. Bravo says that Rose was \u8220\'20\'1Ca kind of predator, and I had been his prey, but I was starting to see an ecosystem that we had both done our part to sustain\u8221\'20\'1D. This, like the opening line, is attention grabbing, but as a central conceit it soon drops away. The tone becomes much more tentative and cautious. Of course, she writes, in one of a number of passages that appear written to preemptively combat criticism, women are not to be blamed for their abusers\u8217\'20\'19 behaviour. \u8220\'20\'1CWhy do anything that risks calling women\u8217\'20\'19s behaviour into question when such questioning is the very MO that has kept them down for millennia?\u8221\'20\'1D It is a point well taken but Bravo sometimes boxes herself in with equivocation.\par \par \uc2 Complicit is about workplace abuse in a post-#MeToo world, but it largely takes the form of a potted history of the explosion of the #MeToo movement into the mainstream. Bravo re-examines some of the biggest cases of sexual misconduct, including those of \uc2 Charlie Rose\uc2 , Louis CK, \uc2 Harvey Weinstein\uc2  and Matt Lauer. She interviews some of the women who came forward with stories about these men, and is able to discuss and disseminate their experiences in greater depth than a newspaper expos\u233\'00\'E9, for example, might afford them.\par \par \uc2 This comes alongside a smorgasbord of pop-feminism and psychology. The culture of the 1990s, Wonderbras, neoliberalism and individualism all shoulder some of the blame for institutionalised misogyny, though many of her examples feel specifically gen X. When she attempts to broaden her scope, touching very briefly on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/news/2024/jan/06/im-andrew-tates-audience-and-i-know-why-he-appeals-to-young-men"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Andrew Tate}}}\uc2 , it feels as though she\u8217\'20\'19s on less steady ground.\par \par \uc2 Bravo\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to understand the complexities of abuse, rather than pointing a finger at the abusers and leaving it there, is a useful endeavour. Yet there is a flaw in the project, which is that it relates to a very particular set of circumstances. It concerns ambitious young women working for powerful, famous older men, in the US, in \u8220\'20\'1Centertainment, journalism, politics\u8221\'20\'1D. This is Bravo\u8217\'20\'19s experience, and of course it is valid to keep asking questions about it, but I wonder if this specificity is why, for all of its provocations, Complicit lacks a convincing, broader diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Complicit: How Our Culture Enables Misbehaving Men by Reah Bravo is published by Gallery (\u163\'00\'A320). To support the Guardian and Observer buy a copy at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://guardianbookshop.com/complicit-9781982154745?utm_source=editoriallink&utm_medium=merch&utm_campaign=article"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 guardianbookshop.com}}}\uc2 . Delivery charges may apply\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240803ek8300106\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
