\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  interest rate cut bets rise as US jobs disappointment fuels growth fears \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Jasper Jolly \par \uc2 6,193 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2024\par 14:54 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Non-farm payrolls show US economy created 114,000 jobs in July, well below expectations, sparking speculation of an emergency interest rate cut\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 Summary: US jobs weakness prompts investors to bet on \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  rate cuts\par \par \uc2 The rise in US unemployment to a three-year high and the lower-than-expected number of new jobs have prompted investors to ramp up their bets on Federal Reserve interest rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 The shift in financial market bets on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  cutting interest rates is visible when comparing \uc2 CME\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s FedWatch tool before and after the jobs data.\par \par \uc2 Before the data, it was still showing a near certainty of rate cuts, but the chance of 0.5 percentage points (or two standard cuts) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/aug/02/stock-markets-nikkei-ftse-us-gdp-growth-non-farm-payrolls-nfp-bank-of-england-andrew-bailey-business-live?page=with:block-66ac8cca8f08cbd70fcdf250#block-66ac8cca8f08cbd70fcdf250"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was put at 28.5%}}}\uc2 . That market-implied probability has now jumped to 58.5% after jobs numbers.\par \par \uc2 Investors have piled into safe haven bonds and dumped US stocks, fearing that a recession may be on the way, and that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  may be \u8220\'20\'1Cbehind the curve\u8221\'20\'1D in supporting the economy. The Nasdaq composite index fell into technical \u8220\'20\'1Ccorrection\u8221\'20\'1D territory, down 10% from its recent peak.\par \par \uc2 Among investors and economists, the question is whether the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has made a fabled \u8220\'20\'1Cpolicy mistake\u8221\'20\'1D by cutting interest rates too late. Bear in mind that this is a US election year: a recession and high unemployment would be bad news for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , running against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for US president.\par \par \uc2 Ronald Temple, chief market strategist at \uc2 Lazard\uc2 , an investment bank, said:\par \par \uc2 The question is no longer whether the Fed will cut in September \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s whether the Fed should cut more aggressively than by 25 basis points in September. Today\u8217\'20\'19s job report suggests that the easing of tightness in US labor markets is threatening to turn into weakness.\par \par \uc2 The Fed was late to recognise the inflation problem. Let\u8217\'20\'19s hope it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t make the same mistake in the opposite direction.\par \par \uc2 Keeping rates on hold this week was indeed a mistake, said \uc2 Ian Shepherdson\uc2 , chief economist at Pantheon Macroeconomics, a consultancy. He said:\par \par \uc2 July\u8217\'20\'19s poor employment report leaves the Fed looking woefully behind the curve with its decision to hold rates this week, and suggests that the outcome of September\u8217\'20\'19s meeting now is finely balanced between 25bp and 50bp easings.\par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, we expect the trend in job growth to continue to deteriorate. [Surveyed] hiring intentions remain consistent with a further slowdown in growth in private payrolls, even though it has recovered a bit since the spring. In addition, jobless claims have picked up over recent weeks, job openings are back to pre-Covid norms, and households have become more fearful that unemployment will rise.\par \par \uc2 The real interest rate for corporate borrowing now is so high that an intense round of cost-cutting, including layoffs, likely lies ahead. Meanwhile, growth in state and local government revenues has petered out, signalling a stalling in government hiring.\par \par \uc2 In other news today from the world of business and economics:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Iberdrola\uc2 , the owner of \uc2 Scottish Power\uc2 , has struck a deal to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/aug/02/stock-markets-nikkei-ftse-us-gdp-growth-non-farm-payrolls-nfp-bank-of-england-andrew-bailey-business-live?page=with:block-66ace6e98f08cbd70fcdf7b1#block-66ace6e98f08cbd70fcdf7b1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 buy a major stake of }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/aug/02/stock-markets-nikkei-ftse-us-gdp-growth-non-farm-payrolls-nfp-bank-of-england-andrew-bailey-business-live?page=with:block-66ace6e98f08cbd70fcdf7b1#block-66ace6e98f08cbd70fcdf7b1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Electricity North West}}}\uc2  which values the power grid operator at approximately \u163\'00\'A34.25bn (\u8364\'20\'AC5bn).\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  chief economist \uc2 Huw Pill\uc2  has said the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/aug/02/stock-markets-nikkei-ftse-us-gdp-growth-non-farm-payrolls-nfp-bank-of-england-andrew-bailey-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66accea38f08cbd70fcdf603#block-66accea38f08cbd70fcdf603"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 battle against inflation is not over}}}\uc2  as he laid out the case against further interest rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Ryanair\uc2  has revealed it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/aug/02/stock-markets-nikkei-ftse-us-gdp-growth-non-farm-payrolls-nfp-bank-of-england-andrew-bailey-business-live?page=with:block-66aca82e8f08a0af63bb4821#block-66aca82e8f08a0af63bb4821"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cancelled 650 flights in July}}}\uc2  because of air traffic delays, while rival \uc2 Wizz Air\uc2  said the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/aug/01/crowdstrike-accused-of-defrauding-investors-in-class-action-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 global technology outage that hit Windows computers}}}\uc2  directly disrupted about 1% of its flights.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Jet engine maker Rolls-Royce is giving each of its employees {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/aug/02/stock-markets-nikkei-ftse-us-gdp-growth-non-farm-payrolls-nfp-bank-of-england-andrew-bailey-business-live?page=with:block-66ac96f38f08cbd70fcdf2c9#block-66ac96f38f08cbd70fcdf2c9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shares worth \u163\'00\'A3700}}}\uc2  after revealing bumper financial results.\par \par \uc2 You can continue to follow our live coverage from around the world:\par \par \uc2 In UK politics, police brace for more far-right riots as minister warns people about rallies in wake of Southport attack\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2024/aug/02/riots-southport-attack-far-right-keir-starmer-uk-politics-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Police brace for more far-right riots as minister warns people about rallies in wake of Southport attack \u8211\'20\'13 UK politics live}}}\par \par \uc2 In US politics, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s campaign raises $310m, far outpacing Trump\u8217\'20\'19s funding\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/aug/02/harris-trump-election-campaign-fundraising-updates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kamala Harris\u8217\'20\'19s campaign raises $310m, far outpacing Trump\u8217\'20\'19s funding \u8211\'20\'13 live updates}}}\par \par \uc2 In our coverage of the Russia-US prisoner swap, the Kremlin confirms hitman Vadim Krasikov worked for FSB security service\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2024/aug/02/russia-us-prisoner-swap-evan-gershkovich-paul-whelan-vadim-krasikov"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Russia-US prisoner swap: Kremlin confirms hitman Vadim Krasikov worked for FSB security service \u8211\'20\'13 live}}}\par \par \uc2 In our coverage of the Middle East crisis, assassinated \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leader \uc2 Ismail Haniyeh\uc2  buried in Qatar\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2024/aug/02/middle-east-crisis-live-killing-of-hamas-leader-doesnt-help-gaza-ceasefire-talks-biden-says"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Middle East crisis live: assassinated Hamas leader buried in Qatar}}}\par \par \uc2 And there is always the joy of our Olympics coverage to see you into the weekend:\par \par \uc2 Team GB wins equestrian gold, while Ireland\u8217\'20\'19s double sculls have defended their title\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/live/2024/aug/02/paris-2024-olympics-day-seven-live-updates-today-schedule-events-athletics-swimming-golf-rowing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Paris 2024 Olympics day seven: GB equestrian team strike gold; boxing, tennis and more \u8211\'20\'13 live}}}\par \par \uc2 And Morocco are beating the USA in the football quarter finals\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2024/aug/02/morocco-v-usa-paris-olympics-mens-soccer-quarter-finals-live-updates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Morocco v USA: Paris Olympics men\u8217\'20\'19s soccer quarter-finals \u8211\'20\'13 live updates}}}\par \par \uc2 Thanks for following the business live blog on a turbulent week. Please do join the Graeme Wearden on Monday as he returns to the hotseat. JJ\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.04pm BST\par \par \uc2 Electricity North West\uc2  valued at \u163\'00\'A34.25bn in \uc2 Iberdrola\uc2  takeover\par \par \uc2 The owner of \uc2 Scottish Power\uc2  has struck a deal to buy a major stake of \uc2 Electricity North West\uc2  which values the power grid operator at approximately \u163\'00\'A34.25bn (\u8364\'20\'AC5bn).\par \par \uc2 Spanish energy giant \uc2 Iberdrola\uc2  has agreed to buy an 88% stake in \uc2 Electricity North West\uc2  (ENW), which runs the electricity network connecting 5m customers across the North West of England, for an equity value of \u163\'00\'A32.1bn.\par \par \uc2 The 60,000 kilometre power network lies between the two existing networks license areas owned by \uc2 Scottish Power\uc2  in central and southern Scotland and Merseyside and North Wales which it runs alongside its energy supply business and one of Britain\u8217\'20\'19s largest renewable energy portfolios.\par \par \uc2 Ignacio Gal\u225\'00\'E1n\uc2 , \uc2 Iberdrola\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s executive chairman, said the deal underlines its strategy to invest significantly in electricity networks \u8220\'20\'1Cwhich are a critical component for supporting the electrification and decarbonisation of the economy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The network was reportedly put up for sale by its owners, Japan\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Kepco\uc2  and investment fund Equitix, last autumn in a sale which attracted interest from French energy group Engie, Canadian investor CDPQ and a consortium including private equity firm \uc2 KKR\uc2  with Dutch pension fund APG.\par \par \uc2 The deal has emerged almost one week after Australian infrastructure investor Macquarie agreed to take full control of Britain\u8217\'20\'19s core gas transmission network in a roughly \u163\'00\'A3700m deal.\par \par \uc2 Macquarie was already the network\u8217\'20\'19s majority owner but agreed to buy the final fifth of the gas network from \uc2 National Grid\uc2  despite the UK\u8217\'20\'19s ongoing efforts to shift away from fossil fuels in the future.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.36pm BST\par \par \uc2 Nasdaq stock index in correction - down 10% from peak\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq stock index has firmly entered {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/aug/02/stock-markets-nikkei-ftse-us-gdp-growth-non-farm-payrolls-nfp-bank-of-england-andrew-bailey-business-live?page=with:block-66acb0298f08cbd70fcdf3ef#block-66acb0298f08cbd70fcdf3ef"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 correction territory}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 down 10% from its peak, after the surprisingly soft US jobs report and poor earnings from some of its key tech industry members, including \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here are the opening snaps, via Reuters.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 S&P 500 DOWN 78.33 POINTS, OR 1.44%, AT 5,368.35\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 NASDAQ DOWN 409.23 POINTS, OR 2.38%, AT 16,784.91\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 DOW JONES DOWN 346.01 POINTS, OR 0.86%, AT 40,001.96\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 The aforementioned \uc2 Ernie Tedeschi\uc2 , director of economics at The Budget Lab at Yale university, thinks the US jobs numbers are not quite as bad as all that.\par \par \uc2 He makes the important point that the jobs numbers move around a fair bit as they are revised and refined over the following months. The average of the last three months is above 170,000 new jobs added, which doesn\u8217\'20\'19t scream \u8220\'20\'1Crecession\u8221\'20\'1D when put in historical context.\par \par \uc2 enltrThis is a treading water jobs report at 114K, which is almost exactly the number of jobs we mechanically need to add to keep up with the labor force.\par \par \uc2 But it's probably weaker than that since there will likely be future downward benchmark revisions. /1\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Ernie Tedeschi\uc2  (@ernietedeschi) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ernietedeschi/status/1819351970878308585?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 2, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrFirst the good news. We should never focus on a single month. The 3MMA is +170K/month, which is solid and nonrecesssionary. And an unemployment rate at 4.3% is consistent with estimates of medium-and-long-run trend. An economy that landed here would be fine. /2\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Ernie Tedeschi\uc2  (@ernietedeschi) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ernietedeschi/status/1819353978486010061?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 2, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Then again, that does not mean that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is in a comfortable place.\par \par \uc2 enltrBut now the bad news. It's not clear the economy is going to settle in here. The evidence of whether monetary policy was behind the curve on labor was debatable before (I thought the Fed should have cut last week). Now it's a lot clearer. /3\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Ernie Tedeschi\uc2  (@ernietedeschi) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ernietedeschi/status/1819354778977423592?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 2, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 A side note on the US jobs data: the recent hurricane did not affect the figures noticeably \u8211\'20\'13 despite some concerns that it might do.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  said:\par \par \uc2 Hurricane Beryl had no discernible effect on the national employment and unemployment data for July, and the response rates for the two surveys were within normal ranges.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 The US dollar was already weakening this morning as earnings and earlier manufacturing data gave a hint that the economy was slowing. But it dropped markedly on the half hour as the jobs data missed economists\u8217\'20\'19 expectations.\par \par \uc2 You can see the drop in the below chart showing today\u8217\'20\'19s trading session (in the New York time zone):\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.07pm BST\par \par \uc2 Financial markets are now pricing in a much higher chance of more interest rate cuts by the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 There is an 88% chance of the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s federal funds rate target range dropping a whole percentage point from its current range of between 5% and 5.25%, according to \uc2 CME\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s FedWatch tool, which is based on derivatives trades.\par \par \uc2 enltrMarkets not messing about!\par \par \uc2 CME Fedwatch now pointing towards a 43% chance that we will see rates slashed by 125bp by year-end (three meetings).\par \par \uc2 Base case is for 100bp worth of cuts (88% chance)\par \par \uc2 The decline in average hourly earnings really helped reiterate the potential for a\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Jq03aqvXO1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Jq03aqvXO1}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Joshua Mahony (@JoshuaMahony) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JoshuaMahony/status/1819357590628839680?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 2, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 Economist: Fed could be forced into intra-meeting move\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will surely have to act to stimulate the US economy given the signs of weakening, say economists.\par \par \uc2 The Fed\u8217\'20\'19s next interest rate committee meeting is in mid-September, but it could potentially hold an unscheduled meeting \u8211\'20\'13 effectively an emergency one.\par \par \uc2 Stephen Brown\uc2 , deputy chief North America economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 , a consultancy, said:\par \par \uc2 The sharp slowdown in payrolls in July and sharper rise in the unemployment rate makes a September interest rate cut inevitable and will increase speculation that the Fed will kick off its loosening cycle with a 50 bp cut or even an intra-meeting move.\par \par \uc2 The 114,000 gain in non-farm payrolls was much weaker than the consensus estimate of 175,000 and, based on the initial market reaction, weaker than what investors were braced for even after the downbeat initial claims and \uc2 ISM\uc2  releases yesterday. The slowdown was broad-based across the service sectors.\par \par \uc2 Neil Birrell\uc2 , chief investment officer at Premier Miton Investors, said:\par \par \uc2 US employment data couldn\u8217\'20\'19t have been released at a more sensitive time; markets are wobbling, concerns over Fed policy abound and corporate earnings are in the spotlight. The weak data will cause more angst, and concerns over the health of the economy will increase. We have pivoted from looking at a robust economy to a weakening one and while markets will reflect this, they will also price in the fact that the Fed still has plenty of scope to act.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 Here is more on the rush for bonds: prices of US Treasuries have jumped, pushing down yields markedly.\par \par \uc2 Reuters reports:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 US 10-year yields dropped as low as 3.79%, the lowest since December, and were last down 15.9 bps at 3.818%.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 U.S. two-year yields fell below 4% for the first time since May 2023. They were last at 3.945%, down 21.7 bps.\par \par \uc2 You can see how immediate the move was in this chart from Bloomberg News:\par \par \uc2 enltrThe bond markets go wild. 2yr now below 3.9% -- falling a stunning 27 basis points after the jobs report comes in much softer than expected. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/PtX6tuTq3L"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/PtX6tuTq3L}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Sonali Basak (@sonalibasak) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/sonalibasak/status/1819351213793517890?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 2, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 A former deputy director of the \uc2 US National Economic Council\uc2  says it is \u8220\'20\'1Cnot time to panic\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Bharat Ramamurti said on X:\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not time to panic \u8212\'20\'14 there are still many underlying signs of strength in the economy and unemployment remains low by historical standards \u8212\'20\'14 but the Fed failing to cut rates in July was a mistake and it needs to rectify that mistake in September with a cut of 50 bps or more.\par \par \uc2 enltrIt\u8217\'20\'19s not time to panic \u8212\'20\'14 there are still many underlying signs of strength in the economy and unemployment remains low by historical standards \u8212\'20\'14 but the Fed failing to cut rates in July was a mistake and it needs to rectify that mistake in September with a cut of 50 bps or more. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/s58FKO3B72"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/s58FKO3B72}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bharat Ramamurti (@BharatRamamurti) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BharatRamamurti/status/1819353798365564942?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 2, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 enltrNASDAQ-100 futures down 2.5% {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/UlhbFoHnWM"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/UlhbFoHnWM}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Joe Weisenthal (@TheStalwart) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TheStalwart/status/1819351031098093869?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 2, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Nasdaq 100 futures are now down 2.5%, while those for the S&P 500 are down 1.6% \u8211\'20\'13 it looks like a painful day ahead for Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 The US jobs data has triggered some financial market volatility.\par \par \uc2 The US dollar has slid. The dollar is down 0.7% against a trade-weighted basket of other currencies.\par \par \uc2 Investors are piling even more into bonds. The yield on the two-year UK gilt fell to its lowest since April 2023, in a sign that demand for government debt has increased.\par \par \uc2 And European stock markets have continued their slide: the FTSE 100 is down 0.5%, but France\u8217\'20\'19s Cac 40 has hit its lowest since November 2023.\par \par \uc2 European bank stocks have slumped, and are down 2.8% as investors brace for interest rate cuts from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 enltrBad U.S. jobs numbers - affirms economic slowdown, will fuel recession talk and need for bolder rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment rate up to 4.3%, higher than expected\par \par \uc2 Payrolls +114k in July, less than expected\par \par \uc2 June payrolls revised down\par \par \uc2 Wage growth slows more than expected\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jamie McGeever (@ReutersJamie) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ReutersJamie/status/1819351193279214045?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 2, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 US unemployment rises to 4.3% in July, highest since 2021\par \par \uc2 Another crucial data point from the US jobs numbers: unemployment rose to 4.3%, the highest since October 2021 during the coronavirus pandemic turmoil.\par \par \uc2 Economists had expected the unemployment rate to stay at 4.1%, but the data, from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , paint a picture of a worsening economy.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 US economy produced 114,000 jobs in July, much less than expected\par \par \uc2 The US economy added 114,000 jobs in July, well below expectations in a sign that will raise questions for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Economists had expected 175,000 new jobs, down from 206,000 in June, according to a poll of economists by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  chief economist warns against 'persistent' UK inflation\par \par \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  chief economist \uc2 Huw Pill\uc2  has said the battle against inflation is not over as he laid out the case against further interest rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 Pill was one of the four economists on the nine-member monetary policy committee who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/aug/01/uk-house-price-growth-rises-bank-of-england-interest-rate-decision-looms-business-live?page=with:block-66ab6b328f08fd552e82d8e3#block-66ab6b328f08fd552e82d8e3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted against cutting interest rates}}}\uc2  yesterday. The split demonstrates just how tight the decision was for the Bank.\par \par \uc2 He does not want the Bank to push ahead just yet with further loosening, because he thinks that inflation may remain above the 2% target, according to a presentation to news agencies on Friday. He said, via Reuters:\par \par \uc2 I think we can\u8217\'20\'19t be complacent, we can\u8217\'20\'19t declare \u8216\'20\'18job done\u8217\'20\'19 because there are some sort of dynamics in the UK economy, a sort of persistent component, that we need to be cautious about.\par \par \uc2 I think we shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be yet promising that rates are going to move down further in the very short term.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 With 15 minutes to go until the non-farm payrolls data, stock market futures suggest the Nasdaq is due to fall 1.7%.\par \par \uc2 The broader S&P 500 index is due to fall 1.1%, while the Dow Jones industrial average is due for a 0.8% decline.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 There is little doubt from economists and strategists that the US economy is slowing. The question is how far the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will go to cut interest rates and prop up growth.\par \par \uc2 Kit Juckes, a strategist at \uc2 Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale\uc2 , a French investment bank, said:\par \par \uc2 To be over-simplistic, the US manufacturing sector has been losing momentum and employment growth has been slowing for months. The question is whether we are near a tipping point where the pace of slowdown picks up enough to make the Fed wish it had started to cut rates sooner than it has.\par \par \uc2 Ernie Tedeschi\uc2 , director of economics at The Budget Lab at Yale university, said, via Reuters:\par \par \uc2 The labour market is in a good place, but there have been clear signs that momentum has also been slowing.\par \par \uc2 It is slowing in a manner that is consistent with a labour market that is reaching a ceiling, not deteriorating.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 So what should we expect from the US jobs data?\par \par \uc2 The key figure is the number of jobs added to non-farm payrolls. (It strips out farm workers because their employment is highly seasonal.) Economists polled by Reuters expect the US economy added 175,000 jobs in July, down from 206,000, but still a level that would indicate a relatively healthy economy.\par \par \uc2 Wage data is published at the same time. The increase in annual wages in June at 3.9% was the smallest in more than three years, suggesting inflationary pressures might be easing. Economists expect that to drop to 3.7%.\par \par \uc2 Anything much lower than the consensus prediction would be likely to deepen the stock sell-off, with investors concerned about whether the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  has done enough to pull off a \u8220\'20\'1Csoft landing\u8221\'20\'1D and avoid a recession as the economy slows.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/jul/31/federal-reserve-interest-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 held interest rates steady on Wednesday}}}\uc2  but signalled that it may loosen monetary policy in the future.\par \par \uc2 A September interest rate cut is a near certainty, according to Matthew Ryan, head of market strategy at Ebury, a financial services firm. He said:\par \par \uc2 The main question now surrounds the pace of easing beyond the September meeting.\par \par \uc2 Market participants have viewed Powell\u8217\'20\'19s remarks as an indication that the Fed could cut rates at every meeting during the remainder of the year. Our base case remains for just two cuts in September and December, although the heavy emphasis placed by the Fed on the labour market suggests that this is not guaranteed.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s NFP report will take on added importance, with economists eyeing a modest easing in both net job creation and annual wage growth.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 Investors dump shares and buy bonds ahead of US jobs data\par \par \uc2 The global stock market sell-off has deepened across many of Europe\u8217\'20\'19s most prominent indices, with investors nervously awaiting US jobs numbers that will be closely watched for further signs of a weakening American economy.\par \par \uc2 Germany\u8217\'20\'19s Dax benchmark is down 1.5%, Italy\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE MIB is down 1.7%, and France\u8217\'20\'19s Cac 40 has fallen 0.6%. London\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE 100 is down 0.4%.\par \par \uc2 Investors sought the safety of government bonds. The price of US 10-year Treasuries rose as demand rose, pushing the yield on the benchmark bond below 4% for the first time since February. Yields (which move inversely to prices) also fell on German and UK 10-year debt.\par \par \uc2 Pierre Veyret, technical analyst at ActivTrades, a trading platform, said:\par \par \uc2 Many investors are losing confidence after the Fed held interest rates unchanged during the latest FOMC meeting earlier this week. The latest batch of disappointing macro data, especially on the employment front, is spreading the sentiment that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  may be behind the curve with its monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Volatility is likely to remain high today as investors wait for the US NFP data, hoping it will provide more clarity on the employment sector of the top economy in the world. If the US NFP and unemployment data confirm a tighter situation, this would likely support the idea that the Fed may be late with the start of its monetary easing cycle, which could fuel the current equity sell-off further.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.04pm BST\par \par \uc2 Gold prices rally as investors look for safety\par \par \uc2 Gold prices have rallied today \u8211\'20\'13 a move often associated with a shift out of riskier growth assets towards safety.\par \par \uc2 The yellow metal should not be a great investment: it has limited uses beyond jewellery and some electronics, and it does not offer any future returns beyond the hope that you can sell it for more later. Yet it continues to play an important part in the balance sheets of central banks, investors and households around the world.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures prices tracked by \uc2 MarketWatch\uc2  reached a new record of $2,500 per troy ounce on Friday, as investors expressed concerns about the health of the US economy and sold off company shares.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold prices tracked by Refinitiv also showed gold prices up 0.8% at $2,464 per troy ounce, although that remained short of the $2,483 record mark hit a fortnight ago.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.23am BST\par \par \uc2 US Nasdaq set to enter 'correction' territory, 10% down from peak\par \par \uc2 Wall Street\u8217\'20\'19s Nasdaq stock index, which traces many of the US\u8217\'20\'19s tech giants, is set to fall heavily when it opens at 14:30pm BST \u8211\'20\'13 and it could be enough to signal a \u8220\'20\'1Ccorrection\u8221\'20\'1D, when shares fall 10% from their peak.\par \par \uc2 Futures trades indicate that the Nasdaq will fall by as much as 1.8% at the opening bell.\par \par \uc2 Stock market corrections often follow periods of exuberance, as economic cycles turn and investors start to consider the potential for slowing output, and therefore lower profits.\par \par \uc2 A bear market is when shares fall by more than 20% \u8211\'20\'13 and that is often associated with recession, such as when the coronavirus pandemic lockdowns stopped activity in many sectors.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2020/mar/12/bear-markets-word-of-the-week"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Bear markets\u8217\'20\'19: why stock markets are giving us paws for thought}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.05am BST\par \par \uc2 One of the biggest fallers among Europe\u8217\'20\'19s large-cap stocks today is computer chip toolmaker \uc2 ASM International\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ASM\uc2  fell by 11% on Friday as it got caught up in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/aug/02/stock-markets-nikkei-ftse-us-gdp-growth-non-farm-payrolls-nfp-bank-of-england-andrew-bailey-business-live?page=with:block-66ac8fb38f08cbd70fcdf277#block-66ac8fb38f08cbd70fcdf277"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 European tech stock sell-off}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Dutch company (whose initials originally stood for Advanced Semiconductor Materials) makes equipment for depositing thin films of material on to silicon. That is a key step in the manufacture of the millions of tiny transistors that make up a computer chip.\par \par \uc2 ASM\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s share price had risen to a record of nearly \u8364\'20\'AC750 three weeks ago as the hype around AI pushed up anything to do with computer chips. But since then its value has slumped by a quarter, as investors have questioned whether the exuberance went too far. It\u8217\'20\'19s market value was \u8364\'20\'AC31bn (\u163\'00\'A326bn) before Friday\u8217\'20\'19s slump.\par \par \uc2 However, \uc2 ASM\uc2  investors won\u8217\'20\'19t be feeling too put out: the company\u8217\'20\'19s shares are still up 19% this year, and have more than doubled since the start of 2023.\par \par \uc2 N.B., \uc2 ASM International\uc2  is different to the much more valuable ASML, a fellow Dutch listed company. ASML makes the lithography machines that use light to etch the transistor patterns in semiconductors. ASML is down by 8% today.\par \par \uc2 The similarity in the names comes from the fact that ASML was once a subsidiary of \uc2 ASM\uc2 . ASML has become much more valuable since spinning off. It was worth \u8364\'20\'AC332bn before today\u8217\'20\'19s fall.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.47am BST\par \par \uc2 Ryanair\uc2  cancelled 650 flights in July because of air traffic control\par \par \uc2 Ryanair\uc2  has revealed it cancelled some 650 flights in July because of air traffic delays, while rival \uc2 Wizz Air\uc2  said the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/aug/01/crowdstrike-accused-of-defrauding-investors-in-class-action-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 global technology outage that hit Windows computers}}}\uc2  directly disrupted about 1% of its flights, Press Association reports .\par \par \uc2 The two airlines reported the impact of disruption as they unveiled how many passengers they flew last month.\par \par \uc2 Low-cost airline \uc2 Ryanair\uc2  said it flew 20.2m passengers in July, an 8% jump on the 18.7m people on its flights the same time last year.\par \par \uc2 Chief executive \uc2 Michael O\u8217\'20\'19Leary\uc2  recently said the group \u8220\'20\'1Csuffered a serious deterioration in European air traffic control capacity which caused multiple flight delays\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 O\u8217\'20\'19Leary called for reform of air traffic control which he described as \u8220\'20\'1Chopelessly inefficient\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.33am BST\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2  managed to buck the trend of weak tech earnings last night. It is once more the biggest listed company in the world by market value after the sell-off in \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 Like \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , the iPhone maker is seen as a possible beneficiary from the artificial intelligence hype cycle. The company hopes its introduction of AI features will drive customers to buy its latest phones \u8211\'20\'13 after a period in which features have stagnated somewhat.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s tech reporter, Kari Paul, last night reported:\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2  reported better-than-expected earnings in the third quarter of 2024, with buzz about its new AI features offsetting a continuing decline in its key {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/china"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 China}}}\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Earnings exceeded analyst predictions despite a year-over-year decline in iPhone sales, with revenue rising 4.9% to $85.78bn in the three months ending 29 June, beating the average analyst estimate of $84.53bn. The company maintained its cash dividend at 25 cents for each share.\par \par \uc2 The strong report represented a bright spot in the tech space after difficult earnings reports from other tech giants like \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , Snap and Intel. The market saw a sell-off on Thursday amid disappointing results, including from Intel \u8211\'20\'13 which announced plans to cut more than 15,000 jobs as it tries to cut billions of dollars in costs to turn its business around. \uc2 Amazon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s stock also dropped more than 4% on Thursday after the company reported lower-than-expected sales this quarter and forward-looking statements indicating a continued slowdown in the next.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/aug/01/apple-earnings-china-market-iphone-sales"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Apple beats earnings forecast despite decline in iPhone sales}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.11am BST\par \par \uc2 Checking in a mid-morning, and every major stock market index in Europe is down on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Germany\u8217\'20\'19s Dax is down 1.1%, while Italy\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE MIB is down 1.2%.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 is faring somewhat better, down by only 0.3%. That is likely in part down to the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision on Thursday to cut interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The mid-cap FTSE 250 index yesterday hit its highest level since February 2022 before getting caught up in the global stock rout. FTSE 250 shares are down by 1.2% today.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.49am BST\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAugust is so far off to a bad start\u8221\'20\'1D, said Russ Mould, investment director at \uc2 AJ Bell\uc2 , an investment platform.\par \par \uc2 The prospect of interest rate cuts might usually be a good thing for share prices, as the cost of borrowing falls. But before that, investors need to price in that lower interest rates reflect weaker economic activity, in the endless cycle of monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Mould said:\par \par \uc2 An economy going through a bad patch is one catalyst for a central bank to cut rates and hopefully stimulate activity. This thought process is likely to be at the top of the agenda for the Fed this week after shocking US economic data that featured bigger than expected jobless claims and contraction in manufacturing. The narrative has changed from rate cuts equating to good news to rate cuts meaning measures to avoid recession.\par \par \uc2 Investors have been on the edge of their seats in recent weeks, taking profits in some of the previously strongest areas of the market like tech and redeploying the proceeds into value stocks that offer slower growth but at a much cheaper price.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.31am BST\par \par \uc2 Rolls-Royce to give each worker \u163\'00\'A3700 in shares, FT reports\par \par \uc2 Rolls-Royce shares are down 0.7% today, but it is one big spender on technology research and development that is not having a tough 2024: on the contrary, its share price hit a record high on Thursday after it published strong financial results.\par \par \uc2 Today the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  reports that the company is giving each employee shares worth \u163\'00\'A3700, the first time that it has gifted stock to workers.\par \par \uc2 Chief executive \uc2 Tufan Erginbilgic\uc2  came in at a good time, when the restart of international travel after the coronavirus pandemic meant that demand for its jet engines was recovering. But he has also improved profitability by renegotiating some contracts with customers.\par \par \uc2 And he also has his eye on new opportunities, such as re-entering the larger market for engines for single-aisle planes \u8211\'20\'13 Rolls-Royce only makes engines for big, twin-aisle planes at the moment \u8211\'20\'13 and also small modular reactors, which advocates hope can be a cheaper source of nuclear energy.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/01/rolls-royce-shares-profit-dividend"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rolls-Royce boss says UK must move fast to be world leader in small reactors}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.08am BST\par \par \uc2 European tech firms are also struggling today \u8211\'20\'13 as the US tech sell-off spreads across the world.\par \par \uc2 The Stoxx Europe 600 tech index has fallen 3.3% today, to its lowest point since January.\par \par \uc2 Tech stocks were buoyed by hype around artificial intelligence that briefly made US chip designer \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/jun/18/nvidia-valuation-most-valuable"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most valuable listed company in the world}}}\uc2 . Yet investors are increasingly questioning whether the AI boom will translate into profits in the next few years.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.49am BST\par \par \uc2 Has the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  acted too late to stop a painful slowdown in the US economy?\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  yesterday cut interest rates for the first time since the coronavirus pandemic, as its rate-setters narrowly judged that inflation looks set to fall back to below target.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/01/bank-of-england-cuts-interest-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bank of England cuts interest rates to 5% in first reduction since March 2020}}}\par \par \uc2 But it is the Fed that will have the biggest effect on global markets, given its control of the supply of money to the world\u8217\'20\'19s biggest economy. It will be a month-and-a-half until the next Fed meeting.\par \par \uc2 Financial markets are now pricing in a 100% chance that the Fed, led by chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , will cut rates in September. They have moved on to asking: by how much?\par \par \uc2 Here is \uc2 CME\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cmegroup.com/markets/interest-rates/cme-fedwatch-tool.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 FedWatch}}}\uc2  tool, which shows that markets are putting a nearly 30% chance on a rate cut of 0.5 percentage points \u8211\'20\'13 although the stronger chances are seen for a standard 0.25 percentage point cut.\par \par \uc2 Matt Britzman, senior equity analyst at \uc2 Hargreaves Lansdown\uc2 , an investment platform:\par \par \uc2 There are now concerns that the soft-landing scenario priced in for most of the year could be a pipe dream, and the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  might have missed its chance to prevent an economic slowdown by not acting on rates earlier in the week. US jobs are out today, and further weakness here will simply exacerbate the current sell-off. Bad news is back to being simply bad news.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.27am BST\par \par \uc2 The biggest riser on the FTSE 100 this morning is \uc2 British Airways\uc2  owner \uc2 International Airlines Group\uc2  (\uc2 IAG\uc2 ). It is up by 3%.\par \par \uc2 IAG\uc2  scrapped a proposed takeover of Spain\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Air Europa\uc2 , saying it would struggle to mollify regulators who had competition concerns. Investors were clearly not enthused.\par \par \uc2 IAG\uc2  also posted stronger-than-expected profits for the second quarter, surprising analysts after rivals \u8211\'20\'13 particularly lower-cost carrier \uc2 Ryanair\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/jul/22/ryanair-profits-lower-summer-fares-tickets"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 warned of falling demand}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And speaking of \uc2 British Airways\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/jul/29/the-british-airways-safety-video-why-have-half-a-million-people-watched-it-online"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The British Airways safety video: why have half a million people watched it online?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.07am BST\par \par \uc2 It is what is known as a \u8220\'20\'1Crisk-off\u8221\'20\'1D day on global stock markets: when traders sell riskier growth-focused shares and batten down the hatches for financial market squalls.\par \par \uc2 London\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE 100 index is down 0.3% in the opening trades, but it is among the better performers in Europe.\par \par \uc2 Here are the opening snaps via Reuters:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 EUROPE\u8217\'20\'19S STOXX 600 DOWN 0.9%\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 FRANCE\u8217\'20\'19S CAC 40 DOWN 0.6%; SPAIN\u8217\'20\'19S IBEX DOWN 1%\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 EURO STOXX INDEX DOWN 0.7%; EURO ZONE BLUE CHIPS DOWN 0.7%\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 GERMANY\u8217\'20\'19S DAX DOWN 1.1%\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.03am BST\par \par \uc2 Global stock market rout as investors consider US recession chances\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our live, rolling coverage of business, economics and financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Stock indices around the world have slumped after weak manufacturing data and company earnings raised concerns that the US economy may be on its way to recession.\par \par \uc2 Japan\u8217\'20\'19s Nikkei index fell 5.8% and the broader Japanese Topix fell 6.1%, Australia\u8217\'20\'19s ASX fell 2.5%, and Hong Kong\u8217\'20\'19s Hang Seng was down 2.2%. That Nikkei performance was the worst since March 2020 \u8211\'20\'13 the coronavirus pandemic slump.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe Nikkei 225 is getting absolutely hosed today -5%, the worst day since March 2020 and for you stats heads a 4.3 sigma move - Volumes are crazy too, 84% above the 30 day average for this time\par \par \uc2 Jump ship is the cry\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Chris Weston (@ChrisWeston_PS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ChrisWeston_PS/status/1819177327248789585?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 2, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 It followed steep equity declines in the US yesterday, with the small-cap Russell 2000 index down 3%, and chip designer \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s slump from record heights continued.\par \par \uc2 Analysts led by Jim Reid at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 , highlighted weak earnings from \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , and said that investors appear to be betting that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  steps in to prop up economic growth. They wrote:\par \par \uc2 The past 24 hours have seen an increasingly precarious backdrop for risk markets, with a risk-off mood on the back of another batch of weak US data yesterday followed by mostly downbeat tech earnings overnight.\par \par \uc2 Futures are now pricing in over 175bps of Fed rate cuts over the next 12 months, which is the sort of pace that we\u8217\'20\'19ve only seen in a recession in recent cycles.\par \par \uc2 Intel, the US chip manufacturer, was one of the biggest additions to the gloom \u8211\'20\'13 and adding to the recent sell-off among semiconductor businesses. It is a huge name, and has received vast subsidies to build new chip factories in America, but it is struggling.\par \par \uc2 Its shares are down 19% in pre-market trading after it reported unexpectedly weak earnings and a plan to cut 15% of its workforce \u8211\'20\'13 a number that translates to more than 17,500 jobs globally.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/aug/01/intel-lay-offs-shares-decline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Intel announces plan to cut 15,000 jobs to \u8216\'20\'18resize and refocus\u8217\'20\'19 business}}}\par \par \uc2 It is a similarly drab picture across the US manufacturing sector, according to the \uc2 Institute for Supply Management\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s closely followed purchasing managers\u8217\'20\'19 index (\uc2 PMI\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 Kyle Rodda, senior financial market analyst at Capital.com, an online trading platform, said:\par \par \uc2 Stocks plunged following an ISM Manufacturing PMI survey which revealed a bigger-than-expected slowdown in factory activity in the States, raising the spectre of a steeper drop in economic growth. Historically, it\u8217\'20\'19s when the ISM Manufacturing number falls below 43 that the US economy falls into recession; so, while the index is a long way from that threshold, the markets will be keeping a close eye on how it trends as the US economy moderates.\par \par \uc2 Investors today will be on tenterhooks for US jobs numbers. A weaker-than-expected non-farm payrolls number could really put the pressure on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to accelerate its expected interest rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1:30pm BST : US non-farm payrolls (July; prev.: 206,000 jobs; cons.:176,000)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1:30pm BST : US unemployment (July; prev.: 4.1%; cons.:4.1%)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1:30pm BST : US average earnings (July; prev.: 3.9% year-on-year; cons.:3.7%)\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.16am BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240802ek820015x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
