\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Your body is completely drained\u8217\'20\'19: US workers toil in heatwaves with no protections\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,065 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 July 2024\par 19:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Though 2,300 people in the US died from heat-related illness in 2023, workers await robust protection laws\par \par \uc2 On 23 June, Shae Parker had to leave her shift early at a gas station in Columbia, South Carolina, to go to the emergency room due to heat exhaustion; she wasn\u8217\'20\'19t paid for missing the rest of her shift. The air conditioning at her work has been on the fritz for weeks, she said, and her station heats up easily as the sun beams through its large windows.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI got nauseated, overheated, lightheaded,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t have free water, we don\u8217\'20\'19t have a water level on the soda machine, the ice machine is broken, so we have to buy water. The last few weeks it\u8217\'20\'19s been extremely hot. It\u8217\'20\'19s very hard to breathe when you\u8217\'20\'19re lightheaded and experiencing dizziness. The fatigue is like 10 times worse because your body is completely drained. I had to get two bags of fluid from being dehydrated even though I was drinking water.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Millions of Americans faced dangerous temperatures earlier this month as a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/article/2024/jul/15/heat-dome-extreme-heat"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 heat dome}}}\uc2  blanketed the midwest and eastern US. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.weather.gov/cae/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National Weather Service}}}\uc2  issued a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wltx.com/article/weather/heat-advisory-south-carolina-july-9-forecast/101-802ca314-9d78-4366-bfc6-aa2517464784"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 heat advisory}}}\uc2  for much of South Carolina as temperatures hit the 90sF (32C).\par \par \uc2 Yet, workers across the country who toil in the heat both indoors and outdoors have to get through the summer without any heat protections in the workplace. Like Parker, many workers are left to try to treat their heat stress symptoms on their own.\par \par \uc2 This past June was the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2024/07/08/earth-warmest-june-warmest-year-climate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hottest month}}}\uc2  of June on record worldwide, while July 2023 to June 2024 have been the hottest 12 months on record, with 2024 on pace to break 2023 as the hottest year on record.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/biden-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Biden administration}}}\uc2  announced the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.osha.gov/news/newsreleases/national/07022024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 proposal}}}\uc2  of an \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration (Osha)\uc2  rule to protect 36 million US workers from the heat on 2 July. But implementation won\u8217\'20\'19t likely occur for several more years as the release of the rule proposal is just the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.osha.gov/sites/default/files/OSHA_FlowChart.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 third of seven steps}}}\uc2  in Osha\u8217\'20\'19s rule-making process. It could face challenges in courts, causing further delays, or be derailed altogether if {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  wins the 2024 election. The rule provides more robust rules and higher fines on employers to protect workers.\par \par \uc2 Destiny Mervin, a restaurant worker in Atlanta, Georgia, and member of the Union of Southern Service Workers, said she has been constantly sweating during work and has had to change shirts during her shift because of how hot she has been.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSomeone fainted two weeks ago and the week before that, someone had a seizure,\u8221\'20\'1D Mervin said in a press release. \u8220\'20\'1CA worker shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have to die for Popeyes for employers to take unbearable heat seriously.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The need for strong federal heat protections is becoming more urgent every summer\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Dixon\par \par \uc2 In 2023, an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/nation/2023-set-a-record-for-u-s-heat-deaths-why-2024-could-be-even-deadlier"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated 2,300 people}}}\uc2  in the US died from heat-related illness, the highest record of heat-related deaths in 45 years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe excessive heat the US has experienced in the last month is particularly dangerous to the people who have to work in it \u8211\'20\'13 hundreds of thousands of workers succumb to heat-related illness, injury and death each year,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Rebecca Dixon\uc2 , president and CEO of the National Employment Law Project.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe risk of workplace heat dangers is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://usw2.nyl.as/t1/81/5jti975juvru4vkp3zuaa8vwz/1/8f7441beff08658d48b0674145699b48879ce1596d89a3a4857c9c4920e20762"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 especially acute for workers of color}}}\uc2 , who are more likely to work in jobs that expose them to excessive heat as a result of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://usw2.nyl.as/t1/81/5jti975juvru4vkp3zuaa8vwz/2/6116dc1978b540998c82e344a3a76a61985980043026111bea8b3569eae563e2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 occupational segregation}}}\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Dixon said.\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CAs human-caused climate change produces more extreme temperatures, the need for strong federal heat protections is becoming more urgent every summer.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Priscilla Hoyle, an airplane cabin cleaner at Charlotte Douglas international airport, has gotten sick and had to go home twice in the past year due to heat exhaustion, with the most recent incident just a few weeks ago. Both times she wasn\u8217\'20\'19t provided any medical treatment when she got sick on the job.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI got really sick, I could hardly breathe, I had to run off the plane and I was standing on the side throwing up,\u8221\'20\'1D Hoyle said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s very draining, it\u8217\'20\'19s very tiring. You have to walk from concourse to concourse in nothing but heat. You\u8217\'20\'19re dripping in sweat and you can\u8217\'20\'19t hydrate.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Damarkus Hudson has also worked as an airplane cabin cleaner at Charlotte Douglas international airport in North Carolina for two years where he is constantly exposed to the heat without adequate protections or support, he claimed.\par \par \uc2 Last year, Hudson passed out on the job due to heat exhaustion and was offered no medical treatment. He was instead only given time to drink water and cool down until his shift ended shortly after the incident.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe break room was full and I tried to go outside to get some fresh air, but there wasn\u8217\'20\'19t any breeze and I just passed out, I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t cool down,\u8221\'20\'1D Hudson said.\par \par \uc2 He noted a coworker poured water on him to cool him down, which sent him into shock, and that other workers have experienced similar symptoms on the job.\par \par \uc2 He cleans four to five planes an hour in the sweltering heat, often walking long distances between concourses in the airport, and he said the air conditioning in the vehicles they travel in between planes doesn\u8217\'20\'19t always work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re always exposed to the heat. Working in the heat, you get nauseated, feel sick, and fatigued,\u8221\'20\'1D he added. \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t get enough water and when we do, it\u8217\'20\'19s usually not cold water, and we don\u8217\'20\'19t get extra breaks to cool down.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 LaShonda Barber, a trash truck driver at Charlotte airport, said heat issues impact airport workers across the US. She claimed the trucks they use don\u8217\'20\'19t have working air conditioning, that she and her workers are rarely provided water or rest breaks, which makes the heat impact even worse as the job is already physically demanding when its not hot outside.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are a lot of people who have been hospitalized, passing out from this heat,\u8221\'20\'1D said Barber. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re sweating so much. We\u8217\'20\'19re not getting time to get water into our bodies. We\u8217\'20\'19re human \u8211\'20\'13 the same way you get hot, we get hot.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240729ek7t0028u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
