\par\par \uc2 Film\par \b \uc2 More than half UK\u8217\'20\'19s film and TV workers still unemployed after Hollywood strikes\b0\par\par\uc2 Nadia Khomami Arts and culture correspondent \par \uc2 541 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 July 2024\par 14:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Bectu\uc2  survey shows just 6% of workers have seen a full recovery in employment a year on from joint industrial action by \uc2 Sag-Aftra\uc2  and the Writers Guild of America\par \par \uc2 More than half of the UK\u8217\'20\'19s film and TV workforce are still out of work a year after the Hollywood strikes of 2023, new research has found.\par \par \uc2 According to a survey of more than 2,300 film and TV workers by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bectu.org.uk/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bectu}}}\uc2  trade union, 52% of workers in the UK film sector are out of work, 51% in TV drama, 57% in unscripted TV and 53% in commercials.\par \par \uc2 Just 6% said they have seen a full recovery in their employment since the joint industrial action by \uc2 Sag-Aftra\uc2  and the Writers Guild of America.\par \par \uc2 The figures are down from the 68% who were out of work in a February 2024 survey, and 74% who were out of work in September 2023, which \uc2 Bectu\uc2  said indicates a slow recovery.\par \par \uc2 However the number of respondents who are planning to leave the film and TV industry in the next five years is increasing, from 24% in September 2023 to 37% in February 2024 and 38% now.\par \par \uc2 Industry diversity is particularly being affected, with 44% of Black/Black British respondents planning to leave the industry in the next five years, 41% of Asian/Asian British and 40% of mixed and multiple ethnic groups, compared to 37% for white respondents.\par \par \uc2 Of those with parenting and caring responsibilities, 41% see themselves leaving the industry in the next five years, compared to 36% of those without; while the figures are at 42% for respondents with a disability and 37% for those without.\par \par \uc2 Older workers are also much less likely to currently be in work, with 31% of 18-24s currently unemployed, compared to 46% of 24-34s, 50% of 35-44s, 57% of 45-54s, 58% of 55-64s and 68% of over-65s.\par \par \uc2 Respondents to the survey noted significant financial and mental health impacts, with 70% saying they have struggled with their mental wellbeing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe industry is changing form,\u8221\'20\'1D said one sculptor. \u8220\'20\'1CIt doesn\u8217\'20\'19t feel temporary. It feels like a long-term change. And it\u8217\'20\'19s been one of the hardest years I\u8217\'20\'19ve ever experienced, both financially and emotionally. We are not supported in any way.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another respondent, a series producer, said: \u8220\'20\'1CI have never struggled to find work like this in 25 years. I\u8217\'20\'19ve had nothing at all in seven months. I feel used and discarded.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Philippa Childs, head of \uc2 Bectu\uc2 , said little had materially improved for the workforce and \u8220\'20\'1Cdiscussions must be laser-focused on how we can collectively make things better for workers, who are critical to the sector\u8217\'20\'19s success but continue to bear the brunt of industry changes\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 She added: \u8220\'20\'1CWith a new government in place, we are doing all we can to ensure workers\u8217\'20\'19 needs are top of the political agenda.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf the industry doesn\u8217\'20\'19t take tangible and collective action soon, we will see a huge skills exodus and a further deterioration in industry diversity, alongside prolonged mental-health challenges and debilitating financial difficulties.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240730ek7u0020f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
