\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Fines for child labor violations would increase under new Democratic bill\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 555 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 July 2024\par 09:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Let\u8217\'20\'19s Protect Workers act would also increase fines for wage and hour violations, from $1,100 each to $25,000 each\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats}}}\uc2  introduced a bill Friday proposing increased penalties for employers found guilty of child labor violations and toughening rules around minimum wage, overtime violations and breaches of health and safety rules.\par \par \uc2 The Let\u8217\'20\'19s Protect Workers act would also set new penalties for retaliating against workers who exercise their right to take family and medical leave, toughen oversight of workplace injury records, improve mine safety and ensure funding for workers affected by black lung.\par \par \uc2 The bill comes as child labor violations have surged in the US. The Department of Labor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/fight-oppressive-child-labor/#:~:text=with%20the%20FLSA.-,The%20recent%20upsurge%20in%20oppressive%20child%20labor,and%202023%20(USDOL%202023d)."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  an increase of 88% in such violations between 2019 and 2023 as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Republican}}}\uc2  states have moved to relax child labor rules. Eight states have passed legislation to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/research/child-labor/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 roll back child labor protections}}}\uc2  so far this year.\par \par \uc2 For child labor violations, the \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2  can currently fine employers up to $11,000 per employee who is the subject of a child labor violation and up to $50,000 for each violation that causes injury or death of a minor. The fines can be doubled if the violation is determined to be willful or repeated. The new bill would increase fines up to $150,000 per employee subject to a child labor violation and up to $700,000 for a violation that causes the death or injury of a minor, which still could be doubled for willful or repetitive violations.\par \par \uc2 Wage and hour violations would increase from up to $1,100 per violation to $25,000 per violation, which may be doubled for willful or repetitive violations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEvery American should be fairly compensated and be able to return home safely at the end of the day,\u8221\'20\'1D said Robert \u8220\'20\'1CBobby\u8221\'20\'1D Scott, Virginia representative and ranking Democratic member of the Committee on Education and the Workforce.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUnfortunately, shortcomings in our labor laws enable unethical employers to exploit workers, endanger children and suppress the right to organize \u8211\'20\'13 with little accountability,\u8221\'20\'1D Scott said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s why I\u8217\'20\'19m proud to introduce the Let\u8217\'20\'19s Protect Workers act, which will hold bad actors accountable and strengthen penalties for labor law violations. This bill will help level the playing field and, once again, restore the balance of power between workers and their employers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The bill would also introduce civil monetary penalties for unfair labor practices committed by employers, up to $50,000 per violation. Currently, employers do not face any civil monetary penalties aside from back pay and reinstatement of workers for unfair labor practice charges.\par \par \uc2 The legislation comes in the wake of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://democrats-edworkforce.house.gov/imo/media/doc/a_slap_on_the_wrist_how_it_pays_for_unscrupulous_employers_to_take_advantage_of_workers1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  published by Scott in April 2024 that outlined the ineffectiveness of low or non-existent civil monetary penalties for labor violations committed by employers. The report outlines how current fines and penalties are merely \u8220\'20\'1Ca slap on the wrist\u8221\'20\'1D, with employers facing little to no deterrents to breaking labor laws.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUnfortunately, unscrupulous employers are emboldened to violate these foundational worker rights and protections because of the weak civil monetary penalties assessed in response,\u8221\'20\'1D the report noted. \u8220\'20\'1CUnder some labor and employment laws, workers are worse off as employers face no monetary penalty and can break the law cost-free.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240726ek7q000xi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
