\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Disneyland workers in Anaheim, California, vote to authorize strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani \par \uc2 441 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 July 2024\par 19:15 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Largest US strike so far this year could take place if negotiations, including over wages, fail next week\par \par \uc2 Members of four unions representing 14,000 workers at the Disneyland in Anaheim, California, have voted to authorize a strike amid contract negotiations.\par \par \uc2 The vote does not mean the workers will go on strike yet, but that the unions can call a strike at any point. If a strike takes place, it will be the largest strike so far in 2024.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have given the company more than enough time to do the right thing,\u8221\'20\'1D the unions, under the collective Disney Workers Rising bargaining committee, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/disneyrising24/status/1814511201709400313"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said on X}}}\uc2  after the vote.\par \par \uc2 Disney Workers Rising said that if \uc2 Disney\uc2  does not seem prepared to agree with the union after two more days of bargaining, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe will move forward with the actions we need to take\u8221\'20\'1D. The next meetings are scheduled for 22 July and 23 July.\par \par \uc2 The union covers workers at the Disneyland Resort, including Disney\u8217\'20\'19s California Adventure, Downtown \uc2 Disney\uc2  and the \uc2 Disney\uc2  hotels in the area.\par \par \uc2 In a survey conducted earlier this year, the union said that 28% of its members have food insecurity, 64% are rent-burdened and 42% missed work for medical treatment because they didn\u8217\'20\'19t have enough sick leave.\par \par \uc2 Workers are also advocating for higher wages. The current minimum wage under Anaheim law is $19.90.\par \par \uc2 The current contract expired on 16 June. Contract negotiations have been taking place since late April.\par \par \uc2 Workers have accused the company of \u8220\'20\'1Cunlawful discipline and intimidation and surveillance\u8221\'20\'1D of union members, especially after employees were told they could not wear union buttons at work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have people who work at Disneyland who have to choose between paying their rent or buying groceries. There are people who go without medical care because they can\u8217\'20\'19t afford it. There are people who rely on the food banks, and the churches, or food supplements,\u8221\'20\'1D Coleen Palmer, a cashier at Disneyland for more than 30 years and a member of the bargaining committee, told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/jul/09/disney-worker-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Guardian}}}\uc2  earlier this month. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a place where people want to work but they want to be able to make a living doing what they\u8217\'20\'19re doing and that\u8217\'20\'19s what we\u8217\'20\'19re fighting for.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a statement, a Disneyland Resort spokesperson said that the company \u8220\'20\'1Cgreatly appreciate the important roles our cast members play in creating memorable experiences for our guests, and we remain committed to reaching an agreement that focuses on what matters most to them while positioning Disneyland Resort for growth and job creation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240720ek7k0025v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
