\par\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 UK politics: Zelenskiy asks Starmer to \u8216\'20\'18show leadership\u8217\'20\'19 and let Ukraine use weapons anywhere in Russia \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Amy Sedghi \par \uc2 5,774 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 July 2024\par 13:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ukrainian president says ability to use western weapons to strike into enemy territory is key to his country\u8217\'20\'19s defence\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 We will shortly be closing the politics live blog for today. Thank you, as always, for reading the updates and for all the messages and comments.\par \par \uc2 You can keep up to date on all the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s UK politics coverage {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 . Here\u8217\'20\'19s a summary of the key events and stories from today:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  welcomed the Ukrainian president, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2 , at Downing Street on Friday, calling the visit a \u8220\'20\'1Creal piece of history\u8221\'20\'1D. The Ukrainian president was greeted by the UK cabinet with a standing ovation as he became the first foreign leader to address the cabinet in person since 1997.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Starmer said the UK will \u8220\'20\'1Cdouble down\u8221\'20\'1D on backing for Ukraine, and confirmed he had accepted this morning an invitation to visit Ukraine again in the future.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian president, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2 , asked \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  to \u8220\'20\'1Cshow your leadership\u8221\'20\'1D and help with Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Clong-range capability\u8221\'20\'1D. In his address to the UK cabinet Zelenskiy said \u8220\'20\'1Cif the restriction on western weapons is lifted\u8221\'20\'1D it would help Kyiv to strengthen its defence and secure its frontline positions. He said \u8220\'20\'1Cit is possible to destroy\u8221\'20\'1D areas in Russia where weapons are being concentrated. On long-range capability, the president said \u8220\'20\'1Cwe are still missing the main answer to this question\u8221\'20\'1D and told the prime minister \u8220\'20\'1CI ask you to show your leadership\u8221\'20\'1D on the issue.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The government will overturn the suspension of UK funding to the United Nations Relief and Works Agency for Palestine (\uc2 Unrwa\uc2 ), foreign secretary \uc2 David Lammy\uc2  has said, adding that Britain will provide \u163\'00\'A321m in new funds. He told the Commons on Friday: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are reassured that after \uc2 Catherine Colonna\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s independent review, \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  is ensuring they meet the highest standards of neutrality and strengthening its procedures including unfettered.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The worldwide IT crisis was discussed by \uc2 UK government\uc2  officials who attended a meeting of the Cobra emergency committee. The prime minister did not attend the Cobra meeting as he was hosting the Ukrainian president at a meeting the cabinet at the time.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Britain will not help Ukraine hit targets in Russia, the defence secretary said. \uc2 John Healey\uc2  was speaking hours before Zelenskiy was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/jul/18/zelenskiy-to-attend-uk-cabinet-meeting-in-effort-to-disrupt-russian-oil-sales"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 due to address}}}\uc2  a cabinet meeting at Downing Street on Friday. Healey did not rule out accepting the Ukrainian president\u8217\'20\'19s request to use British-made missiles against Russian territory, but added that Britain would not be involved in any such attacks.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The UK will provide military aid funding to Ukraine \u8220\'20\'1Cfor every year for the rest of the decade,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 John Healey\uc2  said. The defence secretary told Times Radio: \u8220\'20\'1CIf we take the starting point that the defence of the UK starts in Ukraine and if Putin wins in Ukraine he will not stop there, then we must stand with Ukraine, we\u8217\'20\'19re determined to do that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The government has begun setting up its promised {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://educationhub.blog.gov.uk/2024/07/19/what-is-the-national-curriculum-and-why-is-it-being-reviewed/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 curriculum and assessment review}}}\uc2 , looking at what is taught and tested in England\u8217\'20\'19s state schools, with the announcement that a senior academic, Prof Becky Francis, will lead the review. The review was a Labour manifesto pledge at the general election, and is expected to report back in 2025.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Speaking on Friday, armed forces minister Luke Pollard welcomed the \u8220\'20\'1Ceffort by Border Force to make sure that we are tackling that cross-Channel smuggling operation that\u8217\'20\'19s going on\u8221\'20\'1D. Asked whether there was a new policy for Border Force to return small boats to France, he told LBC Radio: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ll be working out the full policy and how it works. I\u8217\'20\'19m afraid it\u8217\'20\'19s not as simple as taking every single boat back to the French shore. But what we have seen is French ships intercepting some of those dinghies travelling across the Channel and returning to France. We\u8217\'20\'19d like to see more effort put in place like this, so we\u8217\'20\'19re stopping that illegal trade.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Long-awaited changes designed to protect individuals from having to sell their homes to meet large social care bills must be further delayed because of funding and staffing shortages, the leaders of England\u8217\'20\'19s largest councils have said. Plans to introduce a cap on social care costs \u8211\'20\'13 which would limit people\u8217\'20\'19s lifetime care cost contributions to a maximum of \u163\'00\'A386,000 \u8211\'20\'13 in October 2025 will be impossible to deliver, the County Councils Network (CCN) said.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s promised \u8220\'20\'1Creset\u8221\'20\'1D of the UK\u8217\'20\'19s ties with the rest of Europe has drawn a positive response in European media, with one longtime journalist rejoicing that she will never again have to cover {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/article/2024/jul/19/europe-media-welcomes-starmer-reset-in-uk-eu-ties"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBritain as a basket case\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Working parents could be hit with unexpected tax bills because of a clampdown on the misuse of a childcare \u8220\'20\'1Cbenefit\u8221\'20\'1D offered by some employers that lets people enjoy savings of up to \u163\'00\'A310,000 a year. \uc2 HM Revenue and Customs\uc2  has turned its sights on some workplace nursery benefit schemes which allow employees to pay the fees out of their pre-tax salary, resulting in them making big savings on income tax and national insurance.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.08pm BST\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s promised \u8220\'20\'1Creset\u8221\'20\'1D of the UK\u8217\'20\'19s ties with the rest of Europe has drawn a positive response in European media, with one longtime journalist rejoicing that she will never again have to cover \u8220\'20\'1CBritain as a basket case\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister told leaders {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/jul/18/starmer-promises-eu-leaders-at-epc-he-will-repair-ties-with-their-countries"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at a meeting of the European Political Community at Blenheim Palace}}}\uc2  in Oxfordshire on Thursday that he wanted to draw a line under years of fractious relations with the rest of Europe. The relaunch was greeted with a sense of relief that after years of chaotic leadership in London a new age of cooperation was beginning.\par \par \uc2 Annette Dittert, the longtime London correspondent for the German public broadcaster ARD, was effusive about the vibe shift.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CStarmer managed to restore Britain\u8217\'20\'19s reputation in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/europe-news"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Europe}}}\uc2  within a day. Astonishing achievement in such (a) short time,\u8221\'20\'1D Dittert tweeted. \u8220\'20\'1C\u8216\'20\'18[W]ithin a day\u8217\'20\'19 is maybe a bit over the top, but still: what a complete change of tone and such a relief not having to report on Britain as a basket case any more.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Der Spiegel noted that \u8220\'20\'1Cafter the long ice age between Berlin and London\u8221\'20\'1D, the German chancellor, \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2 , and Starmer get along well. It said the new prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s performance in Blenheim could mark the beginning of a new \u8220\'20\'1Ctandem\u8221\'20\'1D for Europe.\par \par \uc2 The news magazine said that given the potential return of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the White House and the weakening of president \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  in France, Europe needed new fulcrums of power.\par \par \uc2 Read more of the piece by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/lisaocarroll"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lisa O\u8217\'20\'19Carroll}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/deborah-cole"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Deborah Cole}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/samjones"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sam Jones}}}\uc2  here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/article/2024/jul/19/europe-media-welcomes-starmer-reset-in-uk-eu-ties"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18No more basket case Britain\u8217\'20\'19: Europe welcomes Starmer reset in UK-EU ties}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s defence and security editor, Dan Sabbagh, has written a profile on Labour\u8217\'20\'19s new defence adviser, Fiona Hill. You can read more about the former White House Russia adviser from County Durham here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/article/2024/jul/19/labour-defence-adviser-fiona-hill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Labour\u8217\'20\'19s new defence adviser Fiona Hill: from the White House to Whitehall}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 The government has begun setting up its promised {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://educationhub.blog.gov.uk/2024/07/19/what-is-the-national-curriculum-and-why-is-it-being-reviewed/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 curriculum and assessment review}}}\uc2 , looking at what is taught and tested in England\u8217\'20\'19s state schools, with the announcement that a senior academic, Prof Becky Francis, will lead the review.\par \par \uc2 Francis is a former director of the UCL Institute of Education and is now chief executive of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Education Endowment Foundation}}}\uc2 , a government-endowed charity that processes research for teachers. The new role sees her leading an expert panel to examine \u8220\'20\'1Cthe key challenges to attainment for young people, and the barriers which hold children back from the opportunities and life chances they deserve,\u8221\'20\'1D especially those from disadvantaged backgrounds, according to the Department for Education.\par \par \uc2 The review was a Labour manifesto pledge at the general election, and is expected to report back in 2025.\par \par \uc2 The DfE said the review \u8220\'20\'1Cwill seek evolution not revolution,\u8221\'20\'1D and will be wary of increasing teacher workloads.\par \par \uc2 Bridget Phillipson\uc2 , the education secretary, said:\par \par \uc2 Our dedicated school and college staff deliver better life chances for countless children but for too long they have been held back by a curriculum and assessment system that fails to prepare enough of our children for work and for life.\par \par \uc2 That is why this government, alongside leading education experts, leaders and staff on the frontline, will breathe new life into our outdated curriculum and assessment system.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Daniel Kebede, general secretary of the National Education Union, said:\par \par \uc2 Assessment in England, across the phrases, is fundamentally broken. It is designed for accountability, rather than teaching and learning.\par \par \uc2 We are pleased to see the review will consider how any changes would affect staff. The previous government\u8217\'20\'19s changes were rushed through at an unmanageable pace and caused significantly more teacher workload.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 The worldwide IT crisis was discussed by \uc2 UK government\uc2  officials who attended a meeting of the Cobra emergency committee, as we {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/jul/19/retail-sales-great-britain-slump-12-government-borrowing-june-figure-lowest-2019-horizon-business-live?page=with:block-669a4d698f08c0d322350e55#block-669a4d698f08c0d322350e55"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported earlier}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The meeting heard there has been no known impact on 999 or emergency services.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister did not attend the Cobra meeting as he was hosting the Ukrainian president at a meeting the cabinet at the time.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  said they were unaware of the problem having any impact on government services.\par \par \uc2 We are certainly aware of the disruption this has caused to business and to those particiualrly looking to travel today. We are keeping in closely monitoring the situation and making sure the relevant industries can use the support we can provide.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.17pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  has posted a photo of himself and the Ukrainian president, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  on X. He described welcoming Zelenskiy to Downing Street as \u8220\'20\'1Can honour\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 enltrAn honour to welcome you to Downing Street, President {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ZelenskyyUa?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @ZelenskyyUa}}}\uc2  ???????? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ktf0f3fSpX"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/ktf0f3fSpX}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  (@Keir_Starmer) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Keir_Starmer/status/1814257055106040188?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 19, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Zelenskiy has also posted on X, describing how the two leaders \u8220\'20\'1Cdiscussed several important issues\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He wrote:\par \par \uc2 Key topics included the frontline situation and the urgent need for long-range weapons to protect our people\u8217\'20\'19s lives. I also informed prime minister about the approval of Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s Maritime Security Strategy. Together, we will continue working to strengthen our Maritime Coalition.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine will always be grateful to the United Kingdom for its steadfast support of our independence and the lives of our people.\par \par \uc2 enltrToday, together with UK Prime Minister {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Keir_Starmer?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @Keir_Starmer}}}\uc2 , we discussed several important issues.\par \par \uc2 Key topics included the frontline situation and the urgent need for long-range weapons to protect our people\u8217\'20\'19s lives. I also informed Prime Minister about the approval of Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/r1IvymXMT9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/r1IvymXMT9}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  / ????????? ?????????? (@ZelenskyyUa) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ZelenskyyUa/status/1814251453751464245?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 19, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 The PA news agency reports that Downing Street said it was a \u8220\'20\'1Ctragedy\u8221\'20\'1D that \u8220\'20\'1Cyet another life\u8221\'20\'1D has been lost in the English Channel.\par \par \uc2 A Number 10 spokesperson said:\par \par \uc2 It is a tragedy that yet another life has been lost and the prime minister spoke to such an incident just yesterday.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The official said the incident \u8220\'20\'1Cunderlines the stark reality of the dangers of these crossings and the callousness of the criminal gangs who are driving this\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The spokesperson said the prime minister and home secretary were \u8220\'20\'1Cworking to address this and step up enforcement\u8221\'20\'1D including through \u8220\'20\'1Cthe work that we\u8217\'20\'19ll be carrying out with European partners on it\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 Downing Street did not confirm whether British Storm Shadow missiles can be used by Ukraine to strike into Russian territory as part of the country\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to defend itself.\par \par \uc2 Asked by the PA news agency whether the prime minister was receptive to Ukrainian president \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s calls for permission to use UK weapons in Russia, a Number 10 spokesperson said:\par \par \uc2 I think you heard from the prime minister when he was asked questions about this in the press conference yesterday, where he made our position very clear.\par \par \uc2 I think what was also clear from the cabinet meeting which you saw and yesterday, was our unwavering support for Ukraine. That will not change and we\u8217\'20\'19re determined to support them for as long as it takes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Pressed on whether Britain has lifted restrictions on using Storm Shadow missiles on targets inside Russia, the official said:\par \par \uc2 There is no change to our position and the prime minister set that out yesterday.\par \par \uc2 We have been providing military aid to support Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s clear right to self-defence against Russia\u8217\'20\'19s illegal acts in accordance with international humanitarian law.\par \par \uc2 Equipment provided by the UK is intended for the defence of Ukraine.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.50am BST\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  welcomed the Ukrainian president on Friday, making \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  the first world leader to visit the newly elected prime minister at Downing Street.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian video team have shared the below, where you can see Starmer greeting Zelenskiy outside No 10 and then also speaking during a bilateral meeting inside:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 This article was amended on 19 July 2024. An earlier version said that \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  was the first world leader to visit the newly elected prime minister. It has been corrected to \u8220\'20\'1Cthe first world leader to visit the newly elected prime minister at \uc2 Downing Street\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.42am BST\par \par \uc2 The Liberal Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 foreign affairs spokesperson \uc2 Layla Moran\uc2  has urged the foreign secretary to \u8220\'20\'1Cpull the lever that would best signify\u8221\'20\'1D the UK\u8217\'20\'19s commitment to the two-state solution.\par \par \uc2 She told the Commons:\par \par \uc2 The situation in Gaza is frankly soul-shattering. It\u8217\'20\'19s been 300 days nearly, the death toll is at 38,000, the vast majority killed being women and children, only three days ago, 22 people lost their lives in a strike on a \uc2 UN\uc2 -run school. That\u8217\'20\'19s the fifth attack on or near a school in the last eight days, and those who survived the bombings are at severe risk of disease and malnutrition.\par \par \uc2 This has to end now. We need that immediate ceasefire, but we need also those hostages out and the aid to come in.\par \par \uc2 But it is also right we start thinking about not just the next day, but how do we stop this from ever happening again?\par \par \uc2 And there is only one viable answer to that, and that is the two-state solution. That is our North Star, it is the keystone to stability in the region as well, but I have to express disappointment that the foreign secretary refuses to pull the lever that would best signify our commitment to it, which is to recognise the Palestinian state on 1967 borders. Ireland, Spain and Norway did it this May. Will he consider it, and if he won\u8217\'20\'19t, why not?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In his reply, \uc2 David Lammy\uc2  said:\par \par \uc2 There is no confusion on this party\u8217\'20\'19s position on Palestinian recognition. We are committed to Palestinian recognition. We hope to work with partners to achieve that when the circumstances are right.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.27am BST\par \par \uc2 Foreign secretary, \uc2 David Lammy\uc2 , has said the \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2  (\uc2 IDF\uc2 ) must vacate Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Former Commons foreign affairs select committee chair \uc2 Alicia Kearns\uc2  told the Commons:\par \par \uc2 Israel has the right to defend itself in accordance with international humanitarian law and we must remove \uc2 Hamas\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s capacity to launch attacks against Israel.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In his reply, the foreign secretary said:\par \par \uc2 I think that Israel\u8217\'20\'19s security is paramount. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  cannot remain in charge of Gaza, but equally, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  cannot remain situated. There has to be a new paradigm. It will involve, I expect, Arab partners and others that can give the security guarantees to Israel. We have to work at pace at what comes afterwards.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 According to the PA news agency, Kearns pressed Lammy on aid entering Gaza. She said:\par \par \uc2 On aid, has he secured any reassurances in his meetings to increase the number of trucks going into Gaza because 70-odd a day is just not enough?\par \par \uc2 In government, we did everything we could to urge Israel to let more humanitarian aid into Gaza, opening more crossings, whether through Rafah, we trebled our own aid commitment within the last financial year, doing everything we could to get aid there by land, sea or air.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lammy told MPs in his reply:\par \par \uc2 Seventy trucks a day when we know that it should be 500 is not enough and all of this House recognise the phrase \u8216\'20\'18flood\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 we were told in April that they would flood Gaza with aid, 70 is no way near enough.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On the topic of the occupied West Bank, Lammy described settlement expansion as a \u8220\'20\'1Cterrible situation\u8221\'20\'1D and added:\par \par \uc2 It was very important for me to meet with the new prime minister on the West Bank to discuss the finances that have been withdrawn, to discuss the febrile situation that we saw on the West Bank and the horrors of an unbelievable expansion against a backdrop in which this breaches international law that this house stands by.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.21am BST\par \par \uc2 The foreign secretary told parliament today that the to reach an end to the conflict in Gaza, the immediate priority is a ceasefire complied with by both sides, the release of all hostages and a surge of aid into Gaza.\par \par \uc2 David Lammy\uc2  said:\par \par \uc2 Britain wants to see an immediate ceasefire. The fighting must stop. The hostages must be released. Much, much more aid has got to enter Gaza \u8230\'20\'26. This horror must end, now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.17am BST\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s announcement on funding for \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  brings the UK into line with partners such as Germany, the EU, Sweden, Japan, France and other donors, says the government.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe government has ended the funding pause on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/UNRWA?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @UNRWA}}}\uc2  - effective immediately.\par \par \uc2 This begins with \u163\'00\'A321m to support the humanitarian response in Gaza, provide essential services for refugees across the region, and alleviate the suffering of civilians.\par \par \uc2 Read more ??\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/mwItbABdf5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/mwItbABdf5}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office\uc2  (@FCDOGovUK) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/FCDOGovUK/status/1814240042061005029?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 19, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Development minister, Anneliese Dodds, will meet the \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  commissioner general, \uc2 Philippe Lazzarini\uc2 , today to discuss how the funding will support \uc2 Unrwa\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s work and to condemn the killing of almost 200 \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  workers in the conflict.\par \par \uc2 Dodds said:\par \par \uc2 The situation in Gaza is intolerable and unacceptable and urgent action is needed to alleviate the suffering of civilians there.\par \par \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  is the only agency that can deliver lifesaving humanitarian aid at the scale needed. But it can only operate effectively if it has access to the whole of Gaza and it is safe for \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  staff to work there.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s why we are calling for unfettered access for humanitarian organisations alongside an immediate ceasefire, the protection of civilians, the release of all hostages and a credible and irreversible pathway towards a two-state solution.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.05am BST\par \par \uc2 Government overturn suspension of UK funding to \uc2 Unrwa\uc2 , says \uc2 David Lammy\par \par \uc2 The government will overturn the suspension of UK funding to the United Nations Relief and Works Agency for Palestine (\uc2 Unrwa\uc2 ), foreign secretary \uc2 David Lammy\uc2  has said.\par \par \uc2 He told MPs:\par \par \uc2 Humanitarian aid is a moral necessity in the face of such a catastrophe and it is aid agencies who ensure UK support reaches civilians on the ground. \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  is absolutely central to these efforts. No other agency can deliver aid at the scale needed. It\u8217\'20\'19s already feeding over half of Gaza\u8217\'20\'19s population. It will be vital for future reconstruction and it provides critical services to Palestinian refugees in the region.\par \par \uc2 I was appalled by the allegations that \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  staff were involved in the 7 October attacks. But the \uc2 UN\uc2  took these allegations seriously. I\u8217\'20\'19ve spoken to \uc2 UN\uc2  secretary-general Guterres and commissioner Lazzarini. We are reassured that after \uc2 Catherine Colonna\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s independent review, \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  is ensuring they meet the highest standards of neutrality and strengthening its procedures including unfettered.\par \par \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  has acted, partners like Japan, the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and Norway have also now acted, this government will act too. I can confirm to the House that we are overturning the suspension of \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  funding.\par \par \uc2 Britain will provide \u163\'00\'A321m in new funds, some directed at supporting the management reforms recommended by the colonial review and \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  supports more than five-and-a-half million Palestinians; almost 200 of their staff have died through this conflict. I thank them for this life-saving work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.01am BST\par \par \uc2 Here are some more images of \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  at Downing Street this morning:\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.47am BST\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  says the UK will 'double down' on backing Ukraine\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  said \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s visit to Downing Street was a \u8220\'20\'1Creal piece of history\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian president was greeted by the UK cabinet with a standing ovation as he became the first foreign leader to address the ministerial meeting in person since 1997.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a very, very important meeting for us and you\u8217\'20\'19re very welcome here,\u8221\'20\'1D the prime minister said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is a real pleasure and a privilege to be able to welcome you to Downing Street this morning. This is a real piece of history.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He said the president becoming the first foreign leader of the 21st century to address cabinet in person shows \u8220\'20\'1Cthe esteem you\u8217\'20\'19re held in \u8230\'20\'26 in this country and around the world\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Starmer said Ukraine had Britain\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cunshakeable support\u8221\'20\'1D and that Russia\u8217\'20\'19s aggression was \u8220\'20\'1Cunforgivable and cannot go unpunished\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said the UK will \u8220\'20\'1Cdouble down\u8221\'20\'1D on backing for Ukraine, and confirmed he had accepted this morning an invitation to visit Ukraine again in the future.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.43am BST\par \par \uc2 Speaking on Friday, armed forces minister Luke Pollard welcomed the \u8220\'20\'1Ceffort by Border Force to make sure that we are tackling that cross-Channel smuggling operation that\u8217\'20\'19s going on\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether there was a new policy for Border Force to return small boats to France, he told LBC Radio:\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll be working out the full policy and how it works. I\u8217\'20\'19m afraid it\u8217\'20\'19s not as simple as taking every single boat back to the French shore.\par \par \uc2 But what we have seen is French ships intercepting some of those dinghies travelling across the Channel and returning to France. We\u8217\'20\'19d like to see more effort put in place like this, so we\u8217\'20\'19re stopping that illegal trade.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 More than 15,000 migrants have arrived in the UK after crossing the English Channel this year as more people made the journey on Thursday, according to new Home Office figures.\par \par \uc2 The PA news agency reports that 317 people travelled across the busy shipping lane in six boats, in the first recorded arrivals since Monday when 427 people made the crossing in seven boats. This brings the provisional total of migrants arriving via the English Channel in 2024 so far to 15,076.\par \par \uc2 This is 9% higher than the number recorded this time last year (13,774) and 0.2% down on the same period in 2022 (15,106), according to PA news agency analysis of government data.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.34am BST\par \par \uc2 Zelenskiy asks Starmer to 'show your leadership' on Ukraine's 'long-range capability'\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian president, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2 , has asked \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  to \u8220\'20\'1Cshow your leadership\u8221\'20\'1D and help with Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Clong-range capability\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In his address to the cabinet Zelenskiy said \u8220\'20\'1Cif the restriction on western weapons is lifted\u8221\'20\'1D it would help Kyiv to strengthen its defence and secure its frontline positions. He said \u8220\'20\'1Cit is possible to destroy\u8221\'20\'1D areas in Russia where weapons are being concentrated.\par \par \uc2 On long-range capability, the president said \u8220\'20\'1Cwe are still missing the main answer to this question\u8221\'20\'1D and told the prime minister \u8220\'20\'1CI ask you to show your leadership\u8221\'20\'1D on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Zelenskiy has said the ability to use western weapons to strike into Russian territory is important to Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to defend itself against Moscow.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UK government\uc2  has suggested the deployment of British missiles is ultimately a matter for Ukraine, as long as international law is upheld.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.27am BST\par \par \uc2 Working parents could be hit with unexpected tax bills because of a clampdown on the misuse of a childcare \u8220\'20\'1Cbenefit\u8221\'20\'1D offered by some employers that lets people enjoy savings of up to \u163\'00\'A310,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 HM Revenue and Customs\uc2  has turned its sights on some workplace nursery benefit schemes which allow employees to pay the fees out of their pre-tax salary, resulting in them making big savings on income tax and national insurance.\par \par \uc2 This tax break was originally introduced in 1990 to encourage employers to provide nursery places for staff, either by opening an onsite nursery or by teaming up with other businesses to jointly finance and run one.\par \par \uc2 These schemes are typically marketed to employers by specialist firms and have increased in popularity as childcare costs have soared.\par \par \uc2 However, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/hmrc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 HMRC}}}\uc2  has said it believes some of the schemes do not meet its rules, which impose quite challenging requirements on employers when it comes to financing and helping to manage the nurseries used by their staff.\par \par \uc2 In an update published on its website, it said that while it believed the majority were compliant, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe have been alerted to a small number of scheme operators advertising their services with HMRC approval where the \u8230\'20\'26 requirements are not met\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 You can read the full piece here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/article/2024/jul/19/parents-saving-up-to-10000-in-nursery-benefits-may-face-unexpected-tax-bills"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Parents saving up to \u163\'00\'A310,000 in nursery benefits may face unexpected tax bills}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.17am BST\par \par \uc2 In a politics live blog/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/jul/19/retail-sales-great-britain-slump-12-government-borrowing-june-figure-lowest-2019-horizon-business-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Microsoft IT outage live blog}}}\uc2  crossover, I bring you this update via the PA news agency:\par \par \uc2 Restaurants in parliament appear to have been affected by the global IT outage. Parliamentary catering services were only accepting cash payments on Friday morning, although cash machines in Westminster were still working.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.18am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.03am BST\par \par \uc2 English councils call for further delay to social care costs cap\par \par \uc2 Long-awaited changes designed to protect individuals from having to sell their homes to meet large social care bills must be further delayed because of funding and staffing shortages, the leaders of England\u8217\'20\'19s largest councils have said.\par \par \uc2 Plans to introduce a cap on social care costs \u8211\'20\'13 which would limit people\u8217\'20\'19s lifetime care cost contributions to a maximum of \u163\'00\'A386,000 \u8211\'20\'13 in October 2025 will be impossible to deliver, the County Councils Network (CCN) said.\par \par \uc2 It called on the health and social care secretary, \uc2 Wes Streeting\uc2 , to urgently decide whether to push ahead with the cap, warning that to do so without a multibillion-pound cash injection would lead to some councils in effect being pushed into bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo put it bluntly, it will be impossible to implement these reforms next autumn in the current timescales and with no funding committed to the reforms,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Martin Tett\uc2 , the adult social care spokesperson for the CCN.\par \par \uc2 The warning presents yet another challenge for the new government, which has said it wants to tackle the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/mar/20/government-has-brought-adult-social-care-in-england-to-its-knees-mps-say"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 social care crisis}}}\uc2  as part of its wider ambitions to rebuild public services but which so far has offered little in the way of concrete proposals.\par \par \uc2 Councils are struggling to hire enough care workers, with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2024/07/18/social-care-crisis-more-workers-labour-nhs-skills-for-care/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 latest Skills for Care workforce data}}}\uc2  published this week revealing that 131,000 care jobs were unfilled. \uc2 Skills for Care\uc2  predicts that more than 400,000 extra staff will have to be recruited over the next decade to meet demand.\par \par \uc2 You can read more here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/article/2024/jul/19/english-councils-call-for-further-delay-to-social-care-costs-cap"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 English councils call for further delay to social care costs cap}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.51am BST\par \par \uc2 This is the parliamentary schedule for Friday, as reported by the PA news agency:\par \par \uc2 House of Commons:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9.30am: A statement from chancellor of the duchy of Lancaster, \uc2 Pat McFadden\uc2 , on the Covid-19 inquiry.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10.30am: A statement on Israel and Gaza.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 11.30am: Debate on the king\u8217\'20\'19s speech (day three).\par \par \uc2 House of Lords:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10am: Debate on the king\u8217\'20\'19s speech \u8211\'20\'13 creating opportunities: education, early years and healthcare.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.51am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.44am BST\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s community affairs correspondent, Neha Gohil, has spoken to minority groups in Reform seats for a news feature on life after the party\u8217\'20\'19s election success in those constituencies.\par \par \uc2 Gohil writes that some Muslim and LGBTQ+ constituents say they would feel uneasy approaching their new MPs including Nigel Farage. You can read the full piece here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/article/2024/jul/19/minority-groups-reform-seats-adjust-life-election-success-nigel-farage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Despair and anger\u8217\'20\'19: minority groups in Reform seats adjust to life after its election success}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.39am BST\par \par \uc2 Back to yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s European Political Community summit momentarily and here is the \uc2 Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s view on it:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/article/2024/jul/18/the-guardian-view-on-the-european-political-community-summit-at-last-britain-is-back-in-the-diplomatic-room"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Guardian view on the European Political Community summit: at last, Britain is back in the diplomatic room | Editorial}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.34am BST\par \par \uc2 UK will not help Ukraine hit targets in Russia, defence secretary says\par \par \uc2 Britain will not help Ukraine hit targets in Russia, the defence secretary has said, as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/volodymyr-zelenskiy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy}}}\uc2  prepares to appeal directly to the cabinet to use British-made weapons more freely.\par \par \uc2 John Healey\uc2  did not rule out accepting the Ukrainian president\u8217\'20\'19s request to use British-made missiles against Russian territory, but added that Britain would not be involved in any such attacks.\par \par \uc2 Healey was speaking hours before Zelenskiy was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/jul/18/zelenskiy-to-attend-uk-cabinet-meeting-in-effort-to-disrupt-russian-oil-sales"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 due to address}}}\uc2  an extraordinary meeting of the British cabinet on Friday, the first foreign leader to do so in nearly 30 years.\par \par \uc2 The defence secretary told BBC Radio 4\u8217\'20\'19s Today programme: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re providing weapons to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/ukraine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ukraine}}}\uc2  for their defence of their sovereign country. And that does not preclude them hitting targets in Russia, but that must be done by the Ukrainians. It must be done within the parameters and the bounds of international humanitarian law.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  had previously indicated Ukraine would be able to use the Storm Shadow missiles against targets in Russia, but only for \u8220\'20\'1Cdefensive\u8221\'20\'1D purposes. The prime minister {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.sky.com/story/keir-starmer-gives-go-ahead-for-british-missiles-to-be-used-in-strikes-against-targets-inside-russia-13176370"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said last week}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CIt is for defensive purposes, but it is for Ukraine to decide how to deploy it for those defensive purposes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Zelenskiy will use the cabinet meeting to appeal to ministers to use Storm Shadow missiles against Russian targets. He will also urge ministers to help build up Europe\u8217\'20\'19s defence industrial base, as leaders around the world adjust to the possibility of a second, more isolationist, Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 You can read the full piece here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/article/2024/jul/19/uk-will-not-help-ukraine-hit-targets-in-russia-defence-secretary-says"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK will not help Ukraine hit targets in Russia, defence secretary says}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.20am BST\par \par \uc2 In the bilateral meeting with Starmer, Zelenskiy thanked the UK for its \u8220\'20\'1Cunity\u8221\'20\'1D with and backing for Kyiv and said \u8220\'20\'1Cwe feel this support\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian president said he wanted to discuss \u8220\'20\'1Cstrong decisions\u8221\'20\'1D in the war in Ukraine. \u8220\'20\'1CThank you for the invitation for the privilege for me to be here,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 He said:\par \par \uc2 Thanks for this unity. We feel this support and your strong decisions \u8230\'20\'26 helped us to defend freedom and democracy in Ukraine and helped very much Ukrainian people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Zelenskiy added: \u8220\'20\'1CI also want to discuss with you today also strong decisions we can have in this war [to] help us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.15am BST\par \par \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  said \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s visit to Downing Street was a \u8220\'20\'1Cvery special moment\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In a bilateral meeting with the Ukrainian president, the prime minister said the UK is \u8220\'20\'1Cunited in our parliament\u8221\'20\'1D in supporting Kyiv for as long as possible.\par \par \uc2 Starmer said:\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a very special moment for us to reiterate our support for you and the Ukrainian people in the fight against Russian aggression and to have this opportunity to confirm again our resolve to stand with you, so thank you so much for accepting the invitation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.14am BST\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a photograph on the newswires of Larry the cat preparing for Zelenskiy \u8217\'20\'19s visit this morning.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.08am BST\par \par \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  arrives in Downing Street to address the cabinet\par \par \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  has arrived in Downing Street, where he is expected to become the first foreign leader to address cabinet in person since 1997.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian president was greeted by prime minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  with a warm hug and handshake before entering Number 10.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.20am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.50am BST\par \par \uc2 The UK will provide military aid funding to Ukraine \u8220\'20\'1Cfor every year for the rest of the decade,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 John Healey\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The defence secretary told Times Radio:\par \par \uc2 If we take the starting point that the defence of the UK starts in Ukraine and if Putin wins in Ukraine he will not stop there, then we must stand with Ukraine, we\u8217\'20\'19re determined to do that.\par \par \uc2 And it isn\u8217\'20\'19t just that. We\u8217\'20\'19ve stepped up extra support in these first two weeks in government.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve also confirmed the commitment of extra military aid funding this year and for every year for the rest of the decade. As long as it takes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He added: \u8220\'20\'1CWhen \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  said Britain will continue our steadfast support for Ukraine he meant it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.42am BST\par \par \uc2 Opening summary\par \par \uc2 Good morning and welcome to our UK politics blog.\par \par \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  is addressing the cabinet this morning to bring fresh impetus to efforts to stop {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/russia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Russia}}}\uc2  evading sanctions on its oil exports.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian president is in the UK after attending yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s summit of the European Political Community, hosted by \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  at Blenheim Palace.\par \par \uc2 At the meeting, European leaders agreed to launch a \u8220\'20\'1Ccall to action\u8221\'20\'1D to disrupt the growing fleet of \u8220\'20\'1Cphantom\u8221\'20\'1D tankers carrying sanctioned Russian oil around the world.\par \par \uc2 You can read our story here\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/jul/18/zelenskiy-to-attend-uk-cabinet-meeting-in-effort-to-disrupt-russian-oil-sales"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Zelenskiy to attend UK cabinet meeting in effort to disrupt Russian oil sales}}}\par \par \uc2 In other news, MPs will continue their debate around measures in the King\u8217\'20\'19s speech earlier this week, stay with us for all the day\u8217\'20\'19s political developments.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.28am BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240719ek7j000e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
