\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s $50,000 arts degree is here \u8211\'20\'13 putting university fees on par with the UK and US\b0\par\par\uc2 Caitlin Cassidy \par \uc2 947 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 July 2024\par 23:47 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Hecs/Help scheme was not designed so people making a regular wage remained in debt until death, expert saysFollow our Australia news live blog for latest updatesGet our morning and afternoon news emails, free app or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 There was a time when would-be poets, historians and writers could expect to pay off an arts degree at an Australian university within the decade, if they were able to find stable employment. Fast forward to now and they may die with their debt.\par \par \uc2 Arts degrees in Australia are poised to cost {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/oct/02/why-are-university-degrees-in-australia-getting-more-expensive-and-how-much-will-they-cost#:~:text=The%20JRG%20scheme%20reduced%20the,and%2039%2C000%20extra%20university%20places."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than $50,000}}}\uc2  for the first time, with experts warning some students will never be able to pay off their debts.\par \par \uc2 The new figure places Australia on par with the UK and public colleges in the US, five years on from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/jul/30/student-debt-reforms-australia-labor-coalition-liberal-changes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 widely condemned jobs ready graduates (JRG) scheme}}}\uc2 .\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.education.gov.au/higher-education-loan-program/resources/2025-allocation-units-study-funding-clusters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Data released by the Department of Education}}}\uc2  this month confirmed the student contribution for a range of degrees including society and culture, communications, law and economics would jump to $16,992 annually from 2025.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s about $650 higher than last year resulting from indexation, and more than $10,000 steeper for humanities courses than 2020, before the implementation of the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s policy.\par \par \uc2 The scheme, introduced by the Morrison government, reduced the overall government contribution to degrees from 58% to 52%, raising student contributions to 48%.\par \par \uc2 The JRG also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2020/jun/19/australian-university-fees-arts-stem-science-maths-nursing-teaching-humanities"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increased fees for some courses, including humanities}}}\uc2 , to fund fee cuts in other courses as well as 39,000 extra university places by 2023.\par \par \uc2 It has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/apr/14/students-choose-arts-degrees-in-droves-despite-huge-rise-in-fees-under-morrison-government"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 widely panned}}}\uc2  by the university sector, including the \uc2 Group of Eight\uc2 , Universities Australia, student bodies and academics.\par \par \uc2 The University Accord interim report said the scheme had failed and \u8220\'20\'1Cneeds to be redesigned before it causes long-term and entrenched damage to Australian higher education\u8221\'20\'1D, while its final report, released this January, called for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/feb/25/cutting-debt-and-paid-internships-key-reforms-in-the-australian-universities-blueprint"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Curgent remediation\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=copyembed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sign up for Guardian Australia\u8217\'20\'19s free morning and afternoon email newsletters for your daily news roundup}}}\par \par \uc2 Before the 2022 federal election, Labor was also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2020/sep/15/labor-accuses-australian-universities-of-being-in-cahoots-with-government-over-funding-changes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scathing in its critique}}}\uc2  of JRG, with the then shadow education minister, \uc2 Tanya Plibersek\uc2 , calling it a \u8220\'20\'1Ctotal fraud that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t deserve backing from anyone with common sense or a conscience\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The successive fee increases means it\u8217\'20\'19s more than twice as costly to study in Australia than in Canada.\par \par \uc2 According to the higher education analytics firm Quacquarelli Symonds, undergraduate humanities courses in Canada average C$5,542 (A$6,028) a year for domestic students, while in the UK all domestic courses are capped at \u163\'00\'A39,250 (A$17,900) annually.\par \par \uc2 University fees in the US vary, ranging from US$12,320 for public colleges to $50,000 a year at elite private ones.\par \par \uc2 Comparatively, a string of European countries including Austria, the Czech Republic, Denmark, Finland, Greece, Iceland, Luxembourg, Norway and Sweden offer free education for some or all courses.\par \par \uc2 The president of the University of Sydney\u8217\'20\'19s Student Representative Council, Harrison Brennan, is midway through his politics and philosophy degree and already owes $33,000. He said it felt like Labor wanted to \u8220\'20\'1Cprolong the attack on critical thinking\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s palpable anger among students who feel they\u8217\'20\'19ve been cheated \u8211\'20\'13 forced to pay even more for an education that many in parliament received for free,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Asked in question time when the federal government would unwind the doubling of costs for affected courses, as was recommended in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2024/feb/26/urgent-higher-education-reform-is-needed-to-fix-a-broken-system-and-secure-australias-future"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the accord}}}\uc2 , the education minister, Jason Clare, said reforms were \u8220\'20\'1Cbigger than one budget\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe announced in the budget that we would establish an Australian tertiary education commission to steer reform here, and that will include the setting of course fees,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Norton, a professor in higher education policy at \uc2 Australian National University\uc2 , said the JRG was always going to be a \u8220\'20\'1Cbooby trap\u8221\'20\'1D for the next government but a temporary fix was needed before the implementation of an education commission.\par \par \uc2 Modelling by Norton suggested arts graduates with median career earnings would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/apr/26/its-perverse-how-australias-student-loan-scheme-traps-graduates-in-a-debt-spiral"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 never fully repay their debts}}}\uc2  under the current rates \u8211\'20\'13 even with low indexation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo change humanities fees in a budget-neutral way would require increasing fees for other courses and they\u8217\'20\'19re reluctant to do that,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Norton suggested the old system \u8211\'20\'13 where a person\u8217\'20\'19s capacity to pay determines what they are charged \u8211\'20\'13 was needed, but pointed out the education commission was not due to start work until mid-2025.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19ll be 2026 before they can start looking at this and [by] 2027 at best it\u8217\'20\'19ll be changed. That\u8217\'20\'19s a whole new cycle through the system of students accumulating debts they can\u8217\'20\'19t pay.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Norton said under the Hecs/Help scheme it was \u8220\'20\'1Cnever intended\u8221\'20\'1D that someone with a normal income for their degree remained in debt until their death.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe reality is for many the government won\u8217\'20\'19t get money back anyway,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CHaving this debt hanging over people for their entire career isn\u8217\'20\'19t sensible policy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Greens deputy leader and higher education spokesperson, Mehreen Faruqi, said Labor\u8217\'20\'19s unwillingness to scrap the fee hikes was exacerbating the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/jun/01/falling-behind-australian-graduates-fear-for-their-future-as-record-high-student-loan-indexation-kicks-in"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 student debt crisis}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSoaring student debt is already locking people out of the housing market, crushing dreams of further study and stopping people from starting a family, and Labor is making things worse,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith the Albanese government backing Morrison\u8217\'20\'19s university fee hikes and student debt ballooning out of control, young people are telling me that they\u8217\'20\'19re finding it harder and harder to tell Labor and the Liberals apart.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240718ek7i0025x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
