\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Mining giant to cut 700 jobs \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Nino Bucci, Daisy Dumas and Emily Wind (earlier) \par \uc2 15,494 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 July 2024\par 03:18 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 This blog is now closed.Screaming, freezing, struggling to breathe: confronting Queensland watch house footage exposes anguish of children locked in isolation cellsAFP asked to investigate \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  corruption allegations as \uc2 ACTU\uc2  suspends union\u8217\'20\'19s construction and general divisionGet our morning and afternoon news emails, free app or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.29am BST\par \par \uc2 That's it for today \u8211\'20\'13 thanks for reading\par \par \uc2 Here are the main stories on Wednesday 17 July:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Workplace relations minister Tony Burke empowered the \uc2 Fair Work Commission\uc2  general manager Murray Furlong to appoint an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/jul/17/australia-news-live-minns-nsw-labor-cfmeu-construction-arm-victoria-wong-mh17-memorial?page=with:block-669719cd8f08e299b1993403#block-669719cd8f08e299b1993403"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 independent administrator to branches of the CFMEU}}}\uc2  ;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ACTU\uc2  also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/jul/17/australia-news-live-minns-nsw-labor-cfmeu-construction-arm-victoria-wong-mh17-memorial?page=with:block-669753dc8f089592211e52ac#block-669753dc8f089592211e52ac"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announce that it has suspended}}}\uc2  the construction and general division of the union after a raft of corruption allegations;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Fortescue{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/jul/17/australia-news-live-minns-nsw-labor-cfmeu-construction-arm-victoria-wong-mh17-memorial?page=with:block-6697705a8f0838cdbaeab5c4#block-6697705a8f0838cdbaeab5c4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announce about 700 redundancies}}}\uc2 , to be finalised within days;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2 , the Australian Ambassador to the US, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/jul/17/australia-news-live-minns-nsw-labor-cfmeu-construction-arm-victoria-wong-mh17-memorial?page=with:block-6697619f8f089592211e5314#block-6697619f8f089592211e5314"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 says Australia should \u8220\'20\'1Cchill\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins the 2024 election;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Victorian MP for Mulgrave Eden Foster has been diagnosed with cancer. Foster, 43, replaced former premier \uc2 Daniel Andrews\uc2  after a byelection in October last year;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The head of the \uc2 Australian Energy Regulator\uc2  doubts nuclear energy is possible in the country by 2050; and\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A memorial service is held in Canberra for the 10th anniversary of the downing of MH17.\par \par \uc2 We will see you here again for more news tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.17am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.24am BST\par \par \uc2 Fortescue\uc2  to make 700 staff redundant\par \par \uc2 About 700 people from \uc2 Fortescue\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s global operations will be made redundant, the Australian company has confirmed in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Fortescue\uc2  says the redundancy process will be finalised by the end of the month. Its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://announcements.asx.com.au/asxpdf/20240717/pdf/065p6n790sr22t.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement}}}\uc2  read:\par \par \uc2 Fortescue\uc2  remains resolute in its commitment to be the world\u8217\'20\'19s leading green technology, energy and metals company with a laser focus on achieving Real Zero by 2030.\par \par \uc2 The Company has undergone a period of rapid growth and transition,and as part of bringing together Metals and Energy into One \uc2 Fortescue\uc2 , initiatives are being implemented to simplify its structure, remove duplication and deliver cost efficiencies.\par \par \uc2 The Company must continually evolve to ensure it remains lean, is best positioned to deliver on its strategy and generate the maximum value for shareholders.\par \par \uc2 As part of this, approximately 700 people from across \uc2 Fortescue\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s global operations will be offered redundancies, with that process to be finalised by the end of July2024. \uc2 Fortescue\uc2  is grateful for the contribution of all those impacted by these changes.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.29am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.52am BST\par \par \uc2 NSW dog track future unclear\par \par \uc2 Greyhound racing in New South Wales has been under the pump, after the release of a damning report prompted a government inquiry and forced the peak body\u8217\'20\'19s CEO to resign.\par \par \uc2 But will the embattled industry get to keep its lease on prime Sydney real estate?\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a greyhound racetrack in Wentworth Park in Pyrmont, right in the city\u8217\'20\'19s CBD. The racers previously agreed to leave the site when their lease expires in 2027 but they\u8217\'20\'19ve since changed their minds.\par \par \uc2 The NSW government won\u8217\'20\'19t rule anything out. It says the greyhound racers still need to provide a business case and would have to make room for \u8220\'20\'1Ca multi-purpose community space that everyone can enjoy\u8221\'20\'1D if they stuck around.\par \par \uc2 But some locals who have long wanted the industry out of their neighbourhood were even more unhappy after reading our coverage last week. Lord mayor \uc2 Clover Moore\uc2  said:\par \par \uc2 This parkland should be for the benefit of the community, not an industry that has admitted to killing up to 17,000 healthy dogs each year [nationwide in 2015].\par \par \uc2 Moore was particularly concerned because the NSW government has told the City of Sydney to build 18,900 new homes by July 2029. Those new inner-city residents will need somewhere to go on their weekends, and they may not be too keen on the races.\par \par \uc2 GRNSW declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more on the push to close Wentworth Park:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/mar/04/wentworth-park-greyhouse-deaths-oceanside-omega-texas-closure-calls-animal-justice-party-petition"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Second greyhound death in a fortnight at Sydney racetrack increases calls for its closure}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.57am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.41am BST\par \par \uc2 Confronting Queensland watch house footage exposes anguish of children locked in isolation cells\par \par \uc2 A girl punches herself in the face, strips naked and urinates on the floor of an isolation cell; a boy wakes in the night and tells officers: \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t breathe.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Children scream in distress at being placed in a \u8220\'20\'1Cfreezing\u8221\'20\'1D isolation cell, known as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe box\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 These are some of the scenes revealed in a cache of video footage seen by Guardian Australia and \uc2 SBS\uc2  The Feed that shows the confronting reality for children imprisoned in Queensland\u8217\'20\'19s adult police watch houses.\par \par \uc2 More here on our In the box investigation:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/jul/17/queensland-youth-crime-watch-house-footage-police-treatment-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Screaming, freezing, struggling to breathe: confronting Queensland watch house footage exposes anguish of children locked in isolation cells}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.47am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.19am BST\par \par \uc2 Rudd speculates first call between Albanese and Trump would \u8216\'20\'18go better than Malcom\u8217\'20\'19s\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Australian ambassador to the US, \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2 , also told Sky News:\par \par \uc2 For us in Australia though, I just say this: it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter whether you vote Labor, vote Liberal or vote National back home. The really good thing about the United States is \u8216\'20\'18brand Australia\u8217\'20\'19 is in good working order. And therefore we are in a good position to work with whichever party is elected. If the American people vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and for [vice-presidential running mate] JD Vance, the Australian government, led by Anthony Albanese, will be in there working hard, positively, constructively, with incoming Republican administration on day one \u8211\'20\'13 that\u8217\'20\'19s as it should be, as it has been in the past.\par \par \uc2 Rudd speculated that a first phone call between Albanese and Trump would go better than the infamous call between \uc2 Malcolm Turnbull\uc2  and Trump, by saying: \u8220\'20\'1CIt will go better than Malcolm\u8217\'20\'19s.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Rudd promptly acknowledged that was \u8220\'20\'1Ca low benchmark\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Sky News interview was conducted by former Australian ambassador turned lobbyist \uc2 Joe Hockey\uc2 . Hockey offered \u8220\'20\'1Ca suggestion\u8221\'20\'1D for how Albanese should approach the first call, advising that the Australian government could advance Aukus-related funding to the US. Hockey said:\par \par \uc2 I think Anthony Albanese should give \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  something, particularly on subs, the second round of checks or something, give him a win in that early phone conversation, to show that \u8230\'20\'26 it\u8217\'20\'19s a symbolic way of saying this is how we work together.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.24am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.17am BST\par \par \uc2 Rudd says Australia should \u8216\'20\'18chill\u8217\'20\'19 if Trump wins\par \par \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2  says Australia and other US allies are \u8220\'20\'1Cnot going over some chasm\u8221\'20\'1D if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins November\u8217\'20\'19s election.\par \par \uc2 The Australian ambassador to the US said he had advised many fellow diplomats in Washington DC, particularly from European countries, \u8220\'20\'1Cto chill, just chill\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Rudd, who prior to his diplomatic appointment called Trump \u8220\'20\'1Cthe most destructive president in history\u8221\'20\'1D, gave an interview to Sky News Australia from the Republican national convention today. He was interviewed by \uc2 Joe Hockey\uc2 , a former Australian ambassador to the US who was once Rudd\u8217\'20\'19s political sparring partner in a regular segment on Seven\u8217\'20\'19s Sunrise program. Rudd said:\par \par \uc2 If president Trump is elected on 5 November, we\u8217\'20\'19re not going over some chasm. For us in Australia, and for allies of the United States, this is a perfectly navigable and manageable set of relationships for the next four years. And everyone needs to have that just as a discipline in their minds.\par \par \uc2 If instead you think, \u8216\'20\'18Oh my god, this is beyond the pale,\u8217\'20\'19 and reach for your smelling salts, well you know something: it\u8217\'20\'19s going to cruel you from day one. That\u8217\'20\'19s actually the wrong conclusion. I say a lot to the Euros along these lines, which is: chill, Bill.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.41am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.05am BST\par \par \uc2 Energy regulator singles out \uc2 AGL\uc2 , \uc2 EnergyAustralia\uc2  for nudging power prices higher\par \par \uc2 Volatile energy prices are nothing new but occasionally they fluctuate more wildly than usual.\par \par \uc2 That was the case in early May when a spate of unplanned outages at coal-fired power plants (the \u8220\'20\'1Creliable baseload\u8221\'20\'1D ones) triggered a series of high-priced events in the wholesale power market in NSW, as the Australian Energy Market notes in a special report.\par \par \uc2 enltrInteresting \uc2 AER\uc2  analysis of high power prices in NSW in May: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/k4UIpg6L2D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/k4UIpg6L2D}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 @phannam@mastodon.green (@p_hannam) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/p_hannam/status/1813449646523777474?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 17, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 In short, a couple of coal plants had unscheduled outages (at Eraring and Vales Point ), which happens from time to time as these clunkers become clunkier. Anyway, what happened next was not helpful.\par \par \uc2 Two rival producers, \uc2 AGL Energy\uc2  and \uc2 EnergyAustralia\uc2  opted to \u8220\'20\'1Cprofit maximise\u8221\'20\'1D by offering their capacity at higher prices and also by rebidding from low to high prices from the Bayswater (\uc2 AGL\uc2 ) and Mt Piper (EA) plants.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile this is permissible under the National Electricity Rules, the behaviour may not have been in the best interests of energy consumers,\u8221\'20\'1D the \uc2 AER\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The particular market price threshold was exceeded for only the second time in the national electricity market\u8217\'20\'19s history, resulting in a price cap being imposed at $600/megawatt-hour for seven days.\par \par \uc2 The behaviour by \uc2 AGL\uc2  and EA was \u8220\'20\'1Cquite a contrast with offer behaviour of the previous week\u8221\'20\'1D, the \uc2 AER\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 One effect of this episode was to nudge future prices significantly higher for NSW.\par \par \uc2 enltrWith implications for future power prices in NSW too. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/BX82AzxNkv"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/BX82AzxNkv}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 @phannam@mastodon.green (@p_hannam) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/p_hannam/status/1813449857115517173?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 17, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Now wholesale prices only make up about a third of your retail power bill but it\u8217\'20\'19s not the downward direction we would all want to see.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve asked \uc2 AGL\uc2  and EA what they think of the regulator\u8217\'20\'19s findings.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.58am BST\par \par \uc2 Queensland premier backs federal stance on \uc2 CFMEU\par \par \uc2 Steven Miles\uc2  has thrown his support behind the federal move, saying the state would also legislate to put the state \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  into administration, if necessary.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf they choose though, to fight it through the process, then the Australian Government have indicated they are willing to legislate to ensure that this administration can be appointed,\u8221\'20\'1D Miles said.\par \par \uc2 And what you should read between the lines in what we\u8217\'20\'19ve said is that we would also be willing to legislate to ensure that this process can be successful.\par \par \uc2 We want Australian workers to have strong, honest, democratic unions. We are proud supporters of trade unions. But there is no place in those unions for illegal activity for underworld links for thuggery. And that\u8217\'20\'19s what we want to see stamped out.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.05am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.54am BST\par \par \uc2 The Queensland CFMEU secretary Michael Ravbar has launched an extraordinary spray at prime minister Anthony Albanese.\par \par \uc2 The PM announced moves to put the state \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  into administration in a Brisbane press conference today. He urged union leaders to enter administration voluntarily, rather than making him do it through legislation.\par \par \uc2 Ravbar said the union will cooperate with any criminal investigation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAlbanese has panicked and soiled himself over some unproven allegations in the media,\u8221\'20\'1D Ravbar said.\par \par \uc2 Workers can see that Albo has lost control and the country is being run by buffoon breakfast show hosts and talkback radio shock jocks.\par \par \uc2 These gutless Labor politicians talk tough about affiliation fees and donations because that\u8217\'20\'19s the only language they understand \u8211\'20\'13 money. The \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  is an industrial union, not a political outfit. Our strength has always come from our members on the shop floor, not from ladder-climbing politicians in the halls of power.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.01am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.44am BST\par \par \uc2 Dutton asked what portion of Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s energy plan would include renewables\par \par \uc2 Circling back to opposition leader \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s earlier press conference: he was asked about energy minister Chris Bowen \u8217\'20\'19s stance that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/jul/17/australia-news-live-minns-nsw-labor-cfmeu-construction-arm-victoria-wong-mh17-memorial?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-669734538f08e299b1993495#block-669734538f08e299b1993495"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voters must chose between renewables or nuclear reactors}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dutton argued the Coalition was planning for a \u8220\'20\'1Cmix of technologies\u8221\'20\'1D including renewables, gas and nuclear.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not a choice between renewables and nuclear. Our policy has renewables, it has gas and it has the underpinning of a baseload power.\par \par \uc2 This comes as Clare Savage, head of the \uc2 Australian Energy Regulator\uc2 , told a summit today that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/jul/17/australia-news-live-minns-nsw-labor-cfmeu-construction-arm-victoria-wong-mh17-memorial?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-66972d898f08e299b199347a#block-66972d898f08e299b199347a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she doubts nuclear will be possible in Australia by 2050}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dutton was asked what portion of the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s energy plan would include renewables, and said \u8220\'20\'1Cwe\u8217\'20\'19ll have more to say in relation to that detail.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Asked for a ball figure, he again replied: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ll talk about it in due course.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.36am BST\par \par \uc2 Housing completions in NSW reach historic lows\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Property Council of Australia\uc2  has lamented new quarterly data from the Bureau of Statistics showing the speed of housing construction is slowing down in New South Wales.\par \par \uc2 The latest data show dwelling completions fell from 11,525 to 10,776 over the March quarter, while commencements fell from 11,361 to 8,961 \u8211\'20\'13 the worst performance since June 2012.\par \par \uc2 Property Council of Australia NSW executive director Katie Stevenson said that for NSW to reach its National Housing Accord target of 377,000 new homes by 2029, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe need to be completing an average of 18,850 new dwellings each quarter for the five years from 1 July\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Stevenson said:\par \par \uc2 The data reflect the cold economic headwinds blowing through the construction sector, with skill shortages, materials costs, access to capital, and interest rates all impacting project feasibility.\par \par \uc2 To get more housing delivered more quickly, the industry needs feasible projects. Currently, project risks are too high, and the sector\u8217\'20\'19s ability to secure finance is proving difficult.\par \par \uc2 Suspending taxes and charges and compressing approval timeframes could enable the delivery of up to 209,000 new homes over the next five years in four of Sydney\u8217\'20\'19s six cities \u8211\'20\'13 100,000 more homes delivered than if no action is taken.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.47am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.32am BST\par \par \uc2 Staying with the news from the \uc2 ACTU\uc2  regarding its move to suspend the construction and general divisions of the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2 , secretary \uc2 Sally McManus\uc2  said the resolution was adopted almost unanimously.\par \par \uc2 She said of 52 votes in the \uc2 ACTU\uc2  executive, she \u8220\'20\'1Ccould count - if I was being really generous \u8211\'20\'13 on one hand, the people against\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.35am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.21am BST\par \par \uc2 ACTU\uc2  suspends construction and general division of \uc2 CFMEU\par \par \uc2 Sally McManus\uc2 , secretary of the \uc2 ACTU\uc2 , said that the union has zero tolerance for any criminal elements and corruption within.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey do not represent us. That behaviour does not represent us, does not represent the trade union movement,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Building workers deserve a strong and a clean union, she said.\par \par \uc2 She also urged \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  leadership to cooperate with the independent external administrator.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/jul/17/afp-asked-to-investigate-cfmeu-corruption-allegations-as-independent-administrator-to-be-appointed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AFP asked to investigate CFMEU corruption allegations as independent administrator to be appointed}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.27am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.08am BST\par \par \uc2 Victorian Labor MP Eden Foster diagnosed with cancer\par \par \uc2 Thanks, Emily Wind.\par \par \uc2 Over to Victoria, where the state\u8217\'20\'19s MP for Mulgrave, Eden Foster, has been diagnosed with cancer. Foster, 43, who was elected to replace former premier \uc2 Daniel Andrews\uc2  at a byelection in October last year, took to \uc2 Instagram\uc2  to make the announcement:\par \par \uc2 Today, I\u8217\'20\'19m sharing some personal news with our Mulgrave community. After undergoing several medical investigations due to a swollen lymph node in my neck, I have been diagnosed with Stage 1 Lymphoma. I have caught this early, and the prognosis is good. I am determined to fight and win this battle and will shortly start chemotherapy. During this time, I will be focusing on my treatment and recovery.\par \par \uc2 She said while it was a \u8220\'20\'1Cshock\u8221\'20\'1D she hoped her diagnosis could \u8220\'20\'1Cserve as a reminder that cancer can touch everyone, no matter your age\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.44am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.02am BST\par \par \uc2 Many thanks for joining me on today\u8217\'20\'19s blog, Daisy Dumas will now be here for the next little while. Take care!\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.48am BST\par \par \uc2 ACA supports \uc2 Fair Work Commission\uc2  to appoint \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  independent administrator\par \par \uc2 The Australian Constructors Association (ACA) has welcomed a decision by the federal workplace minister, Tony Burke, to allow the \uc2 Fair Work Commission (FWC)\uc2  general manager to appoint an independent administrator to oversee the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The ACA chief executive, Jon Davies, welcomed this action as opposed to the de-registration of the union, which \u8220\'20\'1Cimportantly mean[s] that workers will still have access to workplace representation if needed\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It should not be forgotten that lawful and well-run unions play an important role in maintaining a safe workplace and representing workers on pay and conditions.\par \par \uc2 The ACA said it would work to support the \uc2 FWC\uc2  general manager in his investigation and would continue to work with the federal government more broadly.\par \par \uc2 This is an opportunity to create a more balanced, harmonious and productive industry for the benefit of all workers, employers and taxpayers.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.04am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.37am BST\par \par \uc2 Farm kids get their hands dirty, but safety comes first\par \par \uc2 The balance of exposing kids to rural life while maximising their safety is a key focus of Farm Safety Week, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports, as figures reveal 12% of farm injuries occurred in people under 15 last year.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 10% of fatalities were in that age group. Quad bikes, large animals and motorbikes were the top three causes of injuries, according to Farmsafe Australia \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://farmsafe.org.au/safer-farms-report-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 annual trend report}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Research shows parents\u8217\'20\'19 active engagement, anticipation of risks and clear communication are crucial. Farmsafe Australia chair Felicity Richards said:\par \par \uc2 Growing up on a farm is a unique and enriching experience; it instills values of hard work, resourcefulness, and a profound connection to the land. However, with these incredible opportunities come responsibilities.\par \par \uc2 There was a significant decrease in all farm fatalities across Australia in 2023, with 32 deaths compared with 55 the year before. But 30 people have already died in on-farm incidents in the first half of this year.\par \par \uc2 About a third of fatalities in NSW over the last two years have involved quad bikes or motorbikes, according to SafeWork NSW.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.46am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.26am BST\par \par \uc2 The Bureau of Meteorology has also shared a photo of a \u8220\'20\'1Ctarn\u8221\'20\'1D that has reappeared at Mount Wellington in Tasmania after days of heavy rain.\par \par \uc2 A tarn is a lake that forms when a steep hollow fills with rain or snow run-off.\par \par \uc2 The Bureau said 124mm of rain was reported on the mountain in the three days to Tuesday, and the tarn would drain away after a few days.\par \par \uc2 enltrThis stunning tarn has reappeared at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/kunanyi?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #kunanyi}}}\uc2  / {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/MtWellington?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #MtWellington}}}\uc2  ! A tarn is a lake that forms when a steep hollow fills with rain/ snow run-off. In the 3 days to Tuesday, 124mm of rain was reported on the mountain. The tarn will drain away after a few days, so be sure to check it out! {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/qHrj9NmCw8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/qHrj9NmCw8}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bureau of Meteorology, Tasmania (@BOM_Tas) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BOM_Tas/status/1813414659149463621?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 17, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.36am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.21am BST\par \par \uc2 The Bureau of Meteorology has published a national weather update, looking at the cold front moving across southern Australia:\par \par \uc2 enltrNational Weather Update: A front racing across southern Australia, and cool and dry in the north. Video current: Midday AEST 17 July 2024.\par \par \uc2 Latest forecasts and warnings: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/4W35o8iFmh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/4W35o8iFmh}}}\uc2  or the BOM Weather app. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/dl07xutVp3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/dl07xutVp3}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bureau of Meteorology, Australia (@BOM_au) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BOM_au/status/1813426935818809585?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 17, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.15am BST\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Potential\u8217\'20\'19 to deregister \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  as Tasmania weighs options\par \par \uc2 The union representing construction, forestry and maritime workers in Tasmania could be deregistered after allegations of criminal links with bikies, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 The Tasmanian government is investigating to determine what actions to take against the state\u8217\'20\'19s union branch including potential de-registration, after a series of alleged corruption scandals.\par \par \uc2 The revelations of alleged criminal conduct in the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  were extremely disturbing and a betrayal of hard-working construction workers in Tasmania, attorney-general \uc2 Guy Barnett\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 I am seeking advice on what actions can be taken at a state level to protect Tasmanians \u8211\'20\'13 including potential de-registration of the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  in Tasmania.\par \par \uc2 I will also be writing to the independent administrator appointed to investigate the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  to request the investigation also covers the actions of the Tasmanian division.\par \par \uc2 The Tasmanian branch of the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  is managed by the Victorian branch, which has been placed into administration along with its South Australian branch by the union\u8217\'20\'19s national office.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.08am BST\par \par \uc2 Praise for cane toad containment zone\par \par \uc2 The Invasive Species Council has welcomed a move by the Western Australian government to launch a cane toad containment zone, west of Broome.\par \par \uc2 Conservation and biosecurity analyst Lyall Grieve said:\par \par \uc2 This initiative will be a frontline defence against this incoming invasion, which will devastate native species, impact farmers and damage Indigenous connection to Country.\par \par \uc2 These toxic pests have already spread west throughout the Kimberley and are knocking on the door of the Pilbara. The looming threat of cane toads pushing 20 to 24 more native animals to the brink of extinction if they make their way west of Broome, is a crisis we cannot afford to ignore.\par \par \uc2 Some of these species are found nowhere else outside the Pilbara. Small mammals such as Kaluta, Ningaui and Planigales along with reptiles like the Pilbara Rock Goanna are expected to be some of the worst hit of the unique animals found only in this part.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.12am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.02am BST\par \par \uc2 Parks Australia\uc2  trialling AI to detect crocodiles in watering holes\par \par \uc2 Parks Australia\uc2  is trialling using artificial intelligence to detect crocodiles in watering holes to prevent visitors to the parks encountering them unexpectedly, a Senate committee has heard.\par \par \uc2 Parks Australia\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s director of the office of the chief remote pilot, Dr Renee Bartolo, told the Senate select committee on adopting AI that the agency has been using drones to undertake wildlife surveys, and then using AI to do detections:\par \par \uc2 We also use it doing some trials in terms of visitor safety with a crocodile AI detection model for swimming holes. That\u8217\'20\'19s not to replace existing survey methods, but to add further lines of evidence.\par \par \uc2 Bartolo said it was also used for environmental monitoring and management for issues like weeds, ghost nets, and marine debris in northern Australia.\par \par \uc2 The agency has adopted a \u8220\'20\'1Ccrawl, walk, run\u8221\'20\'1D approach to AI and is determining what the benefits might be particularly around environmental monitoring, the committee heard, and for ways to potentially collect real-time information that is a lot more comprehensive and cheaper to collect.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.56am BST\par \par \uc2 What else is the government doing?\par \par \uc2 Tony Burke has asked the \uc2 Fair Work Ombudsman\uc2  to \u8220\'20\'1Cundertake a targeted review of all enterprise agreements made by the Victorian branch of the construction division of the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  that apply to Victorian \u8220\'20\'1CBig Build\u8221\'20\'1D projects\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 That is separate to what the \uc2 Fair Work Commission\uc2  is doing \u8211\'20\'13 this part of the review will be \u8220\'20\'1Cseeking information on coercive behaviour and has no intention of taking any action which would put at risk terms and conditions of employment for the workers covered by these agreements. This is not their fault\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Burke says \u8220\'20\'1Cthe government will use its procurement powers to ensure that enterprise agreements used on Government funded projects are genuinely agreed and that workplaces are free from coercion and intimidation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 On top of all of this, Burke has written to the AFP commissioner, asking that the AFP \u8220\'20\'1Cinvestigate the recent allegations and work cooperatively with state police to investigate and prosecute any criminal breaches\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 There will be more coming \u8211\'20\'13 so watch this space.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.51am BST\par \par \uc2 Let\u8217\'20\'19s go through the announcement Tony Burke just made regarding the \uc2 CFMEU\par \par \uc2 Burke is using industrial relations law to allow the \uc2 Fair Work Commission\uc2  general manager, Murray Furlong, to appoint an independent administrator to oversee the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  while the investigations into the allegations are carried out.\par \par \uc2 To do that will be a little bit complicated.\par \par \uc2 Burke says Furlong has said he wants to take legal action to appoint administrators to the construction division. So far, this at the advice stage.\par \par \uc2 If it cannot be worked out before parliament sits in mid-August, then the government will legislate to allow the work to take place. Burke has said he will back Furlong in any action he believes needs to be taken.\par \par \uc2 So it is nuanced, but Furlong will lead the process appointing administrators, but won\u8217\'20\'19t be the administrator himself, and he\u8217\'20\'19ll have the backing of the government in doing that.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.52am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.49am BST\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the full story from Amy Remeikis on news that workplace relations minister Tony Burke has announced an independent administrator will be appointed as investigations into the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  begin.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/jul/17/afp-asked-to-investigate-cfmeu-corruption-allegations-as-independent-administrator-to-be-appointed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AFP asked to investigate CFMEU corruption allegations as independent administrator to be appointed}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.45am BST\par \par \uc2 Aged care expert says for-profit facilities not passing on public funding\par \par \uc2 When profit is a motive for providing aged care, vulnerable people will miss out, the executive officer of Housing for the Aged Action Group has said.\par \par \uc2 Today we reported on recent analysis from the \uc2 University of Technology Sydney\uc2 , which found nearly two-thirds of residential aged care homes that turned a profit in the first half of the last financial year did not meet mandatory care-minute targets, despite substantial government funding.\par \par \uc2 Since October last year, residential aged care providers have been required to provide a mandatory average of 200 minutes of direct care to each resident a day, including 40 minutes from a registered nurse and are funded as such.\par \par \uc2 But the Ageing Research Collaborative found that 63.9% of residential aged care centres turning a profit were doing so by essentially pocketing the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s direct care subsidy, instead of providing enough staff to meet care requirements.\par \par \uc2 Fiona York, executive officer of Housing for the Aged Action Group, said:\par \par \uc2 Profit making businesses should not be receiving taxpayer dollars to provide aged care services. We know that when there is a profit motive, older people who are the most vulnerable will miss out. This includes financially disadvantaged older people on low incomes and with few assets, older people who have experienced homelessness, older people living in regional and remote areas, culturally and linguistically diverse older people, LGBTIQA+ older people and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander elders.\par \par \uc2 Older people deserve equal access to quality care and government needs to ensure that aged care funding is being spent in the places that need it the most. Enforcement of quality standards and minimum care requirements, and continuing to improve pay and conditions for the aged care workforce would go some way to improving the lives of residents.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/jul/17/aged-care-homes-accused-of-short-changing-older-australians-federal-government-funding"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Aged care homes accused of \u8216\'20\'18short-changing\u8217\'20\'19 Australians as nearly two-thirds fail to meet care-minute targets}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.56am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.36am BST\par \par \uc2 Bowen says renewables investors have raised concerns with him about lack of bipartisan support for 2030 climate target\par \par \uc2 Taking a final question, Chris Bowen is asked whether the renewables rollout has already been \u8220\'20\'1Cmaterially harmed\u8221\'20\'1D because there\u8217\'20\'19s no longer bipartisan support for the 2030 climate target?\par \par \uc2 He responded:\par \par \uc2 It has been raised with me as a concern by renewable energy investors, yes. I\u8217\'20\'19m not aware of any particular decision that has been taken to say \u8216\'20\'18we were going to invest here in renewable energy and now we\u8217\'20\'19re not\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m not aware of any decision that\u8217\'20\'19s been taken on the basis of the uncertainty caused by nuclear power, but has it been raised with me as a concern by renewable energy investors? Have they paid attention and are they worried about it? Yes.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.40am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.32am BST\par \par \uc2 Moving away from energy policy for a moment: A reporter asks Chris Bowen about his electorate of McMahon, after reports indicated the Muslim community is organising candidates to run at the next election.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/jul/02/muslim-vote-political-movement-candidates-labor-federal-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Muslim Vote group says it will target Labor ministers and whip at next federal election}}}\par \par \uc2 As for how this shapes the government\u8217\'20\'19s foreign policy, Bowen responded:\par \par \uc2 We make our foreign policy choices and decisions based on the evidence and good policy, not on electoral politics.\par \par \uc2 It sometimes annoys me to see newspaper articles saying this MP has argued for this, and here\u8217\'20\'19s the percentage of Muslim voters in their electorate, they must by definition have a view about Palestine that\u8217\'20\'19s driven by electoral purposes and not good policy reasons.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll continue to make our foreign policy choices like we have \u8211\'20\'13 like the vote in the \uc2 UN\uc2 , which gave greater rights to Palestine \u8211\'20\'13 we\u8217\'20\'19ll continue to do that thorough cabinet processes and a discussion of the policy merits.\par \par \uc2 As for his electorate of McMahon, Bowen said \u8220\'20\'1Cwe take no vote for granted [and] no seat for granted\u8221\'20\'1D, and that he works closely with the independent mayor.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.53am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.26am BST\par \par \uc2 Outright climate change denial replaced with \u8216\'20\'18all-too-hard-ism\u8217\'20\'19, Bowen says\par \par \uc2 Chris Bowen argued that climate change denial is a minority and \u8220\'20\'1Cthe science of climate change is now as certain as the science that tobacco causes cancer\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Full on, open climate change denial is now less fashionable than it was 10 or 15 years ago in the public debate. It used to be quite common. Now it\u8217\'20\'19s not so common. Andrew Bolt still does it, but most other people don\u8217\'20\'19t bother with it.\par \par \uc2 What it had been replaced with, is what I call all-too-hard-ism [or] climate in-activism.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s all too had. Renewables, it\u8217\'20\'19s all too hard. Building the system is too hard. Not saying we don\u8217\'20\'19t need to do it, but it\u8217\'20\'19s all too hard. That\u8217\'20\'19s more dangerous in the public debate than full on, straight out climate change denial, which you see less and less of.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.22am BST\par \par \uc2 Bowen says \u8216\'20\'18conspiracy theory rabbit hole\u8217\'20\'19 rhetoric needs to be called out\par \par \uc2 A reporter asks what can be done to \u8220\'20\'1Cshepherd a more constructive conversation\u8221\'20\'1D around Australia\u8217\'20\'19s energy future, when \u8220\'20\'1Cmany Australians may listen to this debate and feel cynical about the tone it takes on.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Chris Bowen said it\u8217\'20\'19s not the role of bureaucracy or the department to participate in public debate, but to advise the government.\par \par \uc2 He said that polarisation is a \u8220\'20\'1Csymptom of a broader problem\u8221\'20\'1D and climate change is \u8220\'20\'1Cthe pointy end of that problem\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said that people\u8217\'20\'19s motives are \u8220\'20\'1Cincreasingly\u8221\'20\'1D being questioned, particularly on social media, which is impacting debate. And this occurs with health and vaccination policy as well as the climate, he said.\par \par \uc2 If you\u8217\'20\'19re pro-renewables, you\u8217\'20\'19re accused of being a traitor because somehow it\u8217\'20\'19s called up in a conspiracy about China. Or you\u8217\'20\'19re accused of participating in a climate hoax, which is somehow a conspiracy by scientists to keep getting funding\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Conspiracy theory rabbit hole develops from climate change to anti-vaxing, to, you know, foreign affairs quite easily. And as I said, there\u8217\'20\'19s too much of it allowed and perpetrated in Parliament House in my very strong view. And having climate denial and anti-vaxxer sentiment expressed regularly by a minority \u8230\'20\'26 is not in my view, okay without it being called out.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.15am BST\par \par \uc2 Bowen takes questions about compulsory acquisition of land to rollout transmission links\par \par \uc2 Chris Bowen mentions the rollout of transmission lines for HumeLink \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.transgrid.com.au/projects-innovation/humelink"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a 360km connection }}}\uc2  between the planned Snowy 2.0 expansion in the Mount Kosciuszko national park to Bannaby, north-west of Goulburn.\par \par \uc2 He said \uc2 TransGrid\uc2  has reached agreement with 74% of landholders:\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not 100, but it\u8217\'20\'19s not zero, and it\u8217\'20\'19s good progress [that\u8217\'20\'19s growing] all the time \u8230\'20\'26 I work with all of the states and with the companies to get the job done. Decisions about compulsory acquisition are not ones at the federal government level. Inevitably, you try very hard, the companies try very hard to get as much agreement as possible from land holders before that is contemplated.\par \par \uc2 He later added: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m far from complacent, but I\u8217\'20\'19m relatively satisfied with where we\u8217\'20\'19re at.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some landholders, who were first informed about the project months after the black summer bushfires, have been advocating for the powerlines to go underground.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/aug/23/renewable-energy-new-power-lines-for-transmission-risks-concerns"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australia urgently needs a grid upgrade \u8211\'20\'13 but the march of new power lines faces a bush revolt}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.16am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.10am BST\par \par \uc2 Bowen asked about importance of gas in potential second-term Labor government\par \par \uc2 This is given that the government labelled gas as a key energy source \u8220\'20\'1Cto 2050 and beyond\u8221\'20\'1D, while it is not encouraged under the proposed greens ratings system and excluded from the capacity investment scheme.\par \par \uc2 Chris Bowen responded:\par \par \uc2 I don\u8217\'20\'19t agree with the premise about mixed signals. But we are the sensible people, pragmatic people when it comes to gas.\par \par \uc2 You\u8217\'20\'19ve got the Liberals who had a gas recovery, which was a gas bin fire. You\u8217\'20\'19ve got the Greens who say gas has no role to play in the system going forward. I disagree with both of those and the government disagrees with both of them.\par \par \uc2 Bowen argued gas would play \u8220\'20\'1Can important role\u8221\'20\'1D through firming renewables, and for industry. He later added:\par \par \uc2 By definition, when you turn it on, it\u8217\'20\'19s very profitable, but it can\u8217\'20\'19t compete in terms of cost to build with renewables \u8211\'20\'13 hence the capacity investment scheme is not the right way to deal with gas.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.13am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.05am BST\par \par \uc2 Chris Bowen ended his speech with an election appeal, noting a \u8220\'20\'1Cchoice\u8221\'20\'1D between renewables and nuclear and urging people to \u8220\'20\'1Cstay the course\u8221\'20\'1D with renewables, and Labor:\par \par \uc2 The choice for affected communities will be stark. The choice for Australia is, and will, be stark. We can have a debate on this by all means, but a mature debate requires facts and the Liberals and Nationals have provided pitifully few so far. The government will be staying the course and making the case to the Australian people that we should stay the course, as well.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.00am BST\par \par \uc2 Chris Bowen is arguing that the opposition\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cideological pursuit of nuclear reactors in two decades\u8217\'20\'19 time\u8221\'20\'1D would \u8220\'20\'1Cwreck the renewables rollout today\u8221\'20\'1D. He told the press club:\par \par \uc2 Australia will be trading urgently needed renewable investment for the hope of more costly reactors in two decades\u8217\'20\'19 time \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Baseload nuclear power plants simply don\u8217\'20\'19t stack up economically in a grid with very significant renewable penetration. Is the coalition\u8217\'20\'19s policy to curtail the zero-cost renewable energy to make room for expensive energy when renewables drive down the wholesale prices to very low levels? Some Queensland LNP members say it is.\par \par \uc2 Is it their plan to bankroll the plans into the system to bleed money at taxpayers\u8217\'20\'19 expense? Either one of these is a recipe for Australians to pay more.\par \par \uc2 For these reasons, Australians can choose reliable renewables or risky reactors, but not both.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.55am BST\par \par \uc2 Chris Bowen outlined a number of visits he has made to communities where the opposition has proposed nuclear reactors to be built.\par \par \uc2 He said a message he often heard was that \u8220\'20\'1Cthey need jobs and investment now. Not in 2035, not in 2037 - now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Now, these communities had other very legitimate questions, like how much water will be used in these nuclear plants? And where will the water come from? Will it be at the expense of farmers? All fair questions, well asked. It\u8217\'20\'19s [shadow climate change and energy minister Ted] O\u8217\'20\'19Brien \u8217\'20\'19s place to answer those questions, not mine.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.53am BST\par \par \uc2 Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s nuclear policy a \u8216\'20\'18triumph of dogma over data\u8217\'20\'19: Bowen\par \par \uc2 Chris Bowen speech continues at the national press club.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a front in the post-truth populist culture war, not a serious plan for Australia.\par \par \uc2 A plan that\u8217\'20\'19s less about the future jobs for Australians, and more about keeping \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  in his job. The coalition dress up their ideological anti-renewables obsession as a pro-nuclear plan, but it comes down to a desire to do absolutely nothing. To do nothing, because they don\u8217\'20\'19t think there\u8217\'20\'19s a need to act.\par \par \uc2 If [Dutton] believes this was the critical decade, they wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t serve up policies that only guarantee inaction. It\u8217\'20\'19s obvious this isn\u8217\'20\'19t a serious plan by the way they\u8217\'20\'19re going about it.\par \par \uc2 Chris Bowen said the opposition\u8217\'20\'19s announcement of seven locations for nuclear reactors has \u8220\'20\'1Craised more questions than it answered\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Five of them are in states with nuclear prohibition and six are on sites where the owners have said they don\u8217\'20\'19t want anything to do with nuclear power.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.56am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.49am BST\par \par \uc2 Bowen also outlined the government\u8217\'20\'19s plans for future transmission lines:\par \par \uc2 You may have heard certain politicians say that we have a policy of building 28,000km of transition lines. It isn\u8217\'20\'19t true \u8211\'20\'13 never has been.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re supporting the building of 4,000km of transmission lines over the next decade, plus the upgrading of another 1,000km.\par \par \uc2 Of the 4,000km actually required, 900km are complete, or well under physical construction, while the others are at various stages of advanced planning and approval.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.27am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.47am BST\par \par \uc2 Chris Bowen said that during his travels throughout regional and rural Australia, he understands the energy transition can be \u8220\'20\'1Cconfronting\u8221\'20\'1D for some.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19ve met with passionate renewable energy advocates in the regions who have demanded more action faster [and] more renewables.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19ve met with people who are happy to see renewables, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/aug/23/renewable-energy-new-power-lines-for-transmission-risks-concerns"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 want to see better consultation and better community benefits}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, of course, I\u8217\'20\'19ve heard from some who oppose the renewable rollout all together \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s far from a majority in any place I\u8217\'20\'19ve visited, far from a majority, but it is a contested space.\par \par \uc2 He said the government has set expectations for \u8220\'20\'1Ceffective and responsible\u8221\'20\'1D engagement by transmission companies to improve the outcomes for local communities.\par \par \uc2 To read more about the renewable energy resistance in some parts of regional Australia, you can read this fantastic feature from Aston Brown below:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/jul/07/renewable-energy-australia-rural-resistance-katy-mccallum"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18There\u8217\'20\'19s angry people out there\u8217\'20\'19: inside the renewable energy resistance in regional Australia}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.52am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.39am BST\par \par \uc2 Back to the \uc2 National Press Club\uc2 , where energy and climate change minister Chris Bowen has been speaking.\par \par \uc2 He said that 2050 isn\u8217\'20\'19t the start date for action, but the \u8220\'20\'1Cdeadline for delivery,\u8221\'20\'1D and told the crowd:\par \par \uc2 Some people say they\u8217\'20\'19re committed to net zero by 2050 as if saying it is enough, and they think that they can delay action to the late 2030s, or indeed, to the 2040s. This is a fundamental misunderstanding of the task before us and of the opportunities before our country.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.36am BST\par \par \uc2 Top energy regulator doubts nuclear energy possible in Australia by 2050\par \par \uc2 The Australian Clean Energy Summit 2024 in Sydney doesn\u8217\'20\'19t include many supporters among the attenders for nuclear energy being built in the country.\par \par \uc2 One senior supplier privately said the federal opposition\u8217\'20\'19s pledge to build seven nuclear energy plants were \u8220\'20\'1Ca distraction\u8221\'20\'1D, while one state official described the move as \u8220\'20\'1Cchilling\u8221\'20\'1D for renewable energy investor interest.\par \par \uc2 That didn\u8217\'20\'19t stop federal opposition energy spokesperson Ted O\u8217\'20\'19Brien giving his stump speech (with no Q&A), blaming the government for an \u8220\'20\'1Call eggs in one basket approach\u8221\'20\'1D with renewables, and the need for a more \u8220\'20\'1Cbalanced\u8221\'20\'1D approach that included nuclear energy. (He received polite applause, only.)\par \par \uc2 It was a pity O\u8217\'20\'19Brien was caught in traffic and missed an earlier session that included Clare Savage, the head of the \uc2 Australian Energy Regulator\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Savage said Australia was \u8220\'20\'1Cprobably a decade too late\u8221\'20\'1D to go down to the nuclear track. As a regulator, she estimated that it would take at least two years to remove the legal impediments and introduce supporting legislation at state and federal level (assuming that was possible).\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBy the time you would have to try and build out the safety framework, the environmental framework, the market operations, the underwriting, I reckon that\u8217\'20\'19d take at least \u8230\'20\'26another eight years,\u8221\'20\'1D Savage said. Add another couple of years before any sod could be turned it would then need 15 years to build a reactor.\par \par \uc2 So that\u8217\'20\'19s 25 years from now, so you\u8217\'20\'19re probably at 2050.\par \par \uc2 Bearing in mind coal plants will probably all be gone by 2040 \u8211\'20\'13 assuming their operations can be extended even that long \u8211\'20\'13 we\u8217\'20\'19d have a decade gap to fill. Not very promising.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.41am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.33am BST\par \par \uc2 The minister for climate change and energy is beginning his address at the \uc2 National Press Club\par \par \uc2 Josh Butler gave us a preview of what Chris Bowen will say, which you can read about earlier in the blog {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/jul/17/australia-news-live-minns-nsw-labor-cfmeu-construction-arm-victoria-wong-mh17-memorial?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-6696e5bb8f089592211e5031#block-6696e5bb8f089592211e5031"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/jul/17/australia-news-live-minns-nsw-labor-cfmeu-construction-arm-victoria-wong-mh17-memorial?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-6696e6408f08e299b19932ad#block-6696e6408f08e299b19932ad"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll bring you the highlights from his speech, and questions from reporters.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.41am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.30am BST\par \par \uc2 Safety not valued at Queensland power plant before blast, report finds\par \par \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports that almost half a million households were left without power after pieces of metal weighing almost half a tonne blasted out of a turbine in the May 2021 incident at central Queensland\u8217\'20\'19s Callide power station.\par \par \uc2 Three years after the \u8220\'20\'1Ccatastrophic missile event\u8221\'20\'1D near Biloela, forensic engineer Sean Brady\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.csenergy.com.au/news/release-of-brady-heywood-report-and-c3-cooling-tower-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 final report has been released}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It found state-owned operator \uc2 CS Energy\uc2  did not value safety or implement suitable welfare processes before the explosion.\par \par \uc2 Staff had raised concerns years before the explosion that Callide was an unsafe work environment and the lack of maintenance over the years could lead to an incident, Dr Brady found.\par \par \uc2 CS Energy\uc2  chair Adam Aspinall on Wednesday said it was an unacceptable outcome at Callide that had damaged trust with the community and stakeholders.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI apologise to our employees, the government and the people of Queensland for \uc2 CS Energy\uc2  not having maintained the world-class performance they expect,\u8221\'20\'1D he told reporters.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.39am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.21am BST\par \par \uc2 This is worth a look over your lunch break:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/news/ng-interactive/2024/jul/17/quiz-whats-getting-better-for-australians-today-and-whats-getting-worse"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Quiz: what\u8217\'20\'19s getting better for Australians today, and what\u8217\'20\'19s getting worse?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.15am BST\par \par \uc2 MBA welcomes \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  action, but calls for return of regulator\par \par \uc2 Master Builders Australia\uc2  has welcomed the announcement of an independent administrator for the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2 , but said it was only a first step in \u8220\'20\'1Cstamping out the toxic and ingrained culture \u8230\'20\'26 of bullying, thuggery, and complete disregard for the law\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the body said that the announcement was a significant move in addressing a range of serious allegations against the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It also called for the \u8220\'20\'1Creinstatement\u8221\'20\'1D of a \u8220\'20\'1Cstrong\u8221\'20\'1D building regulator. The Albanese government abolished the \uc2 Australian Building and Construction Commission\uc2  last year.\par \par \uc2 The MBA said in a statement:\par \par \uc2 This culture has existed for decades and has stifled productivity and increased the cost of construction at the expense of the community, taxpayers, jobs and small business.\par \par \uc2 All levers need to be pulled to ensure these investigations are well-resourced, thorough and have the full powers available to them.\par \par \uc2 It has been extensively documented in four royal commissions, dozens of inquiries and reviews, and hundreds of court judgments. The time for talking is over \u8211\'20\'13 what we need now is real action to fix this problem once and for all.\par \par \uc2 More action will still be needed to hold recidivist officials accountable, and a strong building regulator must be reinstated.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.20am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.00am BST\par \par \uc2 ACTU\uc2  secretary \uc2 Sally McManus\uc2 , president Michele O\u8217\'20\'19Neil and members of the \uc2 ACTU\uc2  executive will address the media at 2.30pm this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.53am BST\par \par \uc2 Government doesn\u8217\'20\'19t want to punish workers by stopping projects, says PM\par \par \uc2 Overwhelmingly \u8211\'20\'13 let\u8217\'20\'19s be very clear \u8211\'20\'13 overwhelmingly, people who work in the construction sector go to work, they work hard, it\u8217\'20\'19s dangerous work and they earn for their family and they make a contribution to building and construction, whether it be large homes or whether it be civil construction, railway lines or roads. We don\u8217\'20\'19t want that to stop.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.19am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.51am BST\par \par \uc2 PM says \u8216\'20\'18nothing off the table\u8217\'20\'19 over \uc2 CFMEU\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese has also been speaking to the media from Brisbane, and was asked whether deregistering the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  should be taken off the table.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister said that \u8220\'20\'1Cnothing will be taken off the table but we want action\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Notice that the Master Builders Association and the Constructors Association have all welcomed the actions that the government has taken as well.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s important that the action be effective, and some of the things that have been proposed haven\u8217\'20\'19t been effective \u8230\'20\'26 You can spend a lot of time having talks, what we\u8217\'20\'19ve done is have action. And today\u8217\'20\'19s announcement is decisive. It\u8217\'20\'19s clear and it will make a difference.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.55am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.48am BST\par \par \uc2 Dutton backs continuing Ukraine support\par \par \uc2 Moving to international politics, Dutton was asked about previous comments made by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s running mate, JD Vance, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jul/15/jd-vance-trump-pick-explained#:~:text=He%20will%20support%20Trump's%20nationalist%20foreign%20policy&text=In%20an%20interview%20on%20Steve,one%20way%20or%20the%20other.%E2%80%9D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opposing aid to Ukraine}}}\uc2 , and whether this worried him.\par \par \uc2 Dutton responded:\par \par \uc2 I congratulate JD Vance on his appointment as the running mate to President Trump and obviously, as has been the case over decades \u8211\'20\'13 regardless of whether it\u8217\'20\'19s a Democrat or Republican administration, regardless of whether at this end it\u8217\'20\'19s a Labor or Liberal administration, we work closely with our counterparts in the United States and will continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 We believe very strongly that support should be provided to Ukraine because innocent men, women and children are being slaughtered by President Putin and the world shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t take its eyes away from what\u8217\'20\'19s happening in Ukraine just because time has elapsed and the media cycle moves on.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.04am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.46am BST\par \par \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  argued that NSW premier \uc2 Chris Minns\uc2  and South Australian premier \uc2 Peter Malinauskas\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cshow the leadership that the prime minister can\u8217\'20\'19t demonstrate\u8221\'20\'1D by returning donations from the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The fact is that Labor party has taken over $10m worth of donations from the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  during the prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s time as Labor leader \u8230\'20\'26 they should stop receiving any donations and the money that they have received should be quarantined.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.44am BST\par \par \uc2 Dutton says \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  allegations \u8216\'20\'18only scratching the surface\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  is speaking to the media from Brisbane about the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  allegations.\par \par \uc2 The opposition leader claimed that \u8220\'20\'1Cwe\u8217\'20\'19re only just scratching the surface\u8221\'20\'1D of alleged misconduct and, in his view, \u8220\'20\'1Cthis will turn out to be the biggest defrauding of the Australian taxpayer in our country\u8217\'20\'19s history\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 I don\u8217\'20\'19t believe that the prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s response by announcing an administrator will be appointed and somehow that will be adequate to deal with the issues before us. The weakest possible response from Anthony Albanese would be to appoint an administrator to the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2 \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 If the prime minister had any backbone whatsoever, he would deregister the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Workplace relations minister Tony Burke told a press conference just moments ago why the government was not considering deregistration ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/jul/17/australia-news-live-minns-nsw-labor-cfmeu-construction-arm-victoria-wong-mh17-memorial?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-66971cb28f089592211e5187#block-66971cb28f089592211e5187"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 see our earlier post}}}\uc2  ).\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.00am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.33am BST\par \par \uc2 Q : You\u8217\'20\'19ve spoke when about inadequacies of the [\uc2 Australian Building and Construction Commission\uc2 ]. What about the Registered Organisations Commission? Is there a role for restoring that, or a version of that, so it can better police this behaviour?\par \par \uc2 Tony Burke responded:\par \par \uc2 The power of the Registered Organisations Commission are the powers that were transferred to the general manager of the \uc2 Fair Work Commission\uc2 , who I\u8217\'20\'19ve just said I have full confidence in \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 If it ends up being that the legislation \u8211\'20\'13 and this part of it was not changed when we made that shift \u8211\'20\'13 but if the legislation does not create a clear enough path to putting administrators in different roles, and the attempts from the regulator are challenged, then when parliament returns we\u8217\'20\'19ll legislate.\par \par \uc2 The press conference has now wrapped up.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.40am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.30am BST\par \par \uc2 Tony Burke said what the government had been discussing with the administrator \u8220\'20\'1Cis not limited to Victoria\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But it will be the decision of the regulator to work out where it goes.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.42am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.28am BST\par \par \uc2 The workplace minister was asked if he would move to close this loophole (as mentioned in our last post).\par \par \uc2 Tony Burke responded:\par \par \uc2 I personally have a view in favour of registered organisations having the significant role. The reality is at the moment there is no pathway through the parliament on changing that.\par \par \uc2 There are some organisations that are not registered that traditionally the Coalition have been close to. There are some organisations that are not registered that the Greens party has been quite close to, and I don\u8217\'20\'19t believe that pathway is open.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.38am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.25am BST\par \par \uc2 Burke has \u8216\'20\'18no intention\u8217\'20\'19 of pursuing \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  deregistration\par \par \uc2 Tony Burke was asked about deregistration, and said that non-registered organisations could still act as bargaining agents.\par \par \uc2 Because you don\u8217\'20\'19t have to be a registered organisation to be a bargaining agent. If we simply went down the deregistration path, we would have an organisation still capable of bargaining and doing the entire business model that we\u8217\'20\'19ve been seen reported in recent days, with no layer of regulation or additional oversight that applies to registered organisations.\par \par \uc2 It would be a gift to the worst elements and I have no intention of going down that path.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.52am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.21am BST\par \par \uc2 Tony Burke said he had requested the \uc2 Fair Work Ombudsman\uc2  to undertake a targeted review of all enterprise agreements made by the Victorian branch of the construction division of the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  which applied to Victorian big-build projects.\par \par \uc2 But he said the government had \u8220\'20\'1Cno intention\u8221\'20\'1D of taking any action that would risk the terms and conditions of employment of workers covered by those agreements.\par \par \uc2 This is not their fault.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.43am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.18am BST\par \par \uc2 Tony Burke announces groundwork for independent administrator of \uc2 CFMEU\par \par \uc2 Workplace relations minister Tony Burke has empowered the \uc2 Fair Work Commission\uc2  general manager, Murray Furlong, to appoint an independent administrator to branches of the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  in response to allegations of organised crime links with the union.\par \par \uc2 The general manager of the \uc2 Fair Work Commission\uc2  is the best person placed to take this action\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Any action that the general manager takes \u8211\'20\'13 with respect to any part of the construction division of the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 I, as minister, will intervene in support of that action. I do believe these actions are best taken by the regulator, but any action to appoint an administrator will be supported by me as minister.\par \par \uc2 Burke said if there was a need for further legislation to allow Furlong to take action, then that would be the priority of the government when parliament returned on 12 August.\par \par \uc2 Burke has also asked for the AFP to investigate any allegations of criminality along with state police.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.18am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.09am BST\par \par \uc2 Asked if the price tag for the project is still under a billion dollars, despite inflationary pressures and materials and worker shortages, Allan said:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m not wanting to cut across the final contract negotiations, but we absolutely anticipate that the funding that has been provided will deliver the scope of the project that we have announced. And it is a big scope of a new hospital, with at least 24/7 beds and all of the other services that the minister and I have talked about this morning. We will work with our construction partners to ensure that it\u8217\'20\'19s delivered within that budget.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.08am BST\par \par \uc2 Jacinta Allan\uc2  told reporters:\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s exciting about being able to make this announcement today is major works will start later this year, and it means the hospital can be completed in 2029... significant early works [have] already been under way on that site.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a Greenfield site, which provides a wonderful opportunity to get in there and build the hospital as quickly as possible. But in recent months, utilities have needed to be connected to the site, construction of linking roads to enable movement of people and ambulances and staff around the site, and also most importantly, provide that important access to the local community.\par \par \uc2 And that\u8217\'20\'19s why this hospital is in exactly the right location, because it\u8217\'20\'19s just down the road from the brand new Cobblebank station that [Melton MP] Steve McGhie and I opened in 2019. It\u8217\'20\'19s a big, new modern hospital located in a growing community that already has good, strong transport access.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.16am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.07am BST\par \par \uc2 Victoria starts work on $900m Melton hospital\par \par \uc2 The Victorian government has finally begun works on the long-awaited Melton hospital, with the premier, \uc2 Jacinta Allan\uc2 , today naming the preferred consortium to deliver the landmark project.\par \par \uc2 Speaking earlier this morning, Allan and the health minister, Mary-Anne Thomas, announced exclusive negotiations are set to begin with Exemplar Health consortium to build the $900m hospital, which Allan says remains on track for completion in 2029.\par \par \uc2 The consortium includes Capella Capital, Lendlease, \uc2 Honeywell\uc2  and \uc2 Compass Group\uc2 . The group will design, construct, finance and maintain the new facility in Cobblebank for 25 years.\par \par \uc2 Labor first promised to build a public hospital in Melton before the 2018 election and the lack of progress on the site was a key issue at the following election four years later.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.10am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.01am BST\par \par \uc2 Free Sydney concert to press federal government over climate change\par \par \uc2 A free, all-ages concert will be held in Sydney to call on the federal government to take responsibility to future generations and fight for intergenerational inequity.\par \par \uc2 The \u8220\'20\'1COur Future, Your Duty\u8221\'20\'1D concert \u8211\'20\'13 led by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/mar/16/in-the-sharma-decision-the-federal-court-says-we-see-the-climate-risk-but-cannot-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 climate activist }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/mar/16/in-the-sharma-decision-the-federal-court-says-we-see-the-climate-risk-but-cannot-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Anjali Sharma}}}\uc2  , independent North Sydney MP Kylea Tink and North Sydney council \u8211\'20\'13 will be held at St Leonards Park on Sunday 28 July from 2-5pm.\par \par \uc2 The line-up of artists includes Ruby Jackson, Astrovan and R\u248\'00\'F8semary, as well as Sweet Baby Darlings, who each either lived or attended a school in North Sydney.\par \par \uc2 Sharma, who is also a part of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/jul/31/pocock-seeks-to-impose-duty-of-care-on-australian-government-over-climate-harm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Duty of Care campaign}}}\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a known fact that climate change will disproportionately impact the lives of current and future generations, and our politicians are making decisions right now that will intrinsically shape what our futures will look like. It is only logical that this give rise to a duty that our elected representatives only exercise their powers in a way that will not have detrimental impacts on our health and wellbeing.\par \par \uc2 Tink said:\par \par \uc2 If a government doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have a positive duty of care to ensure the decisions it makes don\u8217\'20\'19t negatively impact future generations, what is its job? Yet two consecutive federal governments have now rejected the idea. We must demand better.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.08am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.55am BST\par \par \uc2 The Star\u8217\'20\'19s pokies on again after shutdown\par \par \uc2 The Star\uc2  has turned its pokies machines back on after a system error earlier this week.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/jul/15/australia-news-live-brittany-higgins-linda-reynolds-royal-visit-king-queen-energy-cost-of-living-housing-foreign-policy?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-6694891d8f08d1ccb5785572#block-6694891d8f08d1ccb5785572"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 On Monday}}}\uc2 , Star Entertainment said it had closed down all of its pokies machines from 10pm on 13 July, after a system error occurred while preparing to go cashless.\par \par \uc2 A statement today said the system issues had been rectified and machines were operational in each of the Star\u8217\'20\'19s properties.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.01am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.48am BST\par \par \uc2 CS Energy\uc2  apologises over Queensland power-plant explosion\par \par \uc2 The chair of \uc2 CS Energy\uc2 , Adam Aspinall, has apologised for the \u8220\'20\'1Ccatastrophic\u8221\'20\'1D engineering failure that wrecked a generator at the Callide coal-fired power station.\par \par \uc2 The government-owned enterprise released two reports today \u8211\'20\'13 one into the 2021 failure and another into a separate engineering failure, which caused the partial collapse of a cooling tower in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Aspinall said:\par \par \uc2 As the current chair of \uc2 CS Energy\uc2 , I apologise to our employees, the government and the people of Queensland for \uc2 CS Energy\uc2  not having maintained the world-class performance which they have rightly come to expect from their government-owned assets.\par \par \uc2 Aspinall and CEO \uc2 Darren Busine\uc2  did not take questions from the media after reading a prepared statement to cameras today, on the grounds that there was legal action under way.\par \par \uc2 Busine repeated that \u8220\'20\'1Ca lack of maintenance did not cause the incident\u8221\'20\'1D but conceded that the report found that \u8220\'20\'1Cmore effective process safety practices could have assisted \uc2 CS Energy\uc2  in identifying and managing the risks\u8221\'20\'1D involved in the installation of a new battery charger.\par \par \uc2 Aspinall said the operator had provided \u8220\'20\'1Cambiguous\u8221\'20\'1D advice to energy minister \uc2 Mick de Brenni\uc2 . The C4 generator wrecked in 2021 has yet to return to service.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.55am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.39am BST\par \par \uc2 Australian federal police commissioner \uc2 Reece Kershaw\uc2  recognised and paid tribute to the 500-plus first responders and officers who helped investigate and respond to the MH17 downing.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at the memorial service in Canberra, Kershaw said that when tragedy occurred, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe hold on to memories to sustain us and turn to those whose job it is to pursue justice\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 For those on the ground, strength was drawn from the blue patch on their shoulders, the insignia of the AFP. Our mission was clear: do everything possible to return loved ones, find the truth, seek justice and hold those responsible to account\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 I pay tribute to our shared mission with international partners, so critical to this case. I acknowledge those who still pursue all avenues to hold Russia to account.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.42am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.34am BST\par \par \uc2 Julie Bishop concluded her speech with the following remarks:\par \par \uc2 It is shocking to think that on that day, ten years ago, Australia found itself at the fulcrum of the Russian-Ukraine conflict that continues to this day [with] such shocking consequences.\par \par \uc2 So today, we remember those we lost and our thoughts are with those who loved and were loved by them.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.39am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.32am BST\par \par \uc2 Julie Bishop reflects on time as foreign minister when MH17 went down\par \par \uc2 Julie Bishop, who was foreign minister at the time of the MH17 downing, is now speaking at the memorial service for victims.\par \par \uc2 Bishop said she would \u8220\'20\'1Cnever forget\u8221\'20\'1D the phone call she received early on the Friday morning advising her of an \u8220\'20\'1Cincomprehensible situation that was unfolding\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19ll never forget the early phone calls with the family representatives, whose grief was inconsolable. Our government\u8217\'20\'19s solemn commitment that we would do everything possible to bring them home \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Without any diplomatic presence in Ukraine, I\u8217\'20\'19ll never forget how we deployed over 500 Australian personnel to a temporary embassy that became a nerve centre for our operations, which became an increasingly complex and dangerous mission.\par \par \uc2 Having to convince president [Petro] Poroshenko and various Ukrainian political parties that we had to recall the Ukrainian parliament that was on summer break in order to pass a resolution to enable us to enter the crash site, and to their everlasting credit, over 500 members returned to parliament\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Confronting president [Vladimir] Putin and demanding that Russia take some accountability for this, and that they\u8217\'20\'19d cooperate with the investigation rather than obfuscation and evasion.\par \par \uc2 Bishop also recognised the \u8220\'20\'1Cpainstaking\u8221\'20\'1D efforts of the joint investigation team. She is wearing an order of merit awarded to her by the Dutch government, which she wears \u8220\'20\'1Cin tribute to those who lost their lives\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.40am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.23am BST\par \par \uc2 Foreign minister \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2  was next to address the memorial service to the MH17 victims in Canberra. She told the family members and loved ones in the room:\par \par \uc2 So I say to you today \u8211\'20\'13 you have lost so much, but you are not alone in your loss. We are with you, and your grief steels us in the fight for justice, as it has steeled for Australia that day. As it steeled our diplomats, led by Julie Bishop, to achieve what first seemed impossible \u8211\'20\'13 a unanimous \uc2 UN\uc2  security council resolution condemning the attack, [which] gave international backing for an independent investigation [that] carried without Russian veto.\par \par \uc2 And your grief has steeled the hundreds of Australian federal police, defence force and \uc2 Australian Transport Safety Bureau\uc2  personnel, whose thorough and painstaking work supported that independent investigation through the MH17 independent joint investigation team. And together these efforts, on behalf of those we lost, on your loss, and on behalf of the country, have seen convictions laid against perpetrators.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.26am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.19am BST\par \par \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  furore sparks Indigenous commission investigation\par \par \uc2 Victoria\u8217\'20\'19s truth-telling commission will inquire into allegations Indigenous workers have not been given a fair go on government construction worksites following the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  scandal, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 The Yoorrook Justice Commission said it was concerned by reports alleging government failures to ensure fairness in the awarding and administration of large contracts in the construction sector.\par \par \uc2 It came after premier \uc2 Jacinta Allan\uc2  acknowledged it took too long to reply to an Indigenous labour-hire firm\u8217\'20\'19s 2022 letter detailing allegations of officials from the Construction, Forestry and Maritime Employees Union (\uc2 CFMEU\uc2 ) threatening violence and banning non-aligned firms from state and federally funded projects.\par \par \uc2 Yoorrook has the powers of a royal commission, meaning it can call witnesses, compel the production of documents and make findings and recommendations. Commissioner and deputy chair Travis Lovett said:\par \par \uc2 Increased participation in the Victorian economy is essential for Victorian First Peoples, if true self-determination is to be achieved.\par \par \uc2 We encourage affected First Nations businesses and individuals to continue to come forward, including on a confidential basis, to contribute to our ongoing inquiries and inform necessary change in the sector.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.28am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.15am BST\par \par \uc2 Governor general Sam Mostyn has been speaking at the memorial service for the MH17 victims. She said:\par \par \uc2 I pay tribute to the Australian police, military, diplomatic and consular personnel who worked so hard then and in the months and years afterwards to support grieving families all over Australia. Your actions testify to the healing power of courage, care and compassion\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 To the families and loved ones of victims, here in this people\u8217\'20\'19s place, your fellow Australians remember and honour the precious ones lost to you now 10 years ago. Those men, women and children are part of you. Each one was cherished for the indelible qualities that defined their efforts \u8230\'20\'26 [That] they died so far away from home is a profound grief and without them here all that is left is to find ways to reflect and remember.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.20am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.11am BST\par \par \uc2 Ted O\u8217\'20\'19Brien misses session at clean energy summit after apparent traffic jam\par \par \uc2 Whether we get any fireworks with federal opposition energy spokesperson Ted O\u8217\'20\'19Brien trying to convince a clean energy summit of the pros of nuclear reactors remains unclear.\par \par \uc2 O\u8217\'20\'19Brien was apparently caught in a traffic jam and missed his 9.50am on the way to the Sydney event at Darling Harbour.\par \par \uc2 An event speaker wryly speculated whether the delay might have been an anti-nuclear protest.\par \par \uc2 A trio of state energy ministers stepped in to fill the void, with their session brought forward.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.19am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.03am BST\par \par \uc2 MH17: memorial service for 10th anniversary of downing begins\par \par \uc2 A memorial service is under way in Canberra for the victims of \uc2 Malaysian Airlines\uc2  flight MH17, on the 10-year anniversary of its downing.\par \par \uc2 Former foreign minister Julie Bishop and Australian federal police commissioner \uc2 Reece Kershaw\uc2  are expected to deliver speeches.\par \par \uc2 Foreign minister \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2 , former prime minister \uc2 Tony Abbott\uc2  and governor general Sam Mostyn can be seen sitting in the front row as the service begins.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.37am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.55am BST\par \par \uc2 NSW opposition calls for royal commission into \uc2 CFMEU\par \par \uc2 The New South Wales opposition has called for a royal commission into the operations of the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Acting opposition leader, \uc2 Damien Tudehope\uc2 , said the premier\u8217\'20\'19s comments had been an \u8220\'20\'1Cinsult to every honest worker in this state\u8221\'20\'1D and accused Labor of being \u8220\'20\'1Cin bed\u8221\'20\'1D with the embattled union. Tudehope said:\par \par \uc2 Now, in the wake of these damning revelations, the premier pretends to take action. This is nothing more than political theatre. The people of NSW deserve a leader who genuinely stands against corruption.\par \par \uc2 He said only a royal commission could properly investigate the claims.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this morning, premier \uc2 Chris Minns\uc2  said he had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/jul/17/australia-news-live-minns-nsw-labor-cfmeu-construction-arm-victoria-wong-mh17-memorial?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-6696e3f78f0838cdbaeab264#block-6696e3f78f0838cdbaeab264"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called on NSW Labor to suspend the CFMEU construction arm}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Minns has been approached for comment.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.04am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.50am BST\par \par \uc2 Man charged after allegedly fleeing police and punching constable in face\par \par \uc2 A man will face court after allegedly fleeing from police after a vehicle stop and punching an officer in the face multiple times.\par \par \uc2 Police said the 25-year-old man was stopped for a random breath test in Broken Hill about 2am on Sunday. The occupant exited the car and police allegedly found drug paraphernalia.\par \par \uc2 As officers attempted to address the driver, he allegedly fled on foot. A pursuit began and the man allegedly stopped and punched a constable multiple times in the face before he kept running, police said.\par \par \uc2 The man was stopped a short time later and allegedly continued to resist. An officer then deployed his taser and the man was arrested.\par \par \uc2 Police allegedly located methylamphetamine and cash during a subsequent search of the man. Police said three constables were injured during his arrest.\par \par \uc2 The man was taken to Broken Hill Hospital for assessment and treatment before being released into police custody and charged with a string of offences, including driving without a licence, escaping police custody and three counts of assaulting an officer in the execution of duty.\par \par \uc2 The South Australian man was refused bail and appeared at Dubbo local court on Sunday, where he was formally refused bail to reappear at Broken Hill local court on 26 July.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.59am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.45am BST\par \par \uc2 Olympic champion slalom canoeist Jess Fox has been chosen as an official torchbearer for the upcoming Paris Olympics torch relay.\par \par \uc2 enltrCongratulations to Jess Fox for being chosen to carry the flame! ??????\par \par \uc2 Jess will be lighting up the rowing and paddle venues this Friday in Paris as an official torchbearer in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Paris2024?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Paris2024}}}\uc2  Olympic Torch Relay. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/AllezAUS?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #AllezAUS}}}\uc2  | {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/jessfoxcanoe?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @jessfoxcanoe}}}\uc2  | {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/paddle_aus?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @paddle_aus}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ZEbJsNhGbY"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/ZEbJsNhGbY}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 AUS Olympic Team (@AUSOlympicTeam) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AUSOlympicTeam/status/1813325260990603300?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 16, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.34am BST\par \par \uc2 Man charged after allegedly shining laser at police helicopter\par \par \uc2 A man has been arrested for allegedly striking a police helicopter with a laser light in Western Australia.\par \par \uc2 Police said that about 12.30am today air wing officers were patrolling the Rockingham area when their helicopter was allegedly struck by a laser light. This was tracked to the rear yard of a residence in Warnbro.\par \par \uc2 Police allege a laser light was located at the residence. A 44-year-old man was arrested without incident, charged by summons for causing fear or alarm with a laser to people in conveyances or others. He will be required to appear in court at a later date.\par \par \uc2 A police spokesperson said pointing laser lights at aircraft was a serious offence:\par \par \uc2 Pointing a laser light at any aircraft is a highly dangerous act and requires the pilot of the aircraft to take evasive action to keep them and their crew safe.\par \par \uc2 Laser lights not only light up the cabin of a plane or helicopter, but can temporarily blind the pilot, which puts everyone onboard in real danger.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.38am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.24am BST\par \par \uc2 NSW firefighters urge heater safety amid cold snap\par \par \uc2 People are being urged not to take any risks to stay warm during the cold snap gripping the east coast at the moment.\par \par \uc2 Fire and Rescue NSW\uc2  says heaters should be kept at least a metre from any materials and furniture in the home to prevent fires, and should ideally be plugged into their own sockets to prevent power board overloads.\par \par \uc2 Residents should inspect any heaters removed from storage, FRNSW urged, to ensure they were in good working order. And \u8220\'20\'1Cunder no circumstances\u8221\'20\'1D should outdoor barbecues and heaters be brought inside homes to provide heat \u8211\'20\'13 as they generate carbon monoxide and could render people unconscious, and cause death.\par \par \uc2 FRNSW\u8217\'20\'19s deputy commissioner of field operations, Paul McGuiggan, said that last time the state experienced a bitter winter cold snap in 2022 there were a record 17 deaths in residential fires.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s crucial that every home has at least one working smoke alarm protecting you and your loved ones.\par \par \uc2 Half the homes that experienced a fire over the last two winters either didn\u8217\'20\'19t have a smoke alarm or those present weren\u8217\'20\'19t working. That\u8217\'20\'19s a recipe for disaster because a smoke alarm may well be the only thing that saves you if a fire breaks out.\par \par \uc2 You can\u8217\'20\'19t smell when you\u8217\'20\'19re asleep so a sounding alarm may be your only hope.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.28am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.14am BST\par \par \uc2 Woman arrested after body discovered in Melbourne river\par \par \uc2 A woman has been charged with murder after a woman\u8217\'20\'19s body was discovered in Victoria\u8217\'20\'19s Maribyrnong River on Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 The woman\u8217\'20\'19s body was located by a passerby in an area of the river near Smithfield Road, Flemington, and the woman is yet to be formally identified.\par \par \uc2 Detectives arrested a 49-year-old woman and 15-year-old boy from West Footscray, who were both interviewed by police. The teenager was released without charge but the woman was charged with murder and remanded to appear at Melbourne magistrates court today.\par \par \uc2 Police believe they are known to the deceased woman. Anyone with information or dashcam footage is urged to come forward.\par \par \uc2 In a separate matter, Victorian police are investigating the discovery of a man\u8217\'20\'19s body in the Maribyrnong River on Sunday morning. He is yet to be formally identified but police are not treating his death as suspicious at this time.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.17am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.05am BST\par \par \uc2 NSW government moves to halt biodiversity decline\par \par \uc2 The New South Wales government says the state\u8217\'20\'19s biodiversity is in crisis and must be put on a path to recovery to reverse the decline of beloved species and ecosystems.\par \par \uc2 The environment minister, Penny Sharpe, has released the government\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cfirst steps\u8221\'20\'1D in responding to a major review of the state\u8217\'20\'19s nature laws, saying:\par \par \uc2 We cannot ignore the truth: biodiversity in NSW is in crisis.\par \par \uc2 The Minns government is proposing to develop a new nature strategy that would be enshrined in law and set targets for conservation and restoration, including landscape restoration, species recovery and addressing threats to nature.\par \par \uc2 You can read more on this below:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/jul/17/nsw-government-says-states-biodiversity-in-crisis-as-it-pledges-first-steps-to-reverse-decline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NSW government says state\u8217\'20\'19s biodiversity \u8216\'20\'18in crisis\u8217\'20\'19 as it pledges first steps to reverse decline}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.12am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 Key event\par \par \uc2 Chalmers says \uc2 IMF\uc2  report shows inflation lingering worldwide\par \par \uc2 Treasurer \uc2 Jim Chalmers\uc2  said the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.imf.org/en/Publications/WEO/Issues/2024/07/16/world-economic-outlook-update-july-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 International Monetary Fund\u8217\'20\'19s July world economic outlook}}}\uc2  update shows that \u8220\'20\'1Cinflation is lingering all across the globe\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In a statement this morning, he responded to the update and said:\par \par \uc2 There is an international element as well as a domestic element to the inflation challenge that we have in Australia.\par \par \uc2 The report rightly highlights that uncertainty in the global economy in areas like international trade are contributing to inflationary pressures all over the world and Australia is not immune to those pressures.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is still higher than we would like, but inflation is less than half its peak and much lower than the 6.1% we inherited from the Coalition.\par \par \uc2 Our responsible, substantial and meaningful cost-of-living relief is making things a bit easier for people, with tax cuts, energy bill relief, cheaper medicines, pay rises and more paid parental leave all kicking in from this month.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.54am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 IMF\uc2  says persistent inflation means \u8216\'20\'18higher-for-even-longer\u8217\'20\'19 interest rates\par \par \uc2 Stubbornly high inflation in advanced economies threatens \u8220\'20\'1Chigher-for-even-longer interest rates\u8221\'20\'1D, the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  warns, citing lingering services-price pressures and escalating trade tensions.\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports, Australia\u8217\'20\'19s economic growth outlook has also been slightly trimmed by the \uc2 IMF\uc2  to 1.4% for 2024 from the 1.5% forecast in April. Gross domestic product is then expected to expand 2% next year, unchanged from previous forecasts.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s latest economic assessment lands as economists warn persistent price pressures threaten the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s inflation fight, which could lead to interest rates staying higher longer.\par \par \uc2 Much hinges on June quarter inflation data out later in the month, with a strong outcome potentially putting another hike on the table at the August cash rate meeting.\par \par \uc2 Globally, the \uc2 IMF\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s growth projections were unchanged and inflation had slowed enough to be broadly on track for a soft landing. Yet deteriorating public finances as well as slowing disinflation progress, particularly due to persistent services price pressures, were highlighted as downside risks to the outlook.\par \par \uc2 Possible escalation of trade tensions was also identified as potential source of bumpiness along the disinflation path, threatening higher costs for imported goods along the supply chain.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.53am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese has shared a message to social media on the 10-year anniversary of the downing of \uc2 Malaysian Airlines\uc2  flight MH17. The prime minister said:\par \par \uc2 Ten years ago the world was shaken by the terrible news that \uc2 Malaysian Airlines\uc2  flight MH17 had been shot from the skies. All 298 passengers and crew onboard that day lost their lives, including 38 people who called Australia home.\par \par \uc2 While in Kyiv in 2022, I had the chance to pay my respects at the memorial to the Australian victims. These were travellers heading home. Young families, lifelong partners, retirees returning from trips they\u8217\'20\'19d long planned, grandchildren alongside their devoted grandfather.\par \par \uc2 We remember them today and our hearts go out to all those who knew them best and loved them most. The people for whom this anniversary brings a new reminder of irreplaceable loss.\par \par \uc2 Australia remains steadfast in our commitment to seeking truth, justice and accountability from those responsible for this horrific act of violence.\par \par \uc2 enltrTen years ago the world was shaken by the terrible news that \uc2 Malaysian Airlines\uc2  flight MH17 had been shot from the skies.\par \par \uc2 All 298 passengers and crew on board that day lost their lives, including 38 people who called Australia home. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Jkspr76YXv"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Jkspr76YXv}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Anthony Albanese (@AlboMP) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AlboMP/status/1813317924700266557?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 16, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.32pm BST\par \par \uc2 Snow in Queensland!\par \par \uc2 Spicer Peak Lodge in the rural town of Maryvale, west of the Gold Coast, witnessed snow \u8220\'20\'1Cfor a few minutes\u8221\'20\'1D yesterday. The last time it experienced snowfall was on 17 July 2015 \u8211\'20\'13 almost exactly nine years prior.\par \par \uc2 The promise of snow across the Granite Belt has lured tourists, photographers and storm chasers from right across Queensland. People were lucky to see a small flurry at Mount Mackenzie Lookout in Tenterfield, just across the border into NSW.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe promise of snow on the Granite Belt has been enough to lure tourists, photographers and storm chasers from right across Queensland. For a few lucky one, their efforts have been rewarded with a flurry just across the border. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/jGTbl0fzfx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/jGTbl0fzfx}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/joshvanstaden?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @joshvanstaden}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/7NEWS?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #7NEWS}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/2YmCV11iP3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/2YmCV11iP3}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 7NEWS Toowoomba (@7NewsToowoomba) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/7NewsToowoomba/status/1813136497849344507?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 16, 2024}}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.weatherzone.com.au/news/snow-possible-in-queensland-on-state-of-origin-night/1889642"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 As Weatherzone reports}}}\uc2 , it snows every few years in the Granite Belt near the NSW border. The town of Stanthorpe holds the record for Queensland\u8217\'20\'19s coldest temperature on record, -10.6C in June 1961, and sees occasional snowfalls.\par \par \uc2 The heaviest snowfalls in Stanthorpe this century occurred on 17 July 2015, when at least 5cm of fluffy coated the region.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 'I should have done more than shirtfront him', Abbott says of Putin\par \par \uc2 Circling back to former PM \uc2 Tony Abbott\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s earlier interview on ABC RN: he was asked about comments he made in the past that he wanted to \u8220\'20\'1Cshirtfront\u8221\'20\'1D Russian president \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , and whether he regretted this?\par \par \uc2 Abbott said that he had \u8220\'20\'1Crather been vindicated by history\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 And I frankly should have done more than shirtfront him, because that was probably the only way to stop him, to be much more forceful than anyone was back then.\par \par \uc2 Q : Although we are against political violence, right?\par \par \uc2 Abbott responded:\par \par \uc2 We are against aggression. We are against unjustified violence, and the aggression by Russia against Ukraine \u8211\'20\'13 whether it was in 2014 or 2022, and subsequently \u8211\'20\'13 was never, ever justified. Ukraine never threatened Russia. The idea of Ukrainians invading Russia was just fanciful, completely fanciful. The idea of Nato invading Russia was completely fanciful.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 Adding nuclear to Australia akin to coaxing Swiss to take up surfing, Bowen riffs\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a busy couple of days for energetic energy minister Chris Bowen, with a national press club speech in Canberra later today, as noted in earlier posts.\par \par \uc2 However, he might have been testing some of his best lines last night in a fireside chat (sans feu) at the Australian Clean Energy Summit dinner in Sydney, where he said:\par \par \uc2 I have no ideological objection to nuclear energy. I have an economic and engineering \u8211\'20\'13 to quote \uc2 Malcolm Turnbull\uc2  [who was at the dinner] \u8211\'20\'13 objection.\par \par \uc2 Nuclear energy was too expensive and would take too long to build, Bowen said. (The market regulator forecasts all coal-fired plants will shut by 2038.) That\u8217\'20\'19s not saying it isn\u8217\'20\'19t the decarbonising energy answer for other nations less blessed with abundant renewable energy resources than Australia.\par \par \uc2 For Australia to go nuclear because other nations were doing so, though, was \u8220\'20\'1Clike us going to Switzerland and saying, \u8216\'20\'18look, I know you\u8217\'20\'19ve got lots of snow, but we really think you should try surfing\u8217\'20\'19,\u8221\'20\'1D Bowen said.\par \par \uc2 Nuclear energy, Bowen said, was actually something of a \u8220\'20\'1Csideshow\u8221\'20\'1D globally, with much more wind and solar being added globally. Nuclear reactor capacity even shrank 1 gigawatt last year as more generation was shut down than was added.\par \par \uc2 As you might expect, Bowen\u8217\'20\'19s comments went down well. His opposition counterpart, Ted O\u8217\'20\'19Brien, is due to speak at the summit this morning, and may find a less sympathetic audience.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.04pm BST\par \par \uc2 Tim Ayres flags \u8216\'20\'18proportionate\u8217\'20\'19 federal response to \uc2 CFMEU\par \par \uc2 A Labor senator says that federal intervention into alleged criminal behaviour within the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  will be effective and proportionate, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 Tim Ayres said steps to deal with allegations against the union would be announced shortly by workplace relations minister Tony Burke. He told ABC TV earlier:\par \par \uc2 Tony and the team in there are doing the careful work of making sure that the government\u8217\'20\'19s response is effective, well-weighted and effective.\par \par \uc2 Ayres said calls from state governments to distance state Labor branches from the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  would be dealt with by the national executive of the party.\par \par \uc2 While I don\u8217\'20\'19t forecast the debates in terms of the national executive, we have received these requests from the premiers, we will act upon them, and we\u8217\'20\'19ll act in accordance with the requests from the premiers.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 Tony Abbott\uc2  doubts an Australian political assassination is \u8216\'20\'18imminent\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Tony Abbott\uc2  was also asked about the assassination attempt on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and if he is worried about something similar occurring in Australia.\par \par \uc2 He responded:\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re very blessed in this country. We don\u8217\'20\'19t have a gun culture, we don\u8217\'20\'19t have a history of political violence, we don\u8217\'20\'19t at this point in time \u8211\'20\'13 thank God \u8211\'20\'13 have the fragmentation and polarisation that sadly exists in America to quite the same extent.\par \par \uc2 So look, can you rule these things out? Of course not. Do I expect it? Well, maybe some time in the next 100 or 200 years, sure. Almost anything could happen in that period of time. But is is imminent? I doubt it very much.\par \par \uc2 I suppose we need constant vigilance, A, to try to ensure that there aren\u8217\'20\'19t people who are, given the ready opportunities, [would] do harm; and B, that we try always to be our best selves and bring out the better angles of our natures.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 Abbott says Russian leadership needs to take responsibility for downing of MH17\par \par \uc2 Former prime minister \uc2 Tony Abbott\uc2 , who was PM when \uc2 Malaysia Airlines\uc2  flight MH17 came down, has reflected on the \u8220\'20\'1Catrocity\u8221\'20\'1D 10 years on.\par \par \uc2 Speaking with ABC RN just earlier, he said that to this day he was \u8220\'20\'1Cvery angry\u8221\'20\'1D and believed Russian president \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  owed the victims\u8217\'20\'19 families an apology and compensation.\par \par \uc2 Because plainly, this aircraft was brought down in the pursuit of Russia\u8217\'20\'19s geopolitical objectives in Ukraine and these were the Australian victims\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 The Hague has sentenced three people \u8211\'20\'13 Igor Girkin, Leonid Kharchenko and Sergey Dubinsky \u8211\'20\'13 to life in prison for their roles in the downing of the flight and murder of all 298 onboard. But Abbott says that \u8220\'20\'1Cin the end, responsibility has got to be taken by the Russian leadership\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 Treaty negotiations in Victoria set to begin within months\par \par \uc2 Victoria\u8217\'20\'19s First Peoples\u8217\'20\'19 Assembly says it is ready to enter treaty negotiations with the state government which are expected to begin in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 The assembly has informed the treaty authority \u8211\'20\'13 the umpire that will oversee the process \u8211\'20\'13 it is ready to commence negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Assembly co-chair and Gunditjmara man Rueben Berg says Victoria\u8217\'20\'19s traditional owner groups and Aboriginal Torres Strait Islander community have worked to \u8220\'20\'1Ccreate a shared understanding and vision of how the journey to treaty should unfold\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re proud of the inclusive structures and ways of working that we have in place to ensure the collective hopes and aspirations in our communities are well represented and that the assembly can be the strongest advocate possible for all mob in Victoria.\par \par \uc2 The state-wide treaty will cover issues impacting all Indigenous Victorians. Traditional owner groups will also be able to enter into separate treaties with the state regarding priorities for their area.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 Rudd weighs in on Tenacious D controversy\par \par \uc2 In case you missed it: Jack Black has put his rock duo Tenacious D on hold following an onstage comment made by his bandmate Kyle Gass about the shooting of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Black said he was \u8220\'20\'1Cblindsided\u8221\'20\'1D by what was said on stage, and he \u8220\'20\'1Cno longer feel[s] it is appropriate to continue\u8221\'20\'1D the tour. You can read more below:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/article/2024/jul/16/jack-black-puts-tenacious-d-on-hold-after-bandmates-trump-shooting-comment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jack Black puts Tenacious D \u8216\'20\'18on hold\u8217\'20\'19 after bandmate\u8217\'20\'19s Trump shooting comment}}}\par \par \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2 , the ambassador to the US and former prime minister, has weighed in on the matter and said:\par \par \uc2 It makes me feel sick that someone would joke about violence. It makes me feel physically ill. People might think that it is funny ha-ha to run off your mouth there. It\u8217\'20\'19s not. It\u8217\'20\'19s about physical life. It\u8217\'20\'19s about the threat to physical life. It\u8217\'20\'19s about the near assassination of a former and prospective president of the United States, depending how people vote, and about the murder of an innocent civilian and two people seriously wounded. These people need to grow up and find a decent job.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 Energy minister says nuclear and renewables \u8216\'20\'18simply incompatible\u8217\'20\'19 in Australia\par \par \uc2 In his speech to the \uc2 National Press Club\uc2 , Chris Bowen will say areas earmarked for nuclear plants \u8211\'20\'13 such as Port Augusta, Lithgow and central Queensland \u8211\'20\'13 require major investment and revitalisation as soon as possible, not a decade from now under the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s nuclear announcement.\par \par \uc2 Bowen said those areas \u8220\'20\'1Cneed jobs and investment now\u8221\'20\'1D, claiming the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s nuclear plan would lead to uncertainty in energy markets and threaten investment in renewable energy. He will claim:\par \par \uc2 The biggest problem of all is that in Australia, nuclear and renewables are simply incompatible. While the opposition purports to support an \u8220\'20\'1Call of the above\u8221\'20\'1D energy mix, their ideological pursuit of nuclear reactors in two decades\u8217\'20\'19 time would wreck the renewables rollout now.\par \par \uc2 Bowen brands the opposition \u8220\'20\'1Carrogant\u8221\'20\'1D over its comments about forcing states and local towns to accept nuclear plants, even if they don\u8217\'20\'19t want them.\par \par \uc2 The minister predicts an \u8220\'20\'1Cinvestment chill\u8221\'20\'1D into Australian industry if the government-backed nuclear policy came into force, asking: \u8220\'20\'1CWhy would domestic or global investors try to compete with unlimited taxpayer subsidies for state-sponsored generation?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Base-load nuclear plants simply don\u8217\'20\'19t stack up economically in a grid with significant renewable generation.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 Coal-fired power \u8216\'20\'18biggest threat\u8217\'20\'19 to power grid reliability \u8211\'20\'13 Bowen\par \par \uc2 Energy minister Chris Bowen says coal-fired power stations are \u8220\'20\'1Cfar and away the biggest threat to reliability\u8221\'20\'1D in Australia\u8217\'20\'19s electricity grid, signalling the Labor government will seek to fight the next election on power policy and the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s controversial nuclear plan.\par \par \uc2 Bowen will address the \uc2 National Press Club\uc2  in Canberra today, with his speech to draw a distinction between what he called \u8220\'20\'1Creliable renewables\u8221\'20\'1D and \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Canti-renewables\u8221\'20\'1D nuclear power plants.\par \par \uc2 According to advance speech notes, Bowen will say:\par \par \uc2 Far and away the biggest threat to reliability in our grid is over reliance on aging coal-fired power stations. Over the last year, not a single day has passed without an unplanned outage at a coal power generator in eastern Australia.\par \par \uc2 Bowen has previously spoken of his hope to \u8220\'20\'1Cend the climate wars\u8221\'20\'1D in Australia, but his speech indicates the government will seek to draw a strong contrast between its energy plan and that of the opposition.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.36am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2  to host memorial service with families of MH17 victims\par \par \uc2 Foreign minister \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2  will today host a memorial service for the families of MH17 victims, along with first responders, investigators and officials.\par \par \uc2 The service will be held at Parliament House, while the attorney general, Mark Dreyfus, will attend an event in the Netherlands hosted by the MH17 Air Disaster (Next of Kin) Foundation, with representatives from victims\u8217\'20\'19 families and loved ones.\par \par \uc2 Today is the 10-year anniversary since the downing of \uc2 Malaysia Airlines\uc2  flight MH17, where 298 lost their lives, including 38 people who called Australia home.\par \par \uc2 A statement from Wong and Dreyfus reads:\par \par \uc2 Ten years on, those killed that day remain in our hearts and in our purpose. Our thoughts are with their loved ones \u8211\'20\'13 the passing of time does not diminish the pain of their loss.\par \par \uc2 We acknowledge and remember the courage and resilience shown by those who responded to the disaster. We recognise the Australian federal police, defence force and \uc2 Australian Transport Safety Bureau\uc2  personnel whose work was central to the thorough and painstaking investigation.\par \par \uc2 Australia remains steadfast in our commitment to seeking truth, justice and accountability from those responsible for this horrific act of violence.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 NSW wants \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  construction arm suspended from ALP\par \par \uc2 The NSW government has called for the construction arm of the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  to be suspended from the NSW Labor party.\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports, premier \uc2 Chris Minns\uc2  and treasurer Daniel Mookhey have written to the NSW Labor party general secretary in the wake of new corruption allegations.\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19ve asked the state branch to take the \u8220\'20\'1Cnecessary steps\u8221\'20\'1D to immediately suspend the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  construction and general division\u8217\'20\'19s affiliation with the party and stop any donations or affiliation fees.\par \par \uc2 Minns and Mookhey said the NSW Labor government was working with the federal Labor government on a \u8220\'20\'1Ccoordinated approach\u8221\'20\'1D towards the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2 , since corruption allegations began circling the Victorian branch on Friday.\par \par \uc2 We will also ask the commonwealth government to review enterprise bargaining agreements in NSW which the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  is party to.\par \par \uc2 Further, the NSW construction compliance unit will work with federal regulatory bodies and act on any allegations.\par \par \uc2 We must take the necessary action to protect honest and hardworking construction workers and union members.\par \par \uc2 The NSW government said it would provide an update later today on other actions it intended to take.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 Welcome\par \par \uc2 Hello! And welcome back to a new day on the Australia news live blog. I\u8217\'20\'19m Emily Wind and I\u8217\'20\'19ll be bringing you our rolling coverage this Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Making news overnight: New South Wales premier \uc2 Chris Minns\uc2  has called for NSW Labor to suspend the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  construction arm. As \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports, Minns and state treasurer Daniel Mookhey have written to the NSW Labor party general secretary in the wake of new corruption allegations.\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19ve asked the state branch to take the \u8220\'20\'1Cnecessary steps\u8221\'20\'1D to immediately suspend the \uc2 CFMEU\uc2  construction and general division\u8217\'20\'19s affiliation with the party and stop any donations or affiliation fees.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, foreign minister \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2  will today host a memorial service with the families of the MH17 victims, on the 10-year anniversary of the \uc2 Malaysia Airlines\uc2  flight downing. 298 people lost their lives, including 38 people who called Australia home.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll have more on both of these stories in a moment.\par \par \uc2 As always, you can get in touch with any thoughts, tips or questions via X, @emilywindwrites, or you can send me an email: emily.wind@theguardian.com. Let\u8217\'20\'19s get started.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.19pm BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240716ek7g0038p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
