\par\par \uc2 Film\par \b \uc2 Disney\uc2  faces strike threat as thousands of California workers vote on walkout\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 553 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 July 2024\par 12:20 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Company could endure biggest US strike of 2024 as unions accuse it of intimidation during contract negotiations\par \par \uc2 Disney\uc2  could face the largest strike in the US this year after it was announced that thousands of theme park and hotel workers in California will vote on whether to stage a walkout.\par \par \uc2 Three trade unions representing 14,000 \u8220\'20\'1Ccast members\u8221\'20\'1D at Disneyland, Disney California Adventure, Downtown \uc2 Disney\uc2  and the \uc2 Disney\uc2  hotels announced that an unfair labor practice strike vote would be held next week amid negotiations over a new union contract.\par \par \uc2 In a critical statement, union leaders accused {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/walt-disney-company"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Disney}}}\uc2  of \u8220\'20\'1Cunlawful discipline and intimidation and surveillance\u8221\'20\'1D of union members. The entertainment giant did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Talks over a new contract began in April. The following month, the unions filed unfair labor practice charges over cast members not being allowed to wear union buttons at work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CInstead of working with us toward a fair contract, \uc2 Disney\uc2  has engaged in multiple instances of conduct we allege are unfair labor practices,\u8221\'20\'1D the Disney Workers Rising bargaining committee said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe know these actions are only an attempt to stop us from exercising our rights and saddle us with a contract that perpetuates the status quo at \uc2 Disney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe won\u8217\'20\'19t accept less than what we deserve because we know our value to \uc2 Disney\uc2 . The theme parks\u8217\'20\'19 profits come from our hard work making a trip to Disneyland a magical experience for guests.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The workers are represented by the \uc2 Bakery, Confectionery, Tobacco Workers and Grain Millers\uc2  (\uc2 BCTGM\uc2 ) Local 83, Service Employees International Union-United Service Workers West (\uc2 SEIU\uc2 -USWW), the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  Local 495 and the \uc2 United Food and Commercial Workers (UFCW)\uc2  Local 324.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe haven\u8217\'20\'19t been able to move the company on the issues most important to our members,\u8221\'20\'1D said Andrea Zinder, president of UFCW Local 324, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/business/story/2024-07-09/the-mouse-gloves-are-off-disney-workers-to-vote-on-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  the \uc2 Los Angeles Times\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThe unfair labor practices that \uc2 Disney\uc2  has committed are so egregious that they interfere with our ability to get a fair contract.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The unions also cited a survey of workers, conducted earlier this year, which found that 28% of \uc2 Disney\uc2  workers reported experiencing food insecurity, 64% reported being rent burdened, and 42% of workers reported missing work for medical treatment because they didn\u8217\'20\'19t have enough sick leave.\par \par \uc2 Workers are fighting for significant wage increases. The current minimum wage under \uc2 the city of Anaheim\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s living wage law is currently $19.90 an hour, which has raised workers\u8217\'20\'19 wages from the $18 an hour they were paid under the expired contract.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have people who work at Disneyland who have to choose between paying their rent or buying groceries. There are people who go without medical care because they can\u8217\'20\'19t afford it. There are people who rely on the food banks, and the churches, or food supplements,\u8221\'20\'1D said Coleen Palmer, a cashier at Disneyland for over 30 years and a member of the bargaining committee. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a place where people want to work but they want to be able to make a living doing what they\u8217\'20\'19re doing and that\u8217\'20\'19s what we\u8217\'20\'19re fighting for.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The current union contract expired on 16 June, with strike authorization votes expected to be released on 20 July.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240709ek790035z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
