\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US economy adds 206,000 June jobs as labor market cools slightly\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani in New York \par \uc2 487 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 July 2024\par 15:09 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Number is close to what was expected, as unemployment rate creeps above 4% for first time in two years\par \par \uc2 The labor market started to ease in June, with 206,000 jobs added to the US economy last month, according to new labor data from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  (\uc2 BLS\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 The number of new jobs is close to what economists had expected for the month. The figures represent a slight cooling compared to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/jun/07/may-jobs-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May}}}\uc2 , when the economy added a revised 218,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate in June was 4.1%, a 0.1% increase compared with May and the first time the rate has been above 4% in over two years.\par \par \uc2 Data released earlier this week suggested the labor market was starting to cool. Payroll firm ADP reported that private employers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://adp-ri-nrip-static.adp.com/artifacts/us_ner/20240703/ADP_NATIONAL_EMPLOYMENT_REPORT_Press_Release_2024_06%20FINAL.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 added}}}\uc2  150,000 jobs in June, down from 157,000 in May. Job losses also appear to be growing. Executive outplacement firm \uc2 Challenger\uc2 , Gray and Christmas {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.challengergray.com/blog/job-cuts-tumble-in-june-led-by-consumer-manufacturing-second-highest-hiring-of-the-year/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  48,786 job cuts in June, down from 63,816 cuts in May but still a nearly 20% increase compared with June of last year.\par \par \uc2 The jobs figures, released on the first Friday of every month, have been closely watched by Wall Street, which is eager to see interest rates drop, and in Washington, where the strength of hiring has been one of the few bright spots for the Biden administration, which is struggling with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/22/poll-economy-recession-biden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 poor polling}}}\uc2  on its economic policies.\par \par \uc2 Along with inflation figures, which come out later in the month, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  uses jobs figures to determine whether the economy is cooling and ready for interest rates to drop.\par \par \uc2 Last month, officials at the Fed kept rates at a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/jun/12/inflation-economy-presidential-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two-decade high}}}\uc2  of around 5.3%, where it has been for nearly a year. The Fed has been trying to bring inflation down to 2%. In May, inflation sat at 3.4% \u8211\'20\'13 lower than its peak of 9.1% in June 2022, but still higher than the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s target rate.\par \par \uc2 Minutes from the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s last meeting were released on Wednesday and showed the central bank was waiting for \u8220\'20\'1Cadditional favorable data\u8221\'20\'1D before making cuts.\par \par \uc2 But in bringing inflation down, the Fed must make sure the labor market is not cooling too much. Earlier this week, the Fed chair, \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/economy/central-banking/powell-says-fed-has-made-a-lot-of-progress-on-inflation-37c2862c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  at an event that the economy has \u8220\'20\'1Cmade a lot of progress\u8221\'20\'1D and has \u8220\'20\'1Cseen a pretty substantial move toward better balance\u8221\'20\'1D in the labor market.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe want to be more confident that inflation is moving sustainably down. We want to understand that the levels that we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing are a true reading on what is actually happening with underlying inflation,\u8221\'20\'1D Powell said.\par \par \uc2 Inflation figures for June will be released on 11 July. The Fed\u8217\'20\'19s next meeting is on 30 and 31 July.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240705ek75001s0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
