\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Most Americans have no idea how anti-worker the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  has become\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,459 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2024\par 10:26 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s current justices are some of the most hostile to labor rights in modern US history\par \par \uc2 Under Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , the supreme court has been supremely pro-corporate \u8211\'20\'13 one study even {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=4178504"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called}}}\uc2  the Roberts court \u8220\'20\'1Cthe most pro-business court in history\u8221\'20\'1D. Not only have many justices been groomed and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://slate.com/news-and-politics/2017/01/how-the-federalist-society-became-the-de-facto-selector-of-republican-supreme-court-justices.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vetted}}}\uc2  by the business-backed \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 , but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/clarence-thomas-scotus-undisclosed-luxury-travel-gifts-crow"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Clarence Thomas}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/samuel-alito-luxury-fishing-trip-paul-singer-scotus-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Samuel Alito}}}\uc2  have taken lavish favors from billionaire corporate titans. Thomas has even {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/clarence-thomas-secretly-attended-koch-brothers-donor-events-scotus"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 spoken}}}\uc2  at two Koch network fundraising \u8220\'20\'1Cdonor summits\u8221\'20\'1D, gatherings of rightwing, ultra-wealthy business barons.\par \par \uc2 While the court is decidedly pro-corporate, most Americans probably don\u8217\'20\'19t know just how anti-worker and anti-union it really is. The justices have often shown a stunning callousness toward workers, and that means a callousness toward average Americans. One of the most egregious examples was a 2014 ruling \u8211\'20\'13 with an opinion written by Thomas \u8211\'20\'13 that held that Amazon, which holds workers up to 25 minutes after the ends of their shifts waiting to be screened to ensure they didn\u8217\'20\'19t steal anything, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/10/business/supreme-court-rules-against-worker-pay-for-security-screenings.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 doesn\u8217\'20\'19t}}}\uc2  have to pay them for that time.\par \par \uc2 Or take this month\u8217\'20\'19s decision in which the court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jun/13/supreme-court-starbucks-union-decision#:~:text=The%20US%20supreme%20court%20sided,as%20unfair%20under%20federal%20law."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled}}}\uc2  in favor of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  by making it harder for the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ) to win rapid reinstatement of workers who are illegally fired for supporting a union. In that case, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  fired five of the six baristas who were heading an effort to unionize a Memphis Starbucks. After \uc2 NLRB\uc2  officials found that the workers had been fired unlawfully for backing a union, a federal judge agreed to the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s request to issue an injunction to quickly reinstate them. Many labor relations experts say it\u8217\'20\'19s important for the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  to be able to win quick reinstatement after companies fire workers who lead unionization drives, as \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has repeatedly done, because those firings often terrify co-workers and cause union drives to collapse.\par \par \uc2 They seem to view workers and unions as unwelcome nuisances that are seeking to make life difficult for corporations\par \par \uc2 Writing the court\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/23pdf/23-367_f3b7.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 majority opinion}}}\uc2 , Thomas ignored all that, oblivious to the injustices and suffering that many workers face when they exercise their right to form a union. Thomas said that federal judges, when issuing such injunctions, should follow a more exacting four-part test, rather than the worker-friendly two-part test the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  favored. Thomas\u8217\'20\'19s opinion also ignored some glaring facts: the union has accused \uc2 Starbucks{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/feb/26/amazon-trader-joes-starbucks-anti-union-measures"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 of firing 150 pro-union baristas}}}\uc2 , and the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has accused \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  of an astoundingly high number of violations of the law \u8211\'20\'13 436 \u8211\'20\'13 in its efforts to block unionization.\par \par \uc2 In contrast to Thomas, \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/23pdf/23-367_f3b7.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 partial concurrence and partial dissent}}}\uc2 , acknowledged the injustices and delays that pro-union workers often face. She wrote that \u8220\'20\'1CCongress, in enacting the National Labor Relations Act, recognized that delay in vindicating labor rights \u8216\'20\'18during the \u8220\'20\'1Cnotoriously glacial\u8221\'20\'1D course of \uc2 NLRB\uc2  proceedings\u8217\'20\'19 can lead to their defeat\u8221\'20\'1D. Jackson noted that the litigation over reinstating the Memphis baristas had dragged on for two years. (It was dismaying that Justices \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  and \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  signed Thomas\u8217\'20\'19s soulless, unsympathetic majority opinion rather than Jackson\u8217\'20\'19s.)\par \par \uc2 A 2022 study {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=4178504"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2  that of the 57 justices who have sat on the court over the past century, the six justices with the most pro-business voting records are the six members of today\u8217\'20\'19s 6-3, rightwing super-majority, all appointed by Republican presidents: Thomas, Alito, Roberts, \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 . The study found that Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s three appointees \u8211\'20\'13 Gorsuch, Kavanaugh and Barrett \u8211\'20\'13 were the three most pro-business justices of the 57 evaluated. (That study also found that the court\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic appointees at the time \u8211\'20\'13 Kagan, Sotomayor and \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 were among the top 20 pro-business justices.)\par \par \uc2 All this is a far cry from when some justices were true champions of workers. Arthur Goldberg had been the general counsel of the \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2  and served as secretary of labor under John F Kennedy. Justice William J Brennan Jr, whose father was a union official, was famous for going to bat for workers. As a lawyer, Louis Brandeis filed famous, detailed supreme court briefs in cases that sought to uphold pro-worker laws.\par \par \uc2 In sharp contrast, today\u8217\'20\'19s conservative judges seem to almost reflexively rule against workers and unions. They seem to view workers and unions as unwelcome nuisances that are seeking to make life difficult for corporations as they pursue their noble mission of maximizing their profits and share prices.\par \par \uc2 Take the court\u8217\'20\'19s 5-4 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/courts_law/supreme-court-rules-that-companies-can-force-workers-into-individual-arbitration/2018/05/21/09a3a968-5cfa-11e8-a4a4-c070ef53f315_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Epic Systems ruling}}}\uc2  of 2018. Gorsuch\u8217\'20\'19s majority opinion blessed corporations\u8217\'20\'19 efforts to prohibit workers from filing class-action lawsuits. It instead let employers require employees to pursue their grievances through individual, closed-door arbitrations, which greatly favor employers, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/the-arbitration-epidemic/#epi-toc-10"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to various studies}}}\uc2 . Because lawyers are far less willing to take individual worker cases than class actions, Epic Systems gutted workers\u8217\'20\'19 ability to vindicate their rights against sexual harassment, racial discrimination and wage theft.\par \par \uc2 In her {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/17pdf/16-285_q8l1.pdf#page=32"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dissent}}}\uc2 , Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  called the majority opinion \u8220\'20\'1Cegregiously wrong\u8221\'20\'1D. She also said the ruling would result in \u8220\'20\'1Chuge under-enforcement of federal and state statutes designed to advance the wellbeing of vulnerable workers\u8221\'20\'1D. Ginsburg added that it\u8217\'20\'19s difficult and potentially perilous to pursue small claims individually. \u8220\'20\'1CBy joining hands in litigation,\u8221\'20\'1D she wrote, \u8220\'20\'1Cworkers can spread the costs of litigation and reduce the risk of employer retaliation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another case that showed shocking insensitivity toward workers\u8217\'20\'19 concerns was the 2007 Lilly Ledbetter case. Ledbetter was a supervisor at a Goodyear tire plant in Alabama, where for years she earned substantially less than the 16 men at the same management level. That pay discrimination was hidden from her, and she learned of it only after more than 15 years on the job. Alito wrote the court\u8217\'20\'19s inflexible, unsympathetic 5-4 majority opinion, ruling that her case should be thrown out because she had failed to file her complaint within 180 days after her pay was set, as the law called for.\par \par \uc2 Ginsburg angrily {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2007/05/29/world/americas/29iht-30scotuscnd.5916978.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dissented}}}\uc2 , writing that the ruling \u8220\'20\'1Cis totally at odds with the robust protection against workplace discrimination Congress intended Title VII to secure\u8221\'20\'1D. Ginsburg added that Alito\u8217\'20\'19s majority opinion \u8220\'20\'1Cdoes not comprehend, or is indifferent to, the insidious way in which women can be victims of pay discrimination\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 There have been some recent anti-union cases. Last year, in \uc2 Glacier Northwest\uc2 , the court made it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/jun/01/us-supreme-court-ruling-unions-strikes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 easier for corporations to sue unions}}}\uc2  for any financial damage they suffer when workers go on strike \u8211\'20\'13 a ruling that could discourage workers from using their most powerful form of leverage. In 2021, in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/06/23/us/supreme-court-unions-farms-california.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cedar Point Nursery}}}\uc2  case, the court put property rights far above worker rights and union rights when it overturned part of a California law, inspired by Cesar Chavez, that granted union organizers a right to go on farm owners\u8217\'20\'19 property to speak with farm workers.\par \par \uc2 By far the most important anti-union decision in recent years was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2018/06/27/us/politics/supreme-court-unions-organized-labor.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Janus v. AFSCME}}}\uc2 , a 5-4 ruling, written by Alito, in which the court held that requiring government employees to pay fees to their union violated their first amendment rights. That ruling allowed any federal, state or local government employees to opt out of paying union fees \u8211\'20\'13 and was immediately seen as a blow that would weaken unions and their treasuries. Ten minutes after the court issued that decision, then president Trump tweeted: \u8220\'20\'1CBig loss for the coffers of the Democrats!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Janus case was underwritten by rightwing foundations and billionaire corporate powerhouses, including Richard Uihlein and the Koch Brothers. (Remember, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  attended their \u8220\'20\'1Cdonor summits\u8221\'20\'1D.)\par \par \uc2 The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s approval ratings have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/509234/supreme-court-approval-holds-record-low.aspx#:~:text=Job%20Approval%20Rating%20of%20the%20U.S.%20Supreme%20Court&text=Currently%2C%2040%25%20approve%2C%20tied,a%2042%25%20reading%20in%202005."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fallen to a record low}}}\uc2 . Many Americans think the court is corrupt and has lost its way \u8211\'20\'13 its justices take all-expenses-paid vacations with billionaires, fail to disclose gifts, ignore blatant conflicts of interest, and one justice\u8217\'20\'19s home hung {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/05/16/us/justice-alito-upside-down-flag.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an upside-down flag}}}\uc2  apparently showing sympathy for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1CStop the Steal\u8221\'20\'1D movement.\par \par \uc2 One easy step the court can take to begin an effort to regain respect and popularity would be to be stop ruling so often in favor of multibillion corporations and instead side with workers, eg typical Americans \u8211\'20\'13 and with labor unions, institutions that fight to improve the lives of average Americans. It just might help, and would further the cause of justice, if the court were to show that it cares more about embattled workers than about billionaires and faceless corporations.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Steven Greenhouse, a senior fellow at the \uc2 Century Foundation\uc2 , is an American labor and workplace journalist and writer\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240628ek6s0018m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*