\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  made a deal to raise drivers\u8217\'20\'19 wages. It was another victory for big tech\b0\par\par\uc2 Edward Ongweso Jr \par \uc2 1,131 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 June 2024\par 22:14 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Minneapolis struck a deal with the companies, but it preserves integral parts of their model, allowing the firms to undermine the compromise later\par \par \uc2 Who came out ahead when the Minneapolis city council announced a deal with \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  to increase driver pay and improve working conditions last month?\par \par \uc2 On 20 May, the city council {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usnews.com/news/us/articles/2024-05-20/uber-and-lyft-say-theyll-stay-in-minnesota-after-legislature-passes-driver-pay-compromise#:~:text=May%2020%2C%202024%2C%20at%209%3A50%20a.m.&text=ST.,Minneapolis%20officials%20earlier%20this%20year."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 heralded}}}\uc2  a compromise with the ride-hailing companies. \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  would agree to an inflation-pegged wage floor matching Minnesota\u8217\'20\'19s minimum wage of $15 an hour after expenses. Some lawmakers have hailed this as a 20% raise for drivers \u8211\'20\'13 however, the deal\u8217\'20\'19s pay rates are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://minnesotareformer.com/2024/05/21/heres-whats-in-the-bill-regulating-uber-and-lyft-driver-pay-and-labor-standards/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lower}}}\uc2  than almost every proposal made over the past two years amid a bitter fight between \uc2 Uber\uc2 , \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , their drivers and lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 Drivers, often arbitrarily fired (\u8220\'20\'1Cdeactivated\u8221\'20\'1D) by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.advancingjustice-alc.org/news-resources/guides-reports/fired-by-an-app-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opaque algorithms}}}\uc2 , are now able to appeal against dismissals. There\u8217\'20\'19s also funding for a \u8220\'20\'1Cnon-profit driver center\u8221\'20\'1D for driver rights education. The real gem may be the expansion of insurance coverage requirements for ride-hail drivers up to $1m that now includes the time immediately after ending a trip, which will help drivers with medical costs and lost wages after assaults or accidents.\par \par \uc2 This deal, however, preserves integral parts of the digital ride-hail model, allowing \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  to continue operating and undermine the compromise later.\par \par \uc2 In the two-year clash over the deal, ride-hail driver groups demonstrated, lobbied lawmakers and even negotiated with \uc2 Uber\uc2 . \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  repeatedly threatened capital strikes, promising to leave the state three separate times over proposed legislation. Each time the companies have drawn political blood: the first threat convinced Tim Walz, the governor, to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/minnesota-veto-lyft-uber-pay-6407e997298464c931d8c0c1e6e371a0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 kill a bill in May 2023}}}\uc2  with his first veto; the second prompted \uc2 Jacob Frey\uc2 , the Minneapolis mayor, to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mprnews.org/story/2023/08/22/minneapolis-mayor-jacob-frey-vetoes-rideshare-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 veto an ordinance}}}\uc2  passed by the city council in August that year; the third came after Frey again {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/minnesota/news/minneapolis-mayor-vetoes-minimum-wage-rideshare-ordinance/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vetoed an ordinance}}}\uc2  in March but was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mprnews.org/story/2024/03/14/minneapolis-city-council-overrides-freys-veto-on-ordinance-boosting-rideshare-driver-pay"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overridden}}}\uc2  by the city council.\par \par \uc2 Threatening capital strikes lets these firms narrow our political horizons while bolstering their own position. We debate driver pay rates that they inevitably undermine while distracted from their inordinate structural power. Cities pre-emptively dilute their ambitions, customers rationalize higher prices and drivers consign themselves to marginal improvements.\par \par \uc2 So who really came out ahead?\par \par \uc2 A sympathetic telling holds that drivers scored an immediate victory. Drivers\u8217\'20\'19 wages would zero out if \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  left the state. A deal that lets drivers keep working and earn better wages positions them to continue fighting for better deals.\par \par \uc2 There are two key pillars of the digital ride-hail model that perpetually degrade driver working conditions: 1 driver misclassification as contractors to lower labor costs, and 2 information asymmetry between workers, regulators and firms. Both are left alone by Minneapolis\u8217\'20\'19s deal, but the decision to abandon data transparency specifically ensures working conditions will deteriorate due to what Veena Dubal, a \uc2 UC Irvine\uc2  law professor, calls \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=4331080"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 algorithmic wage discrimination}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. Utilizing constant worker surveillance, firms like \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  calculate the minimum pay rates necessary to extract the most value from each driver. Dubal observes that even if the work is the same, \u8220\'20\'1Centirely unpredictable and opaque means\u8221\'20\'1D calculate what a driver\u8217\'20\'19s labor is worth. With the removal of predictability goes any notion of fairness as workers are tricked by algorithms to gamble on whether assigned work will be worth the expenses incurred, normalizing longer hours and poorer conditions. A wage floor alone is insufficient to fight this dynamic.\par \par \uc2 Moving away from data transparency creates a huge hole in the bill. \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://minnesotareformer.com/2024/05/21/heres-whats-in-the-bill-regulating-uber-and-lyft-driver-pay-and-labor-standards/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly lobbied}}}\uc2  to block any guarantee of minimum earnings on every trip. Instead, ride-hail firms will top off drivers whose average earnings are below the minimum during a two-week pay period. And while the bill codifies pay transparency on the driver\u8217\'20\'19s end, it dropped the ordinance\u8217\'20\'19s requirement that ride-hail firms make regular and unrestricted data disclosures to Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 After New York City introduced its wage floor, an hourly net income of $17.22, \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  responded by introducing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vice.com/en/article/pkewqb/the-lockout-why-uber-drivers-in-nyc-are-sleeping-in-their-cars"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a tiered quota system}}}\uc2  and forcing a lockout, a coercive strategy where an employer denies employees work until they accept new terms. Drivers were forced to work substantially longer hours to receive priority for scheduling shifts \u8211\'20\'13 the more trips done each day, the better chance a driver had to schedule shifts during peak business hours. Drivers who failed to meet quota requirements simply weren\u8217\'20\'19t allowed to use the apps. The program was a wild success for the companies. Its first phase in June 2019 to March 2020 forced 8,000 drivers off each platform thanks to intentionally deteriorated labor conditions.\par \par \uc2 Misclassified without basic protections and managed by algorithms powered by pervasive surveillance, drivers lived in cars and pushed their bodies to the limit to eke out a living even with a wage floor.\par \par \uc2 The heart of the on-demand labor model is about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nyujlpp.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/JLPP-23.3-Buck.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 preserving lopsided power dynamics}}}\uc2  between these firms, passengers, drivers and cities. Any deal that sidesteps questions of driver misclassification, data extraction and algorithmic management is, at best, temporary.\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  are deft at reducing debates and proposals to superficial treatments. The temptation to follow along is understandable; a recent UC Berkeley Labor Center study {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://laborcenter.berkeley.edu/gig-passenger-and-delivery-driver-pay-in-five-metro-areas/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2  drivers in major US metro areas consistently earn starvation wages \u8211\'20\'13 they need relief now. And yet, the success \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  have enjoyed in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://slate.com/business/2024/05/uber-lyft-gig-economy-driver-classification-business-taxes-unemployment.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dodging billions in business taxes}}}\uc2 , in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.law.harvard.edu/lpr/wp-content/uploads/sites/89/2022/05/3-Dubal.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reshaping labor law}}}\uc2 , and in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://prospect.org/culture/books/2024-01-16-uber-impoverished-public-expectations-review/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 capturing institutions meant to regulate them}}}\uc2 , suggest the companies will always find a workaround. We\u8217\'20\'19ve been led to believe there is no alternative, that policy proposals \u8211\'20\'13 municipal or state-owned ride-hail, expanded public transit \u8211\'20\'13 will fall short.\par \par \uc2 In reality, it is the digital ride-hail model that\u8217\'20\'19s fundamentally broken. It is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://americanaffairsjournal.org/2019/05/ubers-path-of-destruction/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 inefficient and expensive, exploitative and discriminatory}}}\uc2 . It does not work without {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2020/sep/11/why-uber-and-lyft-are-taking-a-page-out-of-big-tobaccos-playbook-in-labor-law-battle"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a deregulated market}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/dae2b90e-1ba0-4e8f-aabc-34aae4ca05d7?ref=disconnect.blog"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 exorbitant investor subsidies}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nybooks.com/online/2024/05/09/inside-uber-political-machine/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an expansive political machine}}}\uc2  to protect it. In almost every city where it\u8217\'20\'19s been allowed to fester, ride-hailing has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/think/opinion/uber-convenient-city-commuters-bad-cities-ncna995626"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decreased the quality of urban transit}}}\uc2 , contributed to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/business/story/2020-02-21/cities-traffic-uber-and-lyft"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 big increases in congestion and pollution}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jacobin.com/2022/02/app-gig-economy-travel-nursing-shortage-lean-staffing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 degraded working conditions in additional industries}}}\uc2 . To what end? The empowerment of saboteurs who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://slate.com/technology/2023/03/silicon-valley-bank-rescue-venture-capital-calacanis-sacks-ackman-tantrum.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pocket billions while offloading every possible cost on to the public}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Somewhere else, there will certainly be another capital strike. Ride-hailing robs drivers of dignity by misclassifying and exploiting them until they fight back. Next time, will it end with another \u8220\'20\'1Ccompromise\u8221\'20\'1D that leaves the core of this strategy unperturbed? Or will we finally reject the Faustian bargain and risk something new at the expense of the parasitic corporations holding countless cities, passengers and drivers hostage?\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240618ek6h00335\par }\page {