\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re not backing down\u8217\'20\'19: US grocery workers take on \uc2 Kroger\uc2  with strike vote\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 880 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 June 2024\par 19:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Food 4 Less\uc2  workers to vote on action if new deal is not reached, as employees say wages and benefits lag behind\par \par \uc2 About 6,000 grocery store workers are set to vote on strike action after the expiration of their union contract with the \uc2 Kroger\uc2 -owned \uc2 Food 4 Less\uc2  chain.\par \par \uc2 The contract with \uc2 United Food and Commercial Workers (UFCW)\uc2  expired on Saturday. Members are voting on whether to authorize an unfair labor practice strike if a new deal is not reached, and results expected late on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Food 4 Less\uc2  is already {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ufcw770.org/internal-news/food4lessunion-busting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 advertising}}}\uc2  for strike replacement workers in anticipation of a work stoppage.\par \par \uc2 Wage increases are among the biggest issues under dispute, with workers claiming their wages and benefits have lagged behind other grocery stores in the area.\par \par \uc2 Clara Vega has worked at \uc2 Food 4 Less\uc2  in Los Angeles for more than 30 years, where she is currently a warehouse clerk and a member of the bargaining committee.\par \par \uc2 She said workers are fighting for fair wages comparable to other grocery stores in the area, and improvements to safety and staffing. She said she had been frustrated by how \uc2 Food 4 Less\uc2  has treated workers during the bargaining process.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe work our butts off and they come back at us with nothing that can help us. We\u8217\'20\'19re tired of living paycheck to paycheck. My daughter is going to college soon and I\u8217\'20\'19m trying to figure out how to pay for that. I can barely pay my mortgage. It\u8217\'20\'19s really hard, and we just want what\u8217\'20\'19s fair,\u8221\'20\'1D said Vega.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need a better contract. I am so frustrated with this company. They keep undermining [us] and doing these sneaky tactics at the bargaining table. We\u8217\'20\'19re tired. We worked through the Covid-19 pandemic and they didn\u8217\'20\'19t even want to give us hazard pay.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Several union members had family members die from Covid-19 as grocery store workers were hailed as essential workers and branded as \u8220\'20\'1Cheroes\u8221\'20\'1D, even as grocery chains saw {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/markets/us/us-regulators-urge-congress-look-into-grocery-profits-2024-03-21/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 surges}}}\uc2  in profits, she said.\par \par \uc2 Susan Hernandez has worked at \uc2 Food 4 Less\uc2  in Los Angeles for 30 years. Before that, she worked at \uc2 Viva\uc2 , a grocery chain acquired by \uc2 Kroger\uc2  in the 1990s where she was laid off and then recalled, losing her seniority pay.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey cut my pay from $18.11 an hour to $11.35 an hour,\u8221\'20\'1D said Hernandez. \u8220\'20\'1CThirty-plus years later, I\u8217\'20\'19m only at $24.21 an hour, so over 30 years that\u8217\'20\'19s only an increase of $12 and some change.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She criticized the lack of pay increases in comparison to \uc2 Kroger\uc2  chief exceutive \uc2 Rodney McMullen\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s salary of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.grocerydive.com/news/kroger-mcmullen-ceo-compensation-2023-grocery/716570/#:~:text=Kroger%20Chairman%20and%20CEO%20Rodney,a%20securities%20filing%20on%20Wednesday."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $15.7m}}}\uc2  in 2023. \uc2 Kroger{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://d18rn0p25nwr6d.cloudfront.net/CIK-0000056873/6b0fa036-24ed-4c1e-b7de-e02ca22d356c.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  a $3.1bn profit in 2023 and over $4.1bn in 2022. The company is continuing to push an acquisition of the grocery chain \uc2 Albertsons\uc2 , which the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2024/02/ftc-challenges-krogers-acquisition-albertsons"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sued}}}\uc2  to block, alleging the merger would result in price increases for consumers and less competition, and would negatively impact thousands of workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re all standing strong and letting the company know that it has been too many years of accepting 10-cent raises, 15-cent raises. It\u8217\'20\'19s to the point we all realize and feel how inflation is hurting us,\u8221\'20\'1D Hernandez. \u8220\'20\'1CI know if we were to go on strike, we will have the loyalty of the customers. We\u8217\'20\'19re a part of their family, they\u8217\'20\'19re part of our family, and many have said they will not cross the picket line. \uc2 Kroger\uc2  needs to realize we\u8217\'20\'19re not backing down.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 UFCW\uc2  Local 770, grocery clerks at \uc2 Food 4 Less\uc2  stores make $4.30 an hour less an hour, receive fewer benefits, and are guaranteed fewer hours than clerks at Ralph\u8217\'20\'19s stores in the area. Ralph\u8217\'20\'19s is also owned by \uc2 Kroger\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The union criticized the pay discrepancies and \uc2 Kroger\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s statements in support of diversity and inclusion, given that \uc2 Food 4 Less\uc2  stores predominantly employ Black and Latino workers and serve lower-income communities of color.\par \par \uc2 A 2022 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://economicrt.org/publication/hungry-at-the-table/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey}}}\uc2  of \uc2 Kroger\uc2  grocery workers by the Economic Roundtable found that 14% of \uc2 Kroger\uc2  workers have or were experiencing homelessness, and nearly two-thirds reported not making enough money to cover basic expenses every month.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou have these people bending over backwards for these companies and not being compensated,\u8221\'20\'1D said Chris Watkins, a meat-cutter who has worked at \uc2 Food 4 Less\uc2  in Inglewood, California, for six years. \u8220\'20\'1CWe shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have to go to work and stress, your workers should be able to come to work and breathe. There are a lot of people underwater right now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 Food 4 Less\uc2  said the company\u8217\'20\'19s final and best offer includes wage increases of $3.25 an hour over the next three years and no healthcare cost increases.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are deeply disappointed that UFCW Southern California chose to leave the bargaining table before contract expiration, rather than working together to prioritize the needs of their members \u8211\'20\'13 our valued associates,\u8221\'20\'1D the spokesperson said in an email.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt remains our goal to put more money in our associates\u8217\'20\'19 pockets, maintain industry leading healthcare, and provide retirement benefits for their futures.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240612ek6c001h2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
