\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Migrant workers \u8216\'20\'18fear for their safety\u8217\'20\'19 after deaths on Diego Garcia\b0\par\par\uc2 Katie McQue \par \uc2 1,160 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 June 2024\par 08:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers for US defence contractor KBR concerned after colleagues die on island with no hospital-grade health facility\par \par \uc2 Migrant workers employed by the US defence contractor KBR on the British-owned island of Diego Garcia have expressed concerns for their safety after the recent deaths of two of their colleagues, the Observer has learned.\par \par \uc2 The most recent death on Diego Garcia, which is host to a strategic American military base in the British Indian Ocean Territory, came on 5 January. Relemay Fabula Gan, 41, from the Philippines, died after suffering a collapsed lung following several weeks of illness after a Covid diagnosis, her family said.\par \par \uc2 Diego Garcia does not have a hospital-grade health facility; workers and military personnel who become seriously ill on the island are typically airlifted to a hospital in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Gan had been sick for several weeks on the base before her death. Her family and colleagues believe she could have been saved if she had been taken off the island for treatment. The family said Gan had no underlying health conditions and was an award-winning amateur cyclist.\par \par \uc2 KBR said that Gan\u8217\'20\'19s death was a tragedy. In a statement, it highlighted that she died after being sent to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://diegogarcia.tricare.mil/Health-Services/Urgent-Emergency-Care"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US military clinic on Diego Garcia}}}\uc2  for medical care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe requested a medevac for her the same day the military doctor requested it. There was no delay in ordering the medevac once KBR received the request from the military doctor,\u8221\'20\'1D a KBR spokesperson said. \u8220\'20\'1CAll of the medical resources available on Diego Garcia were used to try to prevent the tragic loss of Ms Gan.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Gan died several hours before the medevac \u8211\'20\'13 medical evacuation \u8211\'20\'13 flight landed. The situation has led to fears among workers that their lives could be at risk if they suffer a medical emergency.\par \par \uc2 KBR, previously known as \uc2 Kellogg Brown & Root\uc2 , is the main contractor for the base, where it employs about 1,200 workers from the Philippines, India, Kenya and Mauritius.\par \par \uc2 An {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/19/family-still-wait-for-return-of-migrant-workers-body-after-unexplained-death-on-uk-us-military-base"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Observer investigation in November}}}\uc2  revealed the death of Saddam Ali, 33, an Indian employee of KBR at the base. His body was held on the island for seven weeks before being repatriated to his family in Delhi.\par \par \uc2 The family were given Ali\u8217\'20\'19s autopsy report in March. It revealed that he died from sepsis, which had developed after he contracted a bacterial infection \u8211\'20\'13 most likely caused by an insect bite \u8211\'20\'13 on a trip home to India.\par \par \uc2 The day before he died, military doctors attempted to place Ali on a portable ventilator, but it \u8220\'20\'1Cwould not function correctly to deliver the required pressure support\u8221\'20\'1D, according to the autopsy report, which the Observer has seen.\par \par \uc2 Ali was pronounced dead on 18 October, after suffering three cardiac arrests within 12 hours. In a statement, a company spokesperson said that Ali was under the care of the military clinic when he died, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CKBR did all that it could to assist the family in this difficult and tragic time\u8221\'20\'1D. The \uc2 US Department of Defense\uc2  did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Migrant workers recruited for jobs in Diego Garcia are not given formal work visas for their jobs on the island. When they are hired, KBR provides Indian recruits with flights and tourist visas to Bahrain and it then flies them from there to Diego Garcia on a military flight, according to several workers interviewed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are telling lies to the Indian immigration authorities that we are going to visit Bahrain. We are hiding from the Indian government where we work,\u8221\'20\'1D one Indian worker said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf I told the truth to the immigration authorities, they would not allow us to travel because there is no visa in our hands.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A KBR spokesperson said that all employees on Diego Garcia \u8220\'20\'1Chave the documentation and government authorisations required for them to work on the base. We follow applicable laws and regulations to employ people on Diego Garcia\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Workers said that KBR employees who become ill are typically airlifted to a designated hospital in Singapore. Indian workers require a visa to enter the country. Some Indian workers are worried they do not have the necessary documentation to be quickly evacuated to other countries in medical emergencies, they said.\par \par \uc2 A KBR spokesperson said: \u8220\'20\'1CSingapore issues emergency visas for emergency medical matters, and it is only one of several locations where Diego Garcia employees can be medevacked.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The company did not respond to questions about how long it would take to secure a Singaporean visa for a sick worker.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn general, while waiting on an answer from Singapore or any other country, we do everything in our power to identify the fastest option,\u8221\'20\'1D the spokesperson said. \u8220\'20\'1CAll of KBR\u8217\'20\'19s Diego Garcia employees from India, Kenya and elsewhere have the documentation and government authorisations required for them to work on the base.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 KBR began recruiting Kenyan and Indian workers for Diego Garcia in 2022. Some of these workers allege they had been charged recruitment fees of up to $1,500 (\u163\'00\'A31,200) for their jobs by brokers in their home countries.\par \par \uc2 They claimed that a Kenyan broker demanded that workers pay this fee in instalments from their monthly salaries after they began work on the island. The broker placed his wife in a KBR job on Diego Garcia to further intimidate them into repaying, several workers said.Payment of recruitment fees is a significant indicator of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ilo.org/global/topics/labour-migration/news-statements/WCMS_682734/lang--en/index.htm#:~:text=The%20definition%20reiterated%20that%20recruitment,including%20trafficking%20and%20forced%20labour."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 possible labour trafficking}}}\uc2  since it can trap workers in oppressive jobs while paying off their debts, according to the UN\u8217\'20\'19s International Labour Organization.\par \par \uc2 KBR launched an investigation after the Observer approached it in January with allegations that employees had paid to get their jobs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe completed our investigation into this matter and have taken corrective action,\u8221\'20\'1D said the spokesperson. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have zero tolerance for labour trafficking of any kind, including the payment of recruiting fees, and we always strive to operate with integrity in accordance with our values.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As a result of KBR\u8217\'20\'19s investigation, the broker\u8217\'20\'19s wife has been dismissed from her job and removed from Diego Garcia. Some Kenyan workers said they were too scared to return home for holidays because of threats and intimidation from the angered recruitment broker.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe threatened me and said \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19ll kill you\u8217\'20\'19 if I talk about it,\u8221\'20\'1D said one worker, who provided transaction records, text messages and emails to verify their account.\par \par \uc2 The US\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.acquisition.gov/far/52.222-50"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 federal acquisition regulations for combatting trafficking in persons}}}\uc2  prohibit government contractors and their subcontractors and brokers from charging recruitment fees.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRecruitment fees that may lead to involuntary servitude of foreign workers are prohibited by law, executive order and federal regulations,\u8221\'20\'1D said Latesha Love-Grayer, director of International Affairs and Trade at the \uc2 US Government Accountability Office\uc2  (GAO), which is the audit institution of the federal government.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe US has a zero-tolerance policy for these types of potential trafficking activities.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240612ek69002mv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
