\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US adds 272,000 jobs as labor market holds unexpectedly strong\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani in New York \par \uc2 605 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 June 2024\par 15:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Figures show unexpected resiliency of job market as Fed keeps interest rates high try to tamp down 3.4% inflation\par \par \uc2 The US economy added 272,000 jobs in May, a sign the labor market remained strong amid high interest rates, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  announced on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The number of May jobs was far higher than the 190,000 economists had expected and topped {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/may/03/us-april-jobs-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April\u8217\'20\'19s}}}\uc2  gains, when a revised 165,000 jobs were added to the economy. The report offered a mixed view of the jobs market. The unemployment rate rose to 4% for the first time since January 2022, up from 3.9% in April.\par \par \uc2 The White House celebrated the news, which comes amid enduring {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/22/poll-economy-recession-biden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 public skepticism }}}\uc2  about the economy. \u8220\'20\'1CThe great American comeback continues, but we still have to make more progress,\u8221\'20\'1D Joe Biden said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1COn my watch, 15.6 million more Americans have the dignity and respect that comes with a job.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/unemployment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Unemployment}}}\uc2  has now been at or below 4% for more than two years \u8211\'20\'13 the longest streak in over 50 years. But the pace of hiring had slowed considerably in recent months, from 315,000 new jobs in March to 175,000 new jobs a month later.\par \par \uc2 That month-over-month decrease was taken as good news by Wall Street, as a cooling labor market could encourage the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to cut interest rates. The latest report is likely to reinforce the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s resolve to keep rates high.\par \par \uc2 Though Wall Street had long hoped interest rates would come down by the summer, officials at the Fed continue to be skeptical about a rate decrease amid disappointing inflation figures. Inflation was at 3.4% in May, still above the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s targeted rate of 2%.\par \par \uc2 Previous signs of cooling in the labor market included the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 job openings and labor turnover survey from earlier this week, which showed there were just over 8m open job positions in the US at the end of April, the lowest number since February 2021 and down 1.8m over the year.\par \par \uc2 According to the executive outplacement firm \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.challengergray.com/blog/job-cuts-announced-by-us-based-companies-flat-in-may-2024-hiring-falls-to-lowest-ytd-since-2014/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 layoff figures}}}\uc2  have held steady, with US-based employers announcing 63,816 jobs cuts in May, around the same number of cuts announced in April. Layoffs are down 20% from this time last year, when a handful of major tech companies were announcing job cuts.\par \par \uc2 The Fed will hold its next federal open market committee meeting next week and is expected to announce on 12 June whether officials will change interest rates. Interest rates are currently at 5.25% to 5.5%, the highest rates in nearly two decades.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/european-central-bank"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 European Central Bank}}}\uc2  cut its interest rate for the first time in almost five years but few expect the Fed to follow suit this week.\par \par \uc2 At their last meeting in early May, Fed officials {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/may/01/federal-reserve-interest-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  inflation was still too high above their targeted rate of 2% to cut rates. In May, inflation stood at 3.4% \u8211\'20\'13 higher than its peak of 9.1% in June 2022, but also above the rate in June 2023, when it hit 3%. Inflation has not been below 2% since February 2021.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is likely that gaining confidence will take longer than previously expected,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , the Fed chair, said after the meeting in May.\par \par \uc2 Federal officials are trying to manage a tough balancing act in seeking to bring price increases down to a manageable level without offsetting the labor market too much.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240607ek67001ur\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
