\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US dollar rallies as strong jobs report dampens rate cut hopes \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 6,830 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 June 2024\par 14:48 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 US economy created 272,000 jobs in May, many more jobs than expected, but unemployment rate has risen to 4%UK house prices dipped 0.1% in May, up 1.5% year-on-year\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 Time to wrap up\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/useconomy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US economy}}}\uc2  added 272,000 jobs in May, a sign the labor market continued strong amid high interest rates, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  announced on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The number of May jobs was far higher than the 190,000 economists had expected and topped {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/may/03/us-april-jobs-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 April\u8217\'20\'19s}}}\uc2  gains, when a revised 165,000 jobs were added to the economy. The report offered a mixed view of the jobs market. The unemployment rate rose to 4% for the first time since January 2022, up from 3.9% in April.\par \par \uc2 The White House celebrated the news, which comes amid enduring {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/22/poll-economy-recession-biden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 public skepticism }}}\uc2  about the economy. \u8220\'20\'1CThe great American comeback continues, but we still have to make more progress,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COn my watch, 15.6 million more Americans have the dignity and respect that comes with a job.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the full story:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/jun/07/may-jobs-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US adds 272,000 jobs as labor market holds unexpectedly strong amid high interest rates}}}\par \par \uc2 Goodnight, and have a good weekend. GW\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 The US dollar is pushing higher against sterling, knocking the pound down by almost three-quarters of a cent to $1.2722.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 The White House council of economic advisors has published a thread on X, pulling out the key points from today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report:\par \par \uc2 enltrToday\u8217\'20\'19s employment report shows the U.S. economy added 272,000 jobs in May, well above expectations. Revisions to prior months\u8217\'20\'19 estimates were relatively small on net. Through May, the average three-month gain in payrolls was a very healthy 249,000. 1/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/YRrsIjesEe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/YRrsIjesEe}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1799067176441377158?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrEmployment gains were widespread across industries, but were led by education and health, government, and leisure and hospitality sectors. See today\u8217\'20\'19s blog on industry dispersion in employment gains for more details. 2/\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1799067179431993770?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThe unemp. rate ticked up to 4.0% in May. The broadest measure of underemployment, U-6\u8212\'20\'14including those working part-time for economic reasons and the marginally attached\u8212\'20\'14remained steady at 7.4%. 3/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/RmcClUOaH7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/RmcClUOaH7}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1799067181952737355?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThe unemp. rate has been at or below 4.0% for 30 months, the longest such stretch since 1970.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate\u8217\'20\'19s recovery and persistent low level stands out relative to prior recoveries, as it started much higher, fell further, and has stayed lower. 4/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/gCtFsoMEOB"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/gCtFsoMEOB}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1799067184989368639?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThe labor force participation rate (LFPR) fell 0.2 ppt to 62.5% and employment-population (EPOP) fell by 0.1 ppt to 60.1%. These declines were likely driven by young workers 16-24, whose EPOP fell by 1.2 ppt. 5/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/MSJJgbrid6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/MSJJgbrid6}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1799067187564675102?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrHhold. employment fell 456,000 when adjusted to payroll-survey basis. The figure shows a few recent months where these two series have similarly diverged. Analysts consider the payroll survey to send a more reliable signal with a 90% conf int of 130K compared to hhold\u8217\'20\'19s 620K. 6/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Jt4WydgXYE"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Jt4WydgXYE}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1799067190244831553?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrMay was another strong month for prime-age workers. The prime-age (25-54 year-olds) LFPR ticked up to 83.6%. 7/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ENCgri65zT"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/ENCgri65zT}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1799067193096966478?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrFor prime-age women, we saw record LFPR and EPOP for series that began in 1948. LFPR increased 0.1 ppt to 78.1% and EPOP increased by 0.2 ppt to 75.7%. 8/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/tRf7xkwHAb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/tRf7xkwHAb}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1799067195726831992?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrNominal average hourly earnings growth was a bit stronger than expected at 0.4% in May. Year-on-year, nominal wage growth ticked up to 4.1%, still lower than the average in the last half of 2023 (4.4%), consistent with a slowly cooling labor market. 9/\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1799067197597417747?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrReal wages continue to rise, with yearly wage growth outpacing price growth for 12 months in a row. Inflation data for May have not been released yet, but wages likely outpaced inflation over the year in May too. 10/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/fOBmP3TstM"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/fOBmP3TstM}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1799067199661080793?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrFinally, in May, the unemployment rate of Black workers\u8212\'20\'14which has been very volatile of late\u8212\'20\'14ticked up to 6.1%. However, the 2.6 ppt gap between the Black and white unemployment rate remains below its 2019 average of 2.8 ppt. 11/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/P9UE0HqKsj"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/P9UE0HqKsj}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1799067201884102965?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 enltrWomen's employment rates rose to another record high in May! What a historic recovery from the depths of the pandemic {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/BE3s6OpNc5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/BE3s6OpNc5}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Joey Politano ?????? (@JosephPolitano) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JosephPolitano/status/1799057642868080751?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cvery strong jobs report\u8221\'20\'1D has cast further doubt on the prospect of US interest rate cuts this year, reports \uc2 James Knightley\uc2 , chief international economist at ING :\par \par \uc2 He explains:\par \par \uc2 US May non-farm payrolls came in at 272k versus the 180k consensus and higher than any of the 77 forecasts submitted to Bloomberg \u8211\'20\'13 the range was 120-258k. Private payrolls rose 229k versus the 165k consensus expectation.\par \par \uc2 There were 15k of downward revisions to the past 2 months for headline payrolls, but this is still an undeniably strong set of numbers that has seen market interest rate cut expectations reduce significantly.\par \par \uc2 The gains were once again led by the usual suspects of private education & health services (+86k), leisure & hospitality (+42k) and government (43k). Average hourly earnings rose 0.4%MoM/4.1%YoY versus 0.2/3.9% in April and also hotter than expected. This jump likely received a boost from recent minimum wage increases in California.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden: the great American comeback continues,\par \par \uc2 The White House has issued a statement on the May Jobs Report, highlighting job creation under the current administration.\par \par \uc2 In it, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says:\par \par \uc2 The great American comeback continues, but we still have to make more progress. On my watch, 15.6 million more Americans have the dignity and respect that comes with a job. Unemployment has been at or below 4% for 30 months\u8212\'20\'14the longest stretch in 50 years. And a record high share of working-age women have jobs.\par \par \uc2 I will keep fighting to lower costs for families like the ones I grew up with in Scranton. I\u8217\'20\'19m fighting corporate greed by calling on corporations with record profits to lower prices\u8212\'20\'14as Target and \uc2 Walmart\uc2  have for grocery prices. I\u8217\'20\'19m fighting to make rent more affordable by building 2 million new homes. I\u8217\'20\'19m fighting to lower the cost of health care and prescription drugs, like insulin and inhalers.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Republicans have a different vision\u8212\'20\'14one that puts billionaires and special interests first. The Republican plan would increase inflation by repealing the Affordable Care Act, siding with Big Oil to raise utility bills, letting Big Banks rip off Americans, and blow up the debt by slashing taxes for billionaires. I will never stop fighting for Scranton\u8212\'20\'14not Park Avenue.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a possibility that the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  may not be able to cut interest rates at all this year, suggests Janet Mui, head of market analysis at wealth manager \uc2 RBC Brewin Dolphin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At the end of 2023, investors expected a flurry of rate cuts in 2024, but inflation has remained subbornly high while economic growth has also held up pretty well.\par \par \uc2 Mui says:\par \par \uc2 All in all, today\u8217\'20\'19s bumper jobs number and sticky wage growth still skew toward the Fed keeping a tight policy stance. It will not give the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  the confidence it needs to cut rates anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 Markets are still pricing in at least one rate cut by the end of the year but there is a real possibility of no cut at all this year. All eyes are on the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s economic projections, including its expectations for the path of the Fed funds rate next week.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 Wall Street opens lower after hot jobs report\par \par \uc2 Wall Street has opened in the red, as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/jun/07/uk-house-prices-stable-may-interest-rates-us-jobs-report-employment-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-6662fcdd8f08d846f761a921#block-6662fcdd8f08d846f761a921"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 today\u8217\'20\'19s strong US jobs report}}}\uc2  dampens hope of an early interest rate cut.\par \par \uc2 The news that US payrolls swelled by a greater-than-forecast 272,000 in May, while average earnings rose by 0.4% in the month, is weighing on stocks.\par \par \uc2 At the start of trading, the Dow Jones industrial average of 30 large US companies lost 74 points, or 0.2%, to reach 38,811.99 points.\par \par \uc2 The broader S&P 500 index has dropped by 0.3%, while the technology-focused Nasdaq is down 0.35%.\par \par \uc2 David Goebel, investment strategist at wealth manager Evelyn Partners, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis report caps off a mixed bag of a labour market signals this week that does little to make the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s job easier.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe headline non-farms number is a big upside surprise on the estimate, indicating a very healthy level of job creation. These gains were also broad-based, with the diffusion index, which measures the breadth of gains across industries, jumping to 63.4% in May from 56.6% in April.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CStronger than expected wage gains also contributed to the prevailing sticky inflation story.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt the same time, the unemployment rate ticked higher to 4.0%, its highest since January 2022. This looks to be a function of decreasing household employment, which fell 408k, because the participation rate took a dip to 62.5%, which would have exerted downward pressure on unemployment.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.13pm BST\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/jun/07/uk-house-prices-stable-may-interest-rates-us-jobs-report-employment-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-6662fcdd8f08d846f761a921#block-6662fcdd8f08d846f761a921"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s US jobs report}}}\uc2  is \u8220\'20\'1Ca genuine surprise\u8221\'20\'1D compared with market expectations, says \uc2 Daniele Antonucci\uc2 , chief investment officer at \uc2 Quintet Private Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Antonucci adds:\par \par \uc2 Payrolls exceeded the highest predictions in the major forecasters\u8217\'20\'19 polls, the unemployment rate held steady rather than rising, and wage growth was stronger than consensus both on a month-on-month and year-on-year basis.\par \par \uc2 The latest figures don\u8217\'20\'19t show any degree of softening, which was the baseline market scenario. Taken at face value, they represent a hurdle to the view that the labour market is cooling off.\par \par \uc2 In turn, the implication is that the jobs picture is currently standing in the way of a Fed rate cut or two later this year.\par \par \uc2 To be clear, one number doesn\u8217\'20\'19t make a trend and, on balance, the higher rates stay elevated for longer, the more likely it is economic growth and inflation will slow.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 Jobs report 'slams the door shut on a July rate cut'\par \par \uc2 What little hope there was of a US rate cut in July (and there was never much) has been squashed by today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report.\par \par \uc2 With job creation accelerating to 272,000 last month, and earnings growth rising too, Fed rate cut hopes have been doused.\par \par \uc2 Seema Shah\uc2 , chief global strategist at Principal Asset Management, explains:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne step forward, two steps back. Today\u8217\'20\'19s data undermines the message that other recent economic data have been giving of a cooling U.S. economy, and slams the door shut on a July rate cut.\par \par \uc2 Not only has jobs growth exploded again, but wage growth has also surprised to the upside \u8211\'20\'13 both moving in the opposite direction to what the Fed needs to begin easing policy. We still expect the Fed to cut rates in September but another set of prints like today\u8217\'20\'19s would likely also take that off the table. The positive news, however, is that with a labor market this strong, the US economy is nowhere near recession territory.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 The Household Survey, released by the \uc2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  today, shows there were 6.6m unemployed people in America last month.\par \par \uc2 That lifted the unemployment rate to 4.0%\par \par \uc2 A year earlier, the jobless rate was 3.7%, and the number of unemployed people was 6.1m.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe May jobs report tells 2 different stories:\par \par \uc2 The "Household Survey" of workers shows unemployment ticking up to 4% due to fewer people employed.\par \par \uc2 The "Establishment survey" of businesses shows the huge +272,000 job gain in May. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/RxBevbjIaE"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/RxBevbjIaE}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Heather Long (@byHeatherLong) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/byHeatherLong/status/1799059689990418554?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 enltrWhere were the 272,000 job gains in May?\par \par \uc2 Healthcare +68,000\par \par \uc2 Gov't +43,000 (+34k in local gov't)\par \par \uc2 Hospitality +42,000 (restaurants were +25k)\par \par \uc2 Professional +32,000\par \par \uc2 Construction +21,000\par \par \uc2 Social aid +15,000\par \par \uc2 Retail +13,000\par \par \uc2 Transportation +11,000\par \par \uc2 Finance +10,000\par \par \uc2 Manufacturing +8,000 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/mAWKPQWtHT"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/mAWKPQWtHT}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Heather Long (@byHeatherLong) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/byHeatherLong/status/1799061321906061657?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 The bigger-than-expected 272,000 gain in non-farm payrolls in May will soothe recent fears that the bottom had suddenly dropped out of the economy, say \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They add:\par \par \uc2 With average hourly earnings increasing by 0.4% m/m last month, the Fed will remain focused on the upside risks to inflation rather than the downside risks to the real economy.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s US jobs report is \u8220\'20\'1Csomething of a mixed bag\u8221\'20\'1D, says Michael Brown, senior research strategist at Pepperstone.\par \par \uc2 A blowout +272k headline nonfarm payrolls print beating all forecasts is being somewhat overshadowed by a net -15k revision to the prior two months of data, in addition to a surprising rise in unemployment to 4.0%, the highest since January 2022.\par \par \uc2 Despite this, the report overall contained a hawkish bias, with hiring continuing apace, and earnings also growing at a hotter-than-expected 0.4% MoM, and 4.1% YoY, indicating that the labour market at large remains relatively tight.\par \par \uc2 Unsurprisingly, the market reaction has been an aggressively hawkish one, with Treasuries and equities both slumping, as the dollar rallies to fresh day highs.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 Average earnings paid to US workers rose at a faster pace than expected last month, today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report shows.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s a boost to employees but a headache for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Average hourly earnings rose by 4.1% year-on-year in May, and by 0.4% in the month alone.\par \par \uc2 enltr- May avg hourly earnings: +4.1% YoY (vs. +3.9% est., +4% prior rev up from +3.9%),\par \par \uc2 - +0.4% MoM (vs. +0.3% est., +0.2% prior). {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/USjobs?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #USjobs}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/economy?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #economy}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Inflation?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Inflation}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/FOMC?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #FOMC}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/FederalReserve?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #FederalReserve}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/TradersKE?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #TradersKE}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Powell?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Powell}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/NFP?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #NFP}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/gmFwwztWiv"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/gmFwwztWiv}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Rymond_Inc (@rymondIncKenya) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/rymondIncKenya/status/1799059698764898703?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 That also makes an early interest rate cut less likely, as solid wage growth will support inflation.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 US dollar rallying\par \par \uc2 The US dollar is rallying, as investors conclude that today\u8217\'20\'19s strong jobs report makes an early cut to US interest rates less likely.\par \par \uc2 The greenback has gained half a cent against the pound, pushing sterling down to $1.2738.\par \par \uc2 enltrUS Dollar Basket jumps on strong NFP number... {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/2EnJnxx6UM"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/2EnJnxx6UM}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 IG (@IGcom) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/IGcom/status/1799057060019200367?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 Where the US economy added jobs last month\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a breakdown on where the US economy added jobs last month, from the Bureau for Labour Statistics:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Health care added 68,000 jobs in May, in line with the average monthly gain of 64,000 over the prior 12 months. In May, employment growth continued in ambulatory health care services (+43,000), hospitals (+15,000), and nursing and residential care facilities (+11,000).\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Governmentemployment continued to trend up in May (+43,000), in line with the average monthly growth over the prior 12 months (+52,000).\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Employment in leisure and hospitality continued to trend up in May (+42,000), similar to the average monthly gain over the prior 12 months (+35,000). Employment in food services and drinking places continued to trend up over the month (+25,000).\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Professional, scientific, and technical services added 32,000 jobs in May, higher than the average monthly gain of 19,000 over the prior 12 months. Over the month, employment increased in management, scientific, and technical consulting services (+14,000) and in architectural, engineering, and related services (+10,000). Specialized design services lost 3,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Social assistance employment continued to trend up in May (+15,000), primarily in individual and family services (+11,000). Over the prior 12 months, social assistance had added an average of 22,000 jobs per month.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In May, employment in retail trade continued to trend up (+13,000), about in line with the average monthly gain over the prior 12 months (+8,000). Building material and garden equipment and supplies dealers added 12,000 jobs in May, while job losses occurred in department stores (-5,000) and furniture and home furnishings retailers (-4,000).\par \par \uc2 The jobs reports that there was little or no change in other major industries, including mining, quarrying, and oil and gas extraction; construction; manufacturing; wholesale trade; transportation and warehousing; information; financial activities; and other services.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 enltrThe US economy added 272K jobs in May 2024, much higher than a downwardly revised 165K in April, and well above forecasts of 185K\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Army Piper (@Armypiper) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Armypiper/status/1799056963948740659?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 BOOM! US jobs report shows surge in hiring\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: The US economy added 272,000 jobs in May, much more than expected.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s a rather stronger report than economists had expected \u8211\'20\'13 with Wall Street having forecast an increase of around 185,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 The latest non-farm payroll report shows that employment continued to trend up in health care; government; leisure and hospitality; and professional, scientific, and technical services.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  also reports that the US unemployment rate rose to 4% in May, from 3.9% in April. That\u8217\'20\'19s the highest rate since January 2022.\par \par \uc2 March and April\u8217\'20\'19s jobs reports have been revised a little lower too.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BLS\uc2  says:\par \par \uc2 The change in total nonfarm payroll employment for March was revised down by 5,000, from +315,000 to +310,000, and the change for April was revised down by 10,000, from +175,000 to +165,000.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 Financial markets would probably be happiest with a jobs report which showed a small slowdown in hiring \u8211\'20\'13 to encourage interest rate cuts \u8211\'20\'13 but not large enough to suggest the US economy is in trouble.\par \par \uc2 As Danish bank Saxo put it:\par \par \uc2 If headline jobs growth comes in softer than expected, markets may try to bring forward Fed rate cut expectations, and that can fuel risk-on sending stocks higher and the US dollar lower as long as growth concerns do not over-rule the rate cut narrative.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.04pm BST\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  predict the jobs report will come in below expectations.\par \par \uc2 They told clients:\par \par \uc2 We estimate nonfarm payrolls rose by 160k in May, somewhat below consensus of +185k.\par \par \uc2 When the labor market is tight, job growth tends to slow disproportionately during the spring hiring season and particularly in May\u8212\'20\'14when the seasonal factors expect more hiring than is realistic with fewer workers available\u8212\'20\'14and all five of the alternative measures of employment growth we track suggest a below-consensus report.\par \par \uc2 On the positive side, we expect a tailwind from the longer-than-usual May payroll month. While the seasonal factors in principle adjust for these effects, it appears they may have been distorted by a weak payrolls reading in May 2019, which was also 5 weeks long.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.02pm BST\par \par \uc2 A strong US jobs report today could rock the markets, warns Achilleas Georgolopoulos, investment analyst at XM :\par \par \uc2 The stock markets could go into a tailspin if there is a significant upside surprise today.\par \par \uc2 For example, a 250k+ print in the non-farm payroll figures could push the S&P 500 index decisively lower and help the 10-year \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  yield flirt with the 4.5% level again. The Fed does not like acute market reactions and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.xm.com/research/analysis/specialReports/xm/could-stronger-us-data-force-a-fed-rate-cut-special-report-197809"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 remains fearful}}}\uc2  of a repeat of the March 2023 bank failures.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 US jobs report coming up\par \par \uc2 Excitement is building in the financial markets ahead of the latest US jobs report, due in just over half an hour.\par \par \uc2 May\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payroll will show how many jobs were created across the American economy last month. It will be released at 1.30pm UK time, or 8.30am EDT.\par \par \uc2 That, and the latest wage data, will be a crucial factor in how soon the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  feels confidence to start cutting US interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Economists predict the NFP will show around 185,000 new jobs were created in May, which would be a small increase on the 175,000 in April (although that number could be revised today).\par \par \uc2 Wall Street also expects the unemployment rate to remain at 3.9% in May, while average monthly earnings growth could rise to 0.3%, from 0.2% in April.\par \par \uc2 enltrJP Morgan's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/search?q=%24JPM&src=ctag&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $JPM}}}\uc2  Game Plan for Friday's Jobs Report - they got all their angles covered here with 9 different scenarios ?? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/QeUospOSTk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/QeUospOSTk}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Barchart (@Barchart) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Barchart/status/1798893109033808180?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Henk Potts, market strategist at \uc2 Barclays Private Bank\uc2 , sets the scene\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe US economy continues to be resilient and its labour market remains in relatively rude health. Around 200,000 nonfarm payroll jobs are expected to have been created in May, compared with 175,000 registered in April, with the unemployment rate set to remain at 3.9%.\par \par \uc2 We anticipate that we could see a further softening in labour market conditions, but expect that the unemployment rate will peak at just 4% in the coming months, which would still be low when compared to historical standards.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 In other UK supermarket news, \uc2 Waitrose\uc2  has snapped up restaurant meal kit brand Dishpatch, expanding its direct-to-consumer business.\par \par \uc2 The finish-at-home meal kit business was founded in 2020 and offers customers meals designed by celebrity chefs including Angela Hartnett, \uc2 Michel Roux Jr\uc2  and Rick Stein.\par \par \uc2 Waitrose\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the grocery arm of the \uc2 John Lewis Partnership\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 said it sees \u8220\'20\'1Csignificant opportunities\u8221\'20\'1D for Dishpatch following the deal.\par \par \uc2 Dishpatch customers can order food from more than 40 menus, with food and wine delivered on Fridays and dishes pre-prepared to be finished and served at home.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.40am BST\par \par \uc2 Bank of Russia hints at rate rises after keeping borrowing costs on hold\par \par \uc2 Just in: \uc2 Russia\u8217\'20\'19s central bank\uc2  has left interest rates on hold, but threatened to raise them in coming months to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Russia has just announced it is maintaining interest rates at 16%.\par \par \uc2 It warns that inflation in Russia has stopped falling, and that domestic demand for goods and services is rising faster than the economy\u8217\'20\'19s ability to meet it (which is inflationary).\par \par \uc2 According to the estimate as of 3 June, annual inflation stood at 8.1% after 7.8% at the end of April, the Bank says.\par \par \uc2 And it drops a clear signal that rates could be raised soon, saying:\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Russia holds open the prospect of increasing the key rate at its upcoming meeting. Furthermore, returning inflation to the target will require a significantly longer period of maintaining tight monetary conditions in the economy than it was forecast in April.\par \par \uc2 According to the Bank of Russia\u8217\'20\'19s forecast and given the monetary policy stance, annual inflation will return to the target in 2025 and stabilise close to 4% further on.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.26am BST\par \par \uc2 Bundesbank cuts German growth forecasts\par \par \uc2 Germany\u8217\'20\'19s central bank\uc2  has cut its growth forecasts, in another blow to Europe\u8217\'20\'19s largest economy.\par \par \uc2 The Bundesbank now expects growth of just 0.3% in 2024, below the 0.4% forecast in December.\par \par \uc2 For 2025, it now sees growth of 1.1%, weaker than the 1.2% forecast before.\par \par \uc2 The Bundesbank says thee German economy is \u8220\'20\'1Cslowly regaining its footing after a roughly two-year period of weakness\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 As Bundesbank president \uc2 Joachim Nagel\uc2  puts it:\par \par \uc2 The German economy is extricating itself from the period of economic weakness.\par \par \uc2 Nagel adds that \u8220\'20\'1CWe on the ECB Governing Council are not driving on auto-pilot when it comes to interest rate cuts.\u8221\'20\'1D, a day after the first cut in eurozone interest rates in five years.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/jun/06/european-central-bank-cuts-main-interest-rate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 European Central Bank cuts main interest rate by 0.25 points}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.54am BST\par \par \uc2 TDR Capital\uc2  becomes majority owner of UK supermarket \uc2 Asda\uc2  as Issa brothers split\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: the long-anticipated business break-up of the billionaire brothers Zuber and \uc2 Mohsin Issa\uc2  has been agreed.\par \par \uc2 Zuber Issa\uc2  has sold his 22.5% stake in \uc2 Asda\uc2  supermarket, which the brothers bought in a leveraged buyout in 2020, to fellow shareholder \uc2 TDR Capital\uc2 , the private equity firm.\par \par \uc2 Thsi will take \uc2 TDR Capital\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s total shareholding to 67.5%. \uc2 Mohsin Issa\uc2  will continue to own 22.5%, with \uc2 Walmart\uc2  owning the remaining 10%\par \par \uc2 This follows report of a rift between the brothers, following the breakdown of Mohsin\u8217\'20\'19s marriage which is said to have \u8220\'20\'1Csent shockwaves\u8221\'20\'1D through the family.\par \par \uc2 And in another development, \uc2 EG Group\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the petrol station and convenience store chain founded by the brothers \u8211\'20\'13 has just announced the sale of its 'remaining UK forecourt business\u8217\'20\'19 to \uc2 Zuber Issa\uc2  for \u163\'00\'A3228m. He is also stepping down as co-CEO of the business.\par \par \uc2 Zuber Issa\uc2  says:\par \par \uc2 Since Mohsin and I, alongside \uc2 TDR\uc2 , took ownership of \uc2 Asda\uc2 , we have driven a period of significant investment and entrepreneurial growth activity. Notably, \uc2 Asda\uc2  acquired a market-leading UK convenience retail and foodservice store business from \uc2 EG Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With the divestment of my \uc2 Asda\uc2  shares, I will now turn my attention towards leading and managing the remaining EG UK forecourt sites that I have personally acquired, and spend more time on my charitable endeavours.\par \par \uc2 There were reports back in February that such a deal was being explored.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/feb/04/issa-brother-eyes-sale-asda-stake-focus-petrol-station-business"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Issa brother \u8216\'20\'18eyes sale\u8217\'20\'19 of Asda stake to focus on petrol station business}}}\par \par \uc2 The takeover of \uc2 Asda\uc2  by the Issa brothers and \uc2 TDR\uc2  put billions of extra debt onto \uc2 Asda\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s balance sheet.\par \par \uc2 Today, the supermarket says:\par \par \uc2 Asda\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s net debt at the end of Q124 was \u163\'00\'A33.8bn, net of more than \u163\'00\'A31bn cash on the balance sheet \u8211\'20\'13 and the business is fully committed to further deleveraging.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.19am BST\par \par \uc2 We also have confirmation that the eurozone returned to growth in the last quarter, but didn\u8217\'20\'19t expand as fast as the UK.\par \par \uc2 Data bosy \uc2 Eurostat\uc2  has reported that eurozone GDP rose by 0.3% in January-March, in line with its previous estimates, after a 0.1% fall in October-December.\par \par \uc2 enltrEuro area {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/GDP?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #GDP}}}\uc2  up by 0.3% in Q1 2024, +0.4% compared with Q1 2023 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/aFCUMWTXT3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/aFCUMWTXT3}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/n9hXlSX2Fu"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/n9hXlSX2Fu}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 EU_\uc2 Eurostat\uc2  (@EU_\uc2 Eurostat\uc2 ) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/EU_Eurostat/status/1799003369089978787?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s broadly in line with the US, which grew at an annualised rate of 1.3% in Q1 2024 (or just over 0.3% in the quarter).\par \par \uc2 But the UK posted growth of 0.6%, as the economy exited recession.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.29am BST\par \par \uc2 World food prices up in May for third month running\par \par \uc2 Global food commodity prices have risen again, in a blow to households worldwide.\par \par \uc2 The United Nations world food price index rose for a third consecutive month in May, as falling prices for sugar and vegetable oil were counted by rising prices for cereal and dairy products.\par \par \uc2 Prices rose by 0.9% in May compared with April.\par \par \uc2 But, that still left the FAO Food Price Index (FFPI) 3.4% lower than a year ago, and nearly 25% below the peak of 160.2 points reached in March 2022, after Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine drove up prices.\par \par \uc2 Cereal prices jumped by 6.3% in March, which the \uc2 UN\uc2  attribues to \u8220\'20\'1Cgrowing concerns about unfavourable crop conditions for the 2024 harvests\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cdamage to the Black Sea shipping infrastructure\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Dairy prices rose 1.8% in the month, due to \u8220\'20\'1Cincreased demand from the retail and food services sectors ahead of the summer holidays\u8221\'20\'1D, and expectations that milk production may fall below historical levels in Western Europe.\par \par \uc2 But sugar prices fell by 7.5%, due to a \u8220\'20\'1Cgood start\u8221\'20\'1D of the new harvest season in Brazil, and \u8220\'20\'1Cconducive weather conditions\u8221\'20\'1D boosting the global supply outlook.\par \par \uc2 And vegetable oil prices dropped by 2.4%, driven by a fall in the cost of palm oil as outputs across major producing countries in Southeast Asia rose.\par \par \uc2 These higher commodity costs will feed through to shoppers, and may slow the fall in food inflation.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.30am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.03am BST\par \par \uc2 There could be a \u8216\'20\'18bounce\u8217\'20\'19 in house prices this autun, predicts \uc2 Tom Bill\uc2 , head of UK residential research at \uc2 Knight Frank\uc2 , if interest rates start to fall.\par \par \uc2 Bill says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHouse prices remain under pressure as an interest rate cut moves further over the horizon. Demand will typically rise in spring but there has been a 0.3% price decline over the last three months thanks to stubborn services inflation and rising swap rates.\par \par \uc2 There should be a more noticeable bounce this autumn when the first rate cut since March 2020 is likely to have happened and the political backdrop will have stabilised. We expect UK prices to rise by 3% this year as the prospect of more mortgages starting with a \u8216\'20\'183\u8217\'20\'19 gets closer.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re not there yet, though! Here are the latest fixed-rate mortgage rates, just released by Moneyfacts :\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The average 2-year fixed residential mortgage rate today is 5.95%. This is unchanged from the previous working day.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The average 5-year fixed residential mortgage rate today is 5.52%. This is up from an average rate of 5.51% on the previous working day.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.00am BST\par \par \uc2 Boss of Magners maker C&C steps down after overseeing accounting failures\par \par \uc2 The maker of Bulmers, Magners and Tennent\u8217\'20\'19s cider has announced the shock departure of its CEO after revealing it had overstated its profits in recent years by \u8364\'20\'AC5m.\par \par \uc2 C&C told the City that detailed internal and external reviews had discovered a series of mistakes in its accounts in recent years.\par \par \uc2 This means it will take a \u8364\'20\'AC1m adjustment charge for the 2023 financial year, a \u8364\'20\'AC3m adjustment credit in FY2022 and a \u8364\'20\'AC7m adjustment charge in FY2021.\par \par \uc2 And CEO Patrick McMahon is paying the price; he\u8217\'20\'19s leave his role, having stepped up from his position as finance chief a year ago.\par \par \uc2 C&C explains:\par \par \uc2 The Group\u8217\'20\'19s Chief Executive Officer, Patrick McMahon, was Chief Financial Officer during the periods to which these adjustments relate and acknowledges that the relevant shortcomings occurred at a time when he had overall responsibility for the Group\u8217\'20\'19s finance function.\par \par \uc2 Accordingly, he has informed the Board that he will step down as CEO and as a director with immediate effect.\par \par \uc2 C&C also reported a loss of \u8364\'20\'AC111m for the last financial year, down from a profit of \u8364\'20\'AC52m a year before.\par \par \uc2 Shares in C&C are down 7% in early trading, the biggest faller on the FTSE 250 index.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.33am BST\par \par \uc2 Bellway\uc2  has also raised its forecast for the overall average selling price of its houses to around \u163\'00\'A3305,000, up from previous guidance of \u163\'00\'A3295,000.\par \par \uc2 This is \u8220\'20\'1Cmainly due to changes in product mix\u8221\'20\'1D, says \uc2 Bellway\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 meaning it expects to sell more high-end, pricier, homes.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.23am BST\par \par \uc2 UK housebuilder \uc2 Bellway\uc2  has issued an upbeat trading statement, saying it has seen \u8220\'20\'1Cstronger trading through the spring selling season\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Bellway\uc2  says \u8220\'20\'1Cimproved affordability\u8221\'20\'1D is helping to lift customer confidence, leading to an increase in reservations for new houses and \u8220\'20\'1Cfirm\u8221\'20\'1D prices.\par \par \uc2 Bellway\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s private reservation rate per outlet has risen to 0.62 per week in the February-May quarter, a 6.9% increase on a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Jason Honeyman\uc2 , \uc2 Bellway\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s CEO, says the company is on track to build around 7,500 homes this financial year, adding:\par \par \uc2 We have been encouraged by ongoing healthy levels of customer interest and combined with the strength of our outlet opening programme, we continue to expect a year-on-year increase in the forward order book at 31 July 2024.\par \par \uc2 As a result, \uc2 Bellway\uc2  remains in a strong position to return to growth in financial year 2025.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.13am BST\par \par \uc2 EY UK: housing market has passed the bottom\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2024/apr/23/leading-uk-lenders-raise-fixed-rate-mortgage-deals-amid-market-uncertainty"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The recent rise in mortgage rates}}}\uc2  is likely to prevent any meaningful pickup in transactions and prices in the short-term, predicts Peter Arnold, chief economist at EY UK.\par \par \uc2 But Arnold also suggests we won\u8217\'20\'19t see a new downturn in prices either\u8230\'20\'26 saying:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile 2023 was a challenging year for the housing market, data for the first half of 2024 has suggested that the market has passed the bottom. The substantial fall in mortgage rates since last summer, combined with strong growth in nominal wages, has reduced the scale of the mortgage affordability problem. This has helped to entice some buyers back to the market, leading to a recovery in transactions and putting a floor under prices.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COver the past couple of months, mortgage rates have edged up again in response to rising swap rates, as financial markets anticipate a slower pace of \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  interest rate cuts. This is likely to prevent any meaningful pickup in transactions and prices in the near-term. But so far, the increases have been small and mortgage rates remain well below last summer\u8217\'20\'19s peaks. The EY ITEM Club does not expect to see a renewed downturn in the housing market.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2024/apr/23/leading-uk-lenders-raise-fixed-rate-mortgage-deals-amid-market-uncertainty"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Leading UK lenders raise fixed-rate mortgage deals amid \u8216\'20\'18market uncertainty\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.56am BST\par \par \uc2 Labour's mortgage guarantee scheme to offer 'freedom to buy' to young people\par \par \uc2 Building up a deposit to buy a house is a large hurdle for first-time buyers, especially those who can\u8217\'20\'19t turn to wealthy parents for help.\par \par \uc2 But the Labour Party are pledging to help \u8211\'20\'13 last night, they annouced they would permanently sustain and expand the current mortgage guarantee scheme.\par \par \uc2 The measure sees the government act as guarantor for part of a home loan \u8211\'20\'13 to encourage lenders to make low-deposit mortgages available for first-time buyers,\par \par \uc2 Labour, whose offering is dubbed \u8216\'20\'18Freedom to Buy\u8217\'20\'19, says it will help over 80,000 young people to get on the housing ladder over the next five years.\par \par \uc2 Labour leader \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAfter 14 years of Conservative government, the dream of home ownership is out of reach for too many hard working people. Despite doing everything right, they can\u8217\'20\'19t move on and up. A generation face becoming renters for life.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy parents\u8217\'20\'19 home gave them security and was a foundation for our family. As Prime Minister, I will turn the dream of owning a home into a reality.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur changed Labour Party will be on the side of the builders not the blockers, to get Britain building again. My Labour Government will help first-time buyers onto the ladder with a new Freedom to Buy scheme for those without a large deposit, and by giving them first dibs on new developments.\par \par \uc2 Labour are also pledging planning reform to build 1.5 million homes, saying this is the best way in the long term.\par \par \uc2 They say:\par \par \uc2 We will reintroduce housing targets, build on disused grey belt land, fast track permissions on brownfield and build the next generation of new towns.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.45am BST\par \par \uc2 Over the last quarter, average UK house prices were 0.3% lower than in the previous three months, Halifax reports.\par \par \uc2 This chart shows the monthly changes we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen this year:\par \par \uc2 After jumping 1.2% in January, they grew 0.3% in February, before dropping 0.9% in March, stagnating in April and then dipping 0.1% in May.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.35am BST\par \par \uc2 North West has the strongest price growth of nation or region in the UK\par \par \uc2 House prices rose fastest in the North West of England in May \u8211\'20\'13 they were up 3.8% on an annual basis in May, taking the average price to \u163\'00\'A3232,258.\par \par \uc2 In Northern Ireland, prices rose by 3.2%, while in Scotland they were 1.9% higher than a year ago.\par \par \uc2 In Wales, house prices grew annually by +0.7% \u8211\'20\'13 below the UK average of 1.5%.\par \par \uc2 Prices fell fastest in Eastern England, where they dropped by 0.8% in the year to May.\par \par \uc2 In London, prices have risen by 0.2% over the last year, to an average of \u163\'00\'A3536,821 \u8211\'20\'13 still the most expensive part of the UK.\par \par \uc2 enltrBase rate date jitters cause average house prices to take a baby step back on April, dropping -0.1% but up +1.5% annually, making the avg house price \u163\'00\'A3288,688. The North West inc\u8217\'20\'19d +3.8%, Northern Ireland +3.2%, Scotland +1.9% & Wales +0.7% but the East of England fell -0.8% on\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/aAIWnOR2Bh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/aAIWnOR2Bh}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Emma Fildes (@emmafildes) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/emmafildes/status/1798962815174353125?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.28am BST\par \par \uc2 Introduction: UK house prices stable in May\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 Stability has returned to the UK housing market, the latest data from lender Halifax shows, with prices dipping slightly last month.\par \par \uc2 Halifax\u8217\'20\'19s latest house price index, just released, shows that average prices were little changed month-on-month in May,\par \par \uc2 The average house prices slipped to \u163\'00\'A3288,688 last month, a 0.1% drop compared to \u163\'00\'A3288,862 in April.\par \par \uc2 But on an annual basis, house price inflation rose to +1.5% in May, up from +1.1% in April.\par \par \uc2 Rising wages and a pick-up in economic confidence are supporting house prices, says Amanda Bryden, head of mortgages at Halifax.\par \par \uc2 Bryden explains:\par \par \uc2 This has been reflected in a broadly stable picture in terms of property price movements, with the average cost of a property little changed over the last three months.\par \par \uc2 Bryden adds that a period of relative stability in both house prices and interest rates should lift confidence for both buyers and sellers.\par \par \uc2 While homebuyers and those remortgaging will continue to respond to changes in borrowing costs, set against a backdrop of a limited supply of available properties, the market is unlikely to see huge fluctuations in the near term.\par \par \uc2 Last week, rival lender {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/may/31/uk-house-prices-return-to-growth-as-market-shows-signs-of-resilience"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nationwide reported a rise in prices last month}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s important to remember, though, that both Halifax and Nationwide\u8217\'20\'19s data is based on mortgage transactions, so doesn\u8217\'20\'19t capture cash buyers.\par \par \uc2 Also coming up today\par \par \uc2 UK entrepreneur \uc2 Mike Lynch\uc2  will be celebrating after being cleared of all charges by a US jury in a fraud case related to the sale of his software company Autonomy to \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  (HP) in 2011.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/jun/06/mike-lynch-fraud-trial-acquittal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tech tycoon Mike Lynch cleared of all fraud charges in Hewlett-Packard trial}}}\par \par \uc2 Investors are poised for the latest US jobs report, which is expected to show the unemployment rate stuck at 3.9% in May.\par \par \uc2 