\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Eurozone unemployment falls to record low; US GDP growth revised down \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 6,113 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 May 2024\par 14:11 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 BCC calls for improved relations with the \uc2 EU\uc2  to cut the costs for business, while sticky inflation worries markets\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 Time to recap\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/may/30/inflation-fears-markets-ftse-uk-businesses-push-for-closer-relations-with-eu-eurozone-unemployment-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-6658727c8f086b8009a03c89#block-6658727c8f086b8009a03c89"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The US economy grew at a slower pace in the first quarter than initially reported, as consumer spending slowed}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 US GDP rose at an annualized pace of 1.3% in January-March, below the previous estimate of 1.6%, fresh \uc2 Bureau of Economic Analysis\uc2  data shows.\par \par \uc2 The data shows America\u8217\'20\'19s economy had less momentum than expected at the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 But in Europe, the jobs market remains solid. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/may/30/inflation-fears-markets-ftse-uk-businesses-push-for-closer-relations-with-eu-eurozone-unemployment-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-665841b68f08b9b585c794be#block-665841b68f08b9b585c794be"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Eurozone unemployment fell to a new alltime low last month, at 6.4%}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the UK, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/may/30/inflation-fears-markets-ftse-uk-businesses-push-for-closer-relations-with-eu-eurozone-unemployment-business-live?page=with:block-66581c938f082cc3ef068399#block-66581c938f082cc3ef068399"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the British Chambers of Commerce is urging the next government to make improving relations}}}\uc2  with the \uc2 European Union\uc2  a key part of its drive to revive the economy.\par \par \uc2 In the financial markets, the FTSE 100 has shrugged off {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/may/30/inflation-fears-markets-ftse-uk-businesses-push-for-closer-relations-with-eu-eurozone-unemployment-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66580f388f082cc3ef068325#block-66580f388f082cc3ef068325"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fears over sticky inflation}}}\uc2 , and is up 46 points or 0.55% in afternoon trading.\par \par \uc2 But in New York, Wall Street is being held back by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/may/30/inflation-fears-markets-ftse-uk-businesses-push-for-closer-relations-with-eu-eurozone-unemployment-business-live?page=with:block-665885e38f08b090cee99843#block-665885e38f08b090cee99843"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a 19% slump in Salesforce\u8217\'20\'19s shares }}}\uc2  after it missed analyst forecasts last night.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/article/2024/may/30/next-uk-government-should-introduce-scams-legislation-says-city-lobby-groups"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Next UK government should introduce scams legislation, says City lobby group}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/may/30/dr-martens-will-cut-costs-to-counter-weak-us-sales"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dr Martens will cut up to \u163\'00\'A325m in costs to counter weak US sales}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/may/30/why-policymakers-are-more-likely-to-risk-high-inflation-during-periods-of-economic-uncertainty"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Why policymakers are more likely to risk high inflation during periods of economic uncertainty | Kenneth Rogoff}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/may/30/call-for-uk-crackdown-on-plastic-packaging-to-drive-green-economy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Call for UK crackdown on plastic packaging to drive green economy}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.11pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.02pm BST\par \par \uc2 Over on Wall Street, shares in enterprise software maker \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  have plunged by 19% after it disappointed analysts last night.\par \par \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  reported revenues of $9.13bn for the first quarter of this year, below forecasts of $9.15bn.\par \par \uc2 It also predicted that second quarter revenue would be between $9.2bn and $9.25bn, below analyst forecasts of $9.35bn.\par \par \uc2 enltrSalesforce drops on first-ever single-digit sales growth outlook {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/MULXjJQE2J"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/MULXjJQE2J}}}\par \par \uc2 ?? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/search?q=%24CRM&src=ctag&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $CRM}}}\uc2  -19.60% {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/OEwucRbLQH"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/OEwucRbLQH}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Yahoo Finance (@YahooFinance) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/YahooFinance/status/1796180037701390385?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Analysts at Saxo say:\par \par \uc2 [\uc2 Salesforce\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s] revenue outlook came in lower-than-expected highlighting that the current demand for AI workloads is not currently translating into higher revenue growth for \uc2 Salesforce\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 Top brass at \uc2 Burberry\uc2  have paid the price for a disappointing year for the luxury goods maker.\par \par \uc2 Burberry\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s annual report, released, today, shows that chief executive Jonathan Akeroyd had not earned a bonus for last year.\par \par \uc2 This pulled Akeroyd\u8217\'20\'19s total pay for the year to 31 March 2024 down to \u163\'00\'A31.3m, from \u163\'00\'A34.3m the previous 12 months (when he had received a \u163\'00\'A31.3m bonus and \u163\'00\'A31.7m to buy him out of bonus and share awards for leaving his position as boss of rival Versace).\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/fashion/2021/oct/20/burberry-appoints-versace-boss-as-ceo-jonathan-akeroyd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Burberry appoints Versace boss as CEO with \u163\'00\'A36m \u8216\'20\'18golden hello\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 Danuta Gray\uc2 , chair of \uc2 Burberry\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Remuneration Committee, explains:\par \par \uc2 The Committee judged that progress was made on refining our brand image, evolving our product and strengthening distribution, resulting in some of the strategic objectives being partially met. However, in light of the business performance and broader shareholder experience, the Committee and Jonathan Akeroyd agreed that it would not be appropriate for him to receive an annual bonus for FY 2023/24.\par \par \uc2 CFO \uc2 Kate Ferry\uc2  would receive an annual bonus for FY 2023/24 of \u163\'00\'A3121,500, representing 9% of her maximum bonus.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/may/15/burberry-profits-slump-demand-for-luxury-goods-slows"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Burberry\u8217\'20\'19s profits fell by 40% last year}}}\uc2  ; in the last 12 months, its share price has halved.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 US jobless claims inch up\par \par \uc2 The number of Americans filing new claims for unemployment benefits has risen slightly, but still remains low on historical terms.\par \par \uc2 There were 219,000 fresh \u8216\'20\'18initial claims\u8217\'20\'19 for jobless support last week, an increase of 3,000.\par \par \uc2 The initial claims data is a proxy for the number of workers laid off by US companies and has been low in recent years as firms have held onto labo(u)r.\par \par \uc2 enltrUS new weekly unemployment claims last week were 219,000. That was +3,000 WoW. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/a3yko0DCVF"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/a3yko0DCVF}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 RonH (@Ronh999) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Ronh999/status/1796159690235105581?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s US GDP report also shows that disposable personal income by 5.3% in the first quarter\par \par \uc2 Inflation took quite a bite out of that increase; real disposable personal income increased 1.9%. Both figures have been revised higher.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 US GDP growth revised down as consumer spending slows\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: The US economy grew more slowly than first thought at the start of this year.\par \par \uc2 US GDP increased at an annualized rate of 1.3 percent in the first quarter of 2024, according to the second estimate of gross domestic product, just released. That\u8217\'20\'19s the equivalent of quarterly growth just over 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 enltr??Breaking! US GDP Growth Revised Lower: The US economy expanded at an annualized rate of 1.3% in Q1 2024, down from the initial estimate of 1.6%, and significantly lower than the 3.4% growth recorded in Q4 2023. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/MM?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #MM}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/0DTVOvVBT2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/0DTVOvVBT2}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 MacroMicro (@MacroMicroMe) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MacroMicroMe/status/1796158720621092951?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Previousl, the \uc2 Bureau of Economic Analysis\uc2  had estimated that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/apr/25/us-gdp-growth-slows"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 GDP rose at an annualized rate of 1.6%}}}\uc2 , or 0.4% quarter-on-quarter, which was already the weakest rate in almost two years.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s update primarily reflected a downward revision to consumer spending, says the \uc2 BEA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 enltrThe U.S. economy grew at a 1.3% annualized rate in Q1, down from the 1.6% rate estimated a month ago. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/qHGf5n47pp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/qHGf5n47pp}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/GDP?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #GDP}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 BEA News (@BEA_News) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BEA_News/status/1796157297363742916?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s report shows that consumer spending, exports, and state and local government spending all decelerated in the January-March quarter; a downturn in federal government spending also weighed on growth.\par \par \uc2 But, this was partly offset by an acceleration in residential fixed investment.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 Speaking of fiscal rules (see previous post), Oxford economics professor Sir \uc2 Dieter Helm\uc2  last week published {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://dieterhelm.co.uk/economics/both-main-political-parties-have-committed-to-fiscal-rules-but-are-their-rules-credible/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a very interesting blog post}}}\uc2  about the fiscal rules, looking at how both parties are pledging to be responsible.\par \par \uc2 But, he warns, this involves the \u8220\'20\'1Cfudge\u8221\'20\'1D of relabelling capital maintenance as investment.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://dieterhelm.co.uk/economics/both-main-political-parties-have-committed-to-fiscal-rules-but-are-their-rules-credible/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Prof Helm writes}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Both claim that the days of having the cake of tax reductions and the cake of higher spending are over. Tax cuts will mean spending cuts and spending increases will mean higher taxes. No more \uc2 Jeremy Corbyn\uc2 , \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  and \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 . The sinners have apparently repented.\par \par \uc2 Confronted by the promise of a miracle, here are several possible responses. The first is simply to believe them. The sinners have repented. \u8220\'20\'1COh Lord, I promise to be a good and a trustworthy steward of state finances. I won\u8217\'20\'19t sin, I promise, even if I/my Party has repeatedly done so in the past. This time will be different, here are my fiscal rules and I promise to stick to them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The second is to assume that, when the inevitable expenditure pressures become irresistible, taxes are going to go up. The pressures on health spending, more defence, net zero, and workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights in the public sector will simply overwhelm the next government, and possibly as early as spring 2025. Tax cuts will prove unsustainable. Tax increases will be inevitable.\par \par \uc2 The third is to assume that more borrowing will be resorted to, and that it will be the bond markets that bite back, imposing ex post discipline in response to ex ante breaking the new rules. The trick will be to continue pretending that current spending can be relabelled as \u8220\'20\'1Cinvestment\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 enltrFiscal rules are a good idea in a country struggling to meets its private and public means.\par \par \uc2 The rules set out by the Conservatives and Labour are, however, less than they seem.\par \par \uc2 My latest "Fiscal rules \u8211\'20\'13 the cakeism approach" is now available ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/EWpBq6rcfO"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/EWpBq6rcfO}}}\uc2  ). {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/IFHRPkhars"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/IFHRPkhars}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Prof \uc2 Dieter Helm\uc2  CBE (@Dieter_Helm) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Dieter_Helm/status/1792859002516238627?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 21, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 NIESR: Fiscal rules must be reformed\par \par \uc2 The next government must revise the fiscal framework in order to raise living standards and unlock spending on health, infrastructure and housing, according to the National Institute of Economic and Social Research.\par \par \uc2 In January, \uc2 Keir Starmer{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2024/jan/04/keir-starmer-labour-fiscal-rules-green-investment-targets"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled out breaking Labour\u8217\'20\'19s fiscal rules to meet its green investment targets if it wins the election}}}\uc2 . But the current rules are arbitrary, NIESR said, and must be broken or redesigned if the government\u8217\'20\'19s aim is higher growth and higher living standards.\par \par \uc2 The UK fiscal framework was set up with the aim of making public debt sustainable, in other words to ensure that the government is borrowing at a level that means it can meet its payment obligations while having the room to respond to economic shocks.\par \par \uc2 The current framework states that the debt-to-GDP ratio should be falling in the fifth year of forecasts by the \uc2 Office for Budget Responsibility\uc2 , and that net government borrowing should not exceed 3% of GDP.\par \par \uc2 NIESR said that such fiscal targets are arbitrary. The Institute for Government has said that successive governments have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.instituteforgovernment.org.uk/publication/strengthening-uk-fiscal-framework"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gamed the fiscal rules}}}\uc2 , by claiming to be adhering to fiscal discipline while announcing short term giveaways and longer term tax rises or spending cuts that never materialise.\par \par \uc2 Many long-term public investment projects do not make returns within five years, NIESR said, which stymies public investment that could help improve the debt to GDP ratio.\par \par \uc2 To revitalise the economy, NIESR is proposing three pillars of fiscal policy reform.\par \par \uc2 First, including the net worth of the public sector as a target, which provides a broader measure of the sustainability of public debt. Second, discounting public infrastructure investment from the deficit measure, separating it out from day-to-day spending.\par \par \uc2 Third, the government could establish fixed dates for fiscal events like the budget, in the same way that interest rates are decided by the monetary policy committee at set dates in the calendar. This would insulate budget announcements from short-term political goals, like winning elections, at the expense of long-term economic strategy, NIESR said.\par \par \uc2 enltr??And we are off, with our Deputy Director {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AdrianPabst1?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @AdrianPabst1}}}\uc2  setting the scene outlining the main issues the UK economy is currently facing ???? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/UKeconomy?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #UKeconomy}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/GeneralElection?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #GeneralElection}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/GE2024?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #GE2024}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/RoyalEconSoc?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @RoyalEconSoc}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Jrzu9DuKvu"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/Jrzu9DuKvu}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/O6ZQzQMm1f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/O6ZQzQMm1f}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 National Institute of Economic and Social Research (@NIESRorg) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NIESRorg/status/1796121024330629309?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrNow over to Ben Caswell who is presenting the macroeconomic context of the UK economy\par \par \uc2 According to our forecast, the Govt will not meet its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/fiscal?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #fiscal}}}\uc2  targets ??\par \par \uc2 Some of the utility of the fiscal targets are lost here... that's why a new framework is needed ?? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/GE2024?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #GE2024}}}\uc2  \u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/7nkn0ubCjg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/7nkn0ubCjg}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 National Institute of Economic and Social Research (@NIESRorg) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NIESRorg/status/1796122743269675253?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrA new framework based on 3? main points ?? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/PublicInvestment?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #PublicInvestment}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/FiscalEvents?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #FiscalEvents}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/PublicSectorNetWorth?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #PublicSectorNetWorth}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/GE2024?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #GE2024}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/GeneralElection?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #GeneralElection}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Jf6Y1n9pm0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Jf6Y1n9pm0}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 National Institute of Economic and Social Research (@NIESRorg) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NIESRorg/status/1796123259378843678?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s manufacturers have also issued a manifesto for the warring political parties to digest.\par \par \uc2 Make UK are hoping for a \u8220\'20\'1Cbold economic vision\u8221\'20\'1D that puts the importance of the economy across every Ministerial portfolio. That, they say, would spur growth and help the UK compete with the US and China in the race to develop green technologies.\par \par \uc2 Make UK are calling for five specific measures in the first 100 days of the new government \u8211\'20\'13 including a focus on where to stay aligned with Europe, and where to diverge\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Announce a long-term modern Industrial Strategy to underpin all economic policymaking which has cross Government commitment. This should be backed by the re-introduction of an Industry Strategy Council and a new \uc2 Cabinet Office\uc2  backed Committee to ensure the implementation of the Strategy across Government\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Align\uc2  the UK CBAM (Carbon Border Adjustment Mechanism) with the EU CBAM in terms of timescale and design to provide a level playing field with the \uc2 EU\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Commence a root-and-branch review of the Apprenticeship Levy as a funding mechanism, as well as the wider apprenticeship system\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Re-establish an updated, modern Manufacturing Advisory Service\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Establish a mechanism for ongoing and active consultation with industry to decide where it is appropriate to maintain alignment with \uc2 EU\uc2  regulatory changes or, where opportunities for divergence might apply.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.23am BST\par \par \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  allots another record amount in short-term repos\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  facility which gives commercial banks cheap access to funds has seen record demand, again, amid worries that the money markets are running short of cash.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BoE\uc2  allocated over \u163\'00\'A317bn of one-week funds in its latest {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/markets/bank-of-england-market-operations-guide/our-tools#:~:text=Short%2DTerm%20Repo%20(STR),-The%20STR%20is&text=The%20STR%20allows%20participants%20to,liquid%20(Level%20A)%20assets."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 short-term repo operation }}}\uc2  this morning, the largest take-up since the facility was launched in October 2022.\par \par \uc2 The repo operation allows banks to borrow from the \uc2 BoE\uc2  at Bank rate, in return for handing over high-quality assets as collateral.\par \par \uc2 It is meant to keep short-term market interest rates in line with Bank Rate by ensuring banks don\u8217\'20\'19t need to pay more to access reserves in the money markets.\par \par \uc2 But there are concerns that banks are tapping the short-term repo because market liquidity which is being drained as the \uc2 BoE\uc2  continue to sell bonds acquired through years of quantitative easing (QE). This \u8220\'20\'1Cquantitative tightening\u8221\'20\'1D (QT) scheme involves the Bank selling bonds back to the market, leading to a drop in liquidity.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/46e0e773-4a01-4120-9de3-4ffb74733431"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Financial Times reported yesterday}}}\uc2  that analysts at Barclays, Bank of America and \uc2 NatWest\uc2  believe this scarcity of cash could cause the \uc2 BoE\uc2  to slow down the process of shrinking its balance sheet when it reviews its QT policy in September.\par \par \uc2 enltrBank of England bond sales blamed for cash shortage {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/JO88uu8rYd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/JO88uu8rYd}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 FT Breaking News (@ftbreakingnews) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ftbreakingnews/status/1795757528845824509?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 29, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.39am BST\par \par \uc2 In another boost for Europe\u8217\'20\'19s economy, euro-area economic confidence has improved this month.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European Commission\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s monthy sentiment indicator has risen to 96 this month, up from 95.6 in April. However, economists had expected a larger rise.\par \par \uc2 Readings for industrial confidence also ticked up.\par \par \uc2 roltrEurozone Consumer Confidence May F: -14.3 (prev -14.3)\par \par \uc2 - Services Confidence May: 6.5 (est 6.5; prev 6.5)\par \par \uc2 - Industrial Confidence May: -9.9 (est -9.6; prev -10.5)\par \par \uc2 - Economic Confidence May: 96.0 (est 96.1; prev 95.6)\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 LiveSquawk (@LiveSquawk) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LiveSquawk/status/1796104801886908812?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.32am BST\par \par \uc2 Europe\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cstrong job market\u8221\'20\'1D is driving its economic recovery, says \uc2 Bert Colijn\uc2 , senior economist for the eurozone at ING :\par \par \uc2 Following {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/may/30/inflation-fears-markets-ftse-uk-businesses-push-for-closer-relations-with-eu-eurozone-unemployment-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-665841b68f08b9b585c794be#block-665841b68f08b9b585c794be"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 this morning\u8217\'20\'19s drop }}}\uc2  in the eurozone unemployment rate, Colijn says:\par \par \uc2 Unemployment was already sitting near historic lows during the period of stagnation. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/may/30/inflation-fears-markets-ftse-uk-businesses-push-for-closer-relations-with-eu-eurozone-unemployment-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-665841b68f08b9b585c794be#block-665841b68f08b9b585c794be"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 In April, unemployment dropped from 6.5 to 6.4%,}}}\uc2  once again reaching the lowest point since the eurozone was founded in 1999. The strong job market is helping the economic recovery as it keeps wage growth elevated, and has allowed purchasing power to recover after the inflation spike.\par \par \uc2 The question is whether the economic pickup will result in lower unemployment from here. We doubt it. Expectations for employment for both services and industry have actually trended down despite better economic prospects and we expect that the modest economic recovery will largely result in productivity gains.\par \par \uc2 enltrWeakening hiring expectations for businesses in the eurozone while the economy is picking up suggests that unemployment is unlikely to trend lower but that a much anticipated productivity pickup is imminent. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/1RtLRo1DbT"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/1RtLRo1DbT}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Bert Colijn\uc2  (@BertColijn) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BertColijn/status/1796109777073733877?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.32am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.24am BST\par \par \uc2 Eurozone unemployment rate falls to record low\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: unemployment across the eurozone has fallen to a new record low, a week before Europeans head to the polls for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/may/09/what-are-the-eu-elections-and-why-do-they-matter"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the European Parliament elections}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Statistics body \uc2 Eurostat\uc2  reports that the seasonally-adjusted unemployment rate across the euro area fell to 6.4% in April, down from 6.5% in March. That\u8217\'20\'19s the lowest since the single currency was created, \uc2 Eurostat\uc2  data shows.\par \par \uc2 In the wider EU, the jobless rate remained at 6%.\par \par \uc2 This suggests that the higher interest rates imposed by the \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2  to fight inflation is not hurting the labour market.\par \par \uc2 roltrEuro area {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/unemployment?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #unemployment}}}\uc2  at 6.4% in April 2024, \uc2 EU\uc2  at 6.0% {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/1znbPeuR5z"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/1znbPeuR5z}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/kBD983Ycni"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/kBD983Ycni}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 EU_\uc2 Eurostat\uc2  (@EU_\uc2 Eurostat\uc2 ) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/EU_Eurostat/status/1796104272624414873?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Eurostat\uc2  estimates that 13.149 million persons in the EU, of whom 10.998 million in the euro area, were unemployed in April, adding:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Compared with March 2024, unemployment decreased by 103 thousand in the EU and by 100 thousand in the euro area.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Compared with April 2023, unemployment increased by 95 thousand in the EU and decreased by 101 thousand in the euro area.\par \par \uc2 Across the EU, unemployment was highest in Spain (at 11.7%) and Greece (10.8%) in April, and lowest in Czechia (2.7%) and Poland (3%).\par \par \uc2 At its peak, during the eurozone crisis in 2013, eurozone unemployment rose over 12%, but then fell steadily \u8211\'20\'13 before a brief spike in the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.29am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.04am BST\par \par \uc2 Back me for low interest rates, \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  claims\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  has been doing its best to keep out of the general election campaign.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week it cancelled all speeches by its policymakers for the duration of the upcoming 4 July General Election, meaning we shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t hear any public statements from central bank officials until the public has voted.\par \par \uc2 That would prevent the Bank committing news, by commenting on the state of the economy, or hinting when it might lower borrowing costs.\par \par \uc2 Now, though, \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  has piled into the void by claiming that a vote for the Tories is a vote for cuts to interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Sunak told The Times:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COf course it is, because we are the party who has committed to bringing down inflation, which is a necessary condition for bringing down interest rates.\par \par \uc2 And I think people can see we have delivered that. And that allows us to think about the future in a more positive and confident way.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrRishi Sunak has claimed that a vote for his governing Conservative Party at the UK general election is a vote for interest rate cuts, drawing the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  into a campaign it has been trying to avoid {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/eiUM7eMPV2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/eiUM7eMPV2}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bloomberg (@business) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/business/status/1796070994676707519?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Inflation is a funny beast \u8211\'20\'13 politicians are desperate to blame global factors when it\u8217\'20\'19s going up, but rush to take the credit when it falls.\par \par \uc2 And either way, as the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  is independent, it gets the credit for controlling prices (and the blame for losing control).\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Labour are warning that they would be starting from a difficult position if they win the election.\par \par \uc2 Darren Jones\uc2 , Labour\u8217\'20\'19s shadow Treasury minister, ruled out rises to income tax, national insurance or VAT, adding:\par \par \uc2 The fiscal inheritance is going to be really hard, it\u8217\'20\'19ll be the worst that any party has inherited since the second world war.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2024/may/30/uk-general-election-conservatives-labour-rishi-sunak-keir-starmer-jeremy-hunt-politics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Keir Starmer gives speech as Labour rules out rises to income tax, national insurance and VAT \u8211\'20\'13 UK politics live}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.05am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.40am BST\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Do not travel\u8217\'20\'19 alert issued after freight train derails\par \par \uc2 A \u8220\'20\'1Cdo not travel\u8221\'20\'1D alert has been issued after a freight train derailed on a major route between England and Scotland, PA Media reports.\par \par \uc2 Services are being cancelled and delayed because of the incident on the West Coast Main Line between Carlisle and Oxenholme Lake District on Wednesday night, National Rail Enquiries added.\par \par \uc2 National Rail says:\par \par \uc2 A derailed freight train between Carlisle and Oxenholme Lake District means some lines are blocked.\par \par \uc2 Trains may be cancelled, revised or severely delayed by up to 60 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Major disruption is expected until 14:00.\par \par \uc2 Train operators Avanti West Coast and TransPennine Express issued the \u8220\'20\'1Cdo not travel\u8221\'20\'1D alert, urging passengers not to attempt to use their services for journeys between Preston and Scotland on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Ticket holders can delay journeys until Friday or travel with other operators.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.37am BST\par \par \uc2 UK cost of living squeeze eases\par \par \uc2 The cost rises hammering UK households has slowed in the last quarter.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  reports that UK household costs, as measured by the Household Costs Index (\uc2 HCI\uc2 ), rose 4.4%, slowing from the annual rate of 5.3% in January 2024.\par \par \uc2 That chimes with the latest inflation data, which showed a slowdown in price rises this year.\par \par \uc2 Interestingly, higher income households saw the highest inflation rate.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the details:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 By comparison, household costs rose 5.0% for high-income households (decile 9) and 3.9% for low-income households (decile 2).\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 By tenure type, mortgage and other owner occupier households had the highest annual inflation rate of 5.5%, reflecting rising mortgage interest payments; by contrast, the rate for outright owner occupiers was the lowest at 3.3%.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Private renters\u8217\'20\'19 \uc2 HCI\uc2  inflation rate was higher than social and other renters, at 4.6% and 4.3% respectively; this follows a period between October 2023 and January 2024 where the two groups had similar inflation rates.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Non-retired households continued to experience a higher annual rate of inflation (4.8%) than retired households (3.4%).\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Households with children\u8217\'20\'19s annual inflation rate fell to 4.8% while households without children fell to 4.2%.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.23am BST\par \par \uc2 Zoopla: more homes for sale will keep prices in check\par \par \uc2 In the property sector, the number of homes for sale in the UK is at the highest level in eight years, according to website Zoopla.\par \par \uc2 Zoopla reports that the supply of homes for sale is 20% higher than this time last year, with \u163\'00\'A3230bn worth of housing for sale, up \u163\'00\'A345bn on this time last year.\par \par \uc2 This is likely to keep a lid on house price rises this year, Zoopla suspects, even though the upcoming general election could slow the pace of new sales in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe pre-election lead-up is expected to slow momentum for agreed sales, which are currently up 13% on last year, but not stall it. Despite house price inflation over the last quarter improving +0.4%, annually it remained negative at -0.1%. As stock levels increase at a higher\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/9jJr8WMkpE"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/9jJr8WMkpE}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Emma Fildes (@emmafildes) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/emmafildes/status/1796073061122269236?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Zoopla also reports that there were modest house price falls across Southern England, as the north/south divide in annual house price growth continues.\par \par \uc2 They say:\par \par \uc2 This split in house price inflation is most evident at a city level with the the strongest house price growth in Belfast (+3.6%), Burnley (+2.5%) and Bolton (2.4%), and the highest house price falls in Ipswich (-3%), Hastings (-2.7%) and Norwich (-2.4%).\par \par \uc2 enltrWell-stocked property shelves enable buyers to shop around reducing markups and facilitating discounts in southern areas where budgets can no longer afford the bullish price tag and second homes have become burdened with additional tax. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Zoopla?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @Zoopla}}}\uc2  May HPI 2024 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/G7N1Bx6UB5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/G7N1Bx6UB5}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Emma Fildes (@emmafildes) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/emmafildes/status/1796073058161004990?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.21am BST\par \par \uc2 TSB\uc2 : Issue is resolved\par \par \uc2 Those IT problems at \uc2 TSB\uc2  appear to be solved!\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 TSB\uc2  spokesperson tells us:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re aware some customers had issues logging into our app and online banking this morning. This issue is now resolved and we\u8217\'20\'19re sorry for any inconvenience it caused.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.59am BST\par \par \uc2 TSB\uc2  customers report problems with online banking\par \par \uc2 Another day, another problem with Britain\u8217\'20\'19s banking apps.\par \par \uc2 Today it\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 TSB\uc2  whose customers are reporting problems accessing their accounts.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://downdetector.co.uk/status/tsb/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 According to Downdetector,}}}\uc2  there was a spike in reports of glitches at \uc2 TSB Bank\uc2  this morning.\par \par \uc2 Many customers are posting on social media that they can\u8217\'20\'19t get their app, or internet banking, to work:\par \par \uc2 enltr {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TSB?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @TSB}}}\uc2  great time for the system to do down. Can\u8217\'20\'19t log into my app or internet banking\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 ashlie Cunningham (@ashliec1985) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ashliec1985/status/1796070815915520032?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltr {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TSB?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @TSB}}}\uc2  your app is down again. Reads my face then just stays on the login screen.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Lara Boyd (@2008_LJB) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/2008_LJB/status/1796069026306625706?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 TSB\uc2  is telling customers that it\u8217\'20\'19s aware they are experiencing issues with its digital services, and working hard to resolve it.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 TSB\uc2  spokesperson says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re aware that some customers have had difficulty logging into the app and online banking. We\u8217\'20\'19re sorry for any inconvenience and are working hard to resolve it as soon as possible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 They added that the issues were \u8220\'20\'1Cintermittent\u8221\'20\'1D meaning that in some cases, customers were blocked but later able to get through on subsequent login attempts.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, \uc2 NatWest\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s banking app crashed for several hours, leaving thousands of frustrated customers across the UK without access to their accounts.\par \par \uc2 TSB\uc2  customers are used to technical problems; in 2018, a planned IT upgrade turned into a full-scale crisis, with up to 1.9 million customers locked out of their accounts for days.\par \par \uc2 City regulators later fined {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/tsb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 TSB}}}\uc2  \u163\'00\'A348m for \u8220\'20\'1Cwidespread and serious\u8221\'20\'1D failings in its IT migration.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2018/apr/24/tsb-customers-online-banking-chaos-accounts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 TSB bank faces large compensation bill and fines over online chaos}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.12am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.35am BST\par \par \uc2 Banknote and passport printer \uc2 De La Rue\uc2  is in talks with suitors interested in buying each of its core divisions following a strategic review.\par \par \uc2 The Basingstoke-based group, which prints banknotes for the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  and other central banks across the world, said it has spoken with \u8220\'20\'1Ca number of parties who have made proposals\u8221\'20\'1D related to either its currency or authentication operations.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, it stressed that there is no certainty that interest will result in any deal.\par \par \uc2 Clive Whiley\uc2 , chairman at \uc2 De La Rue\uc2 , told shareholders this morning:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSince my appointment a year ago, the board has considered a broad range of possible strategic alternatives including transactions with multiple parties which may involve a combination with, or the sale of, the group\u8217\'20\'19s divisions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe board confirms that the discussions with the relevant parties are advancing, and we expect to update further at the time of the full year results in July.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 De La Rue\uc2  shares have jumped 5% this morning.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.36am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.29am BST\par \par \uc2 Shares in online car marketplace \uc2 Autotrader\uc2  are roaring ahead, after it reported a jump in sales and earnings for last year.\par \par \uc2 Autotrader\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s revenues increased by 14% in the year to 31 March, while operating profits increased by 26%.\par \par \uc2 Autotrader\uc2  benefited from a \u8220\'20\'1Crobust\u8221\'20\'1D market for used cars, which are selling faster than before the pandemic as supplies gradually improved.\par \par \uc2 The new car retail market has been more challenging, though, with discounting starting to return to the market.\par \par \uc2 Autotrader\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s share have jummped 12% this morning, making it the top riser on the FTSE 100 share index.\par \par \uc2 Keith Bowman, equity analyst at interactive investor, says:\par \par \uc2 High interest rates mean it can still be expensive for people to finance the purchase of a vehicle, and it\u8217\'20\'19s interesting to see \uc2 Google\uc2  taking an interest in the UK motoring market. Costs generally for businesses also remain elevated, while potential for forecourt consolidation persists as operators such as Cargiant and \uc2 Motorpoint\uc2  look to grow.\par \par \uc2 On the upside, Auto Trader is still the dominant market player and continues to spend on innovation. More and more dealers are trialling its \u8216\'20\'18Deal Builder\u8217\'20\'19 product, which allows car buyers to value their part exchange vehicle, apply for finance, and reserve a vehicle online. Group net bank debt has also reduced significantly.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.29am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.16am BST\par \par \uc2 In London, the FTSE 100 index has opened in the red, down 16 points at 8167 points, the lowest since 2 May.\par \par \uc2 European markets are also sagging, with the Stoxx 600 down 0.2% in early trading.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.24am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.06am BST\par \par \uc2 Rand weakens as South Africa election results come in\par \par \uc2 South Africa\u8217\'20\'19s rand has fallen over 1% against the US dollar today, as early results from the South African general election come in.\par \par \uc2 Early results from yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s poll in South Africa\u8217\'20\'19s election suggest the governing \uc2 African National Congress (ANC)\uc2  party could lose its majority, although it is still expected to remain as the largest party.\par \par \uc2 enltrBloomberg reporting that \uc2 ANC\uc2  may only get 41.5% of the national vote. The rand weakens to 18.65 vs the USD (from 18.25 yesterday morning).\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Johann Biermann ???? (@JohannBiermann1) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JohannBiermann1/status/1796072517582352798?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Reuters has the details:\par \par \uc2 With results in from 10% of polling stations, the \uc2 ANC\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s share of the vote in Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s election stood at 42.3%, with the pro-business Democratic Alliance (DA) on 26.3% and the Marxist Economic Freedom Fighters (EFF) on 8.1%, data from the electoral commission showed.\par \par \uc2 If the final results were to resemble the early picture, the \uc2 ANC\uc2  would be forced to make a deal with one or more other parties to govern - a situation that could lead to unprecedented political volatility in the coming weeks or months.\par \par \uc2 The rand is down 1.2%, at 18.67 to the US dollar.\par \par \uc2 enltr {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/SouthAFrica?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #SouthAFrica}}}\uc2  : the rand slips as early results show the \uc2 ANC\uc2 's vote dropping a lot.\par \par \uc2 From ballots from 10% of voting districts counted, the \uc2 ANC\uc2 's share is 42.3%. Big drops in Gauteng, KZN and W Cape.\par \par \uc2 NB: it is still very early. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/luqitIcZ5v"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/luqitIcZ5v}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/lyGLL6lFK3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/lyGLL6lFK3}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Paul Wallace (@PaulWallace123) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/PaulWallace123/status/1796072753918861610?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.08am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.44am BST\par \par \uc2 Baroness Martha Lane Fox, president of the BCC, is urging politicians to focus on helping businesses:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn the frenzy of the election campaign, it\u8217\'20\'19s crucial that all politicians focus on the power of British business.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs I travel across the UK meeting Chambers and their businesses, I hear amazing stories of people determined to grow their businesses and make a difference in our remarkable country. But time and again businesses tell me they want to see a long-term vision for the economy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur manifesto showcases practical ideas on how politicians can help companies successfully navigate the challenges and opportunities our economy faces. It\u8217\'20\'19s a blueprint for boosting productivity and a pathway to higher growth.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhichever party is in power after July 4th the immediate focus must be on implementing our five-point-plan for business. The stakes for business from the next government could not be higher.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.39am BST\par \par \uc2 Business group says improving relations with \uc2 EU\uc2  is a priority\par \par \uc2 UK businesses are urging the next government to make improving relations with the \uc2 European Union\uc2  a key part of its drive to revive the economy.\par \par \uc2 The British Chambers of Commerce Election Manifesto, published today, cites improved relations with the \uc2 EU\uc2  as one of its five priorities, saying this would help lower costs and boost trade.\par \par \uc2 The BCC, which operates over 50 UK Chambers across the country, is calling for:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 An Industrial Strategy with green innovation at its heart.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Better skills planning, bringing businesses and training providers together.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Business rates reform to encourage growth and investment.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Improved relations with the \uc2 European Union\uc2  to cut the costs for business.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A Government appointed AI champion for SMEs to spearhead uptake of new technology.\par \par \uc2 Shevaun Haviland\uc2 , director general of the BCC, says this isn\u8217\'20\'19t about reversing Brexit, explaining\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe EU is the UK\u8217\'20\'19s biggest market, so we urgently need to get a better trading relationship with our closest neighbour. It\u8217\'20\'19s not about rewriting the referendum result, it\u8217\'20\'19s about cutting red-tape and promoting trade.\par \par \uc2 Both the Conservatives and Labour have been criticised for not focusing on the impact of leaving the \uc2 EU\uc2  in this referendum, with former deputy prime minister Michael Heseltine warning the election campaign will be the \u8220\'20\'1Cmost dishonest in modern times\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/article/2024/may/27/ignoring-brexit-will-make-election-campaign-most-dishonest-in-modern-times-says-michael-heseltine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ignoring Brexit will make election most dishonest in modern times \u8211\'20\'13 Heseltine}}}\par \par \uc2 UK food importers of food from the \uc2 EU\uc2  have warned that new post-Brexit checks introduced this spring will push up costs, and create painful delays at the border.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2024/apr/30/eu-food-importers-say-post-brexit-checks-could-increase-their-costs-by-60"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Food importers in UK say new Brexit checks could add 60% to costs}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/article/2024/may/04/uk-flower-industry-thrown-into-chaos-by-new-brexit-border-checks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK flower industry thrown into chaos by new Brexit border checks}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.37am BST\par \par \uc2 Dimon warns of stagflation risks\par \par \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 , the head of \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 , has caused further jitters by warning yesterday that the US economy risks stagnation \u8211\'20\'13 the toxic mix of rising prices and slow growth.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at a Wall Street conference yesterday, Dimon warned that the past five years of massive fiscal and monetary stimulus risked tipping the US into deflation.\par \par \uc2 Dimon said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not saying it\u8217\'20\'19s going to happen, I just give the odds much higher than other people,\u8221\'20\'1D Dimon added. \u8220\'20\'1CI look at the amount of fiscal and monetary stimulus that has taken place over the last five years\u8212\'20\'14it has been so extraordinary, how can you tell me it won\u8217\'20\'19t lead to stagflation?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt might not\u8230\'20\'26.But I, for one, am quite prepared for it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Dimon also explained that \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  is prepared both for a soft landing, and something rather bumpier, saying:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we have a soft landing and rates stay where they are, come down a little bit\u8212\'20\'14which is what the world expects, everyone\u8217\'20\'19s fine.\par \par \uc2 If you have a harder landing with stagflation you\u8217\'20\'19re going to see a lot of stress and strain in the system from banks to leveraged companies to real estate to a whole bunch of stuff.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.37am BST\par \par \uc2 Introduction: Inflation fears weigh on markets\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 Fears over sticky inflation \u8211\'20\'13 and even the dreaded stagflation \u8211\'20\'13 are hitting the markets this week.\par \par \uc2 Investors are growing more anxious that global interest rates will stay higher for longer, a worry that has knocked shares in recent days, as the yields \u8211\'20\'13 or rate of return \u8211\'20\'13 on government bonds push higher.\par \par \uc2 Last night, the UK\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE 100 index fell for the sixth day in a row and traders are bracing for further losses today.\par \par \uc2 enltrEuropean Opening Calls: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/FTSE?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #FTSE}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/DAX?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #DAX}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/CAC?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #CAC}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/AEX?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #AEX}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/MIB?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #MIB}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/IBEX?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #IBEX}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/OMX?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #OMX}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/SMI?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #SMI}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/STOXX?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #STOXX}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/whK0de9eeW"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/whK0de9eeW}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Pedro Gallego Gil (@_SirPeter) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/_SirPeter/status/1796059243591647560?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 30, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Central banks are attempting to slow growth through high interest rates to dampen inflation, but without cooling their economies so much that unemployment jumps.\par \par \uc2 But with US inflation running at 3.4% last month, still well over the 2% target, confidence that this soft landing can be achieved is faltering. Yesterday, inflation in Germany came in higher than forecast.\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2  said in a commentary:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHotter and stickier than expected global inflation appears to be taking the air out of asset markets.\par \par \uc2 In other words, \u8220\'20\'1CGoldilocks\u8221\'20\'1D coming undone. And worries about adverse demand impact from higher rates seeping through.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, a surpringly weak auction of US debt sent fresh jitters through trading floors, with buyers demanding higher rates as they bid for the bonds.\par \par \uc2 Kyle Rodda, senior financial market analyst at capital.com, explains:\par \par \uc2 A rise in global yields is forcing a re-rating in global equity prices, with Wall Street falling further overnight. Upward pressure on yields was compounded by a weak seven-year auction, reigniting fears about how the US will fund its rising deficit.\par \par \uc2 Although the European Central Ban seems certain to cut interest rate next week, both the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  and the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  are expected to delay their first cuts until the autumn.\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 8am BST: Swiss GDP report for Q1 2024\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10am BST: Eurozone unemployment report for April\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10am BST: Eurozone confidence stats for May\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10am BST: Statistics on EU trade with Ukraine\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1.30pm BST: Second estimate of US GDP for Q1 2024\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1.30pm BST: US jobless claims\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.45am BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240530ek5u000s1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
