\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 More than 800 faculty and staff at UCLA call for chancellor\u8217\'20\'19s resignation\b0\par\par\uc2 Sam Levin in Los Angeles \par \uc2 811 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2024\par 19:39 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Move comes after counter-protesters\u8217\'20\'19 attack on pro-Palestinian student demonstrators and violent police raid on encampment\par \par \uc2 More than 800 faculty and staff at the \uc2 University of California, Los Angeles\uc2  (UCLA) have called for the chancellor\u8217\'20\'19s resignation following {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/may/01/ucla-campus-violence-protests"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 attacks by counter-protesters}}}\uc2  on pro-Palestinian student demonstrators and a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/04/police-tactics-us-campus-protest-crackdowns"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 violent police raid}}}\uc2  of the Gaza solidarity encampment on campus last week.\par \par \uc2 More than a hundred professors and other teaching staff gathered on Thursday to deliver a letter in support of their students engaged in pro-Palestinian activism, demanding \uc2 Gene Block\uc2  immediately step down as chancellor and an academic senate vote of no confidence in him. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sites.google.com/view/may9uclapresskit/home"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 letter}}}\uc2  also called for authorities to drop all charges against students, staff and faculty who were involved in the encampment.\par \par \uc2 The mass faculty action at one of the most prominent public universities in the US comes as campuses across the country have been roiled by pro-Palestinian encampment demonstrations and aggressive law enforcement crackdowns during graduation season.\par \par \uc2 Holding signs that said \u8220\'20\'1CTake a walk, Block\u8221\'20\'1D, \u8220\'20\'1CUCLA faculty and staff \u8230\'20\'26 stand with our students\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1CDisclose & Divest\u8221\'20\'1D, professors from across departments recounted the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/may/01/violence-erupts-ucla-university-campus-clashes-rival-gaza-protest-groups"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brutal violence by counter-demonstrators}}}\uc2  on 30 April as police {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2024/05/03/us/ucla-protests-encampment-violence.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 failed to intervene}}}\uc2 , and the subsequent mass arrests of the pro-Palestinian demonstrators.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are outraged at the university\u8217\'20\'19s failure to protect its students from vigilante and police violence and its refusal to uphold its stated values as made evident in the forcible removal and arrest of peacefully protesting students, faculty and staff,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dan Froot, a UCLA world arts and cultures and dance professor representing senate faculty from his department.\par \par \uc2 A group of volunteer medics who helped care for demonstrators injured by police and counter-demonstrators told reporters that they had treated a wide range of injuries, including severe head lacerations, facial fractures, subarachnoid hemorrhages, rubber-bullet wounds, broken bones and asthma attacks from chemical irritants. Katherine Marino, a history professor who read a statement from faculty in her department, noted that at least 25 students had been hospitalized and roughly 200 protesters had been arrested, with police \u8220\'20\'1Cdragging visibly injured students away\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The letter also expressed solidarity with students\u8217\'20\'19 demands, calling on UCLA to publish a report within 30 days disclosing all investments and for the university to \u8220\'20\'1Cdivest from military-weapons-production companies and supporting systems\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Susan Slyomovics, an anthropology professor, read a statement on behalf of 75 Jewish faculty and staff, saying Block had \u8220\'20\'1Cmisused Jews\u8221\'20\'1D by suggesting the encampment had to be dismantled to prevent antisemitism: \u8220\'20\'1CWe assert that critiques of Israel are not presumptively antisemitic \u8230\'20\'26 that Jews who support the liberation of Palestine must not be devalued.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The faculty action comes as \uc2 UAW\uc2  local 4811, the largest union of academic workers representing 48,000 graduate student workers throughout the University of California system, is voting on a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/02/university-of-california-union-strike-vote-gaza-protests"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 possible strike}}}\uc2  over administrators\u8217\'20\'19 response to pro-Palestinian protests. Matt Barreto, a professor of political science and Chicana/o studies, said the group of professors was discussing the \u8220\'20\'1Cpossibility to withhold our own additional labor\u8221\'20\'1D until the demands are met. If graduate students go on strike, professors will not cross picket lines or do their work, he said.\par \par \uc2 UCLA did not immediately respond to a request for comment on Thursday. Block announced earlier this week the creation of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/06/ucla-protester-attack-campus-safety"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 campus safety office}}}\uc2 , headed by a former Sacramento police chief, to \u8220\'20\'1Cidentify the perpetrators of the violence and hold them to account\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A Los Angeles police department spokesperson said in an email on Friday morning that the department was \u8220\'20\'1Clooking into its involvement on campus along with other law enforcement agencies\u8221\'20\'1D and that a \u8220\'20\'1Cdetailed report\u8221\'20\'1D would be issued.\par \par \uc2 Purnima Mankekar, a UCLA anthropology professor, said she felt an obligation to defend students at the encampment. The students, she said, had gone to great lengths to de-escalate violent attacks from counter-demonstrators. \u8220\'20\'1CMy job is to make sure the learning and intellectual growth of our students goes unimpeded \u8230\'20\'26 When that gets disrupted by violence that is perpetrated on them by outside instigators or by the police, that makes me very upset,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 If students are unable to engage in peaceful protests, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe university is not a university, it\u8217\'20\'19s a police state\u8221\'20\'1D, she added. \u8220\'20\'1CThe parents of our students entrust us with them. It\u8217\'20\'19s our job to keep them safe.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It was significant that so many faculty had signed on to the letter, said Gary Segura, professor of public policy, political science and Chicano/a studies, and a former dean. \u8220\'20\'1CEven faculty who may not have agreed with the student protesters\u8217\'20\'19 underlying issues were mortified by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nymag.com/intelligencer/article/counterprotesters-shoot-fireworks-into-ucla-encampment.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 video}}}\uc2  of \uc2 LAPD\uc2  cops firing [less-lethal munitions] into the crowd. Those are students. I found it just terrifying.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240509ek5900335\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
