\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We deserve more\u8217\'20\'19: US workers\u8217\'20\'19 share of the pie dwindles\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,426 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2024\par 13:29 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  releases latest estimate of how much labor receives of national income, showing bleak decline\par \par \uc2 When Jesse Motte began working at a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  inside a Target store in Columbia, South Carolina, more than two years ago, $15 an hour sounded great. He was excited to start because it was the most he had ever made after working for years in the service industry.\par \par \uc2 The excitement has dissipated due to his inconsistent and erratic work schedule, the rising costs of necessities and the minuscule raises he and his co-workers receive annually. His most recent annual wage increase was $0.37 an hour.\par \par \uc2 Motte is not alone. This week, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/productivity/tables/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 released }}}\uc2  its latest estimate for the share labor receives of national income for the first quarter of 2024. The statistics shows the income workers receive compared with the productivity their labor generates.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.marketplace.org/2024/04/12/workers-labor-share-of-income/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 According to BLS}}}\uc2 , this income share has declined for non-farm workers from about two-thirds, 64.1% in the first quarter of 2001, to 55.8% in the first quarter of 2024.\par \par \uc2 Target\uc2  raised their company minimum wage to $15 an hour in 2017 and has since only {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://corporate.target.com/press/release/2022/02/target-to-set-new-starting-wage-range-and-expand-a#:~:text=Target%20led%20the%20retail%20industry,milestone%20nearly%20two%20years%20ago."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increased}}}\uc2  the range of starting wages, which vary from $15 to $24 an hour.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve been working here a little over two years and I still have not made it out of the $15-an-hour range,\u8221\'20\'1D said Motte. \u8220\'20\'1CLast week I got paid, my pay was about $700 for about 60 hours of work I did for two weeks. I still haven\u8217\'20\'19t bought groceries, I\u8217\'20\'19m still eating some canned food that I have.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m still eating a bunch of peanut-butter-and-jelly sandwiches, and drinking protein shakes. I\u8217\'20\'19m scared to go buy groceries because I know if I do, I\u8217\'20\'19m going to spend about $100 for not much.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Motte explained that working at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in a Target store, he often has to deal with long lines toward the end of his shift while managing the closing duties, and that he can get pulled to work in different departments within Target. He said that for the work he and his co-workers do and their role in running Target stores, their pay is not enough, especially compared with the Target CEO \uc2 Brian Cornell\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s compensation.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, \uc2 Target\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s CEO was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ca.movies.yahoo.com/target-ceo-brian-cornell-earned-141313692.html#:~:text=Target%20CEO%20Brian%20Cornell%20earned%20total%20compensation%20of%20more%20than,last%20year's%20figure%20of%20775."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 paid}}}\uc2  more than $17.6m in total compensation, 680 times more than the median worker at Target, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.progressivecaucuscenter.org/the-ceo-pay-problem-and-what-we-can-do-about-it#:~:text=In%202022%2C%20the%20ratio%20between,worker%20pay%20stood%20at%20%2461%2C900."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than double}}}\uc2  the average for S&P 500 corporations. The company {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://corporate.target.com/press/release/2024/03/target-corporation-reports-fourth-quarter-and-full-year-2023-earnings#:~:text=Full%2Dyear%20operating%20income%20of,from%20%243.8%20billion%20last%20year."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  $5.7bn in profits in 2023.\par \par \uc2 CEO pay has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/ceo-pay-in-2022/#epi-toc-2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 soared}}}\uc2  1,209.2% from 1978 to 2022, according to an analysis by the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 , compared to a 15.3% rise in typical workers\u8217\'20\'19 pay.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s part of several contributing factors to runaway income and wealth inequality that has resulted in low-income workers\u8217\'20\'19 share of the pie {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.marketplace.org/2024/04/12/workers-labor-share-of-income/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dwindling}}}\uc2  as the distribution of wages in the US has trended toward high-end wage earners receiving more and more.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have an incredibly corrupt corporate governance structure,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dean Baker, a senior economist at the Center for Economic and Policy research. \u8220\'20\'1CCEOs in the US get paid way more than their counterparts in other wealthy countries.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He cited other contributing factors, such as the fallen corporate tax rate, patent and copyright monopolies that enable industries such as pharmaceuticals to price-gouge medications that are cheap to manufacture, industries such as Wall Street that are not taxed properly and are heavily subsidized, and policies that are unfriendly to labor unions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe could structure the market differently in ways that didn\u8217\'20\'19t produce as much inequality as we have now,\u8221\'20\'1D Baker added. \u8220\'20\'1CThe bulk of the story of wage stagnation for the typical worker has been that an increasing share of wage income has gone to high-end earners.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Erica Groshen\uc2 , a senior economics adviser at \uc2 Cornell University\uc2  and the former commissioner of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , also noted the median workers\u8217\'20\'19 share of the national income had declined.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have these different components, and these influences have led both to a widening inequality and this declining share, particularly for workers on the lower end of the education spectrum,\u8221\'20\'1D said Groshen.\par \par \uc2 Rita Blalock has worked at \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  in Durham, North Carolina, for 10 years. She recently received a wage increase that she spent years fighting for, to get her over $15 an hour.\par \par \uc2 Comparatively, the \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief has one of the highest CEO-to-worker pay ratios in the world, at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mylogiq.com/archives/4298#:~:text=Based%20on%202022%20data%20provided,%2414%2C521%2C%20while%20Kempczinski%20made%20%2417%2C770%2C514."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1,224 times}}}\uc2  the median worker pay. A typical \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s worker would have to work {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sanders.senate.gov/press-releases/news-sanders-and-colleagues-introduce-legislation-to-combat-corporate-greed-and-end-outrageous-ceo-pay-2/#:~:text=Today%2C%20a%20typical%20restaurant%20employee,Kempczinski%20was%20paid%20in%202022."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 1,200 years }}}\uc2  to make his compensation for a year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe deserve more than what we are getting paid. We deserve $25 an hour. Because executives are paid seven to eight figures, so they can afford to go have fun, go on vacations. Here I am, I have to sit here and work hard for them at their store,\u8221\'20\'1D said Blalock. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are shorthanded. The customers are not happy. We tell them if pay was better, then maybe we could keep people longer and we wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have to work ourselves to death because we\u8217\'20\'19re so short-staffed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Blalock\u8217\'20\'19s sentiments are reflected in data on income and wealth inequality in recent decades.\par \par \uc2 From 1979 to 2022, wages for the top 1%of wage earners in the US rose by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/blog/wage-inequality-fell-in-2022-because-stock-market-declines-brought-down-pay-of-the-highest-earners-but-top-1-wages-have-skyrocketed-171-7-since-1979-while-bottom-90-wages-have-seen-just-32-9-growth/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 171.1% }}}\uc2  compared to 32.9% for the bottom 90% of wage earners. In the same period, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2024/01/17/opinions/inequality-earnings-economy-gould-bivens/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 share }}}\uc2  of total earnings for the bottom 90% declined 8.8% while it increased for the top 0.1% of earners by 4.6%.\par \par \uc2 Billionaires alone have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://inequality.org/great-divide/billionaire-wealth-is-soaring/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gained $2.7tn}}}\uc2  since March 2020. According to a 2020 report published by the \uc2 Rand Corporation\uc2 , the wealthiest 1% in the US have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://time.com/5888024/50-trillion-income-inequality-america/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 taken $50tn}}}\uc2  from the bottom 90% in recent decades.\par \par \uc2 Wages have failed to keep up with productivity growth: from 1979 to 2019, net productivity for US workers increased by 59.7%, while typical compensation for a worker only grew by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/blog/growing-inequalities-reflecting-growing-employer-power-have-generated-a-productivity-pay-gap-since-1979-productivity-has-grown-3-5-times-as-much-as-pay-for-the-typical-worker/#:~:text=Productivity%20and%20pay%20once%20climbed,released%20ahead%20of%20Labor%20Day."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 15.8%}}}\uc2  in the same period. The median worker would have made an extra $9 an hour if the rates had grown together.\par \par \uc2 The federal minimum wage has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/blog/a-tight-labor-market-and-state-minimum-wage-increases-boosted-low-end-wage-growth-between-2019-and-2023/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 remained $7.25}}}\uc2  an hour since July 2009, the longest period in the minimum wage\u8217\'20\'19s history without an increase, and it\u8217\'20\'19s worth 29% less today than 15 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Cecilia Ortiz makes a little more than $16 an hour as a wheelchair attendant at Phoenix international airport in Arizona, where she has worked for a little less than two years.\par \par \uc2 She made $13.85 when she started and only received a raise after getting a promotion. Ortiz has been homeless and has health issues. But she receives no benefits from work; no healthcare, no vacation time or paid sick time, and she has to pay $50 a month for parking at the airport where she works.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI love my job, I\u8217\'20\'19m very good at my job, I have good relationships with my passengers during the time I\u8217\'20\'19m with them, but I have to kind of sugarcoat the real situation, because they automatically assume that working in the airport I must be making good money. For the 1.5 years I\u8217\'20\'19ve been working for these airlines, I\u8217\'20\'19ve been sleeping on an air mattress. I still can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to buy a dresser for my clothes,\u8221\'20\'1D explained Ortiz.\par \par \uc2 During a shift, Ortiz said, she can walk up to 15 miles. Her ability to take breaks is limited, depending on management\u8217\'20\'19s preference, and she isn\u8217\'20\'19t allowed to carry water with her through the airport, despite the exposure to Phoenix\u8217\'20\'19s intensely hot temperatures in parts of the airport where there isn\u8217\'20\'19t air-conditioning, such as the gate bridges to airplanes.\par \par \uc2 Her employer is a contractor for major US airlines, where CEOs receive millions of dollars in compensation, including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://skift.com/2024/04/12/american-airlines-ceo-made-31-4-million-in-202/#:~:text=American%20Airlines%20CEO%20Robert%20Isom%20was%20awarded%20%2431.4%20million%20in,a%20proxy%20statement%20filed%20Thursday."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $31.4m}}}\uc2  for the \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  CEO in 2023 and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ajc.com/news/business/delta-ceo-ed-bastian-saw-his-compensation-rise-to-34-million-last-year/ZCNCROE645GJNAGEKV6CGSMMZU/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than $34m}}}\uc2  in 2023 for the \uc2 Delta Airlines\uc2  CEO.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are thousands of us doing the same thing, we\u8217\'20\'19re just trying to get by. We would like to live comfortably as well,\u8221\'20\'1D added Ortiz. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19d like to be appreciated a little bit here. and it\u8217\'20\'19s really not too much to ask, it\u8217\'20\'19s a shame we have to ask. It\u8217\'20\'19s an even bigger shame that we have to fight.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240506ek56001jl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
