\par\par \uc2 Environment\par \b \uc2 Florida workers brace for summer with no protections: \u8216\'20\'18My body would tremble\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,103 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2024\par 13:11 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Effects of heat are expected to worsen after bill prohibiting municipalities from enacting shade and water protection is passed\par \par \uc2 For Javier Torres and other workers whose jobs are conducted outdoors in south Florida, the heat is unavoidable. A new law recently signed by \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , Florida\u8217\'20\'19s Republican governor, that prohibits any municipalities in the state from passing heat protections for workers ensures that it is likely to stay that way.\par \par \uc2 Torres has seen a co-worker die from heatstroke and another rushed to the emergency room in his years of working in construction in south Florida. He has also fallen and injured himself due to heat exhaustion.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI work outdoors and have no choice but to work in the heat. I work often in painting and, in the majority of cases, we\u8217\'20\'19re exposed to direct sun and we don\u8217\'20\'19t have shade. Sometimes I feel dizzy and get headaches,\u8221\'20\'1D said Torres.\par \par \uc2 He said employers rarely provide workers with water, leaving workers to ensure they bring enough water to work or find a hose to drink from.\par \par \uc2 The effects of extreme heat on workers are only expected to worsen due to the climate crisis. Many parts of Florida experienced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wtsp.com/article/weather/tampa-florida-hottest-summer-record-2023-temperatures/67-5d1fd7de-5971-4e38-bc6e-975ebd6cf332"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 record heat}}}\uc2  last year. Orlando hit 100F (37.7C) in August {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.clickorlando.com/weather/2023/08/13/orlando-hits-100-degrees-breaks-record-from-1938/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 breaking a record}}}\uc2  set in 1938. The National Weather Service recently issued its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://patch.com/florida/across-fl/fl-summer-outlook-2024-la-ni-expected-heat-sunshine-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 outlook}}}\uc2  for summer 2024, predicting Florida summer temperatures will be warmer than normal.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe heat can be very intense, especially as we get closer to summer,\u8221\'20\'1D added Torres. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we want as workers who labor outdoors is to have water, shade and rest breaks to protect ourselves.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At the behest of agricultural industry lobbyists, DeSantis signed HB433 into law on 11 April, a bill scaling back child labor protections that also included an amendment prohibiting all local municipalities in Florida from enacting heat protections for workers.\par \par \uc2 The exemption came in response to efforts by farm workers in Miami-Dade county to pass heat protections, including proper rest breaks, access to water and shade, as increasingly warming temperatures have expanded the days farm workers are exposed to heat.\par \par \uc2 Ana Mejia, a farm worker, worked for 11 years at Costa Farms in south Florida where she said she experienced two serious heat stress incidents on the job. Costa Farms was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nationalcosh.org/DD24"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 included}}}\uc2  on the National Council for Occupational Safety and Health\u8217\'20\'19s Dirty Dozen report of unsafe employers in 2024. Costa Farms declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI worked outdoors during my entire time at Costa Farms in temperatures that quite often exceeded 100 degrees,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mejia. \u8220\'20\'1CI had headaches, sweat excessively, my body would start to shake and tremble. I started to feel dizzy and a lack of coordination, and this feeling of shock and desperation. It was a very bad experience.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She recounted having to be brought to onsite medical care, but only being given an electrolyte drink and finding no medical professional on site or called to help her.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe high standards of meeting productivity quotas per day combined with working in high temperatures is putting us in danger,\u8221\'20\'1D added Mejia. \u8220\'20\'1CThe rest breaks are at the discretion of supervisors and often they don\u8217\'20\'19t want to give rest breaks because it will reduce the productivity of the business.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There are currently no protections in the US for workers from heat. Only a handful of states such as California, Washington, Oregon, Colorado and Minnesota have passed any heat protections for workers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration (Osha)\uc2  is currently reviewing federal heat standard protections and issues fines against employers citing the general duty clause in cases where workers die due to heat stress, but worker groups have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ufwfoundation.org/federalheatprotections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 advocated}}}\uc2  that heat protections which include water, rest, shade, breaks and acclimatization are needed to save workers from heat illnesses and their lives.\par \par \uc2 Up to 2,000 workers in the US die every year due to heat stress, according to a 2023 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.citizen.org/news/without-protections-excessive-workplace-heat-will-injure-and-kill-thousands/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  by \uc2 Public Citizen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Several business groups are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/jul/31/heat-protections-workers-big-business-lobbies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lobbying }}}\uc2  against heat protections for workers at the federal level, and lobbyists aggressively pushed lawmakers to pass the Florida heat exemption bill.\par \par \uc2 Orlando Weekly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.orlandoweekly.com/news/florida-lobbyists-leaned-on-politicians-to-get-ban-on-local-heat-safety-and-wage-laws-across-the-finish-line-records-show-36667788"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  on texts from corporate lobbyists to lawmakers urging them to pass the heat exemption bill before the end of the legislative session.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI haven\u8217\'20\'19t texted you in weeks\u8211\'20\'13HEAT cannot die,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote Carol Bowen, a lobbyist for the Associated Builders and Contractors in a text message on 7 March to the House speaker Paul Renner\u8217\'20\'19s chief of staff Allison Carter, the day before the last day of the legislative session when the bill was ultimately passed. \u8220\'20\'1CThe entire business community is in lock step on this. Thank you for your attention to this concern.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ahead of a vote on the bill, the Florida chamber of commerce lobbyist Carolyn Johnson {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.orlandoweekly.com/news/florida-lobbyists-leaned-on-politicians-to-get-ban-on-local-heat-safety-and-wage-laws-across-the-finish-line-records-show-36667788"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  Republican lawmakers their vote on the bill would be double-weighted on the How They Voted report the chamber sends to its members.\par \par \uc2 Jeannie Economos, an organizer with the Farmworker Association of Florida, said worker advocacy groups opposing HB433 were hoping the clock would run out for the bill to get passed by the state legislature. Several labor and environmental groups sent letters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.miamiherald.com/news/local/environment/climate-change/article287622550.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 imploring}}}\uc2  DeSantis to veto the bill.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s incomprehensible that people who live in Florida, and are supposed to represent the people of Florida, can vote against the health and safety of the workers that make this economy run, who were considered essential workers just a couple years ago and given PPE, are now treated like this, and not giving protection from extreme heat,\u8221\'20\'1D said Economos. \u8220\'20\'1CThat makes no sense and it\u8217\'20\'19s unconscionable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She said worker advocacy groups in Florida were regrouping and planned on developing strategies on how to override the Florida law, while continuing to advocate for heat protections at the federal level and conducting heat stress trainings for outdoor workers to protect themselves.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor us right now, while HB433 is a setback to our campaign, we know the issue of extreme heat isn\u8217\'20\'19t going away anytime soon,\u8221\'20\'1D said Oscar Londo\u241\'00\'F1o, executive director of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://yaleclimateconnections.org/2023/07/the-fight-to-protect-miamis-outdoor-workers-against-the-deadly-heat/#:~:text=In%202021%2C%20the%20organization%20launched,re%20subjected%20to%20extreme%20heat."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 worker advocacy non-profit WeCount!}}}\uc2 , which has been pushing for heat protections for workers through its \u161\'00\'A1 Qu\u233\'00\'E9 Calor ! campaign. \u8220\'20\'1CWe know that the issue is going to get even more and more relevant, and that workers will need to continue to do what is necessary to protect their lives on a job, whether that is through direct action, through workplace organizing, or through ongoing corporate campaign, workers will find a way to win the protection they deserve in Florida.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240504ek54001md\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
