\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US adds 175,000 April jobs as hiring slows and unemployment stays steady\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 428 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2024\par 20:11 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 April\u8217\'20\'19s figure was lower than forecast and unemployment rate ticked up over the month but still market remains strong\par \par \uc2 Hiring in the US slowed in April with the workforce adding another 175,000 jobs and wage growth slowing.\par \par \uc2 The news cheered investors hoping that a cooling labor market will prompt the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to cut interest rates \u8211\'20\'13 which have been driven to a 20-year high as the US fights stubbornly high inflation. All the major US markets rose on the news.\par \par \uc2 Last month, the labor department reported the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/apr/07/us-jobs-report-march-2023"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US added a revised 315,000}}}\uc2  jobs in March, far higher than the 192,000 jobs economists had forecast. April\u8217\'20\'19s figure was lower than forecast and the unemployment rate ticked up from 3.8% to 3.9% over the month but the figures underlined the continuing strength of the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 It was the 40th straight month of job gains in the US and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cepr.net/april-jobs-preview-24/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 27th consecutive month}}}\uc2  with the unemployment rate below 4% \u8211\'20\'13 the longest such streak since 1953.\par \par \uc2 Gains were broad-based, with healthcare, social assistance, transportation and warehousing, manufacturing and construction all adding jobs.\par \par \uc2 The growth in the jobs market has continued even as the Fed has maintained its rates policy. The Fed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/may/01/federal-reserve-interest-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  this week that inflation remained high and that it did not plan to cut interest rates until it has \u8220\'20\'1Cgreater confidence\u8221\'20\'1D that price increases are slowing to the goal of 2%. \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , the Fed chair, said the slowdown in price increases was taking longer than expected, but that he expected inflation to \u8220\'20\'1Cmove back down\u8221\'20\'1D this year.\par \par \uc2 Wage gains in April \u8211\'20\'13 which are also closely watched by the Fed \u8211\'20\'13 were also lower than forecast, increasing 3.9% from a year earlier after rising 4.1% in March.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDespite missing expectations, signaling an economic cooldown, the labor market has still maintained a pattern of growth and consumers can be cautiously optimistic that the Fed will be able to successfully lower inflation while also avoiding a recession,\u8221\'20\'1D said Steve Rick, chief economist at TruStage.\par \par \uc2 ADP, the US\u8217\'20\'19s largest payroll supplier, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://mediacenter.adp.com/2024-05-01-ADP-National-Employment-Report-Private-Sector-Employment-Increased-by-192,000-Jobs-in-April-Annual-Pay-was-Up-5-0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported }}}\uc2  this week that private sector employment was up 192,000 jobs in the month of April and that annual pay was up 5% year-over-year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHiring was broad-based in April,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Nela Richardson\uc2 , chief economist at ADP. \u8220\'20\'1COnly the information sector \u8211\'20\'13 telecommunications, media, and information technology \u8211\'20\'13 showed weakness, posting job losses and the smallest pace of pay gains since August 2021.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240503ek53001ml\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
