\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Interest rate cut bets rise as US job creation slows; FTSE 100 at record high \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 6,055 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2024\par 14:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rolling coverage of the latest economic and financial news, as latest US non-farm payroll shows weakening job creation\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 Time to recap\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Hiring in the US slowed in April with the workforce adding another 175,000 jobs and wage growth slowing.\par \par \uc2 The news cheered investors hoping that a cooling labor market will prompt the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/federal-reserve"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Federal Reserve}}}\uc2  to cut interest rates \u8211\'20\'13 which have been driven to a 20-year high as the US fights stubbornly high inflation. All the major US markets rose in early trading.\par \par \uc2 Last month, the labor department reported the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/apr/07/us-jobs-report-march-2023"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US added a revised 315,000}}}\uc2  jobs in March, far higher than the 192,000 jobs economists had forecast. April\u8217\'20\'19s figure was lower than forecast and the unemployment rate ticked up from 3.8% to 3.9% over the month but the figures underlined the continuing strength of the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 It was the 40th straight month of job gains in the US and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cepr.net/april-jobs-preview-24/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 27th consecutive month}}}\uc2  with the unemployment rate below 4% \u8211\'20\'13 the longest such streak since 1953.\par \par \uc2 But the slowdown in job creation is fuelling expectations that US interest rates could be cut sooner than expected, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/may/03/oil-prices-weekly-drop-us-jobs-market-wages-stock-markets-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-6634e3e98f080b5e6ba91ff0#block-6634e3e98f080b5e6ba91ff0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a September cut looking more likely}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the full story:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/may/03/us-april-jobs-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US adds 175,000 April jobs as hiring slows and unemployment stays steady}}}\par \par \uc2 With stock markets rallying across Europe and in the US, the UK\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE 100 share index is on track for a new closing high tonight. With less than an hour\u8217\'20\'19s trading to go, the Footsie is up 41 poinst at 8212, having surged over 8,200 points for the first time today.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the rest of today\u8217\'20\'19s stories so far:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/03/minouche-shafik-uk-peer-columbia-university-president-gaza-protests"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Minouche Shafik: the UK peer facing choppy waters over Gaza protests at Columbia}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/article/2024/may/03/train-strikes-and-traffic-jams-uk-travellers-face-delays-in-coming-days-bank-holiday"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Train strikes and traffic jams: UK travellers face delays in coming days}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/may/03/glencore-may-trigger-bidding-war-for-mining-rival-anglo-american"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Glencore may trigger bidding war for mining rival Anglo American}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/article/2024/may/03/ex-post-office-lawyer-denies-covering-up-knowledge-of-it-problems"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ex-Post Office lawyer denies \u8216\'20\'18covering up\u8217\'20\'19 knowledge of IT problems}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 enltrGOLDMAN SACHS STILL EXPECTS THE BANK OF ENGLAND TO START CUTTING INTEREST RATES FROM JUNE.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 FinancialJuice (@financialjuice) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/financialjuice/status/1786399467257614414?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 Back in London, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  shareholders have rejected the resolution to scrap the pension clawback which has cost its retired workers thousands of pounds per year.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/may/03/oil-prices-weekly-drop-us-jobs-market-wages-stock-markets-business-live?page=with:block-6634beaf8f088c1cab74a1ca#block-6634beaf8f088c1cab74a1ca"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 As we covered earlier}}}\uc2 , the Midland Clawback Campaign argues that \uc2 HSBC\uc2  is acting unfairly by cutting their company pension on the grounds that they also receive the state pension.\par \par \uc2 Resolution 17 said:\par \par \uc2 This Special Resolution seeks shareholders to instruct the board to align pension inequality with their commitment to reduce the gender pay gap, by removing the impact of State Deduction from the members of the post 1974 Midland Section of the HSBC Bank (UK) Pension Scheme.\par \par \uc2 HSBC\uc2  had advised investors to vote against this resolution, and they heeded this advice, with just 4% of votes cast in favour.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2019/mar/30/ex-hsbc-staff-voice-anger-over-banks-pension-clawback"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ex-HSBC staff voice anger over bank\u8217\'20\'19s pension clawback}}}\par \par \uc2 A motion to allow \uc2 HSBC\uc2  to remove its cap on bankers bonuses was passed with 99% support\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 Fed\u8217\'20\'19s Bowman says inflation to remain elevated for \u8216\'20\'18some time\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Governor \uc2 Michelle Bowman\uc2  has slightly dampened the mood in the markets, by predicting that US inflation will likely remain elevated for \u8220\'20\'1Csome time\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In remarks prepared for the Massachusetts Bankers Association Annual Convention in Key Biscayne, Florida, Bowman says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI expect inflation to remain elevated for some time.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy baseline outlook continues to be that inflation will decline further with the policy rate held steady, but I still see a number of upside inflation risks that affect my outlook.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrFederal Reserve Governor \uc2 Michelle Bowman\uc2  said inflation will likely remain elevated for \u8220\'20\'1Csome time\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/6y9oDkkhLl"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/6y9oDkkhLl}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bloomberg Economics (@economics) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/economics/status/1786401071260160233?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 The latest US jobs data has further fueled expectations of impending interest rate cuts and paints a picture of a soft landing on the economic horizon, says Stephen Innes, managing partner at SPI Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 But if today\u8217\'20\'19s report is revised lower in a month\u8217\'20\'19s time, there be more concerns over the health of the US conomy.\par \par \uc2 Innes says:\par \par \uc2 According to the \uc2 BLS\uc2  release on Friday, the US economy added 175,000 jobs in April, a figure that could be deemed within the \u8220\'20\'1CGoldilocks zone.\u8221\'20\'1D Leading up to the release, there was a subtle and odd uptick in the \u8220\'20\'1Cwhisper\u8221\'20\'1D number, consequently, the 175,000 figure was perceived as a wider miss compared to the \u8220\'20\'1Cofficial\u8221\'20\'1D consensus set at 240,000.\par \par \uc2 Of particular encouragement for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  was the subdued reading of Average Hourly Earnings (AHE). AHE increased by only 0.2% from March (0.2019% when unrounded), falling below consensus expectations. Similarly, the year-over-year (YoY) growth rate of 3.9% was cooler than anticipated.\par \par \uc2 This data should help alleviate concerns about \u8220\'20\'1Cstagflation\u8221\'20\'1D prompted by the headline nonfarm payrolls (NFP) miss, as well as ease worries exacerbated by the Employment Cost Index (\uc2 ECI\uc2 ) and Unit Labor Costs (ULC) overshooting in the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 While the extent of the headline miss might fuel concerns about a recession, for the time being, 175,000 represents a fundamentally robust pace of hiring. Any significant downward revisions next month alongside another substantial miss may warrant cause for concern.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 James Knightley\uc2 , chief international economist at ING, agrees that today\u8217\'20\'19s weaker US jobs numbers boost expectations of a September rate cut.\par \par \uc2 Knightley explains:\par \par \uc2 Job creation was weaker, unemployment higher and wage growth more subdued than expected in the April employment report. With Fed Chair Powell leaning dovish at Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s press conference this has breathed new life into \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  interest rate cut calls\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 Wall Street surges\par \par \uc2 Stocks have surged at the start of trading in New York, after traders learned that hiring across America slowed last month.\par \par \uc2 News that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/may/03/oil-prices-weekly-drop-us-jobs-market-wages-stock-markets-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-6634d9588f08c1f1f6fa6ff4#block-6634d9588f08c1f1f6fa6ff4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the US non-farm payroll only rose by 175,000 in April}}}\uc2 , weaker than the average monthly gain of 242,000 over the last year, has driven investors into a frenzy of of stock buying.\par \par \uc2 They are excited by the prospect that US interest rates may fall quicker than expected \u8211\'20\'13 and putting aside concerns that slowing job creation may show the economy is weakening.\par \par \uc2 enltrUS NFP comes out at 175k jobs in April 2024, a deceleration compared 315,000 jobs added in March and falling short of market expectations for a 243,000 increase. Good news for Fed.\par \par \uc2 US RATE FUTURES NOW PRICE IN TWO CUTS OF 25 BPS IN 2024 VS ONE BEFORE JOBS DATA- Walter Bloomberg {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/BPXBJyLsAv"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/BPXBJyLsAv}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 400DollaCologne (@400DollaCologne) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/400DollaCologne/status/1786386813659033982?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average, of 30 large US companies, has rallied by 1.4%, gaining 543 points at the open to 38,769 points.\par \par \uc2 The broader S&P 500 index has gained 1.2%.\par \par \uc2 And the tech-focused Nasdaq has surged by 1.9% (interest rate cuts being good for the share price of technology companies).\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 City traders are also more confident that the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  will cut interest rates this year.\par \par \uc2 The money markets are now pricing in two quarter-point cuts by Christmas \u8211\'20\'13 previously, the second cut was not fully priced in.\par \par \uc2 A cut in August is pretty-much priced in (although it could slip to September), with a second expected in November or December.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.19pm BST\par \par \uc2 XTB: US payrolls open the door to a September rate cut\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report has opened to door to a cut to US interest rates in September, says \uc2 Kathleen Brooks\uc2 , research director at brokerage XTB.\par \par \uc2 She report that there has been \u8220\'20\'1Ca huge movement in US interest rate expectations\u8221\'20\'1D, helped by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/may/03/oil-prices-weekly-drop-us-jobs-market-wages-stock-markets-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-6634d9588f08c1f1f6fa6ff4#block-6634d9588f08c1f1f6fa6ff4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the slowdown in job creation in April}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There is now a 50% chance of a rate hike in September, up from 31% in April. Earlier this week there was only 1 rate cut priced in from the Fed for 2024, as we end the week, there are now nearly two cuts priced in.\par \par \uc2 Overall, risk sentiment has been given a boost this week from a less hawkish Fed and payrolls data that is moving in the direction needed for interest rate cuts in the US. The combination of payrolls and the Fed have helped to increase rate cut expectations for 2024, with no chance of a rate hike for this year expected. What a difference a week makes.\par \par \uc2 enltrPost NFP, fed swaps pricing in 2 cuts for full-year '24. Up from 1.15 earlier this week, but down from nearly 7 at start of the year. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/TEZxZKJ5cf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/TEZxZKJ5cf}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Alastair (@StockBoardAsset) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/StockBoardAsset/status/1786383154271404332?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.17pm BST\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s US employment report shows a \u8216\'20\'18historic jobs market\u8217\'20\'19, says Heather Long of the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , who cites the low unemployment rate (which rose to 3.9% in April, from 3.8%).\par \par \uc2 enltrApril jobs report key takeaways:\par \par \uc2 1) This is a historic job market w/unemployment under 4% for 27 months\par \par \uc2 2) Hiring is good, but slowing\par \par \uc2 3) Healthcare is now the major industry hiring\par \par \uc2 4) Wage growth is above inflation\par \par \uc2 5) Signs of cooling = temp help decline, avg work week down {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/sGUHTDZpHJ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/sGUHTDZpHJ}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Heather Long (@byHeatherLong) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/byHeatherLong/status/1786379414806163935?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 America\u8217\'20\'19s health care system was responsible for almost one in three of the new jobs created in April.\par \par \uc2 Health care added 56,000 jobs in April, today\u8217\'20\'19s repost shows, out of 175,000 new additions to the Non-Farm Payroll.\par \par \uc2 Employment in social assistance increased by 31,000 in April, while transportation and warehousing added 22,000 jobs, and retailers made 20,000 new hires.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.12pm BST\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/may/03/oil-prices-weekly-drop-us-jobs-market-wages-stock-markets-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-6634d9588f08c1f1f6fa6ff4#block-6634d9588f08c1f1f6fa6ff4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 While weakening jobs growth}}}\uc2  may mean lower interest rates soon, it may also signal that the US economy is weakening.\par \par \uc2 Seema Shah\uc2 , chief global strategist at Principal Asset Management, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is the jobs report the Fed would have scripted. The first downside payrolls surprise in several months, as well as the dip in average hourly earnings growth, will bring the rate cutting dialogue back into the market and perhaps explains why Powell was able to be dovish on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Of course, today\u8217\'20\'19s weaker numbers need to mark the start of a new slower trend for multiple rate cuts to seriously be back on the agenda - but, by then, the new fear could be a slowing economy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 US non-farm payroll: snap reaction\par \par \uc2 Reaction to the slowdown in US job creation is flooding in.\par \par \uc2 Richard Flynn, managing director at \uc2 Charles Schwab UK\uc2 , says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CInvestors will interpret today\u8217\'20\'19s weak jobs report as a sign that demand is slowing in the labour market.\par \par \uc2 Both markets and central bankers have been looking for evidence that disinflation may be ahead, and today\u8217\'20\'19s figures could indicate that the economy is slowing down. In recent months, it has become clear that the Fed is happy to move slowly in the cutting part of the rate cycle, but unwanted and unexpected weakness in the economy, as we are seeing today, may cause a shift in this approach.\par \par \uc2 A dive in the labour market may be what it takes to push the Fed from a stroll to a sprint.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mahmoud Alkudsi, senior market analyst at ADSS, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a rare miss for the US jobs market which could potentially spell the end of the \u8216\'20\'18higher for longer\u8217\'20\'19 rhetoric we have become accustomed to. This will have surprised the Fed following Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s decision to hold interest rates further.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThough the Fed will see this as a step towards getting comfortable with a cut in interest rates, the relative strength of the jobs market remains too high for them to consider a cut before late Q2.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And here\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Neil Birrell\uc2 , chief investment officer at Premier Miton Investors:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat will the Fed make of this? At last there is evidence of some weakness in the US jobs market. Rate cuts will move back up the agenda as a result and there is little doubt that markets will take this as good news. While we shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t make too much of single data prints, this could be the start of a positive trend for the Fed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 US wage growth slows\par \par \uc2 Wage growth across America has slowed \u8211\'20\'13 a blow to workers, but just what central bankers want to see.\par \par \uc2 Average hourly earnings growth slowed to +0.2% in April, down from 0.3% in March.\par \par \uc2 On an annual basis, earnings rose by 3.9%, down from 4%.\par \par \uc2 That means real wages (adjusted for inflation) are still rising (as US inflation was 3.5% in March).\par \par \uc2 enltrJUST IN: The US economy added a net 175,000 jobs in April \u8212\'20\'14 a solid gain, but below expectations of ~250k. Healthcare was the big sector hiring.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment rate: 3.9% \u8212\'20\'14> The unemployment rate has been below 4% for 27 months. That hasn\u8217\'20\'19t happened since 1967-70.\par \par \uc2 Wages: +3.9% in\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/RKYy1orroq"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/RKYy1orroq}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Heather Long (@byHeatherLong) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/byHeatherLong/status/1786373306993569884?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 FTSE 100 at new record high\par \par \uc2 In London, the FTSE 100 share index has jumped to a new alltime high.\par \par \uc2 The blue-chip share index has rocketed to 8248 points, a new intraday high, mirroring the jump in Wall Street futures.\par \par \uc2 Ocado\uc2 , the grocery technology business, is up 7%.\par \par \uc2 Takeover speculation continues to push up \uc2 Anglo American\uc2  by over 3% too.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/may/03/glencore-may-trigger-bidding-war-for-mining-rival-anglo-american"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Glencore may trigger bidding war for mining rival Anglo American}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 The dollar has tumbled by a cent against the pound, as April\u8217\'20\'19s weak jobs report fuels hopes of an early cut to US interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Sterling has jumped to $1.2624, the highest in over three weeks, up from $1.2531 last night.\par \par \uc2 enltrDXY gap {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/v5xylaQQPU"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/v5xylaQQPU}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Neil Wilson\uc2  (@marketsneil) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/marketsneil/status/1786373025358557284?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 US economy adds 175,000 jobs in April, weaker than expected\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: job creation across the US economy has slowed, news that has weakened the US dollar and driven up share prices.\par \par \uc2 April\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payroll shows that US employment increased by 175,000 in April, less than the 243,000 expected.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s a sharp slowdown on March, when an upwardly revised 315,000 jobs were created, while February\u8217\'20\'19s NFP was revised down from +270,000 to +236,000.\par \par \uc2 The report says that job gains occurred in health care, in social assistance, and in transportation and warehousing.\par \par \uc2 enltrApril nonfarm payrolls +175k vs. +240k est. & +315k in prior month (rev up from +303k) \u8230\'20\'26 revisions for prior two months total -22k {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/v1lHHtwfCF"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/v1lHHtwfCF}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Liz Ann Sonders (@LizAnnSonders) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LizAnnSonders/status/1786373320847368539?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 The US unemployment rate has risen to 3.9%, from 3.8%.\par \par \uc2 The report has knocked the dollar, while shares are higher in pre-market trading in New York.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Simon French\uc2 , chief economist at City bank \uc2 Panmure Gordon\uc2 , on today\u8217\'20\'19s disappointing public sector productivity stats ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/may/03/oil-prices-weekly-drop-us-jobs-market-wages-stock-markets-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-6634a96f8f08d1715574c18b#block-6634a96f8f08d1715574c18b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 see 10.20am}}}\uc2  ):\par \par \uc2 enltrPlenty of reasons you might not want public sector productivity to rise (higher class sizes etc.) but to have it lower than in 1997 given the technology advances in delivering services that has taken place is a truly awful track record. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/zxlQDDuUj1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/zxlQDDuUj1}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Simon French\uc2  (@Frencheconomics) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Frencheconomics/status/1786331813972935166?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more details of the shareholder criticism at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s AGM:\par \par \uc2 enltrHSBC chairman \uc2 Mark Tucker\uc2  has been jeered with cries of \u8216\'20\'18shame\u8217\'20\'19 at its AGM by former staff over what they say is the unfair practice of the bank clawing back a chunk of their pensions once they reach state pension age.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 John-Paul Ford Rojas (@JPFordRojas) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JPFordRojas/status/1786352981668950404?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThe campaigners - drawn from the old Midland Bank which was taken over by \uc2 HSBC\uc2  - drew a contrast between their plight and the bank\u8217\'20\'19s move at the AGN to lift the bonus cap for some of its biggest earners\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 John-Paul Ford Rojas (@JPFordRojas) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JPFordRojas/status/1786353776451838329?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrOne of the campaigners Nancy Ball said: 'I was one of the top performers. I thought you had my back but oh no you didn't. Shame on you all.'\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 John-Paul Ford Rojas (@JPFordRojas) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JPFordRojas/status/1786354444969431332?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrLen Aspill, another campaigner, said pensioners 'should not have to go like Oliver Twist with their begging bowls for a charitable hand out of crumbs from the master's table'\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 John-Paul Ford Rojas (@JPFordRojas) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JPFordRojas/status/1786354738159604152?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 Another \uc2 HSBC\uc2  shareholder qustions how the bank can claim staff were fully aware of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/may/03/oil-prices-weekly-drop-us-jobs-market-wages-stock-markets-business-live?page=with:block-6634beaf8f088c1cab74a1ca#block-6634beaf8f088c1cab74a1ca"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the pensions clawback issue}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Q: If that was true, why are there 10,000 people in a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  campaign group? And was CEO \uc2 Noel Quinn\uc2  fully informed when he was a Midland Bank staff member?\par \par \uc2 Quinn says he was \u8220\'20\'1Cwell aware\u8221\'20\'1D of clawback \u8211\'20\'13 a reply that is greeted by hoots of derision from the floor of the AGM.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.59am BST\par \par \uc2 Midland Clawback Campaign challengs \uc2 HSBC\uc2  over pension cuts\par \par \uc2 HSBC\uc2  are hearing the anger of former employees who argue the bank is simply wrong to have clawed back part of their pensions.\par \par \uc2 The Midland Clawback Campaign group have proposed a resolution at today\u8217\'20\'19s AGM to end the practice, and are unhappy that chair \uc2 Mark Tucker\uc2  is advising shareholders to oppose it.\par \par \uc2 The clawback was introduced in the 1940s and allows workers to pay lower contributions into their occupational pension plans, and for employers to deduct some - or all - of the basic state pension amount from their pension payments.\par \par \uc2 One campaigner, Nancy Ball, tells the AGM of examples of \uc2 HSBC\uc2  pensioners who have lost over 20% of their pensions due to the clawback.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, she points out, CEO \uc2 Noel Quinn\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s pay almost doubled last year.\par \par \uc2 Quinn, who announced his retirement this week, should reflect on whether he could, and should, have supported his Midland Bank colleagues in their retirement [Midland was taken over by \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in 1992].\par \par \uc2 Ball also points to the irony that \uc2 HSBC\uc2  is refusing to end the clawback, while also asking shareholders to remove the cap on its bankers\u8217\'20\'19 bonuses.\par \par \uc2 Ball insists that it is \u8220\'20\'1Cbalderdash\u8221\'20\'1D to say that the clawback was clearly explained to staff, calling the situation \u8220\'20\'1Ca shambles\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 enltrA senior manager retiring on a pension of \u163\'00\'A3150,000 a year might suffer a clawback of \u163\'00\'A32,500 or just 1.7%, whereas a junior clerk with the same on a pension of only \u163\'00\'A310,000 would get the same clawback of \u163\'00\'A32,500 \u8212\'20\'14 25% of their pension...\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jim Edwards (@Jim_Edwards) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Jim_Edwards/status/1785606756774645853?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 1, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Chairman \uc2 Mark Tucker\uc2  tells the AGM that the clawback was lawful, not discriminationary, and was properly communicated.\par \par \uc2 Midland Clawback Campaigners don\u8217\'20\'19t agree, and shout back at Tucker from the floor.\par \par \uc2 The Clawback campaign explain {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.midlandclawbackcampaign.co.uk/history/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  that the policy is unfair because pensioners lose the same monetary amount, regardless on whether they\u8217\'20\'19re on a large or small pension.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.25am BST\par \par \uc2 HSBC\uc2  chair: Expect UK interest rate cut in June\par \par \uc2 The chair of \uc2 HSBC\uc2  has predicted that the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  will cut interest rates in June, for the first time since early in the pandemic in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s annual general meeting in London today, \uc2 Mark Tucker\uc2  explains that inflation data is \u8220\'20\'1Ckey\u8221\'20\'1D to the global interest rate outlook.\par \par \uc2 He predicts that the \uc2 BoE\uc2  will lower interest rates by one and a half percentage points by the end of next year. That would lower Bank Rate to 3.75%, from 5.25% today.\par \par \uc2 Tucker says:\par \par \uc2 Central banks are closely and carefully watching the data and need to be confident that inflation will continue to head down to target on a sustainable basis before lowering rates.\par \par \uc2 Our economists continue to anticipate a gradual reduction in inflation with our global inflation forecasts at 5.8% in 2024 and 3.8% in 2025.\par \par \uc2 We expect the ECB and Bank of England to cut rates in June, cutting by 150bps by year-end 2025. We expect the Fed to cut in September, cutting by 100bps by year-end 2025.\par \par \uc2 The City money markets do not expect the \uc2 BoE\uc2  to cut as early as June, though \u8211\'20\'13 today, the first cut is only fully priced in for September, although there\u8217\'20\'19s a 66% chance it could come in August.\par \par \uc2 Tucker adds that it is hard to achieve \u8220\'20\'1Crelative certainty in the central banks\u8217\'20\'19 decision-making process\u8221\'20\'1D, given that both growth and employment numbers are holding up and inflationary pressures are lingering.\par \par \uc2 As he puts it:\par \par \uc2 It may not be a steady path.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.25am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.20am BST\par \par \uc2 UK public service productivity in 'pretty worrying' fall\par \par \uc2 New experimental statistics show that UK public sector productivity dropped in the final quarter of last year, and is almost 7% below its pre-pandemic levels.\par \par \uc2 The Offiec for National Statistics has reported that public service productivity fell by 1% in October-December 2023 compared with July-September, the third quarterly decline in a row.\par \par \uc2 This left public sector productivity 2.3% lower than in the fourth quarter of 2022.\par \par \uc2 Annual estimates suggest that public service productivity showed no growth (0.0%) in 2023, following a partial \u8220\'20\'1Cbounce-back\u8221\'20\'1D of 6.5% in 2021 and 3.0% in 2022 following the pandemic in 2020, the ONS reports.\par \par \uc2 enltrPublic service productivity in Quarter 4 2023 was 6.8% below its pre-pandemic peak in Quarter 4 2019. Following a large fall during the period affected by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/COVID19?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #COVID19}}}\uc2 , it has remained relatively stable since Quarter 2 2021.\par \par \uc2 Read more ?? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/nBEEJgwhFD"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/nBEEJgwhFD}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/YPz888hJgy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/YPz888hJgy}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  (ONS) (@ONS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ONS/status/1786314755424088353?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Productivity rises if output increases faster than inputs, and vice versa.\par \par \uc2 The ONS reports that inputs (such as wages) rose by 1.2% in the quarter, while output only rise by 0.1%\par \par \uc2 Inputs increased on the quarter for all service areas, however, output fell for healthcare and education, the two largest areas by expenditure share, the ONS adds.\par \par \uc2 Paul Johnson, director of the \uc2 IFS\uc2 , says the \u8220\'20\'1Chuge drop\u8221\'20\'1D in productivity since the pandemic is \u8220\'20\'1Cpretty worrying\u8221\'20\'1D, and shows public services are struggling.\par \par \uc2 enltrPublic sector productivity 6.8% down on Q4 2019 is pretty worrying. Huge drop.\par \par \uc2 Consistent with what we know: genuine increases in spending, but struggling public services. E.g. activity in hospitals nowhere near reflecting additional resources. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/J9uGV0dPHz"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/J9uGV0dPHz}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Paul Johnson (@PJTheEconomist) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/PJTheEconomist/status/1786319961645838542?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.05am BST\par \par \uc2 Over in Oslo, \uc2 Norway\u8217\'20\'19s central bank\uc2  has left interest rates unchanged.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  decided to maintain its policy rate at 4.5%, and predicted that rates will \u8220\'20\'1Clikely be kept at that level for some time ahead\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 enltr????\uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  Review: Kept policy rate unchanged at 4.50%, seeing need for restrictive rates longer. Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Gbm8YfcNi6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/Gbm8YfcNi6}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/w2g4IyHSNS"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/w2g4IyHSNS}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Swedbank Makroanalys (@SwedbankMakro) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SwedbankMakro/status/1786313100007706940?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.42am BST\par \par \uc2 UK pulling out of recession as services sector growth hits 11-month high\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: The UK\u8217\'20\'19s services sector has posted the fastest business activity growth in almost a year, reinforcing hopes that the recession is over.\par \par \uc2 Data firm S&P Global has reported that its UK Services PMI, which tracks activity in the sector, has jumped to 55.0 in April, up from 53.1 in March.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s the highest reading since May 2023, and shows growth accelerated in a sector that makes up around three-quarters of the UK economy.\par \par \uc2 enltrServices \uc2 PMI\uc2  in the United Kingdom increased to 55 points in April from 53.10 points in March of 2024. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Tti1VJNW5C"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/Tti1VJNW5C}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/aQkpmTtkTe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/aQkpmTtkTe}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 TRADING ECONOMICS (@tEconomics) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/tEconomics/status/1786314758888587625?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 3, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Services firms reported that activity and new work rose at the fastest rates for 11 months, with reports that optimism about the economic recovery boosted companies.\par \par \uc2 However, staff hiring remained subdued, with some companies reporting difficulties finding suitable candidates to fill vacancies. Others cited wage pressures, given last month\u8217\'20\'19s near 10% annual rise in the National Living Wage.\par \par \uc2 Input cost inflation was the highest since August 2023\par \par \uc2 Tim Moore\uc2 , economics director at S&P Global Market Intelligence, explains:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CService providers benefited from improving business and consumer spending in April as more favourable demand conditions underpinned the greatest improvement in activity since May 2023.\par \par \uc2 The latest survey results are consistent with the UK economy growing at a quarterly rate of 0.4% and therefore pulling further out of last year\u8217\'20\'19s shallow recession.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRelief at a turnaround in the economic outlook was commonly cited as a factor supporting sales pipelines in April. However, there were also reports that clients remained somewhat risk averse and under pressure from elevated inflation.\par \par \uc2 Next Friday we will learn if the UK recession is over, when GDP data for the first three months of this year is released. Economists predict a return to growth in January-March, of around 0.4%.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s Services \uc2 PMI\uc2  suggests that the second quarter of the year got off to a good start too.\par \par \uc2 Moore adds that business activity expectations for the year ahead were upbeat:\par \par \uc2 Election uncertainty and fading prospects for interest rate cuts were cited as headwinds on the horizon, but survey respondents still mostly reported positive sentiment towards their business investment plans and longer-term growth opportunities.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.46am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.32am BST\par \par \uc2 FTSE 100 hits record high\par \par \uc2 Boom! Britain\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE 100 share index has hit a new alltime high in early trading, as its rally continues.\par \par \uc2 The Footsie has risen over 8,200 points for the first time, touching 8205 points, above Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s intraday high of 8199.95 points.\par \par \uc2 This extends the FTSE 100\u8217\'20\'19s recent rally, which has seen it gain 6% so far this year.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/may/03/oil-prices-weekly-drop-us-jobs-market-wages-stock-markets-business-live?page=with:block-66348fe28f082dacd4220d12#block-66348fe28f082dacd4220d12"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 And as covered earlier}}}\uc2 , analysts believe it has further to rise.\par \par \uc2 Mining company \uc2 Anglo American\uc2  remains the top riser in London, up over 3% on bidding war hopes \u8211\'20\'13 after Reuters reported that \uc2 Glencore\uc2  was considering a takeover approach.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 has benefited this year from traders switching out of tech stocks and into commodities. Hopes of easing tensions in the Middle East also lifted stocks last month, as have signs that the UK economy is pulling out of recession.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.33am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.23am BST\par \par \uc2 Global food prices rose in April\par \par \uc2 Global food prices inched up in April, due to rising meat prices and small increases in the cost of vegetable oils and cereals.\par \par \uc2 The United Nations food agency\u8217\'20\'19s world price index rose in April, for the second month in a row, to 119.1 points, up from 118.8 points in March.\par \par \uc2 That still leaves prices 9.6% lower than a year ago, though.\par \par \uc2 Cereal prices rose by 0.3%, amid concerns about unfavorable crop conditions in parts of the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , the Russian Federation and the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 Vegetable oil prices rose by 0.3%, driven by costlier sunflower and rapeseed oil.\par \par \uc2 Meat prices increased by 1.6% last month, with international poultry, bovine and ovine meat prices all rising.\par \par \uc2 But there were some price falls too \u8211\'20\'13 among sugar and dairy products.\par \par \uc2 Sugar prices fell by 4.4%, due to larger-than-previously-anticipated outputs in India and Thailand and improved weather conditions in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Dairy prices dipped by 0.3%, ending six consecutive months of increases. This was due to \u8220\'20\'1Csluggish spot import demand for skim milk powder\u8221\'20\'1D, and lower world cheese prices. Butter prices rose, though.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.03am BST\par \par \uc2 Asda\uc2  refinances \u163\'00\'A33.2bn debt\par \par \uc2 UK supermarket chain \uc2 Asda\uc2  has successfully refinanced over \u163\'00\'A33bn of debt.\par \par \uc2 Asda\uc2  reports that it saw \u8220\'20\'1Cstrong demand from investors\u8221\'20\'1D for new debt sold yesterday, which has pushed out the majority of its maturities into the next decade.\par \par \uc2 The sals, it says, included the biggest sterling high-yield bond this year and the second-largest sterling bond in the European leveraged finance market.\par \par \uc2 Michael Gleeson, \uc2 Asda\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief financial officer, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe positive reaction followed \uc2 Asda\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s strong FY23 results \u8211\'20\'13 and \uc2 Moody\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  upgrade of its corporate rating to B1 from B2 last week citing a material reduction in leverage and growth in underlying free cashflow.\par \par \uc2 The takeover of \uc2 Asda\uc2  by private equity firm \uc2 TDR Capital\uc2  and billionaire brothers Moshin and \uc2 Zuber Issa\uc2  added billions of pounds of debt to \uc2 Asda\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 with refinancing costs jumping as interest rates have risen.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/aug/26/bumpy-ride-for-billionaire-brothers-who-banked-on-asdas-success"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bumpy ride for billionaire brothers who banked on Asda\u8217\'20\'19s success}}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.standard.co.uk/business/asda-refinances-ps3-2-billion-debt-at-higher-interest-rates-b1155438.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Evening Standard reports}}}\uc2  that \uc2 Asda\uc2  is now paying higher interest rates on its new debts, saying:\par \par \uc2 The firm said it successfully raised \u163\'00\'A31.75 billion of senior secured notes and refinanced its \u163\'00\'A3900 million Term Loan B. The Term Loan B was secured at a EURIBOR+4.25% interest rate, an increase on the previous loan of EURIBOR+2.75%\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.09am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.26am BST\par \par \uc2 Rail ticket platform Trainline are the top riser on the FTSE 250 after doubling its operating profits in the last year.\par \par \uc2 Trainline reported that its operating profits rose by 101% in the year to 29th February, from \u163\'00\'A328m to \u163\'00\'A356m.\par \par \uc2 Trainline, which says it is now Europe\u8217\'20\'19s most downloaded rail app, grew its net ticket sales by 22% in the last year.\par \par \uc2 And interestingly, the company tells shareholders that the Labour party have told Trainline that they have \u8220\'20\'1Cno plans to revive the current Government\u8217\'20\'19s previous proposal for a national retailing website and app\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Last December, Trainline\u8217\'20\'19s share surged when the \uc2 Department for Transport\uc2  scrapped plans to create a Great British Railways ticketing website and app.\par \par \uc2 With Labour pledging to fully nationalise the train network within five years of coming to power, there had been thoughts that the app plan could be revived.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2024/apr/24/labour-promises-rail-nationalisation-within-five-years-of-coming-to-power"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Labour promises rail nationalisation within five years of coming to power}}}\par \par \uc2 Trainline\u8217\'20\'19s shares are up almost 7% this morning, as those fears are shunted aside.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.19am BST\par \par \uc2 Richard Hunter, head of markets at interactive investor, suggests the London stock market has further to climb:\par \par \uc2 US markets may have lost some of their mojo but the same cannot be said for a flourishing FTSE100, where opening strength lifted gains in the year so far to 6%.\par \par \uc2 The mixture of technical factors, such as rising commodity prices and higher interest rates underpinning the likes of the mining, oil and banking sectors has been combined with improving sentiment towards the premier index.\par \par \uc2 Even at these elevated levels at or around record highs, the valuation of the index remains undemanding in comparison to many of its peers which could suggest that the recent rally still has some way to go.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.09am BST\par \par \uc2 FTSE 100 near all-time high\par \par \uc2 In the City, the blue-chip share index has nearly hit a new alltime high at the start of trading.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 index has jumped by 24 points, or 0.3%, to 8197 points \u8211\'20\'13 only slightly below the intraday high of 8199.95 points set on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Anglo American\uc2  is the top riser riser, up 3.3%, after Reuters reported last night that commodities group \uc2 Glencore\uc2  was studying an approach for Anglo.\par \par \uc2 Such an approach could spark a bidding war for \uc2 Anglo American\uc2 , which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/apr/26/anglo-american-rejects-takeover-offer-from-mining-rival-bhp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rebuffed an approach from fellow miner BHP Group last month}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Glencore\uc2  is the top FTSE 100 faller, down 1.7%.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.04am BST\par \par \uc2 The main highlight today will be the US jobs report for April, predicts Jim Reid, strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He explains:\par \par \uc2 This will be an important release for the Fed as well, since the resilience in the labour market has enabled them to keep the focus on inflation.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the March report showed nonfarm payrolls rise by a 10-month high of +303k, whilst the 3-month average growth was at a 12-month high of +276k.\par \par \uc2 However, even as the nonfarm payrolls numbers have been strong, other indicators have pointed to growing weakness in the labour market, and this week\u8217\'20\'19s JOLTS report for March showed that both job openings and the quits rate were down to their lowest in over three years.\par \par \uc2 In terms of what to expect today, our US economists expect nonfarm payrolls growth to moderate to +240k in April, with the unemployment rate unchanged at 3.8%\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.59am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.43am BST\par \par \uc2 Introduction: Oil prices set for steepest weekly drop since February\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 The oil price is on track for its biggest weekly losses in three months, bringing relief to consumers and central bankers alike.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil prices have fallen by over 6% so far this week, helped by easing tensions in the Middle East and uncertainty about demand for energy. That would be its worst week since the start of February.\par \par \uc2 After hefty losses early this week, Brent crude is trading around its weakest level since mid-March, at below $84 per barrel, having ended last week near $90 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 Hopes for a deal to bring about a ceasefire in Gaza, and free the remaining hostages held there since the October 7 attacks, have risen this week. US secretary of state, \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 , told Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe time is now\u8221\'20\'1D for a deal, during his seventh visit to the Middle East since last October.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/audio/2024/may/03/are-we-on-the-brink-of-a-ceasefire-deal-for-gaza"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Are we on the brink of a ceasefire deal for Gaza? Podcast}}}\par \par \uc2 A ceasefire, if agreed, would cut risks to oil supplies from the region.\par \par \uc2 The oil price also weakened after US stocks of crude oil climbed unexpectedly this week. The \uc2 US Energy Information Administration (EIA)\uc2  reported that energy firms added a surprise 7.3 million barrels of crude into stockpiles during the week to April 26.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 EIA\uc2  also reported a surprise 0.3-million barrel build in gasoline inventories; analysts had expected gasoline stocks would decline by 1.1 million barrels.\par \par \uc2 David Morrison, senior market analyst at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tradenation.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trade Nation}}}\uc2  , says:\par \par \uc2 Added together, the data show that the US market has plenty of supply, and crude prices fell to reflect this.\par \par \uc2 Oil has also been hit by fading hopes for early cuts in US interest rates. Lower borrowing costs should boost oil demand, but that seems less likely given the stickiness of US inflation.\par \par \uc2 Also coming up today\par \par \uc2 Financial markets are bracing for the latest US Non-Farm Payroll \u8211\'20\'13 the monthly healthcheck on America\u8217\'20\'19s jobs market, due at 1.30pm UK time.\par \par \uc2 Economists expect a slowdown in job creation; the NFP is expected to rise by 238,000, down on the 303,000 gain in March. The jobless rate is tipped to remain at 3.8%.\par \par \uc2 Attention will also focus on wage growth, which is expected to slow slightly to 4%, from 4.1% in March.\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 7.45am BST: French industrial production for March\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9am BST: \uc2 Norges bank\uc2  interest rate decision\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9.30am BST: UK services \uc2 PMI\uc2  report for April\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1.30pm BST: US non-farm payroll for April\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 3pm BST: US services \uc2 PMI\uc2  report for April\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.49am BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240503ek53000mb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
