\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  \u8216\'20\'18the first domino to fall\u8217\'20\'19 after union vote, says \uc2 UAW\uc2  president\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,189 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2024\par 15:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Shawn Fain tells the Guardian he expects \u8216\'20\'18more of the same to come\u8217\'20\'19 after celebrating union\u8217\'20\'19s historic victory at Tennessee plant\par \par \uc2 After celebrating his union\u8217\'20\'19s historic victory at a \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  plant in Tennessee, Shawn Fain, president of the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 , told the Guardian that he was confident of more unionization wins at auto plants across the US, saying: \u8220\'20\'1CThe workers at VW are the first domino to fall.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey have shown it is possible,\u8221\'20\'1D Fain added in an interview on Sunday evening. \u8220\'20\'1CI expect more of the same to come. Workers are fed up.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The three-day unionization vote at \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  ended last Friday, with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/19/volkswagen-unionize-uaw-tennessee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 union winning}}}\uc2  overwhelmingly, 2,628 to 985 \u8211\'20\'13 the first time workers at a foreign-owned auto plant in the south have unionized. It was the world\u8217\'20\'19s only non-union VW plant. The vote in Chattanooga was the first union vote in the \uc2 UAW\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ambitious $40m campaign targeting 13 automakers, including VW, Mercedes, \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , \uc2 BMW\uc2 , \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , \uc2 Nissan\uc2  and Hyundai, with a total of 35 non-union plants across the US.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s next scheduled unionization vote will be at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/05/mercedes-benz-alabama-union-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mercedes plant}}}\uc2  in Vance, Alabama, where 5,000 workers will vote 13-17 May. Mercedes has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.al.com/news/2024/02/mercedes-exec-leads-alabama-anti-union-meeting-i-dont-believe-the-uaw-can-help-us-to-be-better.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 considerably more outspoken}}}\uc2  against the union than VW was, with a top Mercedes official telling workers: \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t believe the \uc2 UAW\uc2  can help us to be better.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Fain voiced great confidence about winning at Mercedes despite the company\u8217\'20\'19s anti-union efforts. \u8220\'20\'1CAt the end of the day, I believe that workers at Mercedes definitely want a union,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, \u8220\'20\'1Cand I believe a big majority there will vote in favor.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s great to see those workers feeling their power and being able to exercise their power\par \par \uc2 Shawn Fain\par \par \uc2 At Mercedes, rank-and-file workers, not \uc2 UAW\uc2  officials, have taken the lead in organizing the plant. \u8220\'20\'1CWorkers at Mercedes have literally run this campaign with very little help from us,\u8221\'20\'1D Fain said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey wanted it that way. It\u8217\'20\'19s great to see those workers feeling their power and being able to exercise their power.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He scoffed at attacks from corporate executives and southern politicians that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is a third party. He said that the union was its members, not an outside group, and that it was the companies that bring in third parties \u8211\'20\'13 outside consultants who spread anti-union information.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe employees aren\u8217\'20\'19t fooled by the companies\u8217\'20\'19 tricks any more,\u8221\'20\'1D Fain said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s the same tactics companies use every time. Workers have seen it repeatedly. I believe the workers are ready. They know the companies will continue to use fear and continue to use politicians as their surrogates using fear.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the Guardian interview, Fain fired back at the governors of six southern states who had condemned the \uc2 UAW\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s campaign, saying it was \u8220\'20\'1Cdriven by misinformation\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re liars. The people who are doing the misleading are them,\u8221\'20\'1D Fain said. \u8220\'20\'1CThese politicians are showing that they\u8217\'20\'19re just puppets for corporate America, and they don\u8217\'20\'19t give a damn about working-class people. They don\u8217\'20\'19t care about the workers being left behind even though the workers are the ones who elect them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Fain was palpably angry that the Republican governors of Tennessee, Alabama, Georgia, Mississippi, South Carolina and Texas had issued a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tn.gov/governor/news/2024/4/16/gov--lee-joins-coalition-of-governors-in-opposing-uaw-s-unionization-campaign.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joint letter denouncing}}}\uc2  the \uc2 UAW\uc2  as \u8220\'20\'1Cspecial interests looking to come into our state and threaten our jobs\u8221\'20\'1D. Fain said the governors were the ones \u8220\'20\'1Cwrecking the economy because they don\u8217\'20\'19t care about working people having a decent wage. It\u8217\'20\'19s working-class people who move the economy.\u8221\'20\'1D He added that the governors\u8217\'20\'19 \u8220\'20\'1Ceconomy is the economy of the billionaire class and corporate class where they take all the profits, and the workers get left behind\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In a celebratory meeting with the VW workers in Chattanooga late on Friday night and in his interview with the Guardian, Fain said the VW workers now needed to focus on bargaining a first union contract. \u8220\'20\'1CI expect good things to happen for the VW workers,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe want to use the big three contracts as our framework.\u8221\'20\'1D He added that VW\u8217\'20\'19s workers would play a big role in formulating \u8220\'20\'1Ca list of demands\u8221\'20\'1D for the contract talks.\par \par \uc2 Fain acknowledged that unionizing \uc2 Tesla\uc2  might prove especially difficult, considering how fiercely anti-union \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is. \u8220\'20\'1CElon\u8217\'20\'19s extreme hostility to unions tells us about who he is,\u8221\'20\'1D Fain said. \u8220\'20\'1CHe\u8217\'20\'19s a billionaire who\u8217\'20\'19s more concerned about ego and building rockets and flying to the moon than about taking care of the people who enabled him to do that, the people who create \uc2 Tesla\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s profits.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Fain acknowledged that Musk \u8220\'20\'1Cwill probably be a lot more hostile\u8221\'20\'1D to a union drive than other CEOs. \u8220\'20\'1CAt the end of the day, it\u8217\'20\'19s the workers\u8217\'20\'19 choice. It\u8217\'20\'19s not \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s choice,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CAs we continue to organize more and more companies, that makes it more and more inevitable that it [unionizing \uc2 Tesla\uc2 ] is going to happen. We\u8217\'20\'19re going to continue to show up and assist workers who want justice and better treatment on the job.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Asked what Joe Biden should do to attract union members\u8217\'20\'19 votes, Fain said: \u8220\'20\'1CHe has to continue doing what he\u8217\'20\'19s been doing, which is supporting workers in their fights.\u8221\'20\'1D Fain praised Biden for becoming the first sitting president to walk a union picket line \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/sep/26/why-biden-uaw-strike-visit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden joined a UAW picket line}}}\uc2  in Michigan last September when the union was on strike against Detroit\u8217\'20\'19s big three automakers. He also praised Biden for making it easier to unionize EV battery plants.\par \par \uc2 Fain criticized \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s stance on EVs. Trump has warned that Biden\u8217\'20\'19s plans to expand EV sales could be a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2024-election/trump-fights-electric-vehicles-defeat-biden-michigan-violent-rhetoric-rcna145689"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bloodbath for the US economy}}}\uc2  and a boon for Chinese manufacturers. Fain told the Guardian that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s plans to shrink America\u8217\'20\'19s EV industry could endanger many auto workers. \u8220\'20\'1CThere are a lot of workers out there that in the [electric vehicle] industry who stand to lose their jobs,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 At the victory celebration in Chattanooga, Fain urged VW\u8217\'20\'19s workers to help auto workers elsewhere unionize their plants. To inspire the workers, Fain quoted Matthew 17:20: \u8220\'20\'1CFor truly I tell you, if you have faith the size of a mustard seed, you will say to this mountain, move from here to there, and it will move; and nothing will be impossible for you.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Fain told the Guardian that faith is important to him. \u8220\'20\'1CThe foundation of all religion is love \u8211\'20\'13 love of your fellow human beings,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s important when we talk as workers and as labor that we talk about these things, that everything we do is about making life better for human beings. When three families have as much wealth as the bottom 50% of Americans, that\u8217\'20\'19s the antithesis of everything that religion teaches. I\u8217\'20\'19ll continue to lean on my faith. I don\u8217\'20\'19t keep that any secret.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240422ek4m0025v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
