\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s and \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  are helping \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  undermine the \uc2 US government\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,183 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 April 2024\par 11:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  is trying to kill a federal agency that accused it of labor violations. Ostensibly progressive brands have leaped to join in\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/elonmusk/status/1499976967105433600"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 boasts}}}\uc2  that he\u8217\'20\'19s a \u8220\'20\'1Cfree speech absolutist\u8221\'20\'1D, but that didn\u8217\'20\'19t stop his rocket company, \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/11/17/business/spacex-workers-elon-musk.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 from firing eight workers}}}\uc2  who had criticized him for making light of reports that \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  had settled a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businessinsider.com/spacex-paid-250000-to-a-flight-attendant-who-accused-elon-musk-of-sexual-misconduct-2022-5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sexual harassment claim}}}\uc2  against him.\par \par \uc2 Not stopping there, \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  has moved to put the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ), the US\u8217\'20\'19s top labor watchdog, out of business. Earlier this year, a day after the board accused \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/01/03/business/spacex-elon-musk-nlrb-workers.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 illegally retaliating}}}\uc2  against those workers, \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  filed a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.natlawreview.com/article/launching-new-territory-spacex-claims-nlrb-unconstitutionally-structured"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first-of-its-kind lawsuit}}}\uc2  that seeks to have the labor board \u8211\'20\'13 which has successfully overseen relations between business and unions since the 1930s \u8211\'20\'13 declared unconstitutional and shut down.\par \par \uc2 In so doing, Musk and \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  have joined a broader, rightwing effort that hopes to hobble the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s ability to regulate business. Indeed, \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit could serve as a potent wrecking ball in the right\u8217\'20\'19s push to weaken and perhaps demolish the administrative state \u8211\'20\'13 the network of federal agencies that the \uc2 US Congress\uc2  created to, among other things, promote workers\u8217\'20\'19 safety on the job, prevent fraud in financial markets, protect workers\u8217\'20\'19 right to unionize, limit environmental hazards, make sure consumer products are safe and administer Social Security for seniors.\par \par \uc2 With their lawsuit, \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and Musk \u8211\'20\'13 who owns 42% of that company\u8217\'20\'19s shares and controls 79% of its voting power \u8211\'20\'13 are seeking not just to silence the eight employees who criticized Musk, but also to shut down the agency that protects such workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights to speak out at all. Musk, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/real-time-billionaires/#304ba413d788"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $180bn man}}}\uc2 , is throwing a legal temper tantrum because the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has sought to hold him and \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  accountable.\par \par \uc2 Those employees {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/11/17/business/spacex-workers-elon-musk.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  a letter saying: \u8220\'20\'1CElon\u8217\'20\'19s behavior in the public sphere is a frequent source of distraction and embarrassment for us.\u8221\'20\'1D They wrote that letter after \uc2 Business Insider\uc2  reported that \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businessinsider.com/spacex-paid-250000-to-a-flight-attendant-who-accused-elon-musk-of-sexual-misconduct-2022-5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 paid $250,000}}}\uc2  to silence a company flight attendant who accused Musk of exposing himself and propositioning her for sex. Musk {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/elonmusk/status/1527505449905528846"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dismissed her}}}\uc2  in a tweet, saying she was a \u8220\'20\'1Cliar\u8221\'20\'1D and that the incident \u8220\'20\'1Cnever happened\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s complaint against \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  is based on a law, the National Labor Relations Act, that makes it illegal for companies to fire or otherwise retaliate against workers who join together to push to improve work conditions. In their letter, the eight employees also called on \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  to spell out its anti-harassment policies and enforce them more effectively.\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit succeeds in getting the federal courts to declare the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  unconstitutional, it could set a dangerous precedent that other courts seize on to weaken or even eviscerate other federal agencies, such as the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  (\uc2 EPA\uc2 ), the \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration (Osha)\uc2 , and perhaps even the \uc2 Federal Election Commission\uc2  (\uc2 FEC\uc2 ) and the \uc2 Social Security Administration\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit seeks to build on a case in which George R Jarkesy Jr, a rightwing activist and radio talk-show host, persuaded the hard-right fifth circuit court of appeals to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/ideas/archive/2023/11/securities-and-exchange-commission-v-jarkesy-supreme-court/676059/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declare}}}\uc2  the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  unconstitutional after it fined Jarkesy hundreds of thousands of dollars for defrauding investors.\par \par \uc2 In their effort to blow up the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 , Musk and \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  are hoping to capitalize on the federal judiciary\u8217\'20\'19s sharp rightward turn \u8211\'20\'13 a shift accelerated during \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s presidency. It shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be a surprise that \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  filed its lawsuit in Texas, the state that arguably has the nation\u8217\'20\'19s most extreme, most activist conservative federal judges. Following \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s lead, Amazon, Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s and \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  also filed legal papers seeking to have the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  declared unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 Like \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , those companies face \uc2 NLRB\uc2  charges of illegally retaliating against workers. One way to look at all this is that a band of billionaires \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , Amazon\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2 , and Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s German owners, the Albrecht family \u8211\'20\'13 are seeking to kill the federal agency that protects typical workers when they seek to unionize or merely speak up for better conditions.\par \par \uc2 Using uncharacteristically tough language, \uc2 Jennifer Abruzzo\uc2 , the labor board\u8217\'20\'19s general counsel, slammed \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and the other companies as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.huffpost.com/entry/biden-labor-appointee-fires-back-at-spacex-trader-joes_n_6615895fe4b02edf20083c4f?link_id=7&can_id=6342b6b27a7dc11d0ba689e86dada96f&source=email-afscme-fyi-wednesday-april-10-2024&email_referrer=email_2275300&email_subject=afscme-fyi-nil-wednesday-april-10-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cdeep-pocketed, low-road employers\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  that seek to stop the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  from fulfilling its pro-worker mission \u8220\'20\'1Cbecause they have the money to do so\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUnfortunately,\u8221\'20\'1D Abruzzo added, it seems that \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and the others \u8220\'20\'1Cwould rather spend money initiating court litigation than improving their workers\u8217\'20\'19 lives\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 If these \u8220\'20\'1Clow-road employers\u8221\'20\'1D prevail, the whole \uc2 NLRB\uc2  process of holding union elections and prosecuting companies that violate labor laws could crumble. This \u8220\'20\'1Cwould leave US workers more vulnerable to exploitation\u8221\'20\'1D, Kate Andrias, a law professor at Columbia, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theconversation.com/amazon-spacex-and-other-companies-are-arguing-the-government-agency-that-has-protected-labor-rights-since-1935-is-actually-unconstitutional-225611"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  recently.\par \par \uc2 Of course, for \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s, this effort to have the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  declared unconstitutional could backfire \u8211\'20\'13 sabotaging the \u8220\'20\'1Cprogressive\u8221\'20\'1D image they have long sought to cultivate. Many \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s customers might be outraged that the companies that furnish them with lattes and organic produce have joined this conservative legal and political assault.\par \par \uc2 Many legal experts have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://onlabor.org/understanding-the-latest-constitutional-attacks-on-the-nlrb/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 derided}}}\uc2  one of \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s main arguments: that the labor board\u8217\'20\'19s administrative law judges \u8211\'20\'13 who determine, for instance, whether a company violated the law by firing pro-union workers \u8211\'20\'13 should be deemed unconstitutional. \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  asserts that the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s judges exercise executive functions and therefore that the president, as the head of the executive branch, should be free to fire them. (Under federal labor law, they can be fired only for cause.) \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  makes this argument even though it\u8217\'20\'19s crystal clear that the labor board\u8217\'20\'19s judges merely do what judges do: issue judicial decisions.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, what \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  is demanding would allow Trump, if re-elected, to do something that corporate America would hate \u8211\'20\'13 fire labor board judges because they upset him by ruling in favor of companies whose CEOs had criticized him or not donated to his campaign. Administrative judges \u8211\'20\'13 whether labor board judges, immigration judges or Social Security judges \u8211\'20\'13 have legal protections against being summarily fired so that they can make honest, independent decisions without fear of being terminated for political reasons.\par \par \uc2 It is sad, if not altogether surprising, that \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , Amazon, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s have joined a rightwing effort to destroy the federal agencies that set the rules that helped make the US the world\u8217\'20\'19s richest nation and Musk, Bezos, Schultz and other billionaires fabulously wealthy. Now these billionaires are seeking to destroy the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  so that they can become even more fabulously wealthy.\par \par \uc2 This is yet another unsettling example of plutocrats exercising their financial might to reshape government to their liking. It\u8217\'20\'19s an effort that, if successful, will hurt millions of average Americans \u8211\'20\'13 consumers, workers, small investors and anyone who wants the environment protected.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s hoping that public interest prevails over Musk and the billionaires.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Steven Greenhouse, a senior fellow at the \uc2 Century Foundation\uc2 , is an American labor and workplace journalist and writer\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240417ek4h001ba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
