\par\par \uc2 Society\par \b \uc2 Workers at California cannabis-delivery service threaten \u8216\'20\'18unprecedented\u8217\'20\'19 strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 938 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 April 2024\par 17:31 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 After Eaze workers unionized with \uc2 United Food and Commercial Workers\uc2 , company cut pay and wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t address it in negotiations\par \par \uc2 Delivery drivers and other workers at the US\u8217\'20\'19s largest cannabis-delivery company are threatening to strike in what would probably be the biggest work stoppage to hit the cannabis industry.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 600 workers at 11 depot locations owned by Eaze, a cannabis-delivery company in southern and northern California, are threatening to strike over pay and conditions.\par \par \uc2 Eaze, founded in 2014 in San Francisco with millions of dollars in venture-backed funding, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://time.com/3821236/snoop-dogg-eaze-marijuana-delivery/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 including}}}\uc2  from Snoop Dogg, has been characterized as the \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Uber\uc2  of weed\u8221\'20\'1D. The privately held company was valued at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://techcrunch.com/2021/08/19/eaze-to-become-americas-largest-cannabis-delivery-service-after-buying-green-dragon/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than $700m}}}\uc2  in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Two of their biggest investors, Thomas Jermoluk and the tech {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/profile/james-clark/?sh=5b7720f646f8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 billionaire James Henry Clark}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/sites/cyrusfarivar/2023/07/31/cannabis-delivery-firm-eaze-and-its-billionaire-investor-accused-of-fraud-in-new-lawsuit/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly}}}\uc2  own a 35% share of the private company.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Food and Commercial Workers (UFCW)\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ufcw770.org/internal-news/eaze-strike-4-2-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 characterized}}}\uc2  the strike as \u8220\'20\'1Cunprecedented\u8221\'20\'1D and said the strike vote was called after workers resoundingly rejected Eaze\u8217\'20\'19s last and final union contract offer.\par \par \uc2 If the workers walk off the job, it would probably be the biggest work stoppage to hit the cannabis industry in the US, which has seen an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/31/us-cannabis-industry-union-recognition"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 uptick in union organizing}}}\uc2  and labor unrest as the pace of corporatization in the cannabis industry has accelerated.\par \par \uc2 Among the unresolved issues include a mileage rate pay cut implemented by Eaze after the workers unionized that the company has refused to address in contract negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Ron Swallow has worked as a delivery driver for Eaze in Los Angeles for around 2.5 years. He said pay and scheduling were good when he started working but that Eaze started unilaterally implementing cuts to work schedules and staffing at the depot where their deliveries are sourced from. He and his co-workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ufcw.org/actions/victories/cannabis-delivery-workers-in-california-join-local-770/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unionized}}}\uc2  with near unanimous support in March 2023 in response to the changes they were experiencing.\par \par \uc2 Then in the summer of 2023, Swallow said, the pay for drivers was significantly cut. Workers have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/search/case/Stachs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed}}}\uc2  unfair labor practice charges with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  over the unilateral change made outside of bargaining.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey cut our mileage rate. We\u8217\'20\'19d been hired at 56 cents a mile and then they cut it to 42 cents,\u8221\'20\'1D said Swallow. \u8220\'20\'1CThat cost drivers $300 to $700 a month on average, depending on how much you drive and that was pretty annoying. It was also the first time in my whole life I\u8217\'20\'19ve ever come to a job and then been told I was going to get paid less.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Due to the lack of progress in negotiations, the workers voted to authorize a strike if no progress was made before 20 April, one of the busiest sales days in the cannabis industry.\par \par \uc2 Swallow explained the costs he and his co-workers bear on the personal vehicles they use for work. Swallow said he ran over some debris on a highway during work and even with car insurance coverage he still had to pay thousands of dollars in repair costs for the damage and, because of the mileage cuts, he had to take out loans to cover it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey wanted to offer us 45 cents and we of course said no to that because it didn\u8217\'20\'19t get us back up to where we started out. We\u8217\'20\'19re very united,\u8221\'20\'1D said Swallow. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you join with a union, you just become the union, the people working become that and that is one of the coolest things that I\u8217\'20\'19ve seen, drivers come together just being supportive of each other and our staff as well, because our staff is underpaid, too.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lori Riehle, a delivery driver for Eaze at another depot in Los Angeles, criticized the claims from Eaze about the company not financially being able to pay workers more while claiming they are already paid enough.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEaze says they are the No 1 cannabis-delivery provider in the country and while that sounds great, I wonder why they don\u8217\'20\'19t feel like we are important enough to support us in a way that we need to be supported so we can do that job successfully and safely,\u8221\'20\'1D said Riehle.\u8221\'20\'1DWe are struggling to cover our expenses under this lower-tiered mileage.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She claimed the concerns of workers about pay and expenses, and safety concerns over understaffing and a lack of security at depots where expensive cannabis products and money are held, had been ignored by the company.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt can be a dangerous job and we just want to be not only compensated enough so we can take care of ourselves, our families and our vehicles. We\u8217\'20\'19re worth more than what we\u8217\'20\'19re getting,\u8221\'20\'1D she added. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re really proud of what we do, we\u8217\'20\'19re proud of our jobs and I wish that the company would recognize that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a statement by email, Cory Azzalino, the CEO of Eaze, denied the claims from their workers about low pay. He said the company was preparing to maintain operations in the event of a strike.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn an industry being suffocated from high taxes and over-regulation, Eaze pays our drivers fair wages averaging over $25 per hour including tips, as well as benefits and consistent scheduling,\u8221\'20\'1D Azzalino wrote in an email. \u8220\'20\'1CEaze\u8217\'20\'19s driver tenure is over 2.4 years with limited turnover and a sizable applicant pool more than 2x our current labor force, which is evidence of a reasonable compensation package. Eaze has not earned a profit in its history, so this is not the case of old industry hoarding profits.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240415ek4f0015x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
