\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 California fast-food workers\u8217\'20\'19 minimum wage win stirs up old economic debate\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 952 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 April 2024\par 17:42 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Business groups claim hard-fought $20 hourly wage victory will cause reduced hours, layoffs and price hikes \u8211\'20\'13 critics say otherwise\par \par \uc2 As fast-food workers celebrated a pivotal wage increase to $20 an hour in California last week, an old economic debate was awakened by business groups and others claiming the increase will wind up hurting workers through reduced hours and layoffs, hurt customers with price hikes, and harm the franchise owners of fast-food restaurants.\par \par \uc2 Their critics are not so sure.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/sep/28/california-fast-food-workers-minimum-wages-victory"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hard-fought}}}\uc2  wage increase to $20 an hour from California\u8217\'20\'19s current minimum wage, $16 an hour, was a compromise to initial demands of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usatoday.com/story/money/business/2022/12/14/california-fast-food-law-blocked-voters-decide-2024/10891862002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $22 an hour}}}\uc2  with annual wage increases. Representatives of fast-food workers and the fast-food industry came to a deal to avoid what would have been a costly ballot initiative over the passage and signing of the California fast-food sector bill last year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFrontline workers like me organized, went on strike, and fought to pass a historic law that raises our wages and gives us a seat at table with some of the biggest fast-food corporations in the world,\u8221\'20\'1D said Anjelica Hernandez, a McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s worker in Los Angeles for nearly 20 years. \u8220\'20\'1CEven though we are the engine of a billion-dollar industry, too many of us struggle to keep up with rent, our bills and the rising cost of living.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The minimum wage for fast-food workers at larger chains was agreed last September and increased to $20 an hour on 1 April, but numerous reports have already been made that the wage hike is resulting in job losses and price increases for customers.\par \par \uc2 The decision by two Pizza Hut franchises in California to lay off more than 1,000 delivery driver employees and rely on gig delivery drivers instead has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abc7.com/pizza-hut-layoffs-california-delivery-drivers-fast-food-minimum-wage/14229041/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cited}}}\uc2  in claims that the minimum wage rise will result in layoffs and job losses.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe reality is that delivery drivers have become more numerous in the last couple years because of Covid,\u8221\'20\'1D said Michael Reich, \uc2 UC Berkeley\uc2  chair of the Center on Wage and Employment Dynamics. \u8220\'20\'1CThe pizza companies have been moving to make their delivery drivers not employees but instead rely on these delivery services for quite some time. And I think Pizza Hut is going to make this change anyway.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Reich also argued that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.berkeley.edu/2023/03/14/even-in-small-businesses-minimum-wage-hikes-dont-cause-job-losses-study-finds#:~:text=%E2%80%9CA%20minimum%20wage%20increase%20doesn,Turnover%20rates%20go%20down."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 previous research}}}\uc2  had found employment increases as a result of higher minimum wage, lowers turnover rates for businesses, and makes it easier to recruit and retain employees.\par \par \uc2 The research is unlikely to deter attacks.\par \par \uc2 A group representing fast-food franchise owners {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sacbee.com/news/politics-government/capitol-alert/article285712731.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 poured}}}\uc2  more than half a million dollars into political donations for ads opposing elected officials who helped craft and pass the legislation. A Wall Street Journal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/californias-crazy-fast-food-minimum-wage-takes-effect-negative-repercussions-7ae3e9ae?mod=business_trendingnow_opn_pos1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 op-ed}}}\uc2  referred to the California wage increase as \u8220\'20\'1Ccrazy\u8221\'20\'1D for paying \u8220\'20\'1Cburger flippers\u8221\'20\'1D $20 an hour. The New York Post {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nypost.com/2024/04/02/us-news/californias-20-fast-food-wage-raises-prices-by-up-to-1-80/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claimed}}}\uc2  Los Angeles fast-food chains increased prices on 1 April in response to the law, including citing price increases at the actor Kevin Hart\u8217\'20\'19s chain Hart House, which doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have enough locations to be subjected to the new law, which stipulates the chains must have at least 60 restaurants nationwide.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s franchise owner of 21 locations {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ktla.com/news/local-news/mcdonalds-franchisee-you-cant-raise-prices-enough-to-cover-minimum-wage-hike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 complained}}}\uc2  to a local Los Angeles news outlet that they could not \u8220\'20\'1Craise prices enough\u8221\'20\'1D. \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  and Chipotle {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usatoday.com/story/money/2023/11/01/chipotle-mcdonalds-raising-prices-california/71408254007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blamed}}}\uc2  the wage increase for anticipated price hikes in California ahead of the wage increase taking effect. \uc2 El Pollo Loco{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ktla.com/news/california/more-california-fast-food-chains-announce-changes-ahead-of-minimum-wage-hike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claimed}}}\uc2  it would be looking into automation to cut labor costs due to the law.\par \par \uc2 The Center for Union Facts, a non-profit founded by a Washington lobbyist, Rick Berman, who 60 Minutes referred to as \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/meet-rick-berman-aka-dr-evil/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dr Evil}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D for his tactics in advocating for business interests, is running {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://laborpains.org/2024/03/29/tv-commercial-slams-seiu-for-20-minimum-wage-hike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 television advertisements}}}\uc2  in California claiming the wage increase is a \u8220\'20\'1Cwrecking ball\u8221\'20\'1D for the fast-food industry.\par \par \uc2 But researchers have long criticized these claims of causation which have frequently been made in response to minimum wage increases around the US, all of which have come at the state level because Washington has failed to act on raising the federal minimum wage from the $7.25 an hour minimum last passed in 2009.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COverall, study after study shows that increases to the minimum wage actually have a net gain. They produce a stimulus effect. If you give poor people more money, they\u8217\'20\'19re going to spend it locally, including on rent, utilities and housing. It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t get much more local than that, and that increased spending actually produces jobs,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tia Koonse, legal and policy research manager at the UCLA Labor Center.\par \par \uc2 An analysis of financial data over the past decade by researchers at the Roosevelt Institute found the fast-food industry has more than enough profits to raise wages without affecting prices and employment and have been hiking prices far beyond their operating costs for years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn 2023 alone, the 10 largest publicly traded fast-food companies spent $6.1bn on share repurchases,\u8221\'20\'1D noted the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rooseveltinstitute.org/publications/fast-food-industry-profiteering/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CAs with most industries, the fast-food industry has historically charged higher prices than their marginal costs. However, between 2014 and 2023, markups increased at an annual rate of 2.2%, faster than at any period since 1978.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ali Bustamante, author of the report and deputy director of worker power and economic security at the Roosevelt Institute, added: \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s really an opportunity here to revert profits back into workers back into the source of productivity for these businesses in a way that is going to be beneficial, not just for the overall economy, but certainly for these businesses in the long term.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240406ek460012z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
