\par\par \uc2 UK news\par \b \uc2 Digested week: these fallback French hot cross buns are quite wrong\b0\par\par\uc2 Emma Brockes \par \uc2 1,145 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 April 2024\par 13:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 An Easter bake letdown, a cruise ship scramble, and just the man you want to hear from on work-life balance\par \par \uc2 Monday\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s very hard to find proper hot cross buns in New York without stumbling on a range of impostors. There are bread rolls with raisins. There are croissants in disguise. There are a range of what can only be classified as muffins. This year, after the closure of my one reliable go-to at Easter \u8211\'20\'13 a local Aussie bakery \u8211\'20\'13 I fall back on the French. A few days before Easter, the seasonal offerings at the French bakery loom large in the window and I let myself dream things will turn out all right.\par \par \uc2 OK, their hot cross buns are very, very large, an extravagance that feels, in the context of pastry, aggressively French and quite against the nature of a true hot cross bun: that is, a baked good with the density of something that has been left on the back seat of the car and sat upon by multiple children.\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19ve also made the classic American error of using white icing for the cross. I know, icing. It\u8217\'20\'19s not a Belgian bun, for the love of God. Plus the finish is matte, which is quite wrong. The ideal hot cross bun, as we know, should have a glaze on it so fierce you can apply your makeup in the reflection.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, they are the best thing on offer this Easter and I buy six to cut open and toast. Well. The inside is so shocking I take a photo to send to friends. I\u8217\'20\'19m not sure what happened, perhaps the baker panicked or gave up. Either way, I will never erase the image of a large heap of raisins, dumped in the centre like a pie filling, surrounded by a dome of pure air. No mixed peel. No aroma that, while looking forward to the citrus of summer, carries with it a last whisper of Christmas. They\u8217\'20\'19re still buns, obviously, so I finish the lot in two days. But they\u8217\'20\'19re not hot cross buns.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday\par \par \uc2 There is a new category of news story that sits, for popularity, just below that of passengers arguing about seats on a plane. It is this: cruise ship captains refusing to board guests who arrive even marginally late to the dock, leaving them and their wheelie suitcases stranded. They\u8217\'20\'19re corkers, these stories, summoning as they do, for many of us, our worst fears about racing and failing to make an imminent departure.\par \par \uc2 This week it was the captain of a Norwegian cruise behemoth who refused to budge when eight passengers rocked up late after a day trip around S\u227\'00\'E3o Tom\u233\'00\'E9, an island off the coast of Gabon. Even after a coastguard took the group on launches to the side of the ship, the captain wasn\u8217\'20\'19t having it. And so {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/apr/02/stranded-cruise-ship-passengers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the ship sailed}}}\uc2 , taking all their gear, including bank cards, with it.\par \par \uc2 What happened next is the stuff of nightmares or an under-par Todd Phillips movie. The ship\u8217\'20\'19s next stop, scheduled to be in the Gambia, seemed a doable scramble and the passengers hurried to meet it. But this, too, failed, when bad weather prevented the ship from docking. The desperate eight eventually caught up with the 92,000-tonne vessel in Senegal, six countries and a reported $5,000 spend later.\par \par \uc2 Under the delicious subhead \u8220\'20\'1CCostly Lesson\u8221\'20\'1D, two of the stranded passengers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.news.com.au/travel/travel-updates/incidents/ridiculous-cruise-act-sparks-huge-debate/news-story/0860b5a26477d4708b78950d5b4852a7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 appeared}}}\uc2  in the Australian media to declare the experience \u8220\'20\'1Cthe worst of our lives\u8221\'20\'1D, although for my money they had it all wrong. The only thing worse, surely, than missing a cruise of that size would be arriving on time to successfully join it.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday\par \par \uc2 A New York screening of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2024/apr/04/scoop-review-prince-andrew-newsnight-interview-emily-maitlis-gillian-anderson-rufus-sewell"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Scoop}}}\uc2 , the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  film based on Sam McAlister\u8217\'20\'19s book about snagging Prince Andrew for his disastrous interview on Newsnight, delivers a small but real pleasure: that of watching British experience tailored for an American audience. The majority of American viewers, who will have never heard of Newsnight, may be inclined to believe the film\u8217\'20\'19s suggestion that British newsrooms are places where editors make inspirational speeches, rather than a bunch of grumpy hacks wondering when they can break for the canteen.\par \par \uc2 Billie Piper is great as McAlister, Rufus Sewell is appropriately dim-witted as Andrew, and Gillian Anderson as Emily Maitlis steals the show. Yes, says Anderson when I ask her after the screening if she has ever run into Maitlis herself (they live not far from each other in west London). Shortly after filming, while walking in the park, she was nearly knocked sideways by Maitlis\u8217\'20\'19s famous dog Moody. For a hot second, says Anderson, she thought of calling out to the woman she\u8217\'20\'19d just depicted on screen. But on reflection \u8211\'20\'13 Maitlis has her own Prince Andrew-based project in the works and had no involvement with this production at all \u8211\'20\'13 sensibly thought better of it.\par \par \uc2 Thursday\par \par \uc2 Who would you most like to hear from on the subject of how to balance your life with your work? Is it Jeff Bezos, the world\u8217\'20\'19s third richest man? Well, too bad, he has thoughts. Although they were made several years ago, Bezos\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businessinsider.com/jeff-bezos-work-life-balance-debilitating-phrase-career-circle-2021-7#:~:text=He%20said%20the%20reality%20is,%22It's%20not%20a%20balance.%22"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 considerations}}}\uc2  on the subject of how to be an effective human recirculated this week in response to the release of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/billionaires/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Forbes billionaire list}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 with Bezos in at No 3, behind Elon Musk and ahead of Mark Zuckerberg \u8211\'20\'13 and truly, they are inspiration to all.\par \par \uc2 Briefly, Bezos believes the word \u8220\'20\'1Cbalance\u8221\'20\'1D is \u8220\'20\'1Cdebilitating\u8221\'20\'1D and should be replaced with the word \u8220\'20\'1Charmony\u8221\'20\'1D, or the phrase \u8220\'20\'1Cwork-life circle\u8221\'20\'1D. This is because, he says, if he\u8217\'20\'19s happy at home he has \u8220\'20\'1Ctremendous energy\u8221\'20\'1D at work, and if he\u8217\'20\'19s happy at work it makes him happy at home. The role played by his estimated $194bn fortune in the release of this happiness isn\u8217\'20\'19t discussed, but I\u8217\'20\'19m sure those working in his warehouses are, while on their timed sprints to the loo, grateful to ponder the insight.\par \par \uc2 Friday\par \par \uc2 Hard on the heels of women\u8217\'20\'19s football relaxing white uniform codes to alleviate period anxiety, the governing gymnastics body in New Zealand has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2024/apr/02/new-zealand-scrap-archaic-gymnastics-rules-to-allow-shorts-over-leotards"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 revamped}}}\uc2  its dress code to allow women to wear shorts over leotards. Henceforth, gymnasts competing for Gymnastics New Zealand will be given a chance to opt out of the highly sexualised mandatory attire and won\u8217\'20\'19t be penalised for \u8220\'20\'1Cvisible underwear\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a real thing, incredibly, carrying potential point deductions of up to 1.00. The rule change only holds for competitions in New Zealand, however. Everywhere else, female gymnasts remain stuck, alongside the beach volleyball players and the women\u8217\'20\'19s tennis world, in the wasteland between Playboy and a 70s PE class.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240405ek450018l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
