\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 New Biden rule aims to protect US federal employees if Trump is elected\b0\par\par\uc2 Guardian staff and agency \par \uc2 753 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 April 2024\par 20:08 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Regulations will bar career civil servants from being reclassified as political appointees \u8211\'20\'13 who are more easily dismissed\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/joebiden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Joe Biden}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s administration issued a new rule on Thursday making it harder to fire thousands of federal employees, hoping to head off the risk that if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins back the White House in November he won\u8217\'20\'19t be able to bully and decimate the workforce as he imposes the radical ideologies he\u8217\'20\'19s been pushing on the campaign trail, escalating what he did while in office.\par \par \uc2 New regulations coming out of the government\u8217\'20\'19s chief human resources agency, the Office of Personnel Management, will bar career civil servants from being reclassified as political appointees or as other at-will workers \u8211\'20\'13 who are more easily dismissed from their jobs.\par \par \uc2 The move comes in response to so-called \u8220\'20\'1CSchedule F\u8221\'20\'1D, an executive order Trump issued in 2020 that sought to allow for reclassifying tens of thousands of the 2.2m federal employees and thus reduce their job security protections.\par \par \uc2 Biden nullified Schedule F upon taking office. But if Trump were to win the election for the Republicans and revive it during a second administration, he could dramatically increase number of federal employees \u8211\'20\'13 about 4,000 \u8211\'20\'13 who are considered political appointees and typically change with each new president.\par \par \uc2 In a statement issued Thursday, Biden called the rule a \u8220\'20\'1Cstep toward combatting corruption and partisan interference to ensure civil servants are able to focus on the most important task at hand: delivering for the American people\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The potential effects of the change are wide-reaching because the number of federal employees who might have been affected by Schedule F under Trump is unclear.\par \par \uc2 The National Treasury Employee Union used freedom of information requests to obtain documents suggesting that workers such as office managers and specialists in human resources and cybersecurity might have been among those subject to reclassification.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s new rule moves to counter a future Schedule F order by spelling out procedural requirements for reclassifying federal employees and clarifying that civil service protections accrued by employees can\u8217\'20\'19t be taken away, regardless of job type. It also makes clear that policymaking classifications apply to noncareer, political appointments.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt will now be much harder for any president to arbitrarily remove the nonpartisan professionals who staff our federal agencies just to make room for hand-picked partisan loyalists,\u8221\'20\'1D said Doreen Greenwald, president of \uc2 National Treasury Employees Union\uc2 , in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Groups advocating for ethical government, and liberal think tanks and activists, praise the rule. They viewed cementing federal worker protections as a top priority given that replacing existing government employees with new, more conservative alternatives is key to \uc2 the conservative Heritage Foundation\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s nearly 1,000-page playbook known as Project 2025.\par \par \uc2 That plan calls for vetting and potentially firing scores of federal workers and recruiting conservative replacements to wipe out what leading Republicans have long decried as the \u8220\'20\'1Cdeep state\u8221\'20\'1D governmental bureaucracy that allegedly worked against Trump from the inside. This is a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/apr/06/john-boehner-book-trump-obsession-deep-state-conspiracy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 debunked concept}}}\uc2  that even Trump acolyte \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/oct/03/trump-steve-bannon-deep-state-conspiracy-theory"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dismissed}}}\uc2  as untrue {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/mar/03/steve-bannon-trump-maga-2024-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 despite being part of}}}\uc2  the hard right movement that first aggressively promoted the idea and continues to market it.\par \par \uc2 Skye Perryman, president and CEO of Democracy Forward, which has led a coalition of nearly 30 advocacy organizations supporting the rule, called it \u8220\'20\'1Cextraordinarily strong\u8221\'20\'1D and said it can effectively counter the \u8220\'20\'1Chighly resourced, anti-democratic groups\u8221\'20\'1D behind Project 2025.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not a wonky issue, even though it may be billed that way at times,\u8221\'20\'1D Perryman said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is really foundational to how we can ensure that the government delivers for people and, for us, that\u8217\'20\'19s what a democracy is about.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The final rule, which runs to 237 pages, is being published in the federal registry and set to formally take effect next month.\par \par \uc2 Trump as president could direct the Office of Personnel Management to draft new rules, although those would face legal challenges.\par \par \uc2 Rob Shriver, deputy director of the Office of Personnel Management, said the new rule ensures the protections \u8220\'20\'1Ccannot be erased by a technical, HR process\u8221\'20\'1D that \u8220\'20\'1CSchedule F sought to do\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis rule is about making sure the American public can continue to count on federal workers to apply their skills and expertise in carrying out their jobs, no matter their personal political beliefs,\u8221\'20\'1D Shriver said on a call with reporters.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240404ek44001pg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
