\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Seven Gaza aid workers including UK, US and Australian citizens killed in Israeli strike, charity says\b0\par\par\uc2 Bethan McKernan in Jerusalem and Ben Doherty \par \uc2 1,028 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 April 2024\par 15:49 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Israeli military investigating after \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  workers were in convoy struck in central GazaMiddle East crisis live: latest updates\par \par \uc2 Seven people working with \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2 , a charity spearheading efforts to alleviate looming famine in Gaza, have been killed in an Israeli airstrike, the charity said, throwing humanitarian relief efforts in the Palestinian territory into chaos as the organisation said it would suspend operations.\par \par \uc2 The workers were part of a group travelling in three armoured vehicles branded with the charity\u8217\'20\'19s logo, according to a statement released early on Tuesday. \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  (\uc2 WCK\uc2 ) said those killed were from the UK, Australia, Poland and Palestine, as well as a US-Canada dual citizen.\par \par \uc2 The bodies of the aid workers were taken to a hospital in Gaza\u8217\'20\'19s southern city of Rafah, on the Egyptian border, according to an Associated Press reporter at the facility. Hospital records said three UK citizens had died.\par \par \uc2 The charity said: \u8220\'20\'1CDespite coordinating movements with the [Israeli army], the convoy was hit as it was leaving the Deir al-Balah warehouse, where the team had unloaded more than 100 tons of humanitarian food aid brought to Gaza on the maritime route.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Erin Gore, the \uc2 WCK\uc2  chief executive, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not only an attack against \uc2 WCK\uc2 , this is an attack on humanitarian organisations showing up in the most dire of situations where food is being used as a weapon of war. This is unforgivable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The charity will pause its operations in the region and says it will make a decision about the future of its work, raising fears that a nascent maritime corridor from Cyprus to deliver desperately needed aid to Gaza in the face of repeated Israeli obstructions may collapse.\par \par \uc2 Cyprus said on Tuesday afternoon that ships that recently arrived in Gaza were turning back with 240 tonnes of undelivered aid.\par \par \uc2 Israel\u8217\'20\'19s prime minister, \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , lamented the killings, which he said had been caused by an Israeli airstrike. He described the incident as tragic and unintended.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis happens in wartime. We are thoroughly looking into it, are in contact with the governments [of the foreigners among the victims] and will do everything to ensure it does not happen again,\u8221\'20\'1D he said in a video statement.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli military expressed \u8220\'20\'1Csincere sorrow\u8221\'20\'1D over the deaths while stopping short of accepting responsibility, adding that an investigation was under way.\par \par \uc2 A statement from the military said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 IDF\uc2  makes extensive efforts to enable the safe delivery of humanitarian aid, and has been working closely with \uc2 WCK\uc2  in their vital efforts to provide food and humanitarian aid to the people of Gaza.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Aid agencies\u8217\'20\'19 attempts to get humanitarian assistance to where it is most needed in Gaza have been severely hampered by a combination of logistical obstacles, a breakdown of public order and lengthy bureaucracy imposed by Israel. The number of aid trucks entering the territory by land over the past five months has been far below the 500 a day that entered before the war.\par \par \uc2 In February, more than 100 people were killed when Israeli forces opened fire at an aid distribution point in Gaza City. The Israeli military said most died in a crush, but Palestinian officials and witnesses denied this, saying the majority of those taken to hospital had bullet wounds.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UN\uc2  has said at least 576,000 people in the coastal territory \u8211\'20\'13 a quarter of the population \u8211\'20\'13 are on the brink of famine, and pressure has been growing on Israel to increase the flow of aid.\par \par \uc2 Aid ships that arrived on Monday carried 400 tonnes of food and supplies \u8211\'20\'13 enough for 1m meals \u8211\'20\'13 in a shipment funded by the United Arab Emirates and organised by \uc2 WCK\uc2 , but workers had only offloaded 100 tonnes before the attack led the charity to order the vessels to return to Cyprus.\par \par \uc2 Last month another \uc2 WCK\uc2  vessel delivered 200 tonnes of aid in a pilot run enabled by \uc2 WCK\uc2  volunteers and others in Gaza who built a jetty from the rubble of buildings destroyed in Israeli bombing during the past five months. The Israeli military was involved in coordinating both deliveries.\par \par \uc2 Washington, Israel\u8217\'20\'19s most important ally, has promoted the sea route as a new way to get desperately needed aid to northern Gaza, which is largely cut off from the rest of the territory by Israeli forces.\par \par \uc2 Israel has barred \uc2 Unrwa\uc2 , the main \uc2 UN\uc2  agency in Gaza, from making deliveries to the north after claiming several of its employees were involved in the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attack that triggered the war, now in its sixth month. Other aid groups say sending truck convoys north has been too dangerous because of the military\u8217\'20\'19s failure to ensure safe passage.\par \par \uc2 About 1,200 Israelis were killed and a further 250 taken hostage on 7 October, according to Israeli data, while more than 32,000 Palestinians have been killed in the ensuing Israeli offensive, according to the local health ministry in the \uc2 Hamas\uc2 -run territory.\par \par \uc2 Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Andr\u233\'00\'E9s, the founder of \uc2 WCK\uc2 , said on X that the charity \u8220\'20\'1Clost several of our sisters and brothers in an IDF airstrike in Gaza\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He wrote: \u8220\'20\'1CI am heartbroken and grieving for their families and friends and our whole \uc2 WCK\uc2  family. These are people \u8230\'20\'26 angels \u8230\'20\'26 I served alongside in Ukraine, Gaza, Turkey, Morocco, Bahamas, Indonesia. They are not faceless \u8230\'20\'26 they are not nameless.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He said the Israeli government needed to \u8220\'20\'1Cstop this indiscriminate killing\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Australian prime minister, Anthony Albanese, named the Australian national killed as Zomi Frankcom and called her work \u8220\'20\'1Cextraordinarily important\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Albanese said his government would call in the Israeli ambassador over an incident that he said was \u8220\'20\'1Cbeyond any reasonable circumstances\u8221\'20\'1D, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CAustralia expects full accountability for the deaths of aid workers, which is completely unacceptable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/guardian-live-events/2024/feb/16/guardian-newsroom-the-unfolding-crisis-in-the-middle-east"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Guardian Newsroom: The unfolding crisis in the Middle East}}}\uc2  On Tuesday 30 April, 7pm-8.15pm GMT, join Devika Bhat, Peter Beaumont, Emma Graham-Harrison and Ghaith Abdul-Ahad as they discuss the fast-developing crisis in the Middle East. Book tickets {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/guardian-live-events/2024/feb/16/guardian-newsroom-the-unfolding-crisis-in-the-middle-east"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  or at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/guardian-live-events"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 theguardian.live}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240401ek41002xm\par }\page {\