\par\par \uc2 Books\par \b \uc2 Barnes & Noble\uc2  workers plan union drive at largest US bookstore chain\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 999 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 April 2024\par 10:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Despite pushback from CEO \uc2 James Daunt\uc2 , employees are organizing for better pay, \u8216\'20\'18dignity\u8217\'20\'19 and working conditions\par \par \uc2 Workers at America\u8217\'20\'19s largest chain of bookstores are gearing up for a nationwide union drive after six \uc2 Barnes & Noble\uc2  outlets voted to organize over the past year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMany more\u8221\'20\'1D stores will unionize, according to booksellers demanding better pay and conditions.\par \par \uc2 At locations that already have, employees accuse the chain\u8217\'20\'19s management of dragging their heels during contract negotiations. \uc2 James Daunt\uc2 , the CEO, is said to have embarked upon a months-long campaign to dissuade employees from voting in favor.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe would come in and essentially try to talk us out of unionizing,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jessica Sepple, a bookseller for more than two years at \uc2 Barnes & Noble\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s flagship New York City store in Union Square. \u8220\'20\'1CThe big argument against us unionizing was it would make his life harder, which he would repeat several times. It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t very successful.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The store voted 76-2 in favor of unionizing last summer, becoming one of three in New York City to join the Retail, Wholesale and Department Store Union. Another followed in Bloomington, Illinois, while two others \u8211\'20\'13 in San Jose, California, and Hadley, Massachusetts \u8211\'20\'13 joined other unions.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Daunt disputed claims of delays at the negotiating table. He claimed he was in agreement with workers on \u8220\'20\'1Cthe fundamentals\u8221\'20\'1D of their demands \u8211\'20\'13 but warned of \u8220\'20\'1Cpotential upsides and downsides\u8221\'20\'1D to a union.\par \par \uc2 Barnes & Noble\uc2  has some 600 stores across the US, and Daunt \u8211\'20\'13 who became CEO in 2019 \u8211\'20\'13 has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2023/apr/15/barnes-and-noble-bookstores-james-daunt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 worked to turn around the business}}}\uc2 , which had spent years in decline. It is owned by the investment giant \uc2 Elliott Management\uc2 , which also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2018/apr/26/waterstones-bookshops-bought-by-investment-firm-elliott-advisors-james-daunt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 owns Britain\u8217\'20\'19s Waterstones}}}\uc2 , which is also run by Daunt.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur purpose for unionizing is to get some recognition for the dignity of workers,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sepple. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd having sat at the table and currently in negotiations with \uc2 Barnes & Noble\uc2 , it is disappointing that \uc2 Barnes & Noble\uc2  has not treated this as if that dignity is deserved.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Workers at the Union Square site have experienced several issues for years, she claimed, including lagging wages, safety concerns with ladders and book storage, aggressive customers, and being given more duties than a job initially entailed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19re good at your job, you\u8217\'20\'19re just going to get more work,\u8221\'20\'1D added Sepple. \u8220\'20\'1CIt takes a lot of knowledge, research and a love of reading and books to make it happen, and oftentimes I\u8217\'20\'19ve found the company tends to coast on that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At another New York store, in Brooklyn\u8217\'20\'19s Park Slope, workers won a union election in July 2023.\par \par \uc2 Sydul Akhanji, a bookseller of two years at the store, wants to work at \uc2 Barnes & Noble\uc2  for the long term, but says that low pay serves as a deterrent. If the company wants to build itself around knowledgeable booksellers, its workers need to be able to afford rent and food, he said.\par \par \uc2 Daunt tried to deter workers in Park Slope from unionizing, according to Akhanji, who claimed the chain\u8217\'20\'19s CEO \u8220\'20\'1Cjust went on and on about how it\u8217\'20\'19d be hard and make all his plans complicated, if we unionized, and how he has a vision for us, so please, just don\u8217\'20\'19t unionize right now\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Workers say contract negotiations have been slowed by \uc2 Barnes & Noble\uc2  insisting that demands and asks be referred to company lawyers, who have not been at the table. They have also been frustrated by management negotiating separately with individual New York stores.\par \par \uc2 On Manhattan\u8217\'20\'19s Upper West Side, workers at \uc2 Barnes & Noble\uc2  backed unionizing earlier this month. \u8220\'20\'1CWe live in the most expensive city in the country,\u8221\'20\'1D Esther Rosenfield, a barista at the store, said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd our starting wage until very recently was minimum wage, and it\u8217\'20\'19s just not sustainable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Shortly after her store filed for a union election, Rosenfield said, employees there and at other locations received a $2-an-hour market rate adjustment: \u8220\'20\'1CWe were told basically that this had nothing to do with the union. But I think people can draw their own conclusions based on just the proximity to when it happened.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It comes amid tentative signs of a nationwide movement. Workers in Bloomington, Illinois, heard about the union votes in New York last summer, and held their own \u8211\'20\'13 and unanimously voted to unionize \u8211\'20\'13 in November.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 James Daunt\uc2  did a conference call to the store himself saying a vote for the union is a vote against him,\u8221\'20\'1D claimed a senior bookseller, Zane Crockett. \u8220\'20\'1CThe issues we\u8217\'20\'19re facing are companywide. We\u8217\'20\'19re all facing the same issues. If one small store in the midwest can unionize, then anyone can..\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Barnes & Noble\uc2  workers in Bloomington had complained about issues including \u8220\'20\'1Cskeleton crews\u8221\'20\'1D, only to encounter \u8220\'20\'1Cpretty much crickets\u8221\'20\'1D, according to Crockett. When they declared their intent to unionize, he said, executives started visiting the store and asking what they could do improve conditions. \u8220\'20\'1CThe moment we voted,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, \u8220\'20\'1Cthat completely stopped.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Contacted for comment, Daunt claimed he had only pointed out potential downsides of unionizing to workers in town halls, and disputed criticisms from workers about delays in contract negotiations \u8211\'20\'13 characterizing such criticism as evidence of downsides to unions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy argument to the booksellers has been very simple: we have no disagreement with the fundamentals of what is being asked for, and indeed have pivoted the company precisely to achieve them,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote. \u8220\'20\'1COnly a successful business, after all, can deliver the investments necessary to improve pay and the physical condition of our stores.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn this endeavor, I see both potential upsides and downsides to the addition of a union. The most obvious potential upside is to have a clearer articulation of bookseller aspirations. Equally, there are potential downsides, notably if it causes unnecessary confrontation between \u8216\'20\'18management\u8217\'20\'19 and \u8216\'20\'18workers\u8217\'20\'19 and the fact that low-paid booksellers will have to pay significant dues to the union, all other things being equal reducing their pay.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240401ek41000xf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
