\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Vermont senator Bernie Sanders introduces four-day workweek bill\b0\par\par\uc2 Martin Pengelly in Washington \par \uc2 635 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 March 2024\par 13:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Independent lawmaker says it\u8217\'20\'19s time for workers to have a better quality of life with a 32-hour workweek without loss of pay\par \par \uc2 Bernie Sanders, the independent senator from Vermont who twice ran for the Democratic presidential nomination, introduced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sanders.senate.gov/wp-content/uploads/32-Hour-Workweek-Act_Fact-Sheet_FINAL.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a bill}}}\uc2  to establish a four-day US working week.\par \par \uc2 Studies and pilot programmes have shown that four-day workweeks can increase productivity and happiness. Given Republican control of the House and a Senate split 51-49 in favour of Democrats, however, the legislation stands little chance of success.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMoving to a 32-hour workweek with no loss of pay is not a radical idea,\u8221\'20\'1D Sanders {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sanders.senate.gov/press-releases/news-sanders-introduces-legislation-to-enact-a-32-hour-workweek-with-no-loss-in-pay/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  on Thursday. \u8220\'20\'1CToday, American workers are over 400% more productive than they were in the 1940s. And yet millions of Americans are working longer hours for lower wages than they were decades ago.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat has got to change. The financial gains from the major advancements in artificial intelligence, automation and new technology must benefit the working class, not just corporate chief executives and wealthy stockholders on Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is time to reduce the stress level in our country and allow Americans to enjoy a better quality of life. It is time for a 32-hour workweek with no loss in pay.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The legislation is co-sponsored by Laphonza Butler, a Democratic senator from California, and \uc2 Mark Takano\uc2 , a House Democrat from the same state.\par \par \uc2 Butler said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sanders.senate.gov/wp-content/uploads/WIL241041.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Thirty-Two-Hour Workweek Act}}}\uc2  would allow hard-working Americans to spend more time with their families while protecting their wages and making sure profits aren\u8217\'20\'19t only going to a select few.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Takano hailed \u8220\'20\'1Ctransformative legislation that will be a win for both workers and workplaces\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Sanders\u8217\'20\'19 office hailed endorsements from unions and campaign groups and cited predictions of a shorter workweek from business giants including \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 , founder of \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , and \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 , chief executive of \uc2 JP Morgan Chase\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis bill underscores the escalating trend towards diminishing work hours,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Dale Whelehan, chief executive of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.4dayweek.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 4 Day Week Global}}}\uc2 , a pressure group.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIncreasing evidence firmly supports that reducing working hours yields beneficial outcomes for businesses, individuals and the broader community \u8230\'20\'26 We are thrilled to support this endeavour \u8230\'20\'26 marking further progress towards a future of work that prioritises sustainable human performance and wellbeing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Also on Thursday, Sanders staged a hearing of his Senate health, education, labor and pensions committee.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne of the issues that we have to talk about is stress in this country, the fact that so many people are going to work exhausted physically and mentally,\u8221\'20\'1D Sanders said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd the fact that we have not changed the Fair Labor Standards Act \u8211\'20\'13 this was in 1940 \u8211\'20\'13 we came up with the 40-hour workweek in 1940. Who is going to deny that the economy has \u8230\'20\'26 fundamentally and radically changed over that period of time?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  of Louisiana, the ranking Republican on the committee, said 32-hour workweeks could work for individual businesses but should not be mandated.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe won\u8217\'20\'19t maintain the status of being the world\u8217\'20\'19s wealthiest nation if we kneecap the American economy with something which purports to be good for the American worker but indeed will lead to offshoring of jobs seeking for a lower-cost labour force,\u8221\'20\'1D Cassidy said.\par \par \uc2 Shawn Fain, president of the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2 , had fiery words in return: \u8220\'20\'1CThe truth is, working-class people aren\u8217\'20\'19t lazy. They\u8217\'20\'19re fed up.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re fed up with being left behind and stripped of dignity as wealth inequality in this nation, this world, spirals out of control. They\u8217\'20\'19re fed up in America. In America, three families have as much wealth as the bottom 50% of citizens in this nation. That is criminal.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240315ek3f00235\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
FF
