\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Florida passes \u8216\'20\'18cruel\u8217\'20\'19 bill curbing water and shade protections for workers\b0\par\par\uc2 Aliya Uteuova, Nina Lakhani and Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,189 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 March 2024\par 19:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Industry pressure has led state to prevent any city, county or municipality from adopting laborer protections amid extreme heat\par \par \uc2 The Florida legislature passed a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.flsenate.gov/Session/Bill/2024/433/?Tab=BillHistory"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bill}}}\uc2  on Friday that prevents any city, county, or municipality in the state from adopting legislation aimed at protecting outdoor workers from extreme heat, prompting many to call out lawmakers for being \u8220\'20\'1Ccruel\u8221\'20\'1D to the \u8220\'20\'1Cmost vulnerable workers\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Efforts to ensure potentially life-saving water breaks, rest and shade for construction and agriculture workers have failed largely due to industry pressure, a growing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/19/climate-crisis-extreme-heat-regulations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 trend}}}\uc2  across south-western states, where {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/aug/01/heat-related-deaths-us-temperatures-heatwave"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 heat related deaths}}}\uc2  are on the rise.\par \par \uc2 At present, there are no federal standards to protect outdoor workers in the US from heat and humidity \u8211\'20\'13 which can be deadly and is getting worse due to global heating. Protections therefore vary greatly from state to state.\par \par \uc2 The newly passed legislation will affect roughly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ucsusa.org/about/news/extreme-heat-could-threaten-84-billion-annually-florida-outdoor-worker-earnings"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2 million}}}\uc2  outdoor workers across the state and render existing local protections \u8220\'20\'1Cvoid and prohibited\u8221\'20\'1D from 1 July.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis legislation is cruel,\u8221\'20\'1D said Oscar Londo\u241\'00\'F1o, executive director of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://yaleclimateconnections.org/2023/07/the-fight-to-protect-miamis-outdoor-workers-against-the-deadly-heat/#:~:text=In%202021%2C%20the%20organization%20launched,re%20subjected%20to%20extreme%20heat."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 worker advocacy WeCount!}}}\uc2 , which has been pushing for a Miami-Dade heat ordinance. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a bad faith attempt to keep labor conditions very low for some of the most vulnerable workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Miami-Dade county has an estimated 300,000 outdoor workers, more than any other county in Florida. Londo\u241\'00\'F1o added: \u8220\'20\'1CEvery single year, it\u8217\'20\'19s going to get hotter and hotter. Many more workers\u8217\'20\'19 lives are going to be at risk. We will see fatalities, because of what Florida Republicans chose to do this week.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In 2021, the Biden administration ordered \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration (Osha)\uc2  to develop workplace heat standards which have not yet been produced, and could take {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/jul/31/heat-protections-workers-big-business-lobbies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 years}}}\uc2  to do so. This slow process puts even more pressure on state and local governments to enact their own heat regulations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo many of our outdoors workers are just looking for some basic safety and guidance,\u8221\'20\'1D said Florida state representative \uc2 Anna Eskamani\uc2 , who voted against the bill. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have generations of folks who are experiencing heat stress because they\u8217\'20\'19re outside, kidney failure because there are no bathroom breaks.bathroom breaks. I think preempting [safety requirements] in a state as hot as Florida just doesn\u8217\'20\'19t make sense.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In 2021, WeCount! launched the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://yaleclimateconnections.org/2023/07/the-fight-to-protect-miamis-outdoor-workers-against-the-deadly-heat/#:~:text=In%202021%2C%20the%20organization%20launched,re%20subjected%20to%20extreme%20heat."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u161\'00\'A1Qu\u233\'00\'E9 Calor! }}}\uc2  (how hot) campaign to secure heat protections for farm workers and construction workers in Miami-Dade county in response to rising temperatures.\par \par \uc2 Momentum was building, and Miami Dade county was preparing to vote on a heat protection ordinance this month. The vote, which was originally scheduled for November 2023, was postponed due to opposition from industry groups. A few days after the deferral, Florida Republicans filed state legislation to preemptively ban local standards.\par \par \uc2 Londo\u241\'00\'F1o said: \u8220\'20\'1COver the past few months we saw growing industry opposition from billionaire developers, powerful industry associations and their lobbyists who don\u8217\'20\'19t want to see any level of worker progress and advancement in the state.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Republican-controlled senate, which usually rails against top-down federal mandates, argued that the legislation will end the state\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cpatchwork of regulation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Instead, employers will be subject to the federal Osha\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cgeneral duty rule\u8221\'20\'1D, which requires employers to provide workplaces that are \u8220\'20\'1Cfree from recognized hazards that are causing or are likely to cause death or serious physical harm\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Extreme heat is one of those hazards, but across the country it has been rarely enforced.\par \par \uc2 Marleine Bastien, the commissioner, who co-sponsored the now stalled Miami-Dade heat ordinance, previously told the Guardian that \u8220\'20\'1CWorkers are at heightened risk for heat related illnesses because it\u8217\'20\'19s compounded when they have to work long hours in brutal Miami heat\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Proponents of the Miami-Dade heat ordinance followed the lead of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.austintexas.gov/department/rest-break-ordinance"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Austin}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://dallascityhall.com/government/Council%20Meeting%20Documents/2014/DallasRestBreak_090314.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dallas}}}\uc2 , the only places in Texas that passed mandatory rest breaks for construction workers, but other localities might not be able to pass such protections anymore. Last summer, Greg Abbott signed a bill deemed by critics \u8216\'20\'18Death Star Law\u8217\'20\'19 which preempts city governments from passing local workplace safety mandates. A district judge ruled that the state law is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2023/08/30/texas-death-star-bill-unconstitutional/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unconstitutional}}}\uc2 , but despite that, HB 2127 went into {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texasattorneygeneral.gov/news/releases/texas-law-enabling-citizens-sue-over-local-ordinances-pre-empted-state-laws-takes-effect#:~:text=HB%202127%2C%20a%20Texas%20law,judge%20declared%20HB%202127%20unconstitutional."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 effect}}}\uc2  on 1 September 2023.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.shrm.org/resourcesandtools/tools-and-samples/exreq/pages/details.aspx?erid=443#:~:text=Cal%2FOSHA's%20heat%20illness%20prevention,in%20addition%20to%20regular%20breaks."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 California}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://osha.oregon.gov/OSHARules/adopted/2022/ao3-2022-text-alh-heat.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Oregon}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.lni.wa.gov/safety-health/safety-rules/rulemaking-stakeholder-information/ambient-heat-exposure-rulemaking"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington}}}\uc2  are the only states that have protections for outdoor workers from extreme heat. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cdle.colorado.gov/sites/cdle/files/7%20CCR%201103-15%20Agricultural%20Labor%20Conditions%20Rules%20%20-%20Statement%20of%20Basis%20and%20Purpose%20%5BFINAL%5D%201.31.22.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Colorado}}}\uc2  has a heat standard that only applies to agricultural workers, while {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://mn.gov/admin/assets/heat_stress_guide_tcm36-207189.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Minnesota}}}\uc2  has a standard that only applies to indoor workers. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.leg.state.nv.us/App/NELIS/REL/82nd2023/Bill/10436/Votes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nevada}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Legislation/Details/HB0722/?ys=2020rs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Maryland}}}\uc2  legislatures voted in favor of heat standards, but both bills have since stalled, failing to advance to governors\u8217\'20\'19 desks.\par \par \uc2 Outdoor workers are experiencing hotter and hotter conditions each {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/aug/17/outdoor-jobs-us-heat-impacts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 year}}}\uc2 . Between 2011 and 2021, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/opub/ted/2023/36-work-related-deaths-due-to-environmental-heat-exposure-in-2021.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 436 people died}}}\uc2  due to work-related heat exposure, according to the \uc2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , and data on occupational heat injuries and fatalities are likely \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2021-10-27/pdf/2021-23250.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vast underestimates}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 People of color are especially vulnerable to extreme heat. More than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ucsusa.org/sites/default/files/2021-08/Too%20Hot%20to%20Work_8-13.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 40%}}}\uc2  of all outdoor workers in the US are Black or Hispanic, despite accounting for only 32% of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s population. In Florida outdoor workers include as many as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.floridahealth.gov/environmental-health/migrant-farmworker-housing/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 200,000 migrant farmworkers }}}\uc2  toiling in the fields, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/oes/2019/may/oes373011.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 83,000 landscape gardeners}}}\uc2  and almost {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/oes/current/oes472061.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 70,000 construction workers.}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe majority of nursery workers are immigrants like me,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sandra Ascencio, 51, who came to Florida from El Salvador and has been working in fields and nurseries in the Miami-Dade area for 20 years, most recently picking tomatoes.\par \par \uc2 She told the Guardian in November 2023 that she suffered a heat stroke in 2018 and was hospitalized for a week due to intense dehydration: \u8220\'20\'1COnce you experience heat stroke, your body is already damaged and every single time you step into the heat, your body reacts because it feels like it\u8217\'20\'19s going to happen to you again.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Critics have pointed out the hypocrisy in Florida Republicans pushing the legislation, despite passing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.news4jax.com/news/florida/2020/06/24/florida-enacts-heat-stroke-protections-for-student-athletes-named-for-teen-who-died/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a measure in 2020}}}\uc2  to provide heat protections for student athletes following the heat related death of a high school football athlete.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Goodell, author of The Heat Will Kill You First: Life and Death on a Scorched Planet, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/jeffgoodell/status/1765089168882016279?s=20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tweeted}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CInsane, inhuman, racist, but not at all surprising. Florida state Senate passing a bill *ensuring* that the heat will kill outdoor workers. This is 19th century stuff, as barbaric as kids working in coal mines.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Without radical action to curtail heat-trapping greenhouse gas emission, the increased intensity, frequency and geographic spread of extreme heat would cause a three- to four-fold increase in hazardous heat exposure for outdoor workers, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ucsusa.org/resources/too-hot-to-work#read-online-content"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 research by UCS}}}\uc2 . Previous studies have found that outdoor workers in the US have up to 35 times the risk of dying from heat exposure than the general population.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is critical to have heat protection standards in places given summers like last year \u8211\'20\'13 especially in places like Florida and Texas \u8211\'20\'13 because extreme heat is only going to get more frequent and more severe over time,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kristina Dahl, principal climate scientist for the climate and energy program at the \uc2 Union of Concerned Scientists\uc2  (\uc2 UCS\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240308ek38002uz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
