\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Major US corporations threaten to return labor to \u8216\'20\'18law of the jungle\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,272 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 March 2024\par 11:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s and \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  are among businesses challenging the constitutionality of the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\par \par \uc2 Upset by the surge in union drives, several of the best-known corporations in the US are seeking to cripple the country\u8217\'20\'19s top labor watchdog, the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ), by having it declared unconstitutional. Some labor experts warn that if those efforts succeed, US labor relations might return to \u8220\'20\'1Cthe law of the jungle\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  as well as Amazon, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s have filed legal papers that advance novel arguments aimed at hobbling and perhaps shutting down the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the federal agency that enforces labor rights and oversees unionization efforts. Those companies are eager to thwart the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  after it accused Amazon, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s of breaking the law in battling against unionization and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/01/03/business/spacex-elon-musk-nlrb-workers.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 accused SpaceX of illegally firing}}}\uc2  eight workers for criticizing Musk.\par \par \uc2 Roger King, a longtime management-side lawyer who is senior labor counsel for the HR Policy Association, said \u8220\'20\'1Cit will be a lose-lose\u8221\'20\'1D if the federal courts overturn the 89-year-old National Labor Relations Act, which has governed labor relations since Franklin Roosevelt was president. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ll have the law of the jungle, the law of the streets,\u8221\'20\'1D King said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt will be who has the most power. It\u8217\'20\'19s potential for chaos.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kate Andrias, a \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  law professor, said workers would be hurt if the courts issue a sweeping decision that declares both the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  and the National Labor Relations Act unconstitutional. \u8220\'20\'1CWithout them, workers will be even worse off,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s critical that they continue to exist to protect the basic right to organize and engage in collective bargaining. This is an assault on rights we have considered fundamental since the New Deal.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some worker advocates have voiced surprise that these companies are seeking to hobble the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  when, in their view, the labor board is already too weak, its penalties toothless. The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  can\u8217\'20\'19t fine companies even one dollar for breaking the law \u8211\'20\'13 for instance, by illegally firing workers for supporting a union.\par \par \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , Amazon and Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s have put forward three main arguments for holding the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  unconstitutional: it penalizes companies without a jury trial, exercises executive powers without the president being free to remove board officials, and violates the separation of powers by exercising executive, legislative and judicial functions. This corporate attack is part of a wave of lawsuits challenging the constitutionality of various federal agencies that regulate business.\par \par \uc2 Andrias said one factor spurring the challenges to the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  is that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe supreme court over the last decade, but especially in the last couple of years, has signaled a hostility to the administrative state and has radically remade administrative law in a way that would curb the government\u8217\'20\'19s ability to protect workers and consumers. Companies are now trying to capitalize on the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another reason for these anti-\uc2 NLRB\uc2  efforts is that many companies believe that President Biden\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has swung too far to the left. The board is usually pro-business under Republican presidents and pro-labor under Democratic ones. King said that \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and the other companies hope the courts will provide a \u8220\'20\'1Cfirewall\u8221\'20\'1D against the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s leftward shift.\par \par \uc2 William B Gould IV, who was chair of the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  under President Clinton, said anti-union \u8220\'20\'1Ctech billionaires\u8221\'20\'1D like Musk and \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Chave fueled these efforts\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t like unions because they create {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/dec/11/uaw-tesla-elon-musk-unions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a lords-versus-peasants mentality}}}\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Gould said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt certainly seems that Musk is trying to hold down the peasants here.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Benjamin Sachs, a labor law professor at Harvard, said it\u8217\'20\'19s troubling that Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s and \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , which hold themselves out as progressive, \u8220\'20\'1Care willing to sign on to legal theories that threaten not only labor rights, but our ability to have clean air, regulate food safety and assure safe and healthy workplaces\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  led the way in challenging the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 , calling it an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/sites/britneynguyen/2024/01/04/spacex-claims-national-labor-board-is-unconstitutional-in-new-suit/?sh=6efd8e437d21"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cunconstitutionally structured agency\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 . In a lawsuit filed in Texas, \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  asserted: \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s current way of functioning is miles away from the traditional understanding of the separation of powers.\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  quoted James Madison in The Federalist Papers: \u8220\'20\'1CThe accumulation of all powers legislative, executive and judiciary in the same hands\u8221\'20\'1D is \u8220\'20\'1Cthe very definition of tyranny\u8221\'20\'1D. (\uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  are fighting over whether a federal judge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/legal/government/spacex-nlrb-duel-over-proper-venue-attack-agencys-structure-2024-02-23/#:~:text=Feb%2023%20(Reuters)%20%2D%20SpaceX,prefer%20forums%20with%20favorable%20caselaw.%22"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in Texas or California}}}\uc2  should hear the case.)\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  declined the \uc2 Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s request for comment. It has yet to file a legal response defending its constitutionality.\par \par \uc2 Andrias defended the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 , noting that ever since the New Deal, the supreme court has allowed federal agencies to exercise various functions. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing is part of a broad attack on Congress being able to design agencies in the way it deems most effective,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The corporations maintain that the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s administrative law judges should be deemed unconstitutional because, they argue, those judges exercise many executive functions and the president can\u8217\'20\'19t readily remove them. The \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s defenders say the judges exercise judicial, not executive, powers. The board\u8217\'20\'19s defenders further argue that the cases \uc2 NLRB\uc2  judges hear don\u8217\'20\'19t require a jury trial because the judges can\u8217\'20\'19t assess any financial penalties, except back pay to workers who were illegally fired or demoted.\par \par \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and the other corporations might someday appeal these cases to the supreme court. Legal experts say it\u8217\'20\'19s hard to predict whether the courts will uphold the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s constitutionality, rule that parts of its structure are unconstitutional, or wholly void the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  and National Labor Relations Act.\par \par \uc2 The HR Policy Association\u8217\'20\'19s King said that if \uc2 NLRB\uc2  judges are declared unconstitutional, that would essentially halt those judges hearing hundreds of cases each year in which board officials first accused companies and unions of violating labor laws. The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has, for instance, filed 128 complaints against \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , alleging more than 1,000 instances of illegality, including that the company unlawfully fired dozens of baristas for backing unionization. (\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  denies any wrongdoing.) Under this scenario, King said, the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  could still oversee unionization elections.\par \par \uc2 Steven Swirsky, a management-side employment lawyer with Epstein Becker Green, said, \u8220\'20\'1CIt would be pretty problematic for employers and unions if there isn\u8217\'20\'19t some structure in place to administer the functions now done by the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D He said that federal judges won\u8217\'20\'19t want to handle all the cases now litigated before the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 . If the labor board is ruled unconstitutional, workers who feel they were illegally fired or otherwise disciplined for backing a union might file a flood of lawsuits in federal courts.\par \par \uc2 Harvard\u8217\'20\'19s Sachs said that in seeking to have the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  ruled unconstitutional, \u8220\'20\'1Ccorporations should be careful what they wish for\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you eliminate the National Labor Relations Act, then you\u8217\'20\'19ll have 50 states empowered to enact their own labor laws,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. The result: California and other blue states might enact laws that make it far easier to unionize than current federal law does.\par \par \uc2 Legal scholars said if the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  is ruled unconstitutional in whole or in part, that might require congressional action to fix it, but with Congress so dysfunctional, that might be hard to do.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor most workers and unions, the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  is the only game in town,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gould, the board\u8217\'20\'19s former chairman. \u8220\'20\'1CMost employers won\u8217\'20\'19t recognize and bargain with unions without the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  requiring them to.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240310ek3a000xe\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Date\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/2021 to 06/10/2026\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clve